window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"underground-resistance-riot":["Early Underground Resistance at its most confrontational. Where Detroit's first techno records floated and shimmered, 'Riot' is hard, fast and abrasive: pounding machine drums, a relentless, grinding synth line and blasts of noise that feel more like a siren than a melody. There is no chorus and almost no release; the track holds its tension from start to finish, as if the whole thing were a warning. It is dance music that sounds deliberately angry, built to be played loud in a dark room.","Underground Resistance formed in Detroit around 1990 around 'Mad' Mike Banks and Jeff Mills, soon joined by Robert Hood. The collective ran its own label, refused press photos and wore masks, and printed militant statements on its sleeves attacking the music industry and what it called the programmers of mediocrity. Detroit itself was in deep economic crisis, its factories closing and its centre emptying. Records like 'Riot' fed into the harder techno scenes then forming in Berlin, Rotterdam and London.","The title is not decoration. UR presented itself as a resistance movement using sound as a weapon, and the brutal texture is the political content: no vocal message is needed because the track itself stages an uprising. Listen for how little changes and how much pressure that creates. The template of hard, minimal, repetitive aggression here points straight toward what Mills and Hood would do alone a few years later, stripping techno to its bones.","Among DJs the early UR records were instant weapons, and the collective's independence and politics made it a model for underground electronic labels worldwide. Critics usually value the later, more melodic and jazzy UR, such as 'Hi-Tech Jazz' and 'Knights of the Jaguar', above these harsh early tracks, but many now see the early material as the missing link between Detroit's first wave and the hard techno of the 1990s.","Play it loud, on good speakers, and do not wait for a drop; the point is the sustained pressure. Then set it beside Derrick May's 'Strings of Life' to hear how far Detroit techno moved in four years, and beside Jeff Mills's 'The Bells' and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation to hear where the aggression went next."],"destinys-child-survivor":["The trio lineup of Beyoncé Knowles, Kelly Rowland and Michelle Williams at full commercial power. The singles are built on hard, clipped beats and staccato vocal stacks: 'Independent Women Part I' with its rapid-fire verses, 'Survivor' with its marching drums and defiant chorus, 'Bootylicious' riding a borrowed rock guitar riff. Between them come slower ballads, a cover of the Bee Gees' 'Emotion' and a closing gospel section. The recognisable sound is Beyoncé's tightly syncopated lead over three-part harmony packed close together.","Destiny's Child had lost two original members in early 2000 and replaced them, and the public turmoil became material. 'Independent Women Part I', recorded for the Charlie's Angels film, had already spent weeks at number one before the album came out on Columbia in 2001. Beyoncé, not yet twenty, co-wrote and co-produced much of the record, taking more creative control than most R&B singers her age. It entered the Billboard 200 at number one and became the group's peak before its members went solo.","The title song answers the lineup changes directly: the idea reportedly came from a radio joke comparing the group to the TV show Survivor, where contestants are voted off one by one, and it turns mockery into an anthem. 'Bootylicious' is built on the guitar riff of Stevie Nicks's 'Edge of Seventeen', and Nicks herself appeared in its video. Listen to the gospel tracks at the end for the church harmony underneath the pop machinery.","Reviews were mixed at the time; some critics found the album uneven and its many songs about independence repetitive, while praising the singles. Its commercial success was enormous, and 'Survivor' won a Grammy. Later assessments stress its place in Beyoncé's development, the moment she became the group's writer and producer as well as its voice, and hear its self-reliance themes as a preview of her solo work.","Start with 'Survivor' and 'Bootylicious' back to back; listen for the tightness of the vocal rhythms more than the melodies. Then play the Bee Gees cover to hear the three voices open out. Follow with Beyoncé's 'Crazy in Love', only two years later, to hear the leader step forward alone."],"pearl-jam-vs":["Tighter, angrier and more varied than Ten. 'Go' opens at a sprint, 'Animal' and 'Blood' are all clenched riffing, and 'Rearviewmirror' builds from a strummed escape song to a thunderous climax. Against them sit the acoustic 'Daughter' and the gentle 'Elderly Woman Behind the Counter in a Small Town', one of Eddie Vedder's most tender character sketches. Dave Abbruzzese's drumming is busier and funkier than on the debut, and the guitars of Stone Gossard and Mike McCready trade rhythm and lead with real bite.","Ten had made Pearl Jam one of the biggest bands in the world, and the pressure was intense. They recorded Vs. with producer Brendan O'Brien in 1993, largely at a studio in rural California, working more collectively and faster than before. On its release that October it sold close to a million copies in its first week, a record at the time. The band then refused to make music videos for it and began pulling away from the fame machine, a stance that shaped their decade.","Several songs are pointed social portraits hidden inside rock songs. 'W.M.A.' concerns police treatment of Black men, 'Glorified G' mocks a bandmate's gun enthusiasm, and 'Daughter' describes a child whose learning difficulties go unrecognised and who is punished for them. The cover photograph, a sheep pressed against a wire fence, taken by bassist Jeff Ament, is the album's image of feeling trapped. The record was nearly named after a phrase from 'Animal', changed late.","Reviews were largely strong, and many critics then and since have preferred it to Ten for its energy and range. The refusal to promote it through MTV was read at the time as either integrity or perversity. It is now generally seen as the peak of the band's first phase, the point at which grunge's most commercial band proved it could be as raw as its peers.","Put on 'Rearviewmirror' first and wait for the final minute, when the whole band lifts. Then 'Elderly Woman' for the opposite pole. Compare with Nirvana's In Utero, released weeks earlier, two bands responding to sudden fame in opposite ways. Brendan O'Brien's warm, live-sounding mix rewards loud playback."],"pearl-jam-vitalogy":["Pearl Jam's strangest major record. Punk blasts like 'Spin the Black Circle', a song about the joy of vinyl, and 'Not for You' sit beside the reflective 'Nothingman', 'Corduroy' with its rolling riff and slow-burning release, and 'Better Man', the band's most immediately singable song. In between come oddities: 'Bugs', Vedder muttering over an accordion; 'Pry, To'; and the seven-minute sound collage 'Hey Foxymophandlemama, That's Me', built from recorded voices. The album swings between catharsis and deliberate awkwardness.","It was made in 1994 amid turmoil. The band was fighting Ticketmaster over service charges, a battle that reached the US Congress and crippled their touring. Kurt Cobain's suicide that April shook the whole Seattle scene. Drummer Dave Abbruzzese was dismissed during the sessions. The album was released on vinyl two weeks before CD, a gesture of loyalty to the format, in packaging modelled on an old home-medicine book called Vitalogy, full of antique health advice.","The packaging and the music argue with each other: the quaint medical book promises vitality, while the songs are about fame as a sickness and the struggle to stay sane. 'Better Man' was written by Vedder as a teenager, long before the band existed, and he was reportedly reluctant to include it because it sounded too commercial. 'Immortality' was widely read as a response to Cobain's death, a reading Vedder rejected.","It entered the charts at number one and sold heavily, but critics were divided over its experiments; the collage tracks in particular were called self-indulgent. 'Spin the Black Circle' won a Grammy. Over time Vitalogy has grown in standing, and many fans now name it the band's best album, precisely because it refuses to be comfortable. It marks their turn away from grunge stardom toward a long, self-directed career.","Start with 'Corduroy', then 'Better Man', then 'Nothingman': the album's emotional core. Once you know those, play it in order and let the odd pieces act as breathing space and provocation. Neil Young, whom the band would soon record with, is the model for this mix of rage and fragility; try his Tonight's the Night next."],"the-weeknd-after-hours":["A night-drive concept album in fourteen tracks, moving from dark R&B to gleaming 1980s synth-pop. The opening songs, 'Alone Again' and 'Too Late', are cold and spacious; the middle turns to fast, bright pop in 'Blinding Lights', 'In Your Eyes', with its wailing saxophone, and 'Save Your Tears'. 'Hardest to Love' sets his falsetto over skittering drum and bass. The six-minute title track slowly builds from a whisper to a pounding dance groove. Abel Tesfaye's high, pleading voice is the constant thread.","Released in March 2020, just as pandemic lockdowns closed cities worldwide, the album's empty-streets mood suddenly matched real life. Tesfaye, a Toronto artist who had risen from anonymous mixtapes in 2011, worked with Max Martin and Oscar Holter for the pop tracks, and also with Metro Boomin, Daniel Lopatin of Oneohtrix Point Never and Kevin Parker of Tame Impala. He promoted it through a character in a red suit with a bloodied face, carried across videos and performances like a film.","Listen for the film references and the story. The character spirals through Las Vegas and Los Angeles in a single night of regret and excess, and the songs follow that arc, from loneliness to euphoria to collapse. 'Scared to Live' borrows the melody of Elton John's 'Your Song', and the neon synths and gated drums nod to 1980s film soundtracks. Lopatin's hand is audible in the album's glassy, slightly unreal textures, a link to experimental electronic music.","Critics praised it as his most cohesive record, and 'Blinding Lights' became one of the most successful singles in chart history. When the album received no Grammy nominations at all, Tesfaye called the process corrupt, and the controversy contributed to the Recording Academy abolishing its secret nomination committees. Some listeners find its retro sound too close to pastiche; many hear it as the definitive pop record of the lockdown year.","Listen in order, ideally at night and in one go; the sequence matters. Notice the change of light when 'Blinding Lights' arrives after the brooding first half. Then go back to House of Balloons to hear how much he has moved, and forward from Michael Jackson's Thriller and Prince's Purple Rain, the records this one keeps glancing at."],"tool-undertow":["Tool's first full album, heavier and more direct than what followed but already unlike other metal. Adam Jones's guitar plays thick, grinding riffs rather than solos; Paul D'Amour's bass and Danny Carey's drums lock into slow, heavy grooves; Maynard James Keenan moves from a murmur to a scream within a verse. 'Sober', with its creeping bassline and sudden explosion, is the best-known song; 'Prison Sex' and 'Intolerance' are compact and brutal. Henry Rollins guests on 'Bottom'. Long closing pieces point toward the band's later epics.","Tool formed in Los Angeles in 1990 and released Undertow in 1993 on Zoo Entertainment, produced by Sylvia Massy. It arrived at the height of grunge and alternative metal, and the band rode that wave onto the Lollapalooza tour. Jones, a former special-effects artist for Hollywood films, directed the unsettling stop-motion videos for 'Sober' and 'Prison Sex', which set the band's visual identity. Some large retailers refused the original artwork, and the band issued an alternative cover showing only a barcode.","The album ends with a practical joke: after 'Flood', a long run of silent tracks leads to track 69, 'Disgustipated', a bizarre spoken sermon about the rights of carrots followed by minutes of crickets. It announces a band that would always play games with format. 'Prison Sex' is a song about the cycle of child abuse, and its video was restricted by MTV. Listen to how 'Sober' holds its tension on the bass alone before the guitars arrive.","It was a steady seller rather than an overnight hit and eventually went platinum. Critics at first often lumped Tool in with grunge-era metal, and the album is usually rated below Ænima and Lateralus. But it established the band's core sound and its refusal of rock clichés, and 'Sober' remains one of the defining alternative-metal singles of the 1990s.","Start with 'Sober' and follow the bass from the first bar. Then 'Intolerance' and 'Prison Sex' for the band at its most concise. If you know later Tool, notice how much more the guitar dominates here. Compare with Black Sabbath's Master of Reality for the ancestry of the slow, heavy tread."],"tool-aenima":["A seventy-seven-minute album that turns Tool into a progressive band. Long songs such as 'Eulogy', 'Forty Six & 2', 'Pushit' and the closing thirteen-minute 'Third Eye' move through odd meters, quiet passages and huge climaxes; Danny Carey's drums become polyrhythmic and orchestral. New bassist Justin Chancellor gives the low end a melodic, riff-writing voice. Short interludes, including a German-language recipe delivered like a political speech in 'Die Eier von Satan', break up the heaviness. 'Stinkfist' and the title track are the concise, hard-hitting singles.","Recorded in Hollywood with producer David Bottrill and released in 1996, Ænima followed the arrival of Chancellor in place of Paul D'Amour. The band had grown into its interests: psychology, especially Carl Jung, altered states, occult symbolism and a disgust with Los Angeles culture. The album is dedicated to the comedian Bill Hicks, who died in 1994 and whose routines about California sliding into the ocean inspire the title track's apocalypse. Its lenticular cover shifts image as you tilt it.","'Die Eier von Satan' sounds like a furious rally, with a crowd roaring back, but the German text is only a recipe for biscuits; the joke is about how a tone of voice can make anything sound sinister. 'Forty Six & 2' refers to a fringe idea about human evolution to a new number of chromosomes, tied to Jung's notion of confronting one's shadow. Bill Hicks's own voice is sampled at the opening of 'Third Eye'.","It was a commercial and critical breakthrough, going multi-platinum, and the title track won a Grammy for Best Metal Performance. It is widely considered one of the key metal albums of the 1990s and the start of Tool's long-form style that Lateralus refined. Some listeners find the interludes and length indulgent; others hear the whole as a single deliberately paced experience, and its influence on progressive metal is hard to overstate.","Begin with 'Forty Six & 2': follow Chancellor's bass line, then listen for the moment around the midpoint when the drums take over. Then 'Eulogy' and 'Third Eye' for the long forms. Play the album loud and whole at least once. Compare with King Crimson's Larks' Tongues in Aspic for the ancestry of the shifting meters."],"mobb-deep-hell-on-earth":["The third Mobb Deep album is the sound of The Infamous pushed further into the dark. Havoc, who produced nearly all of it, builds each track from a short, detuned loop, usually a sliver of piano or strings, sitting over a dry, heavy drum pattern with almost nothing else in the room. Prodigy and Havoc trade cold, clipped verses about life in the Queensbridge housing projects. Guests include Nas, Raekwon and Method Man. Recognise it by the title track's creeping, off-kilter keys and the absence of any hook built for radio.","Released by Loud Records in November 1996, a year after The Infamous had made the Queensbridge duo the standard-bearers of hard New York rap, Hell on Earth arrived in the tensest season in hip-hop's history: weeks after Tupac Shakur's death and in the thick of the East Coast–West Coast feud. The album entered the top ten of the Billboard chart, proof that uncompromising street records could now sell on their own terms, as Wu-Tang's solo projects and Nas's second album were also showing.","Listen to how little Havoc actually uses. Many beats are a single bar of sampled sound, slightly out of tune with the drums, and the queasiness comes from that mismatch rather than from any added effect. On G.O.D. Pt. III he turns to the theme from Scarface, reshaping Giorgio Moroder's synthesiser line into something grimmer. Drop a Gem on 'Em, a pointed reply to Tupac, reached listeners in the shadow of his death.","Critics received it as a worthy sequel rather than a leap forward, and for years it lived in the shadow of The Infamous. Its reputation has grown since: many listeners now hear it as the purer statement, with Havoc's production at its most austere and Prodigy's writing at its most unsentimental. The debate is whether that consistency is a strength or a narrowness; few disagree that it defined the Queensbridge sound.","Start with the title track, then Extortion with Method Man to hear the album at its most claustrophobic. Listen on good headphones: the low end is the point, and the thin, wobbling samples sit on top of it like frost. Hear it next to Nas's It Was Written, recorded in the same neighbourhood that year, to hear two opposite answers to the same streets."],"robert-hood-internal-empire":["Internal Empire is techno with the furniture removed. Each track is built from a kick drum, a clipped hi-hat, perhaps one short synthesiser figure and a handful of percussive clicks, repeated and nudged rather than developed. There are no breakdowns, no melodies to hum and no builds to a climax; instead a pattern shifts by a single element every few bars, and the listener's attention sharpens to catch it. Recognise it by its dryness: short sounds, almost no reverb, and a swing that is felt more than heard.","Robert Hood had been a member of Detroit's militant Underground Resistance collective before leaving with Jeff Mills in the early 1990s. He released this debut album on Tresor, the Berlin label and club that had become Detroit techno's second home after the fall of the Wall. In 1994 he also founded his own label, M-Plant. Techno at the time was swelling into rave spectacle; Hood deliberately went the other way, describing his aim as the bare skeleton of the music.","The trick is that the parts are not all the same length. A percussion figure may repeat every three beats against a four-beat kick, so the pattern realigns only occasionally, and the track seems to move although nothing new has entered. That is the same principle Steve Reich used in his phase pieces, reached from the dancefloor rather than the conservatory. Hood has also spoken about the gospel and soul he grew up with; listen for the swing beneath the austerity.","It sold modestly and was admired mainly by DJs and producers, but it is now widely credited, together with the Minimal Nation EP of the same year, as the founding document of minimal techno. The later Berlin minimal scene of the 2000s claimed it as ancestry, sometimes to Hood's irritation, since he always insisted his minimalism was funky and rooted in Black Detroit rather than cool and conceptual.","Do not listen for events; listen for changes. Pick one sound, perhaps the hi-hat, and follow it for a whole track, noticing when it drops out or shifts. Play it loud on speakers with real bass, ideally while moving. Then hear Jeff Mills's Waveform Transmission Vol. 1 to hear the same Detroit starting point pushed toward speed and density instead."],"robert-hood-motor-nighttime-world-3":["The third instalment of Hood's Nighttime World series is a concept album about Detroit itself. Where Internal Empire stripped techno to its bones, Motor lets in atmosphere: slow washes of synthesiser, mournful chords, spoken-voice fragments and passages that barely pulse at all, set between tracks with a full, driving kick. It moves like a drive through an emptied city, alternating stretches of forward motion with pauses to look out of the window. Recognise it by its unusually sombre, cinematic mood for a techno record.","Hood made it at the lowest point in Detroit's modern history. The car industry that had given the city its nickname had nearly collapsed in the 2008 crash, population had fallen by more than half since the 1950s, and the city was heading toward the largest municipal bankruptcy in American history, declared in 2013. Techno had always been Detroit music about machines and work; Hood, by then living in Alabama and also a minister, turned it into an elegy for the factories that had inspired it.","The Nighttime World series began in the 1990s as Hood's outlet for music too slow or too reflective for the dancefloor, so this record belongs to a private strand of his output, running alongside his club tracks for nearly two decades. Listen for how the rhythm itself carries the programme: the most hopeful moments are also the most mechanical, as if the assembly line's pulse were the city's heartbeat.","Reviewers welcomed it as one of Hood's most personal records and one of the few techno albums with an explicit social subject since Underground Resistance's heyday. Some found the slower passages indulgent next to his ruthless 1990s work. It has since been read alongside the wider wave of art about Detroit's decline, and as proof that the pioneers of the genre still had something to say twenty years on.","Hear it in one sitting, in order, ideally at night, as the title suggests. Notice when the kick drum is absent and how its return feels like a car pulling away again. Then go back to Internal Empire to hear how far the same producer has travelled from austerity toward storytelling, and to Juan Atkins's Techno City for the utopian Detroit this record mourns."],"tyler-the-creator-call-me-if-you-get-lost":["After two albums built on lush chords and singing, Tyler returned to rapping hard, framed as a travelogue. DJ Drama shouts over the tracks as host, in the manner of the mixtapes Tyler grew up on, and the record lurches between bruising, sample-heavy beats and soft, jazz-inflected soul. It is organised around Tyler's new persona, Tyler Baudelaire, a wealthy traveller writing home. Recognise it by Drama's bellowed interjections and by two long centrepieces, the nearly ten-minute Sweet / I Thought You Wanted to Dance and the monologue Wilshire.","Released by Columbia in June 2021, while touring was still largely shut down by the pandemic, the album played on the fantasy of international travel that listeners could not have. Its guests stretch across generations, from Lil Wayne and Pharrell Williams to 42 Dugg and Lil Uzi Vert. It went to number one on the Billboard 200 and won the Grammy for Best Rap Album, his second in a row after IGOR, whose placement in the rap category Tyler had publicly criticised as a way of sidelining genre-crossing Black artists.","The DJ Drama framing is a homage with a point: Gangsta Grillz mixtapes of the 2000s were where Southern rap proved itself outside the industry, and Tyler, who has always presented himself as a fan, is placing himself in that lineage. Wilshire, told almost without a chorus over a gently looping beat, recounts in detail a love affair complicated by friendship; it is the album's secret heart, and it pays to hear it in full rather than skip it.","Reviews were among the best of his career, with praise for the rapping and for how easily he moved between registers. Some critics felt the Baudelaire persona was thin set dressing, and a few preferred the cohesion of IGOR. It is now generally regarded as the record that confirmed his range: an artist once dismissed as a provocateur could rap with the best and arrange like a soul producer in the same hour.","Start with Lumberjack for the rapping, then Sweet / I Thought You Wanted to Dance to hear the album turn from swagger to longing, and from hip-hop into something close to lovers' rock. Save Wilshire for an attentive listen, as you would a short story. Compare the travel fantasy with Frank Ocean's Blonde, made by his old Odd Future colleague."],"fugees-blunted-on-reality":["The Fugees' debut sounds like three talented people being pulled in several directions at once. Lauryn Hill, Wyclef Jean and Pras Michel rap in turns over hard, busy beats, with flashes of the reggae, Haitian and soul influences that would later define them, and Hill's singing appears only in glimpses. The title plays on being clear-headed amid a harsh world. The track most listeners remember is Vocab, where a simple acoustic guitar replaces the drum machine and the group's real chemistry suddenly comes into focus.","The trio came from New Jersey, two of them children of Haitian immigrants, and took their name from 'refugee', reclaiming a slur used against Haitians. Ruffhouse, a Columbia imprint that had success with harder acts, released the album in early 1994 after a long delay, and the group has said the label steered them toward a tougher, more fashionable sound than they wanted. It sold poorly in America, though remixes of Nappy Heads found club and radio play.","Listen to Hill's verses across the album: she is already the most assured rapper of the three, and the moments where she slips into melody are the seeds of both The Score and The Miseducation of Lauryn Hill. The unplugged Vocab showed the group what their live shows already knew, that their strength was musicianship and warmth rather than aggression. Much of the album's reputation rests on the gap between what it is and what it hints at.","Reviewers were polite but unexcited, and the record was quickly overshadowed by the enormous success of The Score two years later. It is now mainly heard as a prologue, valued for early Hill and for showing how far the group travelled once they took control of their own sound. A few admirers defend its rawness as more honest than the polish that followed, but few would rank it among the essential albums of its year.","Begin with Vocab, then the remix of Nappy Heads, then the album version of Nappy Heads to hear how much a producer's choices changed the group. Follow Hill through the record as you would a soloist in a band. Then play The Score straight after: the difference is the best lesson in what the Fugees became."],"fugees-killing-me-softly":["Lauryn Hill sings the old ballad almost unadorned over a slow, head-nodding drum loop and a deep bass line, while Wyclef Jean and Pras Michel murmur ad-libs and harmonies behind her. There is no rap verse at all. The track opens with a moment of her singing nearly alone before the beat drops in, and it closes on layered voices fading out. Recognise it within a second by that loose, crackling drum pattern and Hill's cool, unhurried phrasing.","The song was written by Charles Fox and Norman Gimbel, prompted by the singer Lori Lieberman's account of being moved by a performance, and it became a Grammy-winning hit for Roberta Flack in 1973. The Fugees included their version on The Score in 1996. It topped charts across Europe, including the UK, dominated American radio, and won a Grammy for best R&B group performance, making Hill a star before her solo career began and carrying hip-hop production to an audience that had never bought a rap record.","The beat is not new: it is lifted from A Tribe Called Quest's Bonita Applebum, itself built on older samples, so the record stacks three generations of Black music on top of each other. The song's subject, a listener who feels a stranger's song is telling her own life, becomes a quiet statement of the Fugees' method, which was to take existing music and make it personal. Listen for Wyclef's voice drifting in the background like a crowd member.","Some hip-hop listeners at the time dismissed it as a soft cover designed for crossover, and the writers of the song reportedly objected to changes the group had once proposed to the lyric, which were dropped. Time has been kinder: it is now heard as one of the defining records of 1990s popular music and a blueprint for the neo-soul and sample-based R&B that followed. Hill's vocal is the part no one argues about.","Play Roberta Flack's 1973 version first, with its piano and strings, then the Fugees'. Notice what the drum loop does to the tempo and mood: the song stops being a confession and becomes a slow walk through a city at night. Then hear Bonita Applebum to find where the beat came from."],"wilco-being-there":["A double album of nineteen songs that ranges from country ballads and bar-band rockers to Beatles-style pop and ragged, noisy experiments. It opens with Misunderstood, which begins in a clatter of feedback, settles into a piano-led lament and ends in an obsessive, repeated outburst, announcing that this is no tidy alt-country record. Elsewhere Far, Far Away and The Lonely 1 are hushed and plain, while Outtasite (Outta Mind) is pure Rolling Stones swagger. Recognise it by Jeff Tweedy's frayed voice and the sense of a band trying everything.","Wilco had formed from the break-up of Uncle Tupelo, the band that helped start the alt-country movement, and their first album, A.M., had been a modest continuation of that sound. For the second, released by Reprise in October 1996, Tweedy wrote about his own ambivalence toward rock stardom and listening to records as a fan. He persuaded the label to sell the double album at a single-album price by giving up part of his royalties. The multi-instrumentalist Jay Bennett joined, widening the palette.","The album is about listening as much as playing: some songs cast the singer as a devoted fan of rock records, and across the sequence you can hear the Band, the Rolling Stones and the Beatles pass through like favourite albums pulled from a shelf. The title comes from the Hal Ashby film Being There, whose hero knows the world only through television. Listen for how the production gets deliberately rougher whenever a song threatens to become too pretty.","Reviewers were enthusiastic, and many named it among the year's best, though sales were modest. It is now seen as the hinge of Wilco's career, the point where they left the alt-country label behind and began the path to Summerteeth and Yankee Hotel Foxtrot. Some fans still prefer its warmth to the later experimentalism; others hear it as sprawling and overlong, a debate the double-album format always invites.","Start with Misunderstood and listen to how the song keeps changing shape. Then hear Sunken Treasure and Far, Far Away for the quiet side, and Monday or Outtasite for the loud one. Take it one disc at a time. Afterwards play Uncle Tupelo's No Depression to hear where Tweedy came from, and The Band's Music from Big Pink for the lineage he claims."],"wilco-a-ghost-is-born":["Twelve songs that swing between fragile piano ballads and long, abrasive instrumental passages. Jeff Tweedy plays much of the lead guitar himself, in jagged, strangled solos that sound like someone thinking aloud. Spiders (Kidsmoke) rides a single chugging groove for more than ten minutes; Less Than You Think ends with a long stretch of electronic drone that many listeners never finish. Between them sit gentle songs such as Hummingbird and Muzzle of Bees. Recognise it by the tension between sweetness and noise.","Recorded in New York and Chicago and co-produced with Jim O'Rourke, the album followed the saga of Yankee Hotel Foxtrot, which Reprise had rejected and Nonesuch had then released to acclaim. Jay Bennett had left; Tweedy was struggling with chronic migraines, anxiety and a dependence on painkillers, and entered treatment shortly before the June 2004 release. The record won Grammys for best alternative album and best recording package, and was the last before Nels Cline joined the band.","The drone at the end of Less Than You Think was, Tweedy has said, an attempt to evoke the physical experience of a migraine, which reframes what sounds like self-indulgence as something closer to testimony. Spiders (Kidsmoke) borrows the relentless one-chord pulse of German bands such as Neu! and Can. Listen for how often a pretty song is interrupted or undercut by a sound that does not belong.","Reviews were largely strong but more divided than for its predecessor, with complaints centred on the drone and the lengthy guitar passages. Some critics heard a band retreating into difficulty, others its bravest record. Two decades on, it is often called the most personal Wilco album, and its guitar playing has been re-evaluated as expressive rather than merely eccentric. The argument about the drone continues and is part of the record's identity.","Start with At Least That's What You Said: a whispered opening that erupts into Tweedy's guitar. Then Hummingbird for the pop side and Spiders (Kidsmoke) for the trance. Hear Less Than You Think once all the way through, as an experience rather than a song. Compare it with Neu!'s first album to hear where the long grooves come from."],"buena-vista-social-club-at-carnegie-hall":["A double live album of the ensemble's single concert at New York's Carnegie Hall. The veterans take turns in the spotlight: Ibrahim Ferrer's soft, high tenor, Omara Portuondo's bolero singing, Compay Segundo's deep voice and his own hybrid guitar, Rubén González's rippling piano, Eliades Ochoa's guitar, the laúd of Barbarito Torres and the trumpet of Guajiro Mirabal, all over the bass of Orlando 'Cachaíto' López. Songs stretch well beyond their studio lengths, with extended solos and audience responses. Recognise it by the roar that greets each old master.","The Buena Vista Social Club album of 1997, recorded in Havana by World Circuit with Ry Cooder, had become a worldwide success, and in July 1998 the musicians played Carnegie Hall, a symbolic event given the decades of hostility between the United States and Cuba. For some of them it was a rare, or first, appearance in the United States. Wim Wenders filmed the concert for his documentary Buena Vista Social Club, but a full audio release waited until 2008, by which time several of the principal performers had died.","Listen to the way the rhythm section never changes its bearing even as the crowd grows noisier: the clave pattern underneath, the two-bar rhythmic key of Cuban son, stays steady while soloists float over it. Ry Cooder and his son Joachim, on percussion, are present but deliberately in the background. The late release means the album also works as a memorial, which gives the applause a weight that listeners in the hall could not have known.","When it finally appeared, critics praised the recording for capturing what the film had only shown in fragments, and many preferred the loose, generous performances to the more composed studio album. Its reception was coloured by nostalgia and by the question, raised since the late 1990s, of whether the Buena Vista phenomenon packaged Cuban music for foreign audiences as a museum piece; admirers answer that the musicians' joy in the hall is its own argument.","Start with Chan Chan to compare with the studio version, then find Rubén González's piano features and Omara Portuondo's duet with Ibrahim Ferrer. Let the long tracks run; the solos are the point. Then watch Wenders's film, which ends at this concert, and hear the Fania All-Stars at Yankee Stadium for the New York salsa that grew from the same roots."],"the-strokes-last-nite":["Three minutes and a quarter of tightly locked guitar rock. Two electric guitars play interlocking parts, one chopping chords on every beat and the other picking a clipped counter-line, while the drums keep a simple, unshowy beat and the bass walks underneath. Julian Casablancas sings through a deliberately degraded, telephone-like tone, sounding bored and anxious at once, about a night that went badly and a partner who does not understand him. Recognise it by the stuttering opening chord pattern and the tossed-off guitar solo.","The Strokes were five young New Yorkers whose first EP had caused a stir in Britain before America took notice. Last Nite was recorded with the producer Gordon Raphael in a small Manhattan basement studio and issued as a single in 2001, shortly before Is This It. In a year when American rock radio was dominated by heavy, over-produced nu-metal, its thin, dry sound and lo-fi vocal felt like a rebuke, and it helped launch a garage rock revival on both sides of the Atlantic.","The opening chord pattern is close to that of Tom Petty's American Girl, a resemblance Petty himself noted in interviews with amusement rather than grievance. The band's style was also a conscious return to the Velvet Underground and Television, New York bands of a generation earlier. Listen to how the two guitars never double each other: every part is a distinct rhythmic idea, which is why a track so simple sounds busy.","It was hailed in the British music press almost before it was released, and reached the UK top twenty, while American success came more slowly. Sceptics called the band a fashion story with rich-kid backgrounds; supporters pointed to the precision of the playing. It is now widely listed among the defining rock songs of the 2000s and credited with reopening the charts to guitar bands.","Listen twice: once to the guitars, noting how one plays chords and the other a separate line, and once to the drums, which barely change. Then play Tom Petty's American Girl, followed by Television's Marquee Moon, to hear the two traditions the Strokes stitched together. The album version and the single are essentially the same recording."],"the-strokes-room-on-fire":["Eleven songs in thirty-three minutes, sharper and slightly glossier than the debut. The guitars of Nick Valensi and Albert Hammond Jr. are more intricate, weaving melodic lines rather than riffs, and Casablancas's voice ranges from crooning to raw shouting. Reptilia is the hardest-driving track; 12:51 features a guitar treated to sound like a vintage synthesiser; The End Has No End bounces on a near-reggae rhythm. Recognise it by the same compact, dry sound as Is This It, with every part cleaner and busier.","After the success of Is This It, the band first tried recording the follow-up with Nigel Godrich, Radiohead's producer, but abandoned the sessions and returned to Gordon Raphael, deliberately keeping the familiar sound. The album was released in late 2003, by which time the garage rock revival they had led had become crowded with imitators, including many British bands. The pressure was therefore to prove that the Strokes were more than the start of a trend.","The keyboard-like sound on 12:51 is not a keyboard at all: it is Hammond's guitar, processed and played to mimic a synthesiser, a nod to the new-wave pop of the early 1980s the band loved. Listen for the way the guitar parts on this record often move in parallel melodies, like two voices in a duet, instead of one rhythm and one lead. That counterpoint is the album's real development over its predecessor.","Reviews were good but faintly disappointed: the most common complaint was that it sounded too much like Is This It. Many listeners have since reversed that verdict, hearing it as the band's most consistent set and preferring its craft. It is often named by musicians as their favourite Strokes record. The debate over whether refusing to change was confidence or caution remains the frame through which it is heard.","Start with Reptilia, then 12:51, then play the whole album once without breaks, since it is shorter than many single songs by progressive bands. Listen specifically for the two guitars answering each other. Compare with Is This It to judge for yourself whether the sameness is a flaw, and with Television's Marquee Moon for the interlocking-guitar model."],"arcade-fire-neon-bible":["Arcade Fire's second album is darker and more anxious than Funeral, and bigger in sound. Pipe organ, orchestral strings, massed choir-like vocals and driving rock drums combine into dense, dramatic arrangements. Its songs circle fear: of the sea, of war, of television evangelism and of a society closing in. Keep the Car Running pounds along like a folk song at speed; Intervention is built on a thundering church organ; No Cars Go is a euphoric escape fantasy. Recognise it by the organ and the sense of dread.","The band, based in Montreal and led by the married couple Win Butler and Régine Chassagne, bought a disused church in a village outside the city and turned it into a studio, and recorded the organ for Intervention in a London church. Released in March 2007 by Merge in North America, it arrived in the Iraq War years, amid heightened security fears and a US political culture shaped by religious broadcasting. It debuted at number two on the Billboard chart, a remarkable result for an independent label.","The title comes from a novel John Kennedy Toole wrote as a teenager, about a boy growing up among religious hypocrisy in the American South. Water imagery runs throughout, from the ocean in the opening track to drowning and floods, knitting the songs into a cycle. No Cars Go is a re-recording of a song from the band's early EP, rebuilt on a much larger scale; comparing the two shows how their ambition grew.","It was widely acclaimed and sold strongly, though some critics found it heavy-handed, preachy or claustrophobic after Funeral's warmth. Admirers heard it as a serious political record in an era of evasive indie rock. It has held its reputation, often viewed as the band's most unified album, and its fusion of church organ and rock anthem influenced a wave of large-scale indie bands.","Start with Intervention and let the organ overwhelm you, then Keep the Car Running for its galloping momentum and No Cars Go for release. Listen in sequence for the water images. Compare it with Bruce Springsteen's Nebraska, whose fearful, small-town America haunts this record, and with Funeral, which faced grief with more warmth than dread."],"arcade-fire-the-suburbs":["Sixteen songs about growing up on the edge of a city, looser and more reflective than Neon Bible. The title track opens with a bouncing piano and a plainly sung memory; later songs range from the chugging rock of Ready to Start and the jittery Month of May to slow, spacious ballads. Near the end, Sprawl II (Mountains Beyond Mountains), sung by Régine Chassagne, turns into glittering synth-pop. The album closes with a brief return to the opening theme. Recognise it by its nostalgic, slightly wistful tone.","Win and Will Butler grew up partly in The Woodlands, a planned suburb of Houston, Texas, and the album draws on those memories. Co-produced with Markus Dravs and released by Merge in August 2010, it debuted at number one in the United States, Britain and Canada. In February 2011 it won the Grammy for Album of the Year, a surprise that briefly set social media alight with people asking who the band was. Spike Jonze made a companion short film, Scenes from the Suburbs.","The album is framed as a cycle: the opening song and the short final reprise share material, so it ends where it began, like returning home after years away. Sprawl II deliberately evokes Blondie and ABBA, bands the Butlers heard as children, making the one burst of dance music also an act of memory. Listen too for how often the songs refer to waiting and to the slowness of pre-internet life; the band's interactive video for We Used to Wait made the point online.","Reviews were very strong, praising its scope and emotional precision, though some found it long and less intense than the first two albums. The Grammy win turned it into a symbol of indie music reaching the mainstream, which drew backlash from both sides. It is now often seen as Arcade Fire's last universally admired record, and its treatment of suburban life has been widely discussed as a portrait of a generation's ambivalence.","Start with the title track, then Ready to Start, then Sprawl II. Listen to the whole album once in order to hear the frame close. Compare Sprawl II with Blondie's Heart of Glass on Parallel Lines, and the record overall with Springsteen's Born to Run, the great earlier album about the need to escape where you grew up."],"abdel-halim-hafez-ahwak":["A love song of the late 1950s, set by Mohammed Abdel Wahab for the young Abdel Halim Hafez and sung in the film Banat al-Yawm. The words are a plain declaration of a love that fills the whole of the singer's life. It is short by Cairene standards: a lilting introduction for massed violins, a verse-and-refrain shape, and a voice that stays close to speech, soft at the edges, never pushing. The recognisable thing is the tenderness of the attack, each phrase begun almost in a whisper.","Egypt after the 1952 revolution wanted new faces, and cinema and radio made them. Abdel Halim, an orphan from the Nile Delta trained as an oboist at the Cairo conservatory, had been booed at an early concert for singing new material rather than the old repertoire, then became the voice of Nasser's young republic. Abdel Wahab, the senior modernist of Egyptian song, owned a production company and cast him in films; the partnership gave Abdel Halim his repertoire of romantic hits.","Listen to how little ornament there is. The older tarab style, with Umm Kulthum at its head, lived on long improvised elaboration of a single line; Abdel Halim deliberately cut this back, bending a note slightly rather than decorating it, so the words stay audible. Abdel Wahab supports him with a Western-style string section playing in unison with Arab instruments, and the harmonies underneath are thin by design: the voice, not the band, carries the colour of the maqam.","Contemporaries called him al-Andalib al-Asmar, the dark-skinned nightingale, and this kind of song is why. Traditionalists heard a crooner's softness where they wanted a tarab singer's power; younger listeners heard someone who sang the way they actually felt. The song has never left Egyptian radio, and its gentle, microphone-aware delivery is now read as a turning point, the moment Arabic popular singing learned intimacy from the recording studio.","Hear it first as a three-minute pop song, then listen again only for the breaths: he phrases like a wind player, which he was. Then play Umm Kulthum's Enta Omri, also composed by Abdel Wahab a few years later, and notice how the same composer writes for a voice that projects to a hall versus one that leans towards a microphone. A clean film-soundtrack transfer is better than later live versions."],"abdel-halim-hafez-zay-el-hawa":["A long concert song by the composer Baligh Hamdi to words by Mohamed Hamza, in which a lover looks back on a love that came like the wind and left him with nothing to hold. On stage it stretches to three quarters of an hour: a lengthy orchestral overture, sections that alternate full band and bare voice, and a refrain the audience knows and waits for. The sound is Cairo at the end of the sixties, violins in massed unison, a qanun rippling underneath, an electric organ adding a modern gloss.","By 1970 Abdel Halim was the biggest male star in the Arab world and also chronically ill with the liver disease, contracted in childhood, that would kill him seven years later. The defeat of 1967 had turned Egyptian public mood inward, and his repertoire shifted from patriotic anthems back towards long, sorrowful love songs. Baligh Hamdi, the most inventive composer of the younger generation, wrote many of them for him, bringing folk rhythms and electric instruments into the grand tarab format.","The live recordings preserve the real work: the audience. Abdel Halim repeats a line again and again, each time shading it differently, until the hall answers with shouts of approval, and he then answers them in turn. The song is designed for this exchange; the written melody is only a skeleton. Listen also for Hamdi's organ and electric guitar, which arrive without any fuss, as if they had always belonged in an Arab orchestra.","Critics of the time argued whether these vast love songs were a retreat from politics after 1967 or a more honest form of it, grief displaced onto romance. Purists found the electric instruments vulgar. Audiences settled the matter by filling theatres, and the song became one of his signature pieces. It is now often cited alongside Umm Kulthum's late concert songs as the last flowering of the long-form Egyptian song before the cassette era shortened everything.","Choose a complete live recording rather than an edited radio version, and do not skip the overture: it sets out the tunes the voice will later transform. Then follow a single refrain across its repetitions and hear how the crowd changes his phrasing. Afterwards compare Qariat al-Fingan, his farewell six years later, where the same scale is put to darker use."],"lalgudi-jayaraman-innum-en-manam":["A varnam, the study-piece-cum-concert-opener of Carnatic music, composed by Lalgudi Jayaraman in the raga Charukesi to a Tamil text in which a devotee pleads with a god who seems not to know the longing in the singer's mind. The form is strict: a first half sung to words and then to solfège syllables, a second half built from passages of increasing density, each returning to the same refrain. What makes it recognisable is the melody's ache, Charukesi's lowered upper notes giving a plaintive colour unusual in the form.","Varnams were traditionally written by composers of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries and treated as technical exercises before the real concert began. Jayaraman, already the leading violinist of the Madras concert circuit and an accompanist to the great singers of the day, began composing his own in the second half of the century, and dancers soon took them up. This one, probably from around 1970, became the most performed, sung by vocalists of every school and used as a centrepiece in Bharatanatyam recitals.","Jayaraman wrote as a violinist who spent his life shadowing singers, and it shows: the fast solfège passages fall naturally under the voice but also under the bow, and many phrases imitate the slide between notes that a violin makes easily and a voice must work for. The Tamil text is placed so that long vowels land on held notes. Dancers use it partly because each passage has an implied gesture built into its rhythm.","Some traditionalists were cool at first towards a living performer writing varnams at all, a form associated with revered composers of the past. The piece's popularity ended that argument; it is now standard teaching repertoire and a regular item in the December Madras music season. It is often cited as proof that the Carnatic repertoire is still being added to, and Jayaraman's varnams and thillanas are among the few twentieth-century compositions to have entered the canon.","Start with a vocal performance so the words carry, then find one by Jayaraman himself on violin and hear how the instrument sings the same text without it. Listen for the refrain returning after each denser passage: that return is the architecture. Follow it with his thillanas, where the same melodic sense turns towards rhythm and dance."],"lalgudi-jayaraman-sringaram":["The score for Sharada Ramanathan's Tamil film Sringaram, set in a temple town in the 1920s and following a devadasi, a dancer dedicated to the temple, at the moment her way of life was being dismantled. Jayaraman wrote it almost entirely in the classical idiom: dance pieces in the old sadir style, devotional songs, quiet instrumental passages built on single ragas. There is no film orchestra in the Bollywood sense; the sound is chamber-sized, voices and violin over drone and drum, and the dances are the heart of it.","Jayaraman was in his late seventies and had never scored a feature film. The director wanted music the devadasis themselves might have danced to, before their tradition was reshaped into the concert Bharatanatyam of the later twentieth century, and turned to a composer whose varnams and thillanas were already the dancers' repertoire. The score won him the National Film Award for Best Music Direction, a rare honour for a classical musician working outside the film industry.","Much of the music is period reconstruction by instinct rather than scholarship: Jayaraman writes slower tempi and more lingering phrase-endings than modern dance recitals use, as if restoring the older, more intimate performance of the salon and temple hall. Listen for the nadaswaram, the double-reed temple oboe, which frames the public scenes, while the private scenes shrink to voice and violin: the orchestration follows the story from the temple into the home.","The film had a limited release and is better known in dance circles than among cinema audiences, but the score was praised for refusing the easy route of film-song glamour. Discussion of the film itself has turned on its portrayal of the devadasi system, sympathetic to the women and their art while acknowledging the exploitation that led to its legal abolition. The music is widely regarded as Jayaraman's late summation.","The soundtrack works on its own; begin with the dance numbers, where his rhythmic writing is at its most alive, and then hear the slow devotional songs. Compare his concert thillanas to see how he slows and softens them for the story. If you can, watch the dance sequences in the film, choreographed to the music, to see how the rhythms turn into steps."],"nikhil-banerjee-raga-malkauns":["Malkauns is the deep late-night raga of five notes, grave and inward, and Nikhil Banerjee's concert performances of it are among the longest and most searching he left. The shape is the classic instrumental one: a long unaccompanied alap in free time exploring the low register, a jor where a pulse appears, a jhala of rapid strumming, then a composition with tabla, first slow and then fast. What identifies him is the sound itself, a sitar tone rounded and almost vocal, the melody pulled sideways on the fret rather than struck.","Banerjee studied with Allauddin Khan at Maihar, alongside Ravi Shankar and Ali Akbar Khan, and absorbed that school's respect for the old dhrupad style of slow, systematic unfolding. He disliked the studio and short formats, and toured widely in Europe and North America in the 1970s, where students and small concert societies recorded him. Much of what survives, including several versions of Malkauns, comes from those concert tapes, released later, mostly after his early death in 1986.","Listen to how rarely he plays the raga's tonic in the alap. He circles it, lands near it, and saves the full arrival for a structurally important moment, a practice drawn from dhrupad singing. His meend, the long pull of a string along the fret, often covers three or four notes in one gesture, which is why his lines sound sung. The raga has no fifth degree at all, and he makes that gap audible, a missing step the ear keeps expecting.","During his life Banerjee was a musicians' musician, less famous in the West than Ravi Shankar but spoken of by other players with particular awe. Posthumous releases of his concert recordings, especially through specialist labels in the United States, built a devoted following, and his Malkauns is often named among the finest recorded. Debate, where there is any, concerns tape quality and edits: the performances were never made for release, and the best ones are sometimes sonically the roughest.","Hear it at night, and give the alap your full attention before the tabla enters: in Banerjee the slow opening is the main event, not a prelude. Notice how each new note is introduced as an event. Then compare Pran Nath's sung Malkauns, which shows where the instrumental phrasing comes from, and the anonymous raga entry for the rules he is working within."],"nikhil-banerjee-raga-bihag":["Bihag is a raga for the first hours of the night, sweet and slightly yearning, built on a bright major-sounding scale with one note occasionally sharpened for colour. Banerjee plays it as a complete concert performance: unmeasured alap, a pulsed jor, then compositions with tabla in medium and fast tempo. His Bihag is lighter on its feet than his Malkauns, more melodic, with long singing phrases in the middle register and, in the fast section, clean articulated runs rather than a blur of speed.","Like most of his recorded legacy, Banerjee's Bihag survives in live recordings from the 1970s and early 1980s, when he toured Europe and North America and taught at the Ali Akbar College of Music in California. He was known to rethink a raga from concert to concert, so versions of the same raga differ markedly. These tapes have been released gradually since his death, becoming the main way listeners outside India have come to know him.","The raga's defining trick is the sharpened fourth degree, used only in passing, a touch of colour in an otherwise natural scale. Banerjee handles it like a secret, letting it appear in a single ascending phrase and vanish, so that the ear notices its absence afterwards. Listen too to the chikari, the high drone strings, which he strikes sparingly; many sitarists fill every gap with them, and his restraint makes the texture feel more open.","Bihag is standard repertoire and every sitarist records it, which makes comparison easy and revealing. Banerjee's versions are often praised for balance, neither sentimental nor showy, with the architecture of the whole performance kept in view. Some listeners find him austere next to Vilayat Khan's more vocal lyricism in the same raga; others hold that his restraint is precisely the point, and that he lets the raga speak before the player.","Begin with the alap and then jump straight to the medium-tempo composition with tabla, where the melody and rhythm meet. Listen for the brief sharpened note and count how rarely it comes. Then play Vilayat Khan's Jalsaghar recordings for another great sitarist's sweetness, and Banerjee's Manj Khamaj for the lighter end of his repertoire."],"kishori-amonkar-raga-bhinna-shadja":["A khayal performance in a rare five-note raga that omits the second and fifth degrees, leaving a scale that sounds open and unresolved. Kishori Amonkar takes it at a very slow tempo in the first composition, drawing out each syllable of the text over many cycles of the tabla, then moves to a faster composition with rapid runs. Her voice is thin-edged and bright, capable of sudden flights to the top of its range, and her treatment of a single sustained note, bending it by fractions, is the signature.","Amonkar trained first with her mother, the great Jaipur-Atrauli singer Mogubai Kurdikar, then widened her study to other gharanas and to music theory, which made her controversial among purists. She was known for refusing to be recorded often and for demanding complete silence from audiences. Bhinna Shadja, rarely sung before her, became one of the ragas most closely identified with her, and her recordings of it, many from concerts, circulated widely on Indian labels.","Without the fifth degree, the drone of the tanpura supplies the only stable reference, and she exploits it: long notes are held against the drone until they almost clash, then settled. Listen for how she treats the fourth degree as a second home, turning around it as other singers would around the fifth. Her fast passages often break into shapes borrowed from other gharanas, which is exactly what her critics complained about and her admirers loved.","Amonkar divided opinion throughout her career. Jaipur traditionalists thought she strayed from the gharana's intricate architecture towards emotion and personal expression; she replied that raga exists to express feeling, and wrote on the theory of rasa. Her Bhinna Shadja is now cited as a case where a performer effectively claimed a raga for herself. Recording quality varies widely, and there is no single definitive version.","Stay with the slow opening, which may seem static at first; listen to what happens inside each held note rather than waiting for the tune. Compare a moment of stillness with the sudden flight when she leaves it. Then hear her Sahela Re for her lighter side, and Kesarbai Kerkar's Jaipur recordings for the tradition she both inherited and argued with."],"kishori-amonkar-geet-gaaya-pattharon-ne":["The title song of V. Shantaram's 1964 Hindi film about a sculptor, sung by the young Kishori Amonkar to music by Ramlal. The song's idea is that stone itself has begun to sing, and the melody is built accordingly: a raga-based line with classical ornaments laid into a film-song frame of strings and flute. It is short, bright and quick, yet the voice never sounds like a playback singer's; it has the slightly austere intensity of the concert hall.","Shantaram was one of the grand directors of Indian cinema, and his films habitually leaned on classical music and dance. Amonkar was then in her early thirties, emerging as a concert singer, and her guru-mother Mogubai Kurdikar reportedly disapproved of film work as beneath a classical artist. Whatever the reason, Amonkar sang almost no further film songs for decades, which makes this one an unusual snapshot: a major classical voice in a popular context before she withdrew from it.","Listen to how the ornaments differ from those of the professional playback singers of the day, Lata Mangeshkar above all. Amonkar's grace notes are heavier and slower, placed as in khayal rather than as quick sparkles. The orchestral writing leaves space for them. The song is also a rare document of her voice before the more severe style of her maturity, lighter in tone and freer at the top.","The film had modest success, but the song lasted, heard on radio and at weddings and remembered as one of the few film songs by a classical singer of her stature. Amonkar herself later spoke ambivalently about the episode and about film music generally. Among critics it is usually taken as a curiosity with real musical merit rather than a central work, but it remains one of the most accessible entry points to her voice.","Treat it as a doorway: listen for the ornaments that do not sound like ordinary film song, then go straight to her Sahela Re and hear the same singer at full classical length. It pairs well with any Hindi film song by a professional playback singer of the 1960s, to hear two different vocal worlds meeting in one studio."],"misora-hibari-ringo-oiwake":["A song about the apple country of Tsugaru in northern Japan, sung by Misora Hibari at fifteen. The title evokes the oiwake, a long-breathed folk song of the northern provinces, and the melody borrows its sweep: slow, ornamented phrases that rise and hang. In the middle the music stops for a spoken passage in which the singer, in dialect, remembers apple blossom and a mother lost, before the song returns. It is the combination of a child's voice and an old woman's melancholy that listeners recognise.","Occupied Japan was poor and hungry, and Hibari, a child star from Yokohama, had become the country's emotional mascot through films and records since the late 1940s. Ringo Oiwake came from a 1952 film in which she starred, and it sold in numbers then almost unheard of for a Japanese record. Nostalgia for the countryside was intense in cities filling with migrants from rural prefectures, and the song gave them a home they had left.","The spoken section is the key. Hibari switches from a trained singing voice to the accent of northern Tohoku, and the orchestra drops to almost nothing; for a moment the record becomes a radio drama. Listen too for the vocal technique borrowed from folk singing, a slight wobble and catch on long notes called kobushi, which later became the defining ornament of enka. Here it is audible in a teenage voice, already fully formed.","Adult critics sometimes found Hibari's early precocity unsettling, a child singing grown-up sorrow, and there was a public argument about whether she should be singing such material at all. The record's success ended the debate commercially. Retrospectively it is seen as one of the foundations of post-war enka, the genre's rural nostalgia and folk ornament arriving in a single hit before the word enka was in common use.","Listen to it twice: once for the melody and once for the spoken passage, noticing how the orchestra makes room. Then play her much later Kawa no Nagare no Yō ni to hear the same voice nearly forty years on. For contrast, try Kyu Sakamoto's Sukiyaki, a decade later, which faces towards American pop rather than the Japanese countryside."],"misora-hibari-kanashii-sake":["Sad Sake, a song by the composer Koga Masao to words by Ishimoto Miyuki, in which a woman drinks alone and finds that the wine only brings back the man who left her. It opens with a long, weeping guitar introduction in Koga's style, and the melody moves slowly in the minor-leaning five-note scale of enka. Hibari sings it low and close, with a heavy vibrato and the folk catch in the voice at the ends of phrases. On stage it became a set piece of controlled collapse.","By the mid-1960s Hibari was the undisputed queen of Japanese popular song, in her late twenties, recently divorced and a figure of enormous commercial power. Koga Masao was the older master of the melancholy popular song, his melodies so recognisable that the phrase Koga melody became a byword. Their collaboration on Kanashii Sake came as enka was consolidating into a named genre, defined partly against the Western-style group sounds and folk boom of the young.","The guitar introduction comes from the guitar style Koga had been developing since the 1930s, and it is doing the work of a second voice: listen to how it imitates a sob. In live performances Hibari famously wept on the spoken or instrumental passages yet returned to pitch exactly on the next phrase, and filmed performances show how precisely the tears are placed. The emotion was real and also rehearsed, which is the whole art of enka.","The song was an immediate hit and has remained among her most loved. Younger critics in the 1960s and 70s often dismissed enka as sentimental and backward-looking, a music of drinking and resignation for an older generation. Later writing has taken it more seriously, as a carefully constructed urban genre rather than a folk survival, and Kanashii Sake is a standard example of how that construction works.","Seek out a filmed live performance to see what she does with the tears; the studio version is more restrained. Listen to the guitar introduction as a miniature of the whole song. Then compare Ringo Oiwake, fourteen years earlier, to hear how the folk catch in the voice became a studied tool, and Teresa Teng for the next generation's gentler approach."],"chaurasia-venu":["A single long performance of the morning raga Ahir Bhairav by Hariprasad Chaurasia on the bansuri, the bamboo transverse flute, with Zakir Hussain on tabla. It follows the full classical order: a slow unmeasured alap in which the flute breathes out the raga phrase by phrase, a pulsed jor, then compositions with tabla rising to a fast finale. The sound is warm and hollow, and his long notes, bent at the end by partly covering a hole, are the thing you will remember.","Chaurasia had spent years as a staff musician at All India Radio and as a Bombay studio player before establishing himself as a solo classical artist, the first to give the bansuri full concert status. By the late 1980s he and Zakir Hussain were touring the West together, and this record was issued by Rykodisc in a series of world recordings aimed at American listeners. For many outside India it was the first time they heard the bansuri as a classical instrument rather than a folk one.","Ahir Bhairav mixes the dark, flattened second of Bhairav with a softer upper half, and the flute exposes that mixture: listen for the low flattened note, which Chaurasia approaches from below with a slow slide, almost a sigh. Hussain barely plays in the early composition, marking the cycle with a few strokes, then answers the flute's phrases directly as the tempo rises. Their exchange in the fast section is partly improvised on the spot.","The record was widely distributed in the West and remains a common recommendation for newcomers. Some Indian critics felt the partnership with Hussain tilted towards spectacle in the fast sections. The alap, by contrast, is almost universally admired, and the album is often paired with Call of the Valley as the two poles of Chaurasia's career, popular crossover and serious classical practice.","Play it in the morning, the raga's proper hour, and give the alap its full length. Notice how the breath is audible: the flute is a wind instrument, and phrases are shaped by lung capacity. When the tabla arrives, listen to Hussain's first stroke. Then compare Call of the Valley, and a sung Bhairav, to hear what the flute borrows from the voice."],"chaurasia-silsila":["The soundtrack of Yash Chopra's 1981 Hindi romance, the first score by Shiv-Hari, the partnership of Chaurasia with the santoor player Shivkumar Sharma. The songs range from soft romantic duets to a boisterous Holi song sung partly by the film's star Amitabh Bachchan. What sets it apart is the sound of two classical instruments at the centre of a Bollywood orchestra: the bansuri and the santoor's shimmering hammered strings carry melodies that would normally go to violins.","Chaurasia and Sharma had played together on Call of the Valley in 1967 and had worked for years as session musicians in Bombay's film studios. Chopra, then the most successful romantic director in Hindi cinema, gave them their first credit as composers. The film, about a love triangle with casting that mirrored real gossip about its stars, was a moderate box-office performer but its songs were immediately everywhere. Shiv-Hari went on to score further Chopra films through the 1980s and 1990s.","Behind the romantic duets, listen for the instrumental interludes: they are miniatures of classical practice, short alap-like passages for flute or santoor over a drone, slipped into a commercial soundtrack. The Holi song Rang Barse, adapted from a folk song, uses its crowd chorus and hand drums to break the score's classical poise completely. The contrast is deliberate, the public festival against the private longing of the other songs.","Critics in 1981 noted the unusual refinement of the score and wondered whether classical musicians could sustain a film career. The songs answered for them: Rang Barse became a fixture of every Holi celebration, and the quieter songs are remembered as among the most delicate of the decade. Film-music historians now place Shiv-Hari among the few classical musicians who changed the sound of Hindi film without abandoning their own idiom.","Listen first to the instrumental links between songs, which are pure Chaurasia and Sharma. Then hear the romantic duets and notice what the flute and santoor add over a normal film orchestra. Finally play Rang Barse loud. Compare Call of the Valley for the same two players in their classical setting fourteen years before."],"cesaria-evora-cafe-atlantico":["Cesária Évora's late-1990s album that opened her sound outward from the small acoustic morna band towards the wider Atlantic. Slow mornas, the Cape Verdean song of longing, sit next to faster coladeiras and the carnival song Carnaval de São Vicente, with strings, brass and Latin percussion joining the guitars and cavaquinho. The voice is unchanged: low, a little rough, completely unhurried, singing in Cape Verdean Creole about the sea, emigration and the islands. The record is warmer and busier than her earlier albums.","After the international success of Miss Perfumado and the self-titled album that followed, Évora, by then in her late fifties, was touring constantly and recording for the Paris-based label Lusafrica. The title points to the Atlantic triangle of her music: Cape Verde, Portuguese-speaking Brazil and Lisbon, and the Caribbean. Cape Verde's history of emigration, with more islanders living abroad than at home, was the constant subject, and the album's arrangements reflect the diaspora musicians who now played with her.","Listen for the Cuban elements: the brass and piano figures in several songs owe a clear debt to son and danzón, a reminder that Cape Verdean sailors and musicians had long moved between Africa and the Caribbean. The carnival song celebrates her home island of São Vicente and its port city Mindelo, and its brass band is the sound of the island's own carnival. The Creole lyrics, largely by older island songwriters, are about specific places.","Reviews were warm and the album sold well in Europe, though some listeners who had loved the bare sound of her early records felt the arrangements overdressed a voice that needed little. Others welcomed the broader palette as a natural extension of Cape Verde's own mixed history. It is now usually seen as the start of her expansive late period, which culminated in the Grammy-winning Voz d'Amor.","Start with a slow morna to hear the voice at rest, then play Carnaval de São Vicente for the island in festival mood. Listen for where the Cuban brass enters. Compare Miss Perfumado for the bare, early sound, and the Buena Vista Social Club recordings from the same years for the Havana side of the Atlantic conversation."],"kyu-sakamoto-miagete-goran-yoru-no-hoshi-o":["A gentle ballad by the lyricist Ei Rokusuke and the composer Izumi Taku, in which the singer tells a listener to look up at the night sky, where a small, unnoticed star is singing its own happiness. The melody climbs step by step as if looking upwards, and Sakamoto's voice, light, slightly nasal, with a boyish lilt, keeps it from sentimentality. The arrangement is modest: strings, a soft chorus, a waltzing pulse. It is the sound of a Japan beginning to feel hopeful.","The song was first written for the stage, and Sakamoto's 1963 recording made it a national standard in the same year that Ue o Muite Arukō, by the same lyricist, topped the American charts as Sukiyaki. Japan was rebuilding fast ahead of the 1964 Tokyo Olympics, and Sakamoto was its cheerful ordinary-boy star. Ei Rokusuke, a radio and television writer, was known for songs about humble people rather than grand romance.","The lyric's star is not a bright one but a tiny one nobody sees, and the song is really about ordinary lives; Ei was drawing on the post-war working people he wrote about for radio. The melody's upward steps mirror the instruction to look up. Listen too for Sakamoto's characteristic slide at the ends of phrases, a light touch of the ornament that folk and enka singers use heavily, here softened into pop.","It became one of the most loved Japanese songs of the century and has been sung in schools for generations, its innocence sometimes mocked but rarely resented. After Sakamoto's death in the 1985 Japan Airlines crash, the song took on a memorial weight it has never lost. Critics generally rank it just behind Ue o Muite Arukō in his output, and some prefer its quieter optimism.","Listen to it straight after Sukiyaki: same singer, same lyricist, but one looks up to hold back tears and the other looks up in hope. Notice the rising melody. Then try Misora Hibari's Ringo Oiwake for the older, folk-rooted style Sakamoto was moving away from."],"kyu-sakamoto-shiawase-nara-te-o-tatako":["A cheerful action song: each verse invites the listener to show happiness by clapping, stamping or another gesture, and the sound of the gesture follows. The tune is the one known in English as If You're Happy and You Know It, with Japanese words by Kimura Riichirō. Sakamoto sings it brightly over a light band, with children and handclaps answering him. It is barely three minutes, repetitive by design, and made to be joined in rather than listened to.","Kimura, a young writer, set Japanese words to the tune, which is often said to be a Spanish folk song though its origin is disputed. Sakamoto's 1964 recording made it a huge hit in the year of the Tokyo Olympics, and it passed straight into kindergartens, where it has stayed. After Sukiyaki he was the one Japanese pop singer the wider world knew, and this record reinforced his image as a smiling, wholesome national figure.","The song is so embedded in Japanese childhood that many listeners assume the tune is Japanese. Listen to how the recorded claps and stamps are placed exactly on the beat, turning the audience into the percussion section. There is also a gentle social idea under it: happiness shown together rather than kept private, which carried weight in a country still recovering from war and poverty.","Critics treated it as a novelty and it is never ranked among his great records, but it may be his most widely sung. Its reach into early-childhood education in Japan means that nearly every Japanese person knows it. It is sometimes cited in discussions of how 1960s pop in Japan blurred the lines between entertainment, education and nation-building.","Do the actions; the record is incomplete without them. Then listen to the voice alone and notice the same brightness that makes Sukiyaki so affecting, here put to plain joy. Hear it alongside Miagete Goran Yoru no Hoshi o for his gentler optimism, and the Japanese new wave of Yellow Magic Orchestra to hear how far Japanese pop travelled in fifteen years."],"mulatu-astatke-afro-latin-soul":["Mulatu Astatke's first records, made in New York in the mid-1960s and issued in two volumes: Latin jazz with an Ethiopian accent. The core is his vibraphone over piano, bass and a Latin rhythm section of congas and timbales, playing mambos, boogaloo-flavoured grooves and slower pieces. Most of it sounds like the New York Latin scene of the day, bright and danceable, but here and there a melody slides into an Ethiopian scale, and the vibraphone's floating tone lets those strange intervals ring.","Mulatu had left Ethiopia to study in Wales and London, then became the first African student at the Berklee College of Music in Boston, before moving into New York's Latin and jazz scene. The Latin boom of the 1960s, with Tito Puente and Eddie Palmieri at its centre, was then at its height, and a small label released these records aimed at dance audiences. He was still looking for a way to join his own musical heritage to jazz; these records are that search in progress.","Listen for the moments where the harmony stays put and the vibraphone plays a melody on one of the Ethiopian pentatonic modes over a Latin groove; this is the seed of Ethio-jazz, several years before the name existed. The Latin influence did not disappear later either: the percussion patterns under his Addis Ababa records of the early 1970s often derive from what he learned in New York. The two volumes are a laboratory.","The records sold modestly and were largely forgotten until the Éthiopiques reissues and the Broken Flowers soundtrack revived interest in Mulatu in the 2000s. Collectors then sought out the originals, and reissues followed. Critics generally treat them as apprentice work, uneven but full of surprises, and important chiefly for showing where Ethio-jazz came from: not from Ethiopia alone, but from a New York Latin dance floor.","Put it on as dance music first. Then listen for the few passages where the melody seems to come from somewhere else, and compare them with the fully formed sound of Mulatu of Ethiopia six years later. Tito Puente's Dance Mania shows the New York style he was working in, and Gillespie's Manteca the earlier meeting of jazz and Cuban rhythm."],"thomas-mapfumo-ndangariro":["Ndangariro is Thomas Mapfumo and the Blacks Unlimited at full stretch in the first years of Zimbabwean independence: long, rolling songs in which two or three electric guitars pick out interlocking figures modelled on the mbira, the Shona thumb piano, over a drum kit that keeps the hosho shaker's triple swing. Mapfumo's voice sits low and grainy in the middle, half chant, half conversation, answered by backing singers. Nothing hurries; each track is a cycle that deepens rather than builds.","The album was made in Harare shortly after 1980, when the war songs that had made Mapfumo a hunted man under Ian Smith's Rhodesia had suddenly become the music of the new state. Chimurenga music, as it was called after the Shona word for struggle, now had to find subjects beyond liberation. The title means something close to memories or thoughts, and the record turns inward, to the ancestors, to loss and to village life, while the band became one of the tightest in southern Africa.","Listen to how the guitars are divided. One plays the low, repeating left-thumb pattern of an mbira piece, another the higher right-hand figures, and a third fills the gaps, so that together they reconstruct a single instrument's music spread across three players. The effect is the mbira's characteristic buzzing halo, which Shona players create with bottle caps on the resonator, imitated here by guitar tone and heavy reverb.","Outside Zimbabwe the record reached Western listeners through the British world-music labels of the mid-1980s, and critics heard it as proof that African pop could be hypnotic rather than simply danceable. Inside Zimbabwe it confirmed Mapfumo as the national voice, a status he would later spend by criticising the government. Some purists regard these guitar-led records as the peak of his style, preferring them to the later band built around real mbira players.","Give each track time: the pleasure is in a pattern that seems static for two minutes and then turns out to have been shifting all along. Try following only the highest guitar, then only the bass. Compare it with the earlier Chimurenga Singles to hear how the war-era urgency relaxed into something more reflective, and with a field recording of Shona mbira to hear the source."],"king-sunny-ade-synchro-system":["Synchro System is the second of King Sunny Adé's international albums, and the one where the African Beats sound most at ease with the studio. Four or five guitars trade short, bright phrases in a slowly turning web; the Hawaiian-style pedal steel swoops across the top; talking drums speak in Yoruba tones underneath; and Adé leads a call-and-response choir. The title track and its companions run long, sliding from song into extended grooves that seem to have no fixed end.","After Juju Music in 1982, Island Records hoped Adé could fill the space left by the death of Bob Marley as the label's global star. He toured Europe and America with a band of around twenty musicians, and Synchro System was made to consolidate that success. The name was his own term for the way his band's many parts lock together. At home in Lagos, jùjú was the music of Yoruba parties and praise singing, where wealthy patrons pressed banknotes onto the singer's forehead.","The talking drum is doing more than keeping time. Its player bends the pitch by squeezing the cords along the drum's sides, and in Yoruba, a tonal language, those rising and falling pitches can be heard as words. Some of the drum's phrases are proverbs or praise names that Yoruba listeners understand, a second, wordless lyric running under the sung one.","The album earned Adé a Grammy nomination and strong reviews, and many critics preferred its looser, dub-inflected production to the tidier Juju Music. Commercially, though, it did not make him the crossover star Island wanted, and the debate about whether jùjú was shaped too much for Western ears began here. Nigerian fans tend to prize his home-market albums, but this record remains the usual door in for everyone else.","Start with the title track and listen for the pedal steel, an instrument from Hawaiian and country music that Adé made central to jùjú. Then pick one guitar and follow its small repeated phrase as others enter around it. Play it next to Talking Heads' Remain in Light, recorded when that band was absorbing exactly this kind of interlocking West African texture."],"king-sunny-ade-aura":["Aura is the third and last of King Sunny Adé's Island albums, and the most polished. The core of the African Beats is intact, with its layered guitars, pedal steel, talking drums and chorus, but synthesisers and drum programming are more prominent, and the songs are shaped a little closer to Western album tracks. The most famous moment is Ase, where Stevie Wonder's chromatic harmonica weaves through the guitars as if it were one more voice in the band.","By 1984 Island had spent heavily on promoting Adé as an international star, and Aura was the attempt to secure that. The sessions reflected the mid-eighties fascination with digital keyboards and cleaner mixes. Adé's large touring band was expensive and under strain, and after the album's modest sales the label relationship ended. He returned to recording mainly for the Nigerian market, where he had never stopped releasing records at a remarkable rate.","Stevie Wonder does not play a solo in the Western sense on Ase. Listen to how his harmonica phrases are short and repeated, exactly like the guitar figures, entering the jùjú texture on its own terms. It is a small lesson in how a visiting star can fit into another tradition instead of standing on top of it, and it links two musicians who both treated the groove as the song's real subject.","Reviews were warm but less excited than for the first two Island records, and some critics felt the new production smoothed away the rawness that had made jùjú sound new. Over time the album has been reheard more kindly, its shimmering synthesisers now seeming of a piece with African pop of that decade. The larger argument, about how far a local style should adapt to reach the world, still gathers around this record.","Begin with Ase for the harmonica, then go back through the album listening for what the synthesisers replace and what they leave alone: the talking drums and the vocal choir never yield. Compare it directly with Juju Music to hear two years of change. Stevie Wonder's own Songs in the Key of Life is a useful companion for its similar joy in dense grooves."],"salif-keita-moffou":["Moffou is Salif Keita unplugged: after fifteen years of synthesiser-heavy productions, he strips his music back to acoustic guitars, the kora harp, the ngoni lute, balafon, hand percussion and a small group of women singers. His voice, one of the most powerful in African music, is placed close and unforced, rising to its famous piercing cry only at moments of need. The best-known track is Yamore, a gentle duet with the Cape Verdean singer Cesária Évora.","Keita recorded the album as he was moving his life back from Paris to Bamako, where he opened a club named Moffou after a small Malian flute and later built a studio. The turn to acoustic Mande sound matched a wider moment: Ali Farka Touré, Toumani Diabaté and others had shown international audiences that Malian music needed no Western gloss. Keita, born an albino into a noble family descended from the empire's founder, had long lived between those two worlds.","The moffou itself, the little flute of the title, appears only briefly, but its sound is a pointer to the rural music Keita heard as a child in the village of Djoliba, before he moved to Bamako. Listen for how often the arrangements leave a single instrument alone with his voice. After years of full bands, these silences are the record's statement.","Moffou was received as a homecoming and a creative rebirth; many critics, who had found some of his Paris albums over-produced, called it his best work since Soro. Its success in Europe encouraged a run of acoustic Malian records and fed his later albums in the same vein, such as M'Bemba. A few listeners still prefer the brassy energy of his 1970s bands, but Moffou is the usual recommendation for a first encounter.","Start with Yamore and listen to how two very different voices, his high and fierce, hers low and tired, meet on neutral ground. Then play the opening track and follow the kora line under the singing. Set it beside Ali Farka Touré's Talking Timbuktu to hear two Malian ways of going acoustic, and beside Soro to hear how far Keita had travelled."],"joe-hisaishi-princess-mononoke":["Joe Hisaishi's score for Hayao Miyazaki's Princess Mononoke is his darkest and largest for Studio Ghibli: full orchestra with heavy brass, thundering Japanese drums, and long string lines for the ancient forest. Its main theme, heard over the opening, is a slow, grave melody that returns throughout as the voice of Ashitaka's journey. The title song, sung by a countertenor in a high, pure, almost ghostly voice, gives the wolf-girl San a sound between human and spirit.","The film was Miyazaki's most ambitious project, an epic set in medieval Japan about a war between an ironworking town and the gods of the forest. It became the highest-grossing film ever released in Japan until Titanic overtook it. Hisaishi, Miyazaki's composer since Nausicaä in 1984, wrote on an epic scale to match, and the score marked his shift from synthesiser-based writing towards the symphonic manner of his later concert work.","Miyazaki himself wrote the words of the title song, and the choice of a countertenor, Yoshikazu Mera, was deliberate: a male voice singing in a woman's range suits a character who belongs to neither the human nor the animal world. Listen too for how the forest spirit's scenes lose melody altogether, reduced to held tones and percussion, as if the music itself held its breath.","In Japan the title song became a hit in its own right and made Mera famous. Abroad the film's English-language release in 1999 introduced many Western listeners to Hisaishi, and the score is now often ranked among the best of the decade from any country. Some admirers of the lighter Ghibli films find it heavy-handed in places, but its main theme has become a concert staple in Hisaishi's own programmes.","Begin with the opening title music and hold on to its melody; you will hear it again at the film's most important turns. Then find the title song and notice how the orchestra stays almost motionless beneath the voice. Compare with the gentle Totoro score to hear Hisaishi's range, and with John Williams to hear another composer building an epic from recurring themes."],"joe-hisaishi-spirited-away":["Hisaishi's score for Spirited Away moves between two worlds, as the film does. The bathhouse of the spirits gets bustling, brassy music with Japanese drums and a slightly grotesque swagger; the girl Chihiro's inner life gets a simple, wistful piano melody, One Summer's Day, that has become one of the most played film themes anywhere. In between are waltzes, chases and long, still passages of strings and piano.","Miyazaki's film, about a ten-year-old girl trapped in a spirit bathhouse after her parents are turned into pigs, broke Princess Mononoke's box-office record in Japan, shared the Golden Bear in Berlin and won the Oscar for animated feature. Hisaishi had worked with Miyazaki for nearly twenty years by then. The end song, Always With Me, was written and sung by Youmi Kimura, not Hisaishi, and is often wrongly credited to him.","The most admired sequence is the one with the least happening: Chihiro's train ride across a flooded landscape, where the score, called The Sixth Station, is a quiet repeating piano figure over soft strings. It sounds like travel and grief at once, and it prepares the story's resolution far more than any dialogue. Listen also for the piano theme in disguise, in orchestral and waltz versions, throughout the film.","The score won the major Japanese film-music honours and has become the most recorded of Hisaishi's works, thanks largely to One Summer's Day, which piano students around the world now learn. Some critics consider it more a collection of beautiful cues than a unified dramatic score like Mononoke, but its place in the Ghibli concerts that Hisaishi conducts, and in the wider affection for the film, is secure.","Listen first to One Summer's Day on its own, then to The Sixth Station, and notice how little material each needs. Then play the bathhouse cues for contrast. Hisaishi's own live recordings with orchestra are the best way to hear the score as concert music. Pair it with Satie's Gymnopédies, whose plain, sad piano lines it quietly resembles."],"master-musicians-of-jajouka-apocalypse-across-the-sky":["Apocalypse Across the Sky is the clearest recording of the Jajouka musicians ever made. A wall of rhaitas, loud double-reed oboes played with circular breathing, drones and wails over pounding drums; then the sound drops to breathy lira flutes, or to voices singing Sufi devotional songs over hand drums. The pieces are long and cumulative, building from ceremony to frenzy, and the players' interlocking reed lines create a dense, almost electric shimmer.","The album was recorded in the village of Jajouka in the Rif mountains of northern Morocco and produced by the American bassist and producer Bill Laswell for his Axiom label. It was led by Bachir Attar, son of the leader who had hosted Brian Jones in 1968. The musicians belong to a hereditary lineage whose music is tied to a local saint and to the Boujeloud rite, in which a man dressed in goatskins dances as a half-animal spirit.","The Boujeloud music, which ends the album's main sequence, has a famous afterlife: Western writers, from Brion Gysin onward, linked the goat-dancer to the Greek god Pan, and that romantic idea shaped how the music was sold abroad. Listen for the long drum pattern that shifts under the rhaitas, and for how the reeds slide between pitches rather than landing on fixed Western notes.","Critics welcomed it as the first recording to capture the ensemble's power with modern fidelity, in contrast to Brian Jones's heavily treated tapes of 1971. Its release also came during a bitter split in the village, with a rival group adopting the older spelling Joujouka, and debate over which group can claim the tradition continues. The recording itself is rarely criticised; its sound is the benchmark.","Play it loud, and give the opening rhaita pieces ten minutes before judging: the point is saturation. Then compare the same music on the Brian Jones record to hear what his tape effects added and hid. Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head, recorded with these musicians in 1973, shows a jazz master stepping into this sound."],"zakir-hussain-melody-of-rhythm":["The Melody of Rhythm centres on a triple concerto for tabla, banjo and double bass with orchestra, composed jointly by Zakir Hussain, Béla Fleck and Edgar Meyer. Three movements set the soloists as a small, fast-talking group against the orchestra's broad strings and winds. The tabla, usually thought of as an accompanist, carries melodic phrases of its own, bending the pitch of its bass drum, while banjo and bass trade lines drawn from bluegrass, Indian and classical practice.","Fleck, Meyer and Hussain had been playing together as a trio for several years, and the album pairs the concerto, recorded with the Detroit Symphony Orchestra under Leonard Slatkin, with shorter trio pieces. The collaboration fits Hussain's lifelong pattern: son of the tabla master Alla Rakha, he had moved from accompanying Ravi Shankar to Shakti with John McLaughlin to projects with the Grateful Dead's Mickey Hart and with orchestras worldwide.","The concerto's cadenzas are where the three traditions really meet, and they work because each player uses the others' timing systems. Listen for passages where Hussain recites or implies a rhythmic cycle and Fleck answers it on the banjo, which in bluegrass is itself a rhythm instrument. The orchestra often simply drops out at these moments, turning the concerto into chamber music.","Reviewers praised the players' virtuosity and warmth but were divided about the orchestral writing, which some found less inventive than the trio sections. The album won a wide audience beyond the classical world and set up the trio's later work together. It is now heard as a sign of Hussain's unusual reach: few Hindustani musicians have been treated as co-composers in the concert hall.","Start with the concerto's slow movement, where the tabla is at its most songlike, then listen to the fast finale for the cross-rhythms. Afterwards play a trio track to hear the same three musicians without the orchestra. Compare with Ravi Shankar's sitar concertos, an earlier attempt to fit a Hindustani soloist into the orchestral world, and with Reich's Drumming."],"zakir-hussain-as-we-speak":["As We Speak brings Zakir Hussain back together with the banjo player Béla Fleck and the bassist Edgar Meyer, now joined by the bansuri flautist Rakesh Chaurasia. Without an orchestra, the quartet is intimate and quick: the flute takes long, breathy melodic lines, the banjo picks rapid runs, the bass anchors or bows a second melody, and the tabla does everything from pulse to commentary. Pashto, built on an irregular rhythmic cycle, is the best-known track.","Recorded more than a decade after The Melody of Rhythm, the album reunited players who had toured together for years. Chaurasia is the nephew and student of Hariprasad Chaurasia, whose flute playing Hussain had accompanied since the 1970s, so the record also continues a family partnership. It was among Hussain's last major releases before his death in San Francisco in December 2024, at seventy-three.","The rhythmic cycles are unusual for Western listeners: some pieces count in groups that do not divide evenly, and the musicians signal the return to the first beat of the cycle, the sam in Indian terms, with shared accents. Listen for the moments when everyone lands together after a long, independent passage; that arrival is the whole drama of Hindustani rhythm, relocated into a folk-and-jazz quartet.","At the Grammy Awards in February 2024 Hussain won three prizes on one night: the album took Best Contemporary Instrumental Album, Pashto won Best Global Music Performance, and Shakti's This Moment won Best Global Music Album. Reviews praised the record's lightness and communication. Some listeners find crossover projects of this kind too polite, but this one is widely heard as a genuine conversation.","Start with Pashto, count along if you like, and see whether you can feel where the cycle restarts. Then listen for how the tabla changes its sound, from dry clicks to a deep, bending bass. Compare it with Hariprasad Chaurasia's Call of the Valley for the flute's classical roots, and with Making Music, Hussain's own acoustic quartet from 1987."],"tatsuro-yamashita-ride-on-time":["Ride on Time is Tatsuro Yamashita's breakthrough album: bright, tightly played pop built on the grooves of American soul and funk, with crisp guitar, springy bass, horns, and his own clear tenor stacked into walls of harmony. The title track, with its driving rhythm and soaring chorus, is the emblem. Around it are slower ballads and mid-tempo songs in which every guitar part and vocal overdub has clearly been planned to the last detail.","Yamashita had been making records since the early 1970s, first with the band Sugar Babe, without mass success. In 1980 the title song was used in a television advertisement for Maxell cassette tapes in which he himself appeared, and it made him a star. The moment mattered: Japanese listeners were buying car stereos and Walkmans, and this polished, outward-looking pop fitted the new urban affluence that would later be called city pop.","Many of the harmony vocals are Yamashita alone, overdubbed again and again, a technique he learned from records like the Beach Boys' and later took to extremes in his a cappella albums. Listen also for My Sugar Babe, which nods back to his first band. And the album's long absence from streaming, since Yamashita resisted putting his catalogue online, is part of why it became a collector's object abroad.","In Japan the album confirmed Yamashita as the country's leading pop craftsman and is still regarded as a turning point in his career. Western interest came much later, in the 2010s wave of city pop rediscovery, when listeners found in it a Japanese mirror of American soft rock and funk. Some critics wonder whether it adds anything to its models; admirers answer that the playing and arranging simply exceed them.","Play the title track loud and listen to the rhythm section first: the bass and drums are the engine. Then follow only the backing vocals in the chorus to hear how many voices are stacked. Compare it with Earth, Wind and Fire's That's the Way of the World, which shows where the horns and groove came from, and then play For You to hear the style at its peak."],"tatsuro-yamashita-christmas-eve":["Christmas Eve is a quiet, minor-tinged song about waiting alone on Christmas Eve, as the rain outside turns to snow and the person the singer hopes for will probably not come. The verses are restrained, over soft keyboards and a steady beat. Then comes the song's signature: a wordless interlude in which dozens of layers of Yamashita's own voice sing the harmonies of Pachelbel's Canon, like a one-man cathedral choir.","The song first appeared on the 1983 album Melodies and was released as a single that December, with modest results. Its fortunes changed in 1988, when the railway company JR Tokai used it in a series of advertisements for its bullet trains, showing lovers reunited at stations at Christmas. The song climbed to number one on the Oricon chart in December 1989 and has returned to the charts every December since.","The a cappella section is not merely decoration. Its borrowed Baroque chord pattern, the same sequence that has driven countless pop songs, gives the song its sense of inevitability, and every voice in it is Yamashita, recorded one track at a time in an era before digital editing. Notice too that the lyric never resolves: we never learn whether the lover arrives.","It is now among the best-selling Japanese singles in history and a permanent part of the Japanese Christmas, which is chiefly a romantic holiday for couples rather than a family one. Critics admire its craft, especially the choir interlude, though Yamashita has at times seemed ambivalent about its dominance of his reputation. Its long chart life is the subject of Japanese records and endless seasonal commentary.","Wait for the interlude and listen to how the choir enters one line at a time before the whole texture is complete. Then listen to Pachelbel's Canon itself and hear the same pattern in strings. Compare with Phil Spector's Christmas album for another producer building a wall of sound around a holiday, and with Ride on Time for Yamashita's brighter side."],"teresa-teng-tian-mi-mi":["Tian Mi Mi is a light, lilting love song in a gentle swaying rhythm, sung by Teresa Teng in the soft, breathy, almost whispered style that made her the most beloved Chinese-language singer of her age. The singer compares the beloved's smile to flowers opening in a spring breeze and wonders where she has seen that face before: in a dream. The melody is simple and circling, the arrangement light, and the whole song lasts barely three minutes.","The tune is adapted from an Indonesian folk song, Dayung Sampan, with new Mandarin words by the lyricist Zhuang Nu. Teng, born in Taiwan to a family that had fled the mainland, was by 1979 a star across Taiwan, Hong Kong, Japan and Southeast Asia. In mainland China, just opening after the Cultural Revolution, her songs circulated on smuggled cassettes and were officially condemned as decadent, which only increased their appeal.","Teng's apparent simplicity is carefully made. Listen to how she lightens the ends of phrases, letting a note fade almost to breath, and how she avoids the forceful projection of Chinese opera or revolutionary songs. For mainland listeners in 1979, used to martial choruses, that private, confiding tone was itself the novelty: a voice addressing one person rather than a nation.","The song became one of the most widely known in Chinese pop and is still covered constantly. Its fame was renewed by Peter Chan's 1996 Hong Kong film Comrades: Almost a Love Story, whose Chinese title is Tian Mi Mi and whose plot is built around Teng's songs and her death. Critics now treat it as a model of how Mandopop blended regional folk melody with Western arrangement.","Listen once for the melody, then again only for Teng's breathing and phrase endings. Compare it with The Moon Represents My Heart to hear the same quiet intimacy in a slower song. Then watch Comrades: Almost a Love Story, which shows what her voice meant to young mainland migrants in Hong Kong. Cui Jian's Nothing to My Name is the opposite voice of the next decade."],"teresa-teng-dandan-youqing":["Dandan Youqing is a concept album of twelve songs, each setting a classical Chinese poem, mostly ci lyrics from the Song dynasty and the late Tang, by writers including the poet-emperor Li Yu and Su Shi. The arrangements mix Chinese instruments such as the erhu, pipa and zheng with strings and gentle synthesisers, and Teng sings the thousand-year-old words in her lightest, most unhurried voice. The best-known song sets Su Shi's mid-autumn poem addressing the moon and an absent brother.","Teng conceived the album at the height of her fame and considered it one of her most personal projects. Several Hong Kong and Taiwanese composers were commissioned to write new melodies, since the original tunes to which these lyrics were sung had long been lost. The ci form itself began as song lyrics written to popular melodies, so the album returned old poetry to its first purpose, and brought it to listeners who knew the poems only from school.","Some of the settings are by Li Yu, the last ruler of the Southern Tang, who wrote his most famous verses as a captive after losing his kingdom, longing for a homeland he could not see again. For listeners on either side of the Taiwan Strait in 1983, that theme of exile and a divided country carried a quiet present-day charge that the album never states.","The album was admired on release as a bold step for a pop star, and several of its songs, especially the Su Shi setting, became standards sung at Mid-Autumn festivals across the Chinese world. Critics now often call it her artistic peak and one of the most significant Chinese pop albums of the 1980s, a model for later singers who have set classical poetry.","Begin with the Su Shi moon song, ideally after reading the poem in translation, and notice how the melody follows its questioning, circling shape. Then listen through for the traditional instruments threaded under the pop arrangement. Mahler set Tang poetry in Das Lied von der Erde; comparing the two shows how differently East and West have sung the same poetic world."],"mariya-takeuchi-variety":["Variety is Mariya Takeuchi's comeback album and the first on which she wrote every song herself. As the title suggests, it ranges widely: sleek dance-pop, fifties-style doo-wop pastiche, lush ballads and gentle mid-tempo songs, all sung in her warm, slightly husky alto. The arrangements, by Tatsuro Yamashita, are immaculate, with tight rhythm guitar, bright horns and layered harmony vocals, and the album contains Plastic Love alongside the single Mō Ichido.","Takeuchi had been a successful singer in the late 1970s, but the idol-style schedule of promotion exhausted her. She married Yamashita in 1982 and stepped back, returning two years later as a songwriter in control of her own work, now on Moon Records, the label associated with Yamashita. Japan's economy was booming, and this polished, urban music suited the new Tokyo of nightclubs and cars that city pop soundtracked.","Yamashita's multi-tracked voice is all over the backing vocals, so the album is also a record of a marriage as a working partnership. Listen for the doo-wop and girl-group touches, which reflect the couple's shared love of American pop of the late fifties and early sixties; Takeuchi was writing from the same record collection that gave Yamashita his harmony style.","Variety was a commercial success in Japan and re-established Takeuchi as a major artist on her own terms. For decades it was regarded at home as a solid, well-crafted album; the global rediscovery of Plastic Love in the late 2010s sent a new, largely non-Japanese audience back to the whole record, which is now often ranked among the defining albums of city pop.","Do not stop at Plastic Love: play the album in order and notice how the styles change from track to track, as the title promises. Listen for Yamashita's backing harmonies. Compare it with For You to hear husband and wife working the same palette, and with Carole King's Tapestry for another songwriter taking back control of her own voice."],"mariya-takeuchi-request":["Request is Mariya Takeuchi singing her own songs that other artists had made famous, plus new material. It is quieter and more adult than Variety: mid-tempo ballads and wistful songs about love, parting and ordinary life, arranged by Tatsuro Yamashita with warm keyboards and strings. The best-known track is Eki (Station), a song about glimpsing a former lover on a crowded train platform years later and choosing not to speak.","Through the mid-1980s Takeuchi had become one of Japan's leading songwriters for idol singers, providing hits for performers such as Naoko Kawai and Akina Nakamori. Request gathered several of those songs in her own versions, answering, as its title implies, fans who wanted to hear the writer sing them. It became one of her biggest sellers and confirmed that she no longer needed promotion or television to reach a mass audience.","Eki was first recorded by Akina Nakamori, whose version is darker and more dramatic. Yamashita reportedly felt that reading misunderstood the song, and his arrangement for his wife is deliberately understated, as if restoring the writer's intention. Listening to both versions side by side is a lesson in how an arrangement decides what a lyric means.","The album was a major commercial success in Japan and is often cited as proof of Takeuchi's quality as a songwriter rather than a performer only. Its gentler style places it closer to mainstream kayōkyoku than to the funk-driven city pop that later audiences sought, so it is less celebrated abroad than Variety, though Japanese listeners have long held it as dearly.","Start with Eki and listen to how little the arrangement does beneath her voice; then find Nakamori's version for comparison. Follow with the songs she wrote for other singers and imagine them with a younger idol's voice. Pair it with Carole King's Writer, another songwriter reclaiming songs made famous by others."],"ladysmith-black-mambazo-long-walk-to-freedom":["A look back over more than forty years of songs, re-sung by the choir with a line of guests from South Africa and abroad. The core is unchanged: a deep bank of bass voices that moves like one body, tenors and altos stacked above it, and Joseph Shabalala's high lead calling phrases for the group to answer. Guest voices and a few discreet instruments slip into that frame rather than replacing it, so the record sounds like a gathering of friends round a very old table.","The title borrows the name of Nelson Mandela's autobiography, and the choice is pointed: Ladysmith had sung at his inauguration and travelled with him, and their music had become a kind of sound-emblem of the new South Africa. By the mid-2000s Shabalala was in his sixties and planning for his sons to take over the group. This album, made for an American label, was a deliberate summing-up, pairing the choir's own repertoire with musicians who had been shaped by it.","Listen underneath the singing for the feet. Isicathamiya takes its name from a Zulu word for walking softly on tiptoe, and the choir's gentle, synchronised stepping, developed in migrant workers' hostels where loud stamping was forbidden, is part of the rhythm. Notice too the small vocal effects the bass line throws in, clicks, hums and breathy sighs, which act as percussion in music that has no drummer.","Reviewers generally welcomed it as a warm, well-made career anthology, though some felt that the guest spots smoothed the edges of a style whose early records sound rawer and stranger. That is the standing argument about Ladysmith since Graceland: whether international fame softened isicathamiya or simply carried it into rooms it could never otherwise have reached. The album sits on the side of reconciliation, and makes no apology for it.","Treat it as an introduction and then a doorway. Start with a track where the choir sings alone, so you learn the basic sound: lead call, massed answer, bass cushion. Then hear how a guest voice has to fit inside that texture. Afterwards go back to the 1970s albums such as Amabutho to hear the same songs younger, tighter and more austere."],"cui-jian-rock-n-roll-on-the-new-long-march":["The first full-length rock album from mainland China, and a surprisingly varied one. Cui Jian's hoarse, half-spoken voice rides over electric guitars and a heavy backbeat, but the arrangements keep reaching for Chinese colours: the reedy blare of the suona, the bamboo flute, melodies that lean on northern folk song. The collection gathers his breakthrough Nothing to My Name alongside the marching title track, which turns a revolutionary slogan into a restless, rolling rhythm.","Cui Jian had trained as a trumpeter in a state orchestra in Beijing before hearing smuggled cassettes of Western rock in the early 1980s. By 1989 his songs were the unofficial soundtrack of a student generation testing the limits of reform, and he performed for the protesters in Tiananmen Square that spring. The album came out in the same year the movement was crushed; afterwards his large concerts were cancelled and his tours curtailed.","The title is a sly piece of politics. The Long March of 1934 to 1935 is the founding legend of the Communist Party, and a New Long March was the official phrase for Deng Xiaoping's modernisation drive. By attaching rock and roll to it, Cui claims loyalty to the national project while quietly suggesting that the real journey is personal, noisy and unsupervised. Listen for how often the lyrics describe walking without knowing the destination.","Chinese listeners and critics have long treated it as the foundation stone of Chinese rock, the record that proved the music could be sung in Mandarin about Chinese life rather than imitated. Western writers first heard it mostly as a protest document, which Cui has always resisted: he insists the songs are about individual feeling as much as politics. Later bands such as Tang Dynasty took it as their starting point.","Begin with the title track and listen to the way the rhythm section chugs forward like feet on a road, then to the moment the suona cuts across the guitars. Compare the whole record with Western rock of 1989 and it will sound a decade behind in production and a decade ahead in stakes. Read a translation of the song titles before listening."],"cui-jian-a-piece-of-red-cloth":["A slow, brooding song that builds from a quiet, almost tender opening into a heavy, grinding climax. Cui Jian sings as a man who has been blindfolded with a strip of red cloth by someone he trusts, and who finds, disturbingly, that he is content not to see; he describes being led, feeling warm, and losing any wish to look around. The band holds back for long stretches, so that when the guitars finally surge the effect is claustrophobic rather than liberating.","Written after the suppression of the 1989 protests and released on the 1991 album Solution, it arrived when open political comment was impossible and rock concerts were closely watched. Cui performed it wearing a red blindfold over his eyes, an image that audiences instantly understood and officials could not quite name. Red is the colour of the Party and the flag, but also of weddings and good fortune, and the song lives inside that ambiguity.","The song never names a government, a party or a date, which is exactly what made it survivable. Everything is carried by the metaphor of intimacy: the blindfolder could be a lover. Listen to how the melody stays gentle even as the words grow darker, a mismatch between comfort and captivity that is the whole argument. The trumpet, Cui's own first instrument, appears like a voice from outside the room.","Many Chinese listeners regard it as Cui's deepest song, more ambivalent and therefore more lasting than the defiant anthems. Western commentators often read it simply as an allegory of the Party, which is fair but incomplete: the uncomfortable point is the singer's own willingness to stay blind. The image of the red blindfold has since become one of the defining visual symbols of post-1989 Chinese culture.","Listen once without a translation, following only the shape: the hush, the long build, the weight of the ending. Then read a summary of the words and listen again, noticing where gentleness and threat coincide. Pair it with Nothing to My Name to hear how far Cui's voice travelled in five years, from a shout of longing to something closer to confession."],"happy-end-happy-end":["The debut of the band that taught Japanese rock to speak Japanese. The sound is shaggy and American, closer to Buffalo Springfield and the West Coast than to the British beat groups most Tokyo bands copied: loping guitars, a sturdy bass, drums a little behind the beat. What is new is the singing, which fits the rhythm and vowels of Japanese to rock phrasing without sounding translated, and the lyrics, which dwell on weather, streets and small moods rather than slogans.","Haruomi Hosono, Eiichi Ohtaki, Shigeru Suzuki and the drummer and lyricist Takashi Matsumoto formed the group in Tokyo in 1969 and released this on the independent URC label, home of the underground folk scene. At the time the orthodoxy held that rock had to be sung in English to sound right, and the album set off a public argument about whether Japanese-language rock was possible at all. It is nicknamed Yudemen, boiled noodles, after a shop sign in its cover art.","Matsumoto's lyrics use an old-fashioned, almost literary Japanese, full of seasonal imagery drawn from poetry rather than pop. That archaism is the trick: by reaching back past postwar pop to an older written language, the band found words that could sit inside a rock rhythm without sounding like translated English. Listen for how the singers stretch or clip syllables to land the stresses where a rock line wants them.","Sales were modest, and at first the band was a cause among musicians more than a public success. The so-called Japanese-language rock debate, played out in the music press, made them notorious. Within a decade their side had plainly won, and the four members went on to shape Japanese pop for the next forty years, through Yellow Magic Orchestra, Ohtaki's solo records and Matsumoto's hundreds of lyrics for idols.","Hear it as a first draft. It is rougher than Kazemachi Roman, and the playing sometimes wanders, but you can hear four people inventing a language. Listen to the guitars trading lines like Neil Young and Stephen Stills, then follow the vocal melody, which always bends toward the natural fall of Japanese speech. Then play Kazemachi Roman to hear the idea fully formed."],"happy-end-kaze-wo-atsumete":["A short, unhurried song from Kazemachi Roman, sung by Haruomi Hosono in a soft, slightly nasal voice over a gently strummed acoustic guitar, a warm electric line and a relaxed rhythm section. The title means Gathering the Wind. The narrator drifts through a morning city that turns dreamlike, noticing a tram, a harbour, a haze over the rooftops, and wishes to gather up the wind and sail across the blue sky. It is over almost before it has begun.","Hosono wrote the music and Takashi Matsumoto the words, as part of Matsumoto's plan for Kazemachi Roman as a portrait of an old Tokyo that the Olympic building boom of the 1960s had swept away. In 2003 Sofia Coppola used the song in Lost in Translation, set in the modern Tokyo that had replaced Matsumoto's city, and a track that had been a cult item abroad suddenly reached a huge international audience.","The city in the song is imaginary as much as remembered: Matsumoto called it Kazemachi, wind town, a composite of the neighbourhoods of his childhood. So the dreamy drift is not only mood but content, a memory of places already demolished. Notice how the bass keeps walking steadily while the voice floats above it, as if the ground were solid and only the singer were adrift.","In Japan it has long been treated as a small classic of 1970s rock and has been covered many times. Its second life after Lost in Translation led some fans to worry that it had become a kind of exotic mood music for foreign audiences; others saw the film as the start of Western interest in Japanese rock and city pop that has only grown since.","Listen on headphones and let the song be as small as it is. Notice the stillness of the arrangement and the way the voice sits in the middle of it. Then hear it in its place near the middle of Kazemachi Roman, where it forms a pause between busier songs, and after that try Tatsuro Yamashita's For You to hear where this relaxed city feeling went next."],"ac-dc-let-there-be-rock":["Eight tracks of the band at its rawest: Angus Young's lead guitar squealing and sliding over Malcolm Young's relentless, clipped rhythm chords, a drummer who almost never leaves the plain backbeat, and Bon Scott's leering, conversational howl. The title track tells a mock-biblical creation story in which rock and roll is born and spreads, and ends in a long, frantic guitar solo. Whole Lotta Rosie, a tribute to a memorably large lover, became their concert centrepiece.","Recorded quickly in Sydney early in 1977 with the production team of Harry Vanda and George Young, Angus and Malcolm's older brother, who had been in the Easybeats. The band had relocated to Britain the year before and were building a following in pubs and clubs alongside the first wave of punk. Punks and hard-rock fans rarely agreed on anything, but many of them agreed on AC/DC, whose short, loud songs had no time for progressive-rock grandeur.","The rhythm guitar is the secret engine. Malcolm Young plays open, ringing chords with a lot of space between them, so the band swings rather than pummels; the gaps are as important as the notes. Listen also for the legend attached to the title track: the band have said that Angus's amplifier began to smoke during the take and that they kept rolling. Whatever the truth, you can hear a performance pushed past its safe limits.","British and Australian rock papers loved it; American critics were largely dismissive, hearing only crude noise, a judgment that took years to reverse. Now it is often cited by musicians, from metal to punk, as the band's purest statement, with a harder, dirtier sound than the later, more polished Mutt Lange productions. Debate continues over which of the several different mixes and national pressings sounds best.","Play it loud and follow one guitar at a time. On the first pass, listen only to Malcolm on the rhythm part and feel how it locks with the drums. On the second, follow Angus. Then play Highway to Hell and Back in Black to hear the same band made sleeker and bigger, and decide which you prefer."],"shakti-a-handful-of-beauty":["The band's first studio album, and a tighter, more composed record than the live debut. John McLaughlin plays a custom acoustic guitar with extra strings and a scalloped fingerboard that lets him bend notes like a veena player; L. Shankar's violin sings in the Carnatic manner, sliding and ornamenting every pitch; Zakir Hussain's tabla and T. H. Vinayakram's clay-pot ghatam weave interlocking rhythms underneath. Pieces range from gentle, songlike melodies to breakneck unison runs in long, asymmetric rhythmic cycles.","McLaughlin had disbanded the loud electric Mahavishnu Orchestra and turned to the Indian music he had studied for years, founding Shakti with musicians from both the North Indian Hindustani and South Indian Carnatic traditions. That mix was itself unusual in India. Recording for Columbia, a major American label, gave the group a global platform at a moment when Western interest in Indian music had cooled after its late-1960s fashion.","The rhythmic counting is the hidden architecture. Many passages rest on Carnatic methods of dividing a long cycle into groups of five, seven or nine, and the musicians often end phrases with a tihai, a figure played three times so that its last note lands exactly on the first beat of the cycle. Once you hear one, you start anticipating them, and the climaxes suddenly make structural sense.","Jazz critics who had followed Mahavishnu were puzzled by the acoustic turn, and some found the album too exotic; Indian musicians and listeners, by contrast, were struck by how seriously McLaughlin had absorbed the rules. Its reputation has risen steadily. It is now seen as a model of genuine collaboration in world fusion, where no side merely decorates the other, and the group's reunion decades later confirmed the point.","Start with a slow piece to hear the guitar and violin sing together, then move to a fast one and focus on the percussion. Try clapping along to find the start of each cycle; losing count is part of the fun. Compare the result with the Mahavishnu Orchestra's Birds of Fire to hear the same guitarist's intensity rewired for acoustic instruments."],"shakti-this-moment":["A reunion record from the reformed group, nearly half a century after its first incarnation. McLaughlin now plays mostly electric guitar with a clean, singing tone; Shankar Mahadevan's voice carries the long melodic lines and the rapid spoken rhythmic syllables of Indian percussion; Ganesh Rajagopalan's violin keeps the Carnatic ornamentation; and Zakir Hussain's tabla works in close dialogue with V. Selvaganesh's kanjira, a small frame drum. The mood is warmer and more reflective than the 1970s records, though the fast unisons remain.","Shakti had re-formed in the late 1990s as Remember Shakti and reunited again for a fiftieth-anniversary tour. Much of the album was assembled from sessions recorded in different places, a method that had become normal during the pandemic years. It won the Grammy Award for Best Global Music Album in 2024, and turned out to be one of the last major records of Zakir Hussain, who died at the end of that year.","Listen for the vocal rhythm syllables, called konnakol in South India and bol in the North, which Mahadevan and the percussionists recite at speed. They are not words but spoken drum strokes, and they let the singer become part of the rhythm section. The kanjira player Selvaganesh's father, T. H. Vinayakram, played ghatam on the original 1970s records, so the band's lineage runs literally through families.","Most reviewers welcomed it as a graceful late chapter rather than a nostalgia act, singling out the interplay of tabla and kanjira. Some longtime fans missed the fierce acoustic guitar of the first incarnation and found the electric tone too smooth. Its Grammy, and Hussain's death months later, gave it the aura of a farewell, which colours how it is heard now.","Put it beside A Handful of Beauty and listen for what has changed: the electric guitar, the singing voice, the more spacious tempos. Then focus on the percussion exchanges, where tabla and kanjira toss phrases back and forth like a conversation. Let the singer guide you through the melodies before you chase the counting."],"samulnori-binari":["A prayer for blessing and good fortune, and the most vocal piece in the group's repertoire. One singer chants a long, winding invocation in a strained, heavily ornamented voice while the four percussion instruments, the small bright gong, the large deep gong, the hourglass drum and the barrel drum, keep a steady, rolling pulse beneath. The other players answer in refrains. The chant wishes health, harvest and luck on the house and the listeners, and the energy slowly builds as the rhythm tightens.","Samulnori was founded in Seoul in 1978 by Kim Duk-soo and colleagues who had grown up in travelling folk troupes. They took the music of village farmers' bands and ritual performers, which was dying out under rapid urbanisation, and adapted it for a seated quartet on a concert stage. Binari comes from the tradition of shamanic and village rites that opened festivals, blessed new houses and began the performances of the wandering troupes.","The chant is built to be adapted: a performer traditionally inserts the names of the place, the house or the occasion, so every Binari is partly composed on the spot. Listen to how the singer's line and the gongs are loosely coordinated, not rigidly locked, a flexibility that lets the rhythm breathe. The small gong player is effectively the conductor, signalling changes of tempo with sharp accents.","Purists in Korea at first criticised Samulnori for pulling ritual and village music off the ground and onto the stage, fixing into set pieces what had been open and communal. The group's international success and its role in teaching a generation of young Koreans to play the instruments have largely settled that argument. Samul nori is now a genre with thousands of amateur groups, and Binari is one of its standard openers.","Do not try to follow the words; follow the voice as another instrument, rising and bending over the drums. Notice the difference in sound between the two gongs, one sharp and metallic, one a long wash. Then listen to Uttari Pungmul to hear the same instruments without the singer, and to a pansori recording to hear where that vocal style comes from."],"samulnori-samdo-seoljanggu":["A showpiece for four players of the janggu, the hourglass drum struck on one head with a thin stick and on the other with the palm or a mallet, giving a sharp crack and a soft thud from a single instrument. The piece moves from a slow, spacious opening through a sequence of rhythmic patterns that grow faster and denser, the players sometimes in exact unison and sometimes splitting into interlocking parts, until it ends in a whirling climax.","Seoljanggu was the solo drum dance of the farmers' bands, a moment when the lead drummer stepped forward to show off. Samulnori's founders gathered rhythmic patterns from three different provinces, which is what Samdo, three regions, refers to, and combined them into a single concert piece for the stage. It became a standard of the repertoire and a teaching piece for the many samul nori groups that sprang up in Korean schools and universities.","Watch or listen for the breathing. Korean rhythm is often described as built on the breath rather than the metronome, and the players lengthen or compress the beat in unison, a slight stretch at the start of each cycle that no written score could show. The patterns are also mostly in groups of three inside a longer cycle, which is why the music sways rather than marches.","Among Korean percussionists the piece is regarded as a test of ensemble discipline, and performances are compared closely for their precision and their feel. Western listeners, who first heard it on the group's international tours of the 1980s, often compared it to Japanese taiko, which Korean musicians find misleading: the janggu tradition is lighter, quicker and more closely tied to dance.","Start by listening to one drum sound at a time: the crack of the stick, the thud of the hand. Then let the whole ensemble wash over you as the speed builds. If you can, watch a filmed performance, because the players' shoulders and heads move with the rhythm, and that movement is part of the music."],"tinariwen-amassakoul":["The record that carried Tinariwen out of the Sahara. Electric guitars play looping, hypnotic figures that circle around one or two notes, the bass throbs, and hand drums and clapping keep a loping rhythm like a camel's walk. Over this, the singers trade lead lines and a chorus of voices, often women's, answers in call and response. The title means the traveller in Tamasheq, and the songs speak of exile, the desert, homesickness and the hardship of the Tuareg people.","The band's core members met in Libyan and Algerian camps in the 1980s, exiles and rebels from northern Mali, and their songs circulated for years on home-made cassettes. After a peace settlement they returned to Mali. Their appearance at the Festival au Désert near Timbuktu in 2001 brought them to European attention, and this second internationally released album, recorded in Bamako, reached a far wider audience than their first.","The guitar style is borrowed from older music. The drones and repeated figures imitate the tende, a drum-and-voice tradition, and the imzad, a one-stringed fiddle played by Tuareg women; the electric guitar simply took over their roles. Listen for the small bent and slurred notes, which recall these instruments more than the blues they are usually compared with.","Western critics embraced it quickly, often calling it desert blues and comparing it to John Lee Hooker; the band have been ambivalent about the label, and the guitar lineage lies closer to home. Rock musicians became fans and later collaborators. The later rebellion and conflict in northern Mali from 2012 gave the album's themes of exile a painful new relevance.","Let the repetition work on you; the pieces do not develop so much as deepen. Focus on the interplay between the lead voice and the chorus, then listen for the slightly different guitar patterns running alongside each other. Afterwards play Aman Iman to hear the band with a fuller production, and Ali Farka Touré for a neighbouring Malian guitar tradition."],"utada-hikaru-fantome":["Eleven songs of grief, love and survival, sung almost entirely in Japanese. The textures are cleaner and more acoustic than Utada's earlier work: string arrangements, piano, guitar and restrained beats, with the voice mixed close and unadorned. Guest singers appear on a few tracks, including Sheena Ringo on a playful duet about a stolen short holiday. The title, French for ghost, points to the presence that haunts the whole record: the singer's late mother, the enka singer Keiko Fuji.","Utada had stepped away from music in 2010, moved to London, and then lost their mother in 2013. They married and had a child in the same years. The album came out in 2016 after a gap of eight years since their previous studio album, amid intense expectation in Japan. It was released by a major label and became one of the year's best-selling Japanese records, a notable feat in a declining physical market.","Listen to how often the songs address someone who cannot answer, and how the arrangements leave space where a response would go. The opening and closing songs mirror each other in theme, framing the record as a single arc from loss toward acceptance. Some fans hear echoes of enka phrasing in the vocal line, a quiet bridge to the mother's own genre.","Critics in Japan and abroad hailed it as Utada's most mature work, and it signalled a shift in how their career was judged, from teen sensation to serious songwriter. A few listeners missed the R&B urgency of First Love. Its frankness about bereavement was unusual for mainstream J-pop, and it has been widely credited with opening room for more personal writing in the genre.","Listen straight through, in order, ideally with the lyric translations to hand, since the emotional story depends on the sequence. Notice how the singing avoids big displays and stays close to speech. Then go back to First Love and hear the same voice at fifteen, and you will understand how far this record travelled."],"utada-hikaru-bad-mode":["Utada's first album sung in both Japanese and English, switching languages within songs. The sound is warm electronic pop and R&B, built from soft synthesisers, house and garage rhythms and live touches. The centrepiece is Somewhere Near Marseilles, a twelve-minute track co-produced with Floating Points that drifts from a dance groove into a long instrumental stretch. Elsewhere the songs deal with self-care, bad days and being gentle with oneself, sung with a new lightness.","Made largely during the pandemic, partly in London and partly remotely, it followed Utada's announcement in 2021 that they are non-binary. Collaborators included Floating Points and A. G. Cook of the PC Music collective, and the album gathered earlier singles such as One Last Kiss, the theme for the final Evangelion film. It was released in January 2022 to wide international attention, a sign of Utada's reach outside Japan.","The title is a pun: bad mode is a joking phrase for being in a low mood, and the album treats that state with humour rather than drama. Listen to how the switches between English and Japanese often mark a change of voice, from outward to inward. In Somewhere Near Marseilles, notice the long passage where the singing stops and the synthesisers simply keep travelling.","It was one of the best-reviewed albums of Utada's career, especially in English-language publications that had not previously covered J-pop closely; many placed it on year-end lists. The collaborations with Floating Points and A. G. Cook were seen as evidence of Utada's standing among electronic musicians. Some Japanese fans found the bilingual approach less immediate than earlier work.","Begin with the title track to get the mood, then set aside twelve minutes for Somewhere Near Marseilles and let it unfold without skipping. Compare it with Floating Points' Promises to hear what he brings. Then play Fantôme: the grief of that record and the self-kindness of this one read as two chapters of the same story."],"seo-taiji-and-boys-hayeoga":["The title track of the trio's second album, and a startling collision. It opens with the piercing, nasal sound of the taepyeongso, the Korean double-reed shawm used in court processions and farmers' bands, which returns throughout; underneath it the band lays down heavy metal guitars, hip-hop beats and rapped verses. The chorus is sung, pleading and melodic. The song is a plea to a lover who is leaving, asking why and begging them to reconsider.","Seo Taiji, a former heavy-metal bassist, and the dancers Yang Hyun-suk and Lee Juno had transformed Korean pop in 1992 with Nan Arayo. For the follow-up Seo chose to go stranger rather than safer. South Korea was newly democratic, the censorship regime was loosening, and a young consumer generation wanted music that sounded like their own. Putting a traditional instrument at the centre of a hit was without precedent in Korean pop.","The taepyeongso part follows the ornamented phrasing of traditional music rather than a Western scale, so it clashes slightly with the guitars. That friction is deliberate. Listen for the moment the metal guitars drop out and the shawm and beat are left alone together, which briefly makes the song sound like a street procession.","It was a huge hit and made the group the defining youth act of the decade. Critics in Korea have since treated it as a milestone in fusing traditional sound with modern pop, a move later K-pop acts would repeat. Some traditional musicians thought the shawm was being used as an exotic effect; others welcomed its introduction to a teenage audience.","Wait for the opening shawm, then notice how quickly the heavy guitars arrive. Follow the three layers, the old instrument, the metal band and the rap, and listen for where they meet and where they refuse to blend. Then play Nan Arayo to hear how much bolder this second single is, and a later K-pop track that uses traditional instruments."],"seo-taiji-and-boys-come-back-home":["A dark, heavy hip-hop track from the group's fourth album, built on a slow, pounding beat, deep bass and gritty guitar. The rapping is harder and angrier than on their earlier hits, closer to American gangsta rap in tone than to the bright pop that made them famous. The song is addressed to teenagers who have run away from home, acknowledging their frustration and loneliness, and asking them to return where they are wanted.","By 1995 the trio were the most famous musicians in South Korea, and the fourth album set out to prove their seriousness. Its songs took on school pressure, national division and social hypocrisy. The same album triggered a fight with the state's pre-release censorship board over another song, Sidae Yugam, whose lyrics were ordered changed; the group released it without words, and the public outcry helped bring an end to that censorship the following year.","The sound is closely modelled on West Coast and East Coast American hip-hop of the early 1990s, but the message runs against gangsta rap's usual themes: it is essentially a song about returning to family. That tension, tough sound and tender message, is the song's hook. Listen for how the chorus lifts from the grim verses into something closer to a hymn.","It was a major hit and is widely reported to have prompted runaway teenagers to go home, a story that became part of its legend. Critics credit it with helping to establish hip-hop as a serious form in Korea. The group disbanded early in 1996, which gave the fourth album an extra weight as their final statement.","Listen for the contrast between the heavy beat and the pleading chorus. Compare it with Dr. Dre's The Chronic to hear the American sound it borrowed, and then with Hayeoga to hear how quickly the group changed styles. Knowing the censorship story of the same album adds weight to how seriously its young audience took these songs."],"blind-willie-johnson-its-nobodys-fault-but-mine":["One man, one guitar played with a knife or bottleneck, and a voice like gravel in a bucket. Johnson sings a short, stubborn gospel verse and then lets the slide finish his sentences, the guitar answering each phrase with a whining, vocal line that climbs to the same pitch his throat has just left. The song barely changes over three minutes; its force comes from repetition, from the rasp of the false bass voice he used on street corners, and from the way voice and string trade places until you cannot tell which is singing.","Johnson recorded it at his first session for Columbia in Dallas in December 1927, the same day that produced Dark Was the Night, Cold Was the Ground. He was a blind itinerant evangelist from Texas who sang for coins outside churches and on town squares, and the record companies of the late 1920s were scouring the South for 'race' and sacred records to sell to Black buyers. His discs sold well by the standards of religious records, then fell silent when the Depression gutted the market after 1930.","The song is about a person who owns their own salvation: if the singer dies unable to read the Bible, no one else is to blame. Sung by a man who could not see a page, that ordinary gospel sentiment turns sharp and strange. Listen, too, for how often the slide completes a line the voice leaves unsaid, a trick Johnson used so consistently that the guitar becomes a second preacher rather than an accompaniment.","In its own day it was one more sacred single; its afterlife is what made it famous. Folk-revival collectors reissued Johnson on LP in the 1950s and 1960s, and rock musicians discovered him there. Led Zeppelin's heavy, rearranged 'Nobody's Fault but Mine' on Presence in 1976 carried the title to millions, and a long line of blues and gospel singers have covered it since. Debate now is less about the song than about credit, and how little of the rock-era money reached its source.","Play it straight after Dark Was the Night, which has no words at all, and notice how the same slide technique serves here as call and response. Then listen to Son House's Preachin' the Blues for the Mississippi side of the same argument between church and juke joint. Any good remaster of the Columbia sides will do; surface noise is part of the texture, not a flaw to fear."],"fletcher-henderson-the-stampede":["A fast, crowded three minutes from the band that taught New York how to swing in sections. Don Redman's arrangement pits trumpets against reeds in quick, clipped exchanges, the saxophones sometimes playing a written chorus in close harmony as if they were one enormous instrument. Between the ensemble passages come hot solos, and the one everybody remembers is Coleman Hawkins on tenor saxophone, gruff and muscular, turning an instrument then thought of as a novelty honker into a lead voice.","Henderson's orchestra was the house band at the Roseland Ballroom on Broadway, a whites-only dance hall where a Black band played for white dancers. Louis Armstrong had spent 1924 and 1925 in the trumpet section, and his Chicago-born sense of rhythm had rearranged the whole band's idea of what hot playing meant. The Stampede, recorded for Columbia in 1926, shows the lesson absorbed: Redman writing for sections, soloists like Hawkins and cornettist Rex Stewart stretching out in Armstrong's wake.","Listen to the reed chorus rather than the solos. Redman writes the saxophones a phrase that swings on its own, harmonised so the section breathes as a single player. That idea, scored ensemble lines that feel improvised, is the seed of the whole swing era; Benny Goodman later bought Henderson's charts to get exactly this sound. The tuba and banjo keep the rhythm old-fashioned, a reminder of how new the idea still was.","Jazz writers have long treated this record as the moment Hawkins found the tenor saxophone's voice, a claim that holds up when you compare his rubbery earlier solos. Henderson himself was underrated for decades because his records were ragged next to Ellington's and his business sense poor; recent scholarship credits the band, and Redman especially, with inventing the big-band grammar that Goodman, Basie and Ellington all spoke.","Count the choruses and mark who plays each one; you will hear a written ensemble, a solo, a written ensemble, like verses and refrains. Then play Henderson's own King Porter Stomp from 1933, where the tuba has become a string bass and the rhythm floats. Hawkins's Body and Soul from 1939 shows where that solo voice ended up."],"memphis-minnie-bumble-bee":["A lithe, buzzing guitar figure, picked rather than strummed, and a clear, unhurried voice. Memphis Minnie builds the song on one extended image, a lover pictured as a bee who comes and goes and stings, and plays around it with a lilting groove that sits somewhere between country dance and city blues. The guitar is the star as much as the singing: fast treble runs, a steady thumbed bass, and small hesitations that make the rhythm hum like the insect in the title.","Minnie, born Lizzie Douglas, had busked on Beale Street in Memphis since girlhood and was a professional guitarist in a field dominated by men. A Columbia talent scout recorded her in 1929 with her partner Kansas Joe McCoy, and Bumble Bee became her first real success. She cut it more than once over the next few years, sometimes with Joe's second guitar, and it established the persona that carried her to Chicago and a career lasting into the 1950s.","The insect is a double entendre, as blues listeners of 1929 would have heard at once; the song is frankly about desire, but Minnie keeps it playful rather than lurid. The real signature is instrumental: listen for the way the bass line under her thumb stays rock-steady while the treble runs speed up and slow down, an independence of hands that many male guitarists of the day could not match.","The record sold well enough to spawn imitations and answer songs, and its image passed into the blues vocabulary; Muddy Waters's Honey Bee of 1951 is one descendant. For decades histories of the blues sidelined her in favour of solitary Delta men. Feminist and revisionist scholarship since the 1980s has restored her as one of the most commercially successful and technically assured blues artists of the pre-war era.","Listen once for the words, once for the guitar alone. Then set it beside Bessie Smith's Empty Bed Blues, where a similar double meaning is carried by a big vaudeville voice and horns, and hear how differently Minnie does it with only her own hands. Her later Chicago sides, like Me and My Chauffeur Blues, show the same wit electrified."],"earl-hines-blues-in-thirds":["A slow blues for solo piano in which the right hand plays almost everything in pairs of notes a third apart, a sweet, slightly tart doubled line that sounds like two horns in harmony. Underneath, the left hand refuses to settle into the steady oom-pah of stride piano: it drops out, lands off the beat, answers the melody. The piece is brief and quiet, but its phrasing is so unpredictable that it feels improvised even though Hines returned to it throughout his life.","Hines made it among a handful of solo recordings in 1928, the year he was also making history on Louis Armstrong's Hot Five records in Chicago. He was twenty-five, newly the leader of his own band at the Grand Terrace, and developing what critics called his 'trumpet style': melody lines played with the attack and phrasing of a horn, so a piano could be heard over a band in the days before amplification.","Listen for the moments the steady pulse seems to vanish. Hines deliberately suspends the beat for a bar or two, tumbling through cross-rhythms, then drops back in exactly where the band would be; it is the same daring he shows opposite Armstrong on Weather Bird. The doubled thirds are the other clue: they are the piano imitating a brass section, which is why his playing later translated so readily to bebop's horn-like lines.","Pianists noticed at once; Teddy Wilson, Nat King Cole and a generation of swing pianists learned from these sides, and historians now mark 1928 as the year jazz piano stepped out of ragtime and stride. Hines kept the piece alive, recording it again in 1940 in a trio with Sidney Bechet and Baby Dodds. Critics debate only whether the solos or the Armstrong duets are the purer statement of his style.","Hear it next to James P. Johnson's Carolina Shout, whose left hand never stops striding, and the difference becomes obvious. Then play Hines's own 57 Varieties from the same year for the fast, show-off side of the same technique. Follow the right hand first, then on a second hearing listen only to the left."],"earl-hines-rosetta":["An easy-going, medium-tempo song with a long, affectionate melody, the kind of tune that sounds as if it has always existed. In Hines's own big-band recording the reeds state the melody smoothly, the brass punctuate, and Hines's piano cuts through in bright, octave-doubled phrases between the ensemble passages. It became his signature composition, a jazz standard that every swing pianist and many small groups took up, prized for chords that give improvisers plenty to hold on to.","Hines wrote Rosetta with Henri Woode, an arranger in his circle, and his orchestra recorded it in 1933 while in residence at the Grand Terrace Café in Chicago. The club, run with backing from Chicago's underworld, broadcast its band nightly on radio across the country, so Hines's sound reached listeners from Texas to Canada at the bottom of the Depression. The band's steady employment there made it one of the best-drilled Black orchestras of the decade.","Listen to how the melody is built: it moves in long arcs rather than short riffs, and its harmonies pass through several keys in the middle section before returning home, which is why pianists love to take it apart. Fats Waller's and Teddy Wilson's recordings of the mid-1930s spread the tune; hearing theirs next to Hines's shows how one composer's piano style can be reimagined by others.","The song outgrew its recording. Jazz histories of the 1930s often overlook the Hines band in favour of Ellington, Basie and Lunceford, but musicians never did; the orchestra would later nurture Charlie Parker, Dizzy Gillespie and Sarah Vaughan in its early-1940s edition. Rosetta survives in jam sessions and trad-jazz repertoires alike, and Hines played it for the rest of his career.","Start with Hines's 1933 band version for the tune, then find one of his later solo piano recordings of it from the 1960s or 1970s, where he unpacks it at length. Compare Fats Waller's rollicking take. Notice how the middle section wanders and then finds its way back; that journey is the song's charm."],"arlen-stormy-weather":["A torch song in which bad weather stands for a love that has left: the singer cannot shake the gloom since the lover went away, and the skies keep answering her mood. Arlen's melody opens on a long, sighing phrase that bends toward blue notes, then moves in unhurried lines rather than the snappy hooks of most Tin Pan Alley songs. The middle section lifts in hope before sinking back. Ted Koehler's lyric is plain; the melody does the weeping.","Arlen and Koehler wrote the song in 1933 for the Cotton Club Parade, the lavish floor show at Harlem's Cotton Club, where Black performers entertained white audiences. Ethel Waters introduced it there and recorded it that year. Arlen himself, a cantor's son from Buffalo with a fine voice, sang it on a hit record with Leo Reisman's orchestra. A decade later Lena Horne made it the title song of the 1943 all-Black film musical Stormy Weather.","Listen for how little of the melody sits squarely on the major scale: Arlen lets phrases fall onto flattened, blues-tinged notes, a colour he absorbed from Harlem musicians and, some biographers suggest, from synagogue chant heard in his father's services. The accompaniment matters too: Arlen, a working pianist and arranger before he was a songwriter, heard the song with its rumbling low chords already in place, and the best recordings keep that dark bass under the vocal like distant thunder.","It was an immediate hit and has been recorded hundreds of times since. Ethel Waters's reading, restrained and aching, is often held up as a turning point in her career from vaudeville comedienne to serious singer. Lena Horne's film performance, with its storm sequence and Katherine Dunham dancers, became the version most people picture, and she kept reinterpreting it, harder and angrier, in concert for the rest of her long career.","Hear Ethel Waters's 1933 record first for the song's birth, then Lena Horne's film version and her later, harder live readings. Billie Holiday's recording is another essential. Follow the melody in the opening line: notice how it keeps falling and never quite resolves until the end, the musical equivalent of clouds that will not lift."],"arlen-blues-in-the-night":["A popular song that behaves like a blues suite. Instead of the usual verse and thirty-two-bar chorus, Arlen strings together blues-shaped sections, a slow, moaning opening, a quicker passage, a lonesome train whistle in the melody, and a return, so that the piece feels like a night journey rather than a tune. Johnny Mercer's lyric is about a mother's warning against faithless lovers, remembered by a singer who learned the lesson too late, with trains and whistles carrying the loneliness across the landscape.","Arlen and Mercer wrote it in 1941 for a Warner Bros. film about a travelling jazz band, which was renamed Blues in the Night after the song. It was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Original Song that year. Big bands jumped on it just before America entered the war: Jimmie Lunceford, Woody Herman, Cab Calloway and Dinah Shore all recorded it, and several versions charted at once in an era when songs, not records, were the unit of a hit.","The best-known phrase, the one about what the singer's mother told him, was not originally at the start. Mercer had buried it in a later stanza, and Arlen, reading the draft, recognised it as the hook and pushed it to the top. Listen also for the melody's imitation of a train whistle, falling in two tones, a sound Arlen and Mercer both knew from Southern childhood memories and blues records.","Critics have long held it up as proof that Arlen understood the blues from the inside better than any other Broadway or Hollywood songwriter; Alec Wilder's study of American popular song praises its structure as unlike anything else in the popular song of its time. Some jazz musicians found it overdressed, a Hollywood idea of the blues. It lost the Oscar to Kern's The Last Time I Saw Paris, a verdict few listeners now share.","Start with a big-band version from 1941 such as Woody Herman's to hear it as the wartime public did, then a singer like Ella Fitzgerald for the melody. Track the sections as they pass: the song is a small blues journey in several stages, and the pleasure is in noticing each change of gait."],"hampton-hot-mallets":["A brisk, riff-driven swing number built around Hampton's vibraphone, whose metallic, shimmering tone floats over a front line of saxophones. The reeds play short, punchy riffs in harmony, and between them come a series of solos from musicians who would each change jazz: a young trumpeter with a fast, darting line, heavyweight tenor players, and Hampton himself, attacking the mallets with the percussive glee of a drummer, which is what he had been before he took up the vibes.","Between 1937 and 1941 Victor let Hampton, then a star of Benny Goodman's integrated quartet, lead a series of all-star studio dates in New York. This September 1939 session gathered an extraordinary cast: Dizzy Gillespie, still a swing trumpeter in Cab Calloway's band, Benny Carter on alto, Coleman Hawkins, Ben Webster and Chu Berry on tenors, and Charlie Christian on guitar. These pick-up sessions were a way to showcase stars from rival bands on one record.","The historical prize is Gillespie's solo: one of his earliest on record, still modelled on Roy Eldridge's swing trumpet but already reaching for faster, oddly angled phrases that would become bebop within five years. Hampton's vibraphone itself is a hidden story; the instrument, with its electric motor making the vibrato pulse, was barely a decade old, and he was the first to make it a jazz voice.","Collectors and historians have long prized the Hampton Victor series as a who's-who of late swing, and Hot Mallets is among the most cited for the Gillespie connection. The records were not great sellers compared with Hampton's later big-band hits, but they have aged better. Some critics note that the brief format leaves the stars too little room; others hear exactly that compression as the charm.","Listen for each soloist entering and try to tell the three tenors apart: Hawkins dense and authoritative, Webster breathier, Berry fleet. Then play Gillespie's Groovin' High from 1945 to hear where the young trumpeter's ideas went. Hampton's own Flying Home shows the vibraphonist fronting a full big band."],"hampton-midnight-sun":["A slow, dreamy ballad in which Hampton trades his usual exuberance for patience. The vibraphone states a long, winding melody full of chromatic slides, notes stepping down by the smallest intervals, while the band sustains soft, cushioned chords beneath. The motor-driven vibrato of the instrument makes each held note shimmer, so the whole piece seems lit from within. It is only a few minutes long, but its melody was striking enough to become a standard sung and played by others for decades.","Hampton led one of the loudest, most crowd-rousing big bands of the 1940s, famous for Flying Home and for whipping audiences into a frenzy. Midnight Sun, written with arranger Sonny Burke and recorded for Decca in 1947, showed another side. Swing was in commercial decline and bebop was rising; ballads like this kept a band on the radio. In 1954 Johnny Mercer added a lyric, and the tune moved into the vocal repertoire.","Mercer's lyric is one of his most ornate, packed with unusual rhymes and images of cold northern skies lit by an unexpected brightness; he matched the melody's sliding chromatic line with words that slide too. Listen for how the tune never lands where you expect: it keeps side-stepping by a half-step, which is why singers find it hard and why it sounds so unlike a standard pop ballad.","The instrumental was a modest success, but the song's reputation grew through singers. Ella Fitzgerald's recordings made it a vocalist's showpiece, and jazz writers have praised the Mercer lyric as among the most sophisticated in the American songbook. Some critics see the piece as proof that Hampton, often dismissed as a showman, had real compositional gifts beneath the flash.","Hear Hampton's instrumental first, listening to how the vibraphone's shimmer carries the melody without words. Then find Ella Fitzgerald's vocal version and notice how the lyric follows each chromatic twist. Compare Hampton's Flying Home to hear the two faces of one bandleader."],"machito-afro-cuban-jazz-suite":["A continuous suite of about seventeen minutes, composed and arranged by Chico O'Farrill for Machito's Afro-Cubans, with guest soloists from American jazz. It moves through several linked sections, lush slow passages, a driving mambo, a lilting six-eight, a straight-ahead swing episode, stitched together by the congas, bongos and timbales. Over this Charlie Parker's alto flies in and out, sometimes riding the clave rhythm, sometimes cutting across it. It was one of the first extended compositions in Latin jazz.","Machito and his brother-in-law and musical director Mario Bauzá had led the Afro-Cubans in New York since 1940, fusing Cuban dance rhythms with big-band jazz arranging. Norman Granz, the jazz impresario, produced this session in December 1950, bringing in Parker, tenor saxophonist Flip Phillips and drummer Buddy Rich. O'Farrill, a young Cuban arranger newly arrived in New York, wrote something far longer than a dance record, possible now that the long-playing record was new.","Listen for the moments when the jazz drum kit and the Cuban percussion section must share the same beat. Buddy Rich plays swing time while the congas lock to clave, and O'Farrill arranges sections so that one or the other lays out, avoiding collisions. The seams are audible if you listen for them, and they show how two rhythmic systems were negotiated in real time.","Critics at the time were divided: some jazz writers found the Latin rhythms foreign to Parker's playing, while Latin musicians admired the ambition. Today it is widely considered a founding document of Latin jazz as concert music rather than dance music, and O'Farrill's reputation as a composer rests heavily on it. Debate continues over how comfortable Parker sounds; many hear him thriving.","Listen straight through without skipping, the way a suite asks to be heard, and try to mark each section change by the shift in percussion. Then hear Gillespie's Manteca from 1947 for the other founding moment of Afro-Cuban jazz, and Parker with Strings for his other experiment with an unfamiliar backdrop."],"machito-kenya":["An album of a dozen short tracks in which Machito's Afro-Cubans play at full power: ferocious brass, dense percussion and jazz solos sharing the spotlight. The arrangements are by René Hernández and A. K. Salim, and the tracks range from pounding mambos to slower, darker pieces. Cannonball Adderley's alto takes a showcase on Cannonology; other soloists include trumpeter Joe Newman; and the percussion, with Cándido Camero and Patato Valdés on congas, sounds huge even today.","By 1957 the mambo craze had swept America and Machito's band was a fixture of New York's Palladium Ballroom, where Latin dancers and jazz fans mixed. Roulette recorded the band for this LP, and the title and imagery reflected a wider fascination with Africa during the independence movements of the 1950s. The record came when high-fidelity LPs were changing what listeners expected a band to sound like at home.","Listen for how the jazz soloists and the Cuban rhythm section are made to interlock rather than coexist. Salim, an American jazz arranger, and Hernández, a Cuban pianist-arranger, bring different instincts, and you can often hear which one wrote a track by whether the horns ride the clave or push against it. The album's cover and title were exotic marketing; the music itself is sophisticated New York big-band writing.","Kenya was admired by musicians more than by the charts and has grown in reputation since. Many Latin-jazz historians now rank it among the finest big-band Latin jazz albums, and its reissues are sought after. Some writers find the exotic packaging dated; almost none dispute the playing. Its influence runs through later Latin big bands, from Tito Puente's to the salsa orchestras of the 1970s.","Start with the title track and Cannonology to hear the two sides of the album, then play the whole record through for its variety. Afterwards compare Tito Puente's Dance Mania from the next year, a record aimed squarely at dancers, to hear two paths the same scene took."],"artie-shaw-concerto-for-clarinet":["A nine-minute showpiece spread across two sides of a twelve-inch 78, in which Shaw's clarinet dominates a big band from start to finish. It opens with a slow blues section of long, soaring lines, then gathers speed into a boogie-woogie stretch, a pounding drum feature and a final climb in which the clarinet keeps ascending until it reaches a piercing top note that ends the piece. The title borrows the classical word; the form is really a string of jazz episodes.","Shaw wrote it for the 1940 Paramount film Second Chorus, starring Fred Astaire, in which Shaw and his band appeared. He was then one of the most successful bandleaders in America after Begin the Beguine, and also its most restless, repeatedly disbanding groups and complaining about the jitterbug fans. He had real classical ambitions and would later play classical repertoire in concert, and this piece was his attempt to give swing a concert-style showcase for his instrument.","The final climb is the famous part, but the opening blues is where Shaw's style is purest: listen for his tone, slightly veiled and silky where Benny Goodman's was brighter and harder, and for how he phrases like a singer. The boogie-woogie section borrows the rolling left hand of pianists like Albert Ammons and Pete Johnson, then fashionable, and hands it to the saxophones.","It became a popular record and has been a clarinettist's test piece ever since, taken up by jazz and classical players. Critics have long called it more display than structure, and 'concerto' promises more than the episodic form delivers. Its place in history is as a marker of swing's ambitions toward the concert hall, alongside works written for Goodman by Copland and Bartók in the same decade.","Listen to the whole thing in one go; the two-part 78 split is invisible on modern reissues. Then hear Copland's Clarinet Concerto, commissioned by Goodman, to hear a classical composer answering the same question from the other side. Mozart's Clarinet Concerto shows what the word concerto meant before jazz borrowed it."],"cartola-as-rosas-nao-falam":["Cartola's most famous song is a slow samba-canção in which a man grieving a love that has ended talks to the roses in his garden and knows they cannot answer. In his own 1976 recording the tempo is barely faster than a walk, the guitars rock gently underneath, and a thin halo of strings and flute surrounds a voice that never raises itself. It lasts about three minutes and has the shape of a short art song: two contrasting halves, then a return.","Cartola was in his late sixties when he recorded it for his second album, two years after the first had rescued him from decades of obscurity. The song arrived at a moment when Brazilian audiences, living under military rule and surrounded by electric MPB and imported rock, were rediscovering the older samba of the Rio hills as something dignified and native. It quickly passed beyond his own record to become one of the most sung songs in the Brazilian repertoire.","The song works by understatement. The words lean on a single conceit, flowers that cannot speak, and the melody follows suit, moving mostly by small steps so that the few wider intervals feel like a catch in the throat. Listen to the bass line of the seven-string guitar: it walks downward under the voice in long chains, a choro inheritance, and it carries much of the sadness the singer refuses to show openly.","Critics have long placed it among the finest Brazilian songs of the century, and it has been recorded by dozens of singers, from established MPB stars to young samba groups and jazz instrumentalists. Some purists prefer slower, barer versions without the strings, arguing that the orchestral touches soften Cartola's austerity; others hear the arrangement as exactly the frame the melody needed. There is no real argument about the song itself.","Hear Cartola's own recording first, ideally late at night and at low volume. Follow the guitar's lowest line rather than the voice the second time through. Then hear a jazz instrumental version to discover how much of the song's beauty is pure melody. Compare João Gilberto's hushed bossa nova singing and notice how much of that style is already here."],"cartola-alvorada":["A samba that greets the dawn rising over the hill of Mangueira, written by Cartola with his old friend Carlos Cachaça and the younger poet Hermínio Bello de Carvalho. On Cartola's 1974 recording the groove is light and swaying, with cavaquinho chords ticking at the top, a flute weaving around the voice and soft hand percussion. The melody climbs gradually as if following the light up the hillside, and a short refrain returns between verses to anchor it.","Carlos Cachaça had been Cartola's partner since the founding of the Mangueira samba school in the late 1920s, and Hermínio Bello de Carvalho belonged to the generation of middle-class enthusiasts who sought out the old sambistas in the 1960s. The song brings those two worlds together. Cartola's own version opened a listening public's ears to him in 1974, when the small label Discos Marcus Pereira was documenting older Brazilian musicians whom the big companies had ignored.","Mangueira is both a hill and a community here, and the song is as much about belonging as about sunrise: the dawn is the moment when the night of music on the hill ends and ordinary life resumes. Listen for how the refrain lands on a held, open note that leaves the harmony hanging slightly, like light not yet fully up. The three-author credit is typical of samba, where songs passed between friends before anyone wrote them down.","Alvorada became one of Cartola's best-loved songs and a staple of samba circles, where it is sung by everyone present rather than performed for them. Writers on samba often cite it as proof that the music of the hills could carry lyric poetry of real delicacy, against an older prejudice that saw it as street entertainment. Its reputation has only grown since Cartola's death in 1980.","Listen first for the cavaquinho, the small four-string guitar whose bright chords give samba its shimmer, and then for the flute's answering lines. Sing the refrain on the second hearing; that is how the song lives. Afterwards play As rosas não falam to hear Cartola in a darker mood, and Pixinguinha's Carinhoso for the choro tradition behind the flute."],"cartola-verde-que-te-quero-rosa":["Cartola's third album, made for a major label after two acclaimed independent records. The sound is a little fuller, with strings and woodwinds sometimes framing the small samba group, but his voice remains the centre: soft, conversational and unhurried. The songs range from love songs of quiet tenderness, such as Nós dois, to reflections on freedom and pride such as Autonomia. Like its predecessors, it is a short record of reflective samba-canção rather than carnival music.","By 1977 Cartola, nearly seventy, had become an unlikely star of Brazilian music, interviewed, celebrated and recorded by RCA. He and his wife Dona Zica had finally moved into a modest house of their own. The album's title salutes the colours of his samba school, Estação Primeira de Mangueira, whose banners are green and pink, and his late fame was bound up with the school's renewed prestige in Rio's cultural life.","The title is a pun with a literary pedigree: it bends a famous line by the Spanish poet Federico García Lorca, which repeats the word green, into a declaration of love for Mangueira's green and pink. Listen beneath the strings to the guitars and cavaquinho; the rhythm section is the same kind of choro-trained group as on the earlier records, and it keeps the arrangements anchored in the hills rather than the studio.","Some critics found the major-label production slightly glossier than the two Marcus Pereira albums, and those earlier records remain the usual starting point. But the songwriting was judged as strong as ever, and the album confirmed that Cartola's late flowering was not a one-off. It is now valued as part of a remarkable sequence of four albums made in his last years.","Come to this after the 1974 and 1976 albums, as a continuation of the same story. Notice how the strings are used sparingly, mostly to cushion phrase endings. Listen to Nós dois for Cartola at his most tender. Then try Marisa Monte's later recordings of old Mangueira samba to hear how this music passed to a new generation."],"luiz-gonzaga-juazeiro":["A baião in which a man asks a juazeiro tree, a hardy evergreen of the northeastern backlands, whether it has seen the woman he lost beneath its shade. Luiz Gonzaga sings and plays accordion over the classic trio sound he made standard: the zabumba bass drum thumping a syncopated pattern, the triangle ringing steadily on top. The melody is short and plaintive, built from the modal scales of northeastern folk song, and the accordion answers each vocal line.","Gonzaga and the lawyer-poet Humberto Teixeira wrote a run of songs in the late 1940s that turned the music of the Brazilian northeast into a national craze, broadcast from Rio de Janeiro to millions of listeners, many of them migrants from the drought-stricken sertão. Juazeiro came right after the success of Asa branca and Baião, and it showed that the new style could carry lyric tenderness as well as dance energy.","The tree matters: the juazeiro stays green through droughts that kill almost everything else, so addressing it is asking the one witness that endures. Listen to the accordion's bellows, which Gonzaga shakes to make the notes pulse, imitating the sustained wail of the northeastern fiddle and fife. The scale sounds oddly bright and sad at once, because it raises one note and lowers another compared with an ordinary major scale.","The song has been a standard of northeastern music ever since, recorded by later generations from Gilberto Gil and Caetano Veloso's circle to forró bands. Scholars of Brazilian popular music cite the Gonzaga and Teixeira partnership as the moment the sertão gained a voice in national media. Debate concerns mostly how far Gonzaga stylised rural music for the radio; few dispute that he gave it lasting form.","Listen first to the three instruments separately: the deep zabumba, the clicking triangle, the accordion. Then hear how they lock together into the baião rhythm. Compare it with Asa branca, its more famous sibling, and then with Gilberto Gil's 1970s records, which borrowed this rhythm for MPB. Any good compilation of Gonzaga's early RCA recordings will serve."],"luiz-gonzaga-assum-preto":["A slow toada about a black songbird that has been blinded so that it will sing more sweetly, whose fate the singer compares with his own after losing his love. Gonzaga sings it with unusual restraint, the accordion sustaining long notes behind him and the percussion kept to a gentle, almost funereal tread. The melody falls in sighing phrases and the whole performance has the bare, austere quality of a backlands lament rather than the bounce of a dance baião.","Written with Humberto Teixeira at the height of their partnership, Assum preto drew on the real practice, once common in the northeastern countryside, of treating captive songbirds harshly to make them sing. Gonzaga was by then a national radio star, dressed in the leather hat and clothes of the backlands cowboy, and he was presenting the northeast to city audiences in all its harshness as well as its festivity.","The song's force is that it never states its comparison outright until the end; it describes the bird plainly and lets the listener make the connection. Listen to the accordion's long, held chords, which behave more like a church harmonium than a dance instrument. Gonzaga's voice drops its usual nasal brightness and sits lower, so the song sounds less performed than confided.","Assum preto is widely regarded as one of the most moving songs in the Brazilian repertoire, and it has been recorded by singers across genres, including Caetano Veloso and Gal Costa. Critics often single it out to show that the Gonzaga and Teixeira catalogue was not only dance music but contained real poetry of suffering. It is regularly taught as an example of the northeastern toada.","Hear it straight after Baião or Xote das meninas to feel the contrast in mood. Notice how little the percussion does, and how that emptiness becomes part of the sadness. Listen for the final line's shift in meaning. Then try the Villa-Lobos Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5, another northeastern-tinged Brazilian song of longing in a very different medium."],"luiz-gonzaga-xote-das-meninas":["A xote, the northeastern descendant of the European schottische, about a girl who has stopped playing with dolls and whose worried father mistakes her first love for an illness. Gonzaga's accordion sets a relaxed, swinging two-step, slower and more swaying than the baião, with the zabumba and triangle rolling underneath. The tune is playful and catchy, with a call-and-response feel between voice and accordion and a chorus built for dancing couples.","In the early 1950s Gonzaga moved from Humberto Teixeira to a new partner, Zé Dantas, a doctor from Pernambuco who knew rural life and humour intimately. Together they wrote some of his most enduring hits, and Xote das meninas became a fixture of the June festivals of São João, the great midwinter celebrations of the northeast where Gonzaga's music is still the main soundtrack.","The xote came to Brazil as a nineteenth-century ballroom dance from Europe, the schottische, and was absorbed into rural dance halls until it sounded entirely local. Listen for that ancestry in the even, strolling pulse. The song's image of the cactus flowering, a sign of rain in the sertão, ties the girl's coming of age to the seasons: Zé Dantas's lyrics often mix comedy with exact rural detail.","The song is among the most performed in the forró repertoire and has been covered countless times, by Marisa Monte among many others. Critics credit the Dantas partnership with giving Gonzaga's work wit and a storyteller's eye. Its place in festival culture means it is less often discussed as art than simply danced to, which is arguably the best tribute it could receive.","Imagine a dance floor at a June festival: listen for how the rhythm invites close, swaying couple dancing rather than display. Compare the gentler pulse of the xote with the sharper syncopation of Baião. Follow the accordion's fills between lines, which are as much a part of the tune as the singing. Then try a live forró band to hear the tradition today."],"sarah-vaughan-lover-man":["A ballad of longing for an absent lover, sung by a twenty-one-year-old Sarah Vaughan with Dizzy Gillespie's quintet, Charlie Parker on alto saxophone. The tempo is slow, the band plays a hushed introduction, and Vaughan enters with a voice of startling range and control, sliding into notes and bending them with a horn player's freedom. Gillespie and Parker add brief, elaborate obbligatos and solo passages that sound like a new musical language set beside an old popular song.","The song, by Jimmy Davis, Roger Ramirez and James Sherman, had been a hit for Billie Holiday in 1944. Vaughan had sung with Earl Hines's and Billy Eckstine's big bands, where she met Gillespie and Parker, the two young architects of bebop. This 1945 recording, made for the small Guild label at the same period as their landmark small-group sides, captured her among the revolutionaries just as their style was emerging into public view.","Listen to how Vaughan treats the harmony: she leans on the extended, altered notes in the chords, the same notes Parker and Gillespie were exploring in their solos, so that the voice is part of the bebop experiment rather than a guest. Her vibrato is held back until the ends of long phrases. The pianist on the date is usually given as Al Haig, whose delicate chords leave room for the voice.","Musicians recognised at once that Vaughan was a singer who thought like an instrumentalist, and the record is often cited as the first great bebop vocal. Comparisons with Holiday's version remain instructive: Holiday is intimate and wounded, Vaughan more expansive and harmonically adventurous. The early recording quality is thin, but historians treat the session as a founding document of modern jazz singing.","Play Billie Holiday's version first, then this one, and notice how differently each singer handles the same melody. Listen for Parker's alto entering behind the voice; it is like overhearing a conversation in a new dialect. Then hear Vaughan's later recordings with Clifford Brown to follow how her voice deepened and grew richer over the next decade."],"sarah-vaughan-swingin-easy":["Sarah Vaughan with only a piano trio, singing standards at relaxed and medium tempos. Without horns or strings her voice is fully exposed, moving from a deep, almost baritone bottom to a light, bright top, and the trio, with Roy Haynes on drums, swings lightly behind her. The highlight for many listeners is Shulie a Bop, a wordless scat number in which she improvises like a bebop horn player, but the ballads show equal command at slow speeds.","Vaughan was signed to Mercury in the 1950s on an unusual arrangement: commercial pop material with orchestras on the main label, and jazz dates with small groups on its EmArcy subsidiary. Swingin' Easy gathers trio sessions from 1954 and 1957 made for the jazz side. It shows the singer musicians most admired, rather than the hitmaker radio audiences knew, at the point when her voice had reached full maturity.","Listen to the drumming: Roy Haynes, one of the great bebop drummers, plays with brushes and small accents that answer Vaughan's phrases almost word for word. On the scat number notice that her improvised lines follow the chord changes as closely as an instrumental solo would. Her famous control lets her drop an octave mid-phrase without any change in tone, a trick she uses for wit rather than display.","Swingin' Easy is often recommended alongside the Clifford Brown album as the best introduction to Vaughan the jazz singer. Critics have praised its intimacy against the heavier orchestral settings of her pop records, and Shulie a Bop is regularly cited in discussions of scat singing. Some prefer one session over the other, but the album as a whole is consistently ranked among her finest small-group work.","Start with Shulie a Bop for the thrill of the improvising, then move to a slow ballad to hear the same voice in stillness. Follow the drummer's brushes as a second voice. Compare Ella Fitzgerald's scatting, which is joyful and rhythmic, with Vaughan's, which is more harmonically daring. A good mono remaster suits these sessions best."],"sarah-vaughan-sassy-swings-the-tivoli":["A live recording made at the Tivoli Gardens in Copenhagen, with Vaughan fronting her working trio led by pianist Kirk Stuart. In concert she is looser and funnier than in the studio: she stretches ballads into long rubato meditations, takes up-tempo tunes at a gallop and talks to the audience between songs. Her voice in 1963 is at its fullest, with a rich low register and a vibrato she can widen or narrow at will.","European audiences in the early 1960s welcomed American jazz musicians as serious artists, and Copenhagen in particular had a lively jazz scene and a number of expatriate Americans. Vaughan had become an international concert star after years of club and recording work. Mercury recorded the Tivoli shows to capture a quality her studio albums, often weighed down by orchestral arrangements, rarely showed: the spontaneity of a working trio that knew her every move.","Listen to how the trio follows her rather than setting a fixed tempo: on the ballads Stuart's piano waits, breathes and resumes in step with her, a sign of musicians who had played together night after night. Vaughan likes to finish a song with a long, swooping cadenza, sliding down through her whole range; it is a bit of operatic showmanship that the audience clearly waits for.","The album was later reissued in expanded form and is widely considered one of the best live vocal jazz recordings, often named alongside Ella Fitzgerald's Berlin concert. Critics note that the relaxed setting brings out her humour, which studio sessions rarely did. Some find the long ballads indulgent, but most hear in them the freedom of a singer at the top of her powers.","Listen to it as a concert, in order, letting the audience's reactions guide you. Notice the difference between the tight up-tempo numbers and the free-floating ballads. Compare it with Ella in Berlin to hear two great singers working live in the same years. Seek out the expanded reissue for the fuller picture of the evenings."],"jimmy-smith-bashin":["Jimmy Smith's Hammond organ set against a full big band arranged and conducted by Oliver Nelson on one side, and with his small group on the other. The opener, Walk on the Wild Side, Elmer Bernstein's theme from the film of that name, became his biggest hit: a slinky, menacing groove in which the brass blasts short chords and the organ answers with bluesy runs and long, shrieking held notes. The trio side is looser, closer to his Blue Note records.","Smith had made his name on Blue Note in the late 1950s with organ trio and jam session albums. In 1962 he moved to Verve, where the producer Creed Taylor paired him with big bands and arrangers to reach a wider audience. Walk on the Wild Side, released the same year as the Hollywood film, put a jazz organist onto popular radio and turned soul jazz into a commercial force.","Listen to the way Nelson's arrangement treats the organ as one section of the band, trading phrases with the trumpets and saxophones rather than sitting on top. Smith uses the Hammond's drawbars and its rotating Leslie speaker to change tone colour mid-phrase, moving from a mellow flute sound to a harsh, cutting one. The famous long held chords are made by simply leaving his hands down while the speaker swirls.","Jazz critics at the time were divided about the Verve big-band records, some hearing them as commercial compromise after the raw Blue Note sessions. Listeners and later critics came to value them for their drama and for Nelson's arranging. Bashin' is now seen as the start of Smith's most popular period and a model for organ-and-orchestra records by many who followed.","Start with Walk on the Wild Side and turn it up; this is music about weight and swagger. Then play the trio side to hear the same player in a small room. Compare it with The Sermon! from his Blue Note years for the contrast. Then try Oliver Nelson's own The Blues and the Abstract Truth for the arranger's style."],"jose-alfredo-jimenez-camino-de-guanajuato":["A ranchera about the road through the state of Guanajuato to José Alfredo Jiménez's birthplace, Dolores Hidalgo, sung by a man who has lost faith that life is worth much. The mariachi sets a strong, swinging pulse, the violins play the sweeping introduction, and the trumpets sound bright fanfares between verses. The melody is broad and open, with the long held notes at phrase endings that rancheras demand, and it moves through a list of towns along the way.","Jiménez, who could not read music or play an instrument and composed by singing his melodies, rose in the early 1950s to become the most prolific and beloved writer of Mexican ranchera songs. His work coincided with the golden age of Mexican cinema and radio, when mariachi music became the sound of national identity. This song drew on his own roots in Guanajuato, the state at the heart of Mexico's independence history.","The towns named in the song are real stops on the road, so the verses work almost like a map; Mexican listeners can trace the journey. Notice the contrast between the bitter, fatalistic refrain and the bright, festive major-key music: the sorrow is sung, not wept, which is the ranchera manner. The grito, the whooping cry from the musicians, comes exactly where the emotion peaks.","The song is regarded as one of Jiménez's most important works and one of the anthems of Mexican regional pride. Its fatalistic refrain is endlessly quoted in Mexico as a summary of a certain stoic attitude to life. Critics have discussed how Jiménez gave voice to the provincial and working-class Mexican experience, and this song is often the first example they cite.","Listen for the trumpet fanfares that open and divide the song, then for how the singer holds the last note of each verse. Count the towns as they pass. Compare Jiménez's own plain, unpolished delivery with the operatic versions by later singers such as Vicente Fernández. Then hear El Rey, his other great anthem of pride."],"jose-alfredo-jimenez-si-nos-dejan":["A tender love song in which a man dreams of going away with his beloved to a place of their own, if only the world will let them. Unlike the defiant cantina rancheras, this is a slow bolero-ranchera: the mariachi plays softly, the violins sustain sweet harmonies in thirds and the trumpets are muted or held back. The melody is gentle and lyrical, rising to a warm climax in the refrain that has made it a favourite at weddings.","By the early 1960s José Alfredo Jiménez had written hundreds of songs and was a major figure in Mexican music, his work sung by the great film stars and mariachi singers of the day. The bolero, the romantic song form from Cuba and Mexico, had merged with the ranchera tradition, and Si nos dejan sits exactly at that meeting point, sentimental rather than tough.","The title phrase, a simple conditional, carries the song's quiet sadness: the dream is always qualified by the possibility that others will not allow it. Listen for how the violins answer the voice at the end of each phrase, completing the line like a second singer. The melody avoids leaps almost entirely, so that it can be sung softly by anyone, which is why it has spread so widely.","The song has become one of the most recorded in the Mexican repertoire, sung by mariachi stars and later by pop singers, including Luis Miguel. Critics often cite it as proof of Jiménez's range: the same writer who gave Mexico its songs of drinking and defiance could also write delicate romance. It is still a staple of serenades and celebrations across Mexico and the Mexican diaspora.","Listen to it as a serenade, imagining it sung under a window. Notice how quiet the trumpets are compared with El Rey. Hear Jiménez's own version for its directness, then one of the lush later recordings to hear how the song takes orchestration. Compare it with Chavela Vargas's spare style for yet another way into his songs."],"jose-alfredo-jimenez-el-ultimo-trago":["A farewell song in which a man asks a departing lover to share one last drink with him before she goes. In mariachi versions the tempo is slow and swaying, the violins weep in long phrases, and the singer stretches the final words of each verse. Stripped to voice and guitar, as later performers often did, the song becomes stark and dramatic. Its melody is simple and singable, built for a whole cantina to join in at the end.","The cantina song, about love lost and drowned in drink, was a central ranchera theme, and Jiménez wrote many of its classics. His life and music were tied to the bars and night culture of Mexico City, and his songs were performed in cantinas long before they reached the concert hall. This one gained new life decades later through the Costa Rican-born singer Chavela Vargas, who made it one of her signature songs.","Behind the drinking is a song about dignity: the singer does not plead for his lover to stay, only for one more moment of shared time. Listen for the pause before the final line, where the singer holds back as if waiting for an answer that never comes. Chavela Vargas's slow, almost spoken versions reveal how much the melody depends on silence rather than volume.","El último trago is one of the essential cantina songs and has been recorded by many ranchera and bolero singers. Critics see Vargas's late interpretations as having transformed it from a barroom favourite into a tragic art song, a reading that influenced how younger audiences understood Jiménez. It is also cited in discussions of how his songs speak to loss with a kind of proud acceptance.","Hear a mariachi version and a Chavela Vargas version back to back; the contrast is a lesson in how much interpretation matters. Listen for the held notes and the silences around them. Then play Cucurrucucú paloma or La llorona to explore the broader tradition of Mexican songs of grief and longing."],"lefty-frizzell-i-love-you-a-thousand-ways":["A slow, aching love song in which a man promises his wife his devotion after a time apart. Lefty Frizzell sings it with the curling, legato style that became his trademark, sliding each word across several notes. The honky-tonk band is small and quiet: a steel guitar sighs between lines, the fiddle adds sweetening and a soft rhythm section keeps a gentle pulse. The melody is simple, but his phrasing makes every line sound like a confession.","Frizzell wrote the song in the late 1940s while serving a jail term in New Mexico, as a letter to his young wife Alice. It became one of the two sides of his first Columbia single in 1950, recorded at Jim Beck's studio in Dallas, with If You've Got the Money I've Got the Time on the other side. Both sides became hits and launched him as one of the brightest new stars in country music.","Listen to how Frizzell bends the vowels at the end of lines, letting them waver and fall, a device later called the Frizzell slur. It is less a vocal trick than a way of making the voice sound vulnerable. Notice too the restraint of the steel guitar, which plays sparse answering phrases rather than filling space; the arrangement is built around the voice, unusual for dance-hall country of the time.","Country historians regard this single as one of the most remarkable debuts in the music's history, with two very different hit sides. The ballad is often cited as the purest example of Frizzell's influence on later singers such as Merle Haggard, George Jones and Randy Travis. Its origin in a jail cell has become part of country legend, which happens in this case to be true.","Play it right after If You've Got the Money to hear the same voice in two moods. Concentrate on single words and how far he stretches them. Then listen to a Merle Haggard ballad and hear Frizzell's phrasing transplanted almost exactly. A good compilation of his early Columbia singles is the best way to have it."],"lefty-frizzell-always-late":["A medium-tempo honky-tonk song in which a man gently complains that his sweetheart's affection always comes too late. Frizzell draws out the word in the title across a long, falling line, and the band rolls along behind him: steel guitar and fiddle trading fills, a loping bass, a steady shuffle. It is a lighter song than his ballads, but the singing gives it the same wistful, slightly melancholy colour that makes his records instantly recognisable.","Written with his guitarist Blackie Crawford, the song came at the height of Frizzell's early fame. For a stretch of 1951 he had several records in the country top ten at the same time, a feat few artists have matched. He was touring constantly and rivalled Hank Williams as the biggest name in honky-tonk, the jukebox and dance-hall country music of postwar Texas and the Southwest.","Listen to the title line: Frizzell stretches the opening word over several notes, rolling it down and up so that the music itself seems to be late. The trick is the song. Notice how the band leaves a little space before each chorus so that his phrasing can breathe; the instrumental breaks are short and functional, keeping attention on the voice.","The song was one of his biggest hits and remains a country standard. Dwight Yoakam's recording in the late 1980s brought it to a new generation, and Merle Haggard, Willie Nelson and others have cited Frizzell's phrasing here as formative. Critics treat it as a textbook case of how a singer's style can carry a song more than its words or melody.","Listen for the first word of the title and how long he takes over it; then notice the same trick recurring. Compare Frizzell's version with Dwight Yoakam's to hear how a later Bakersfield-style singer handles the same phrasing. Then play a George Jones ballad to hear how the style passed down."],"lefty-frizzell-saginaw-michigan":["A story song about a poor young man from Saginaw who wins the hand of a wealthy man's daughter by outwitting her greedy father with a tall tale of gold. The arrangement is smoother than Frizzell's early honky-tonk, with piano, gentle drums and a backing chorus in the Nashville manner, and the melody unfolds like a ballad. Frizzell's voice is deeper and rougher than in 1950, but the curling, sliding phrasing remains.","Written by Bill Anderson and Don Wayne, the song was a late comeback for Frizzell, whose career had faded during the late 1950s as rock and roll and the Nashville Sound changed country music. Released in 1964, it returned him to the top of the country charts and became one of his best-remembered records, though he never again matched that success before his death in 1975.","Listen to the narrative structure: the song is a complete short story, with setup, conflict and twist, told in a few verses. Frizzell's delivery is unusually understated, almost conversational, so the punchline lands with wry humour. Notice the smooth backing vocals, a sign of the Nashville Sound, contrasting with the older honky-tonk grain in his voice.","The song is often cited as one of the finest country story songs of the 1960s and as proof that Frizzell could adapt to the smoother Nashville style without losing his identity. Critics sometimes regret that it is better known than his early, more influential recordings, but many regard his singing here as his most mature.","Follow the story from start to finish, as you would a short film. Then listen to his 1950 singles to hear how his voice aged. Compare it with George Jones's The Grand Tour or Johnny Cash's story songs for the narrative tradition in country music."],"nina-simone-i-put-a-spell-on-you":["An album of ballads, torch songs and dramatic numbers, with Simone backed by an orchestra arranged by Hal Mooney. It includes her versions of Screamin' Jay Hawkins's title song, Jacques Brel's Ne me quitte pas sung in French, and Feeling Good, from the Anthony Newley and Leslie Bricusse musical The Roar of the Greasepaint. The sound is lush but sharp, with brass punctuation and strings framing her husky, commanding voice.","In 1964 Simone moved to the Philips label, and her records there mixed protest songs with standards, show tunes and international songs. The mid-1960s were a time of rising civil-rights activism, and she had become one of its fiercest voices. Yet this album shows another side: her love of European art song, musical theatre and blues, all filtered through her classical piano training.","Feeling Good opens with her voice alone, unaccompanied, before the big band explodes in; that moment of silence is what makes the song famous. In Ne me quitte pas, listen for her careful French and how she slows the tempo, turning a desperate plea into a dignified lament. Simone recasts the title track, originally a raucous novelty, as a slow, smouldering torch song.","The album was well received at the time and has grown in reputation, partly because Feeling Good became one of her most famous recordings through later use in films, advertisements and covers. Critics praise her ability to make songs from very different worlds sound like personal statements. Some find the orchestral arrangements dated, but most hear them as theatrical frames she controls.","Start with Feeling Good and listen to the unaccompanied opening. Then play Ne me quitte pas and compare it with Brel's own version. Notice how she slows songs down to make them heavier. Follow with Pastel Blues to hear her with a small band in a harder, bluesier mood."],"kris-kristofferson-me-and-bobby-mcgee":["A road song about two drifters hitching across America together, and the man's memory of the woman who later left him. In Kristofferson's own recording the arrangement is simple: strummed acoustic guitar, a light rhythm section and harmonica. His voice is rough and conversational, and the song moves from a relaxed opening into a rousing, sing-along refrain before slowing again into the memory of loss. The melody is plain, like a folk song.","Kristofferson wrote the song in 1969 from a title suggested by the producer Fred Foster, and it was first recorded by Roger Miller. He included his own version on his debut album in 1970. Janis Joplin's version, recorded shortly before her death in 1970 and released posthumously, became an enormous hit and made Kristofferson's name known far beyond Nashville.","The song's title was originally a woman's name, Bobbie McKee, a secretary in Foster's building; Kristofferson made the character a woman named Bobby. The famous refrain about freedom, which many hear as celebratory, is in fact bittersweet: it equates freedom with having nothing left to lose. Listen for how the music grows cheerful just as the lyrics turn sad.","The song is regarded as one of the great American songs of its era, and its many recordings, by Joplin, Gordon Lightfoot, the Grateful Dead and others, testify to its range. Critics often cite it as the moment Nashville songwriting entered the rock and folk mainstream. Debate centres on whether Joplin's raw version or Kristofferson's understated original best reveals its meaning.","Listen to Kristofferson's version first for the story, then to Janis Joplin's on Pearl for the release. Notice how both singers slow down at the end. Then explore Kristofferson's debut album for other songs written in the same period, which country singers turned into hits."],"kris-kristofferson-the-silver-tongued-devil-and-i":["Kristofferson's second album, a set of literate country-folk songs sung in his rough, half-spoken voice. It includes Loving Her Was Easier (Than Anything I'll Ever Do Again), a tender love song, and The Pilgrim, Chapter 33, a portrait of a restless, contradictory artist. The arrangements are fuller than on his debut, with piano, organ and backing singers, but remain spare enough to keep the lyrics at the front.","By 1971 Kristofferson, a former Rhodes Scholar and Army helicopter pilot, had become one of the most sought-after songwriters in Nashville, and the success of Joplin's Me and Bobby McGee gave him a wider audience. The album, produced by Fred Foster for Monument, was his breakthrough as a performer, selling far better than his debut and establishing him as a singer in his own right.","The title song is a self-portrait: the devil is the singer's own charm and talent for seduction, which he blames for his misbehaviour. The Pilgrim, Chapter 33 names several of Kristofferson's friends in its dedication, among them fellow songwriters and performers, and its character is a composite of them and of himself. Listen for how his weak voice becomes an asset, sounding honest rather than polished.","Critics hailed the album as proof that Kristofferson was more than a songwriter for others, and it remains one of his best-regarded records. Some found his singing limited, but most accepted that the voice suited the songs. It is now seen as a key album of the early 1970s singer-songwriter movement in country music.","Start with Loving Her Was Easier for the tenderness, then The Pilgrim for the self-portrait. Listen to the words first and the music second. Compare it with John Prine's debut or Townes Van Zandt's albums, which share its literary approach. Then hear later artists such as Sturgill Simpson, who carry the tradition on."],"kris-kristofferson-why-me":["A gospel song in which a man asks God what he has done to deserve divine grace. The arrangement is slow and stately, with piano, gentle drums and harmony vocals in the style of a country hymn. Kristofferson's voice, rough and humble, carries the simple melody without ornament. It is the most direct song he ever wrote, without the irony or wordplay of his other work, and it builds quietly to a heartfelt plea in the chorus.","Kristofferson wrote the song after a religious experience at a Nashville church in the early 1970s, during a period of heavy drinking and personal turmoil. It appeared on his album Jesus Was a Capricorn and became his biggest hit as a performer, released as a single in 1973. Its success surprised many who knew him as a hard-living outlaw writer.","Listen to the backing harmonies, which give the song the feel of a congregation joining in; it is closer to a hymn than to any of his other records. The melody uses only a handful of notes, so that anyone can sing it. The song's humility is its strength: there is no attempt at argument or theology, only gratitude and doubt.","The song was a major hit and has been sung ever since by gospel and country performers, in churches as well as concert halls. Critics have often noted the contrast between its simplicity and the complex lyrics of Kristofferson's other work. For many fans it is his most personal song; for others it shows the gospel roots that run under all country music.","Listen to it quietly, perhaps on a Sunday morning, and notice how little the arrangement does. Then play Sunday Mornin' Comin' Down for the other side of Kristofferson's Sunday. Compare it with Hank Williams's I Saw the Light or Mahalia Jackson for the gospel tradition behind it."],"kris-kristofferson-this-old-road":["A late-career album of spare, reflective songs, produced by Don Was. Kristofferson, then seventy, sings and plays acoustic guitar and harmonica with minimal accompaniment, his voice deeper and more weathered than ever. The songs look back on a long life with humour, regret and anger at the state of the world, from the title song's meditation on age to tender songs of family love. The sound is intimate, like a private performance.","After decades as a songwriter, actor and member of the Highwaymen with Johnny Cash, Willie Nelson and Waylon Jennings, Kristofferson had recorded only occasionally. This album, his first of new songs in many years, followed the model of Rick Rubin's American Recordings with Johnny Cash: stripped-down production putting an older artist's voice and words in the foreground. It was released by the independent label New West.","Listen for the harmonica, which Kristofferson plays roughly, as a folk singer would rather than a virtuoso. A few songs turn outward to the state of the world, sitting beside private songs of love and mortality, and the juxtaposition feels deliberate: the public and the private travel the same old road. His voice cracks on some notes, and the producer left those cracks in.","The album was warmly received, with critics praising its honesty and the quality of the songwriting, and many hailed it as his best work in decades. Some found his singing frail, but most accepted that frailty as the point. It led to further late albums in the same vein and renewed attention to his place as a writer.","Listen in one sitting, late in the day. Notice the long silences between phrases. Compare it with Johnny Cash's American Recordings and Leonard Cohen's You Want It Darker, other late albums by old songwriters facing age. Then go back to his 1970 debut and hear the same voice when young."],"jorge-ben-jorge-ben-1969":["Jorge Ben's self-titled album of 1969, a joyful mix of samba, soul, rock and funk built around his choppy, percussive acoustic guitar. It includes País tropical, a cheerful celebration of Brazil, and Charles, Anjo 45, about a Robin Hood-like figure from the favelas. Horns and strings add colour, and his relaxed, almost conversational voice glides over grooves that are danceable but never pushy. The sound announced what would later be called samba-rock.","Jorge Ben had broken through in 1963 with Mas que nada but drifted between styles in the following years. By 1969 Brazil was under a harsh military dictatorship, and the Tropicália movement of Caetano Veloso and Gilberto Gil had challenged musical boundaries. Ben, influenced by American soul and rock, found his own blend, and this album, with its string of hits, restored his place at the centre of Brazilian popular music.","Listen to Ben's guitar: he plays with a strumming hand that hits the strings like a percussion instrument, mixing samba rhythm with the backbeat of soul. That technique is the foundation of his whole sound. País tropical was also a hit that year for Wilson Simonal, and comparing versions shows how flexible Ben's songs are.","The album is widely regarded as one of Ben's finest and one of the key Brazilian records of the late 1960s. Critics praise its synthesis of Brazilian and Afro-American music, which later influenced samba-rock and Brazilian funk. It is often cited alongside A Tábua de Esmeralda as his best work.","Start with País tropical and follow the guitar rather than the voice. Notice how the drums and percussion layer samba patterns over a soul groove. Compare it with Tim Maia's first album, released the next year, for another Brazilian take on soul. Then go on to A Tábua de Esmeralda for Ben's more mystical side."],"jorge-ben-forca-bruta":["A warm, melancholic album with Jorge Ben accompanied by the percussion group Trio Mocotó, whose cuíca, pandeiro and hand drums give the music a light, swinging pulse. His acoustic guitar is again the engine, playing choppy rhythms over which he sings in a relaxed, intimate voice. The title song and much of the record are slower and more pensive than his previous album, though the grooves still invite dancing.","Released in 1970, a year after his self-titled album, Força Bruta was recorded with Trio Mocotó, who had become his regular backing group in the clubs of São Paulo. The dictatorship's censorship was at its height, and many Brazilian musicians were in exile. Ben stayed, and his music, mostly about love and everyday life rather than politics, continued to be popular.","Listen to the cuíca, the friction drum that produces a squeaking, almost vocal sound; Trio Mocotó use it as a second voice to Ben's singing. The album's mood shifts from the exuberance of 1969 toward introspection, foreshadowing the more mystical A Tábua de Esmeralda. Notice the subtle string arrangements, which add a soft melancholy without overwhelming the groove.","The album has long been admired by Brazilian critics and musicians, and its reputation abroad grew with reissues. It is often seen as a transitional record between Ben's samba-soul phase and his later mystical work. Some prefer its intimacy to the brightness of the 1969 album; others find it less focused.","Listen for the interplay between Ben's guitar and the percussion of Trio Mocotó. Notice the cuíca's voice-like squeaks. Compare it with the 1969 album to hear the change in mood, then go on to A Tábua de Esmeralda. For another Brazilian take on intimacy, try João Gilberto's records."],"andrae-crouch-my-tribute":["A slow gospel ballad of thanksgiving, sung by Andraé Crouch with his group the Disciples. It opens almost privately, a single voice over gentle piano chords, and builds in long steps until the full group enters on the refrain and the whole band swells behind them. The song is about giving all credit for a life to God. Its shape, from quiet confession to a broad, ringing chorus, is the gesture everyone recognises, and the reason choirs adopted it so quickly.","Crouch wrote the song as a young Los Angeles minister's son who had already shown, with The Blood Will Never Lose Its Power, that he could write hymns congregations would sing as if they were old. In the early 1970s he was recording for Light Records, a label aimed at the new Jesus Music audience, and he was deliberately bridging Black church gospel, white evangelical congregations and the pop and soul radio of the day. My Tribute became one of the songs that made that bridge real.","Listen for how little happens harmonically in the first verse: Crouch holds back, letting the melody sit in a narrow range so that the leap into the refrain feels like a door opening. The title echoes the old Fanny Crosby hymn To God Be the Glory, though the tune is entirely new, a nod from a new gospel writer to the nineteenth-century hymnbook.","The song travelled further than almost anything else in Crouch's catalogue. It entered denominational hymnals across very different churches, Baptist, Methodist, Pentecostal and beyond, which is a rare kind of canonisation for a pop-era gospel song. Some traditionalists at first heard Crouch's soft-soul arrangements as too close to secular radio, but that argument faded long ago, and the song is now treated simply as a standard of congregational worship.","Hear the Disciples recording first, then a big church choir version, and notice how the song survives both intimacy and grandeur. Pay attention to the moment the group enters on the refrain. Afterwards compare it with Thomas Dorsey's Precious Lord, a song of the previous gospel generation built on a similar arc from private prayer to public declaration."],"henry-threadgill-air-lore":["The trio Air, Henry Threadgill on saxophones and flute, Fred Hopkins on bass and Steve McCall on drums, plays old music as if it had just been written. The album takes rags by Scott Joplin, among them The Ragtime Dance and Weeping Willow, and pieces associated with Jelly Roll Morton, including King Porter Stomp and Buddy Bolden's Blues. Three players share the lead equally: the bass carries melody as often as the horn, and the drums talk back rather than keep time.","Air came out of Chicago's AACM, the musicians' collective that had spent the 1960s and 1970s insisting that Black experimental music was a tradition with its own history, not an imitation of European avant-gardes. By 1979 the trio was working in New York's loft scene and recording for Arista's Novus label. Turning to Joplin and Morton was a pointed move at a moment when free jazz was often described as a break with the past rather than a continuation of it.","Joplin wrote his rags down note for note and asked that they never be played fast; Air keeps his notated melodies surprisingly intact while dissolving the steady left-hand beat underneath them. Listen to Hopkins in the rags: he often takes over the march-like bass line a pianist's left hand would play, then abandons it, so the ground keeps disappearing and reappearing beneath Threadgill's horn.","Critics welcomed the record as one of the clearest statements of what the AACM generation meant by tradition, and it is often named as Air's best album. Some listeners at the time heard it as a retreat from the trio's freer originals; later writers see the opposite, a demonstration that the avant-garde had always been an argument with ragtime, New Orleans and the blues rather than a rejection of them.","Play a piano roll or a straight recording of Joplin's Weeping Willow first, then Air's version, and listen for what has been kept and what has been taken away. Follow the bass rather than the saxophone on a second listen. After that, Jelly Roll Morton's own King Porter Stomp makes a revealing comparison with the trio's reading."],"henry-threadgill-easily-slip-into-another-world":["Threadgill's Sextett, which actually has seven players because he counted the two drummers as one voice, plays compositions that sound like small orchestras. Cello and bass give the bottom of the band a dark, bowed weight; trumpet and trombone set off against Threadgill's alto and flute; the two drum kits interlock in patterns that feel like a marching band heard through a wall. Pieces swing, then lurch into a funeral tread, then into something like a tango, often within a few minutes.","By the late 1980s Threadgill had left Air behind and was writing for larger, oddly balanced ensembles, and this was one of a run of Sextett albums made for RCA's Novus imprint. It appeared during the years when the jazz conversation in New York was dominated by a neo-traditionalist revival; Threadgill's answer was a band that knew the whole history of Black American music and refused to reproduce any single period of it.","Listen to how the cello is used: not as a soloist decorating the top, but as a second bass line, so the band has a doubled low end that pulls in two directions. Threadgill writes counter-melodies for every instrument, which means that when a soloist steps forward the written parts keep moving behind him; what sounds like collective improvisation is frequently composed.","Reviewers singled the album out as one of the strongest records by Threadgill's Sextett, and it is often cited as proof that a jazz composer could build a personal orchestral language with only seven players. Its reputation has risen with Threadgill's own; after his Pulitzer Prize in 2016 the Sextett recordings were reconsidered as the place where his mature writing first came fully into focus.","Do not try to follow a soloist; listen to the whole band as a moving texture, and notice the moments when everything drops out except the two drummers. Then go back and follow only the cello. For comparison, set it beside Mingus, whose bands also blur the line between written and improvised music, and then forward to Threadgill's Zooid recordings."],"henry-threadgill-too-much-sugar-for-a-dime":["Very Very Circus was Threadgill's most outlandish band: two electric guitars, two tubas, French horn, drums and his own alto and flute. On this album the tubas lock into thick, rolling bass patterns borrowed from brass bands, while the guitars scratch and chime over them like a funk rhythm section. Threadgill's tunes keep changing gait, so a passage that sounds like a parade turns into a slow, almost Middle Eastern melody and then into dense counterpoint for the whole ensemble.","The album came out on Axiom, the label run by producer Bill Laswell, which in the early 1990s was gathering avant-garde jazz, funk, dub and world music under one roof. Threadgill had moved to a major-label orbit after the Sextett years, and Very Very Circus showed what he could do with a rhythm section that came from the street band and the rock club rather than the jazz trio. The New York downtown scene of the time prized exactly this kind of collision.","The two tubas almost never play the same thing; one usually holds a repeating figure while the other moves around it, so the bass line is itself a duet. Listen for how often Threadgill hands the melody to the French horn, the most unexpected voice in the band, and how the guitars avoid chords for long stretches, behaving like extra percussion instead.","Reviewers were divided between those who found the band exhilarating and those who found its textures cluttered, but the album has aged into one of the most admired records of 1990s jazz. Musicians in particular point to it as a model of how to write for unusual instruments without novelty, and the tuba-and-guitar sound has been widely imitated by later composers working between jazz and brass-band music.","Start by listening only to the two tubas and treat everything else as decoration; the whole record makes sense once you hear its floor. Then switch to the guitars. A New Orleans brass band recording and a Funkadelic album make good companions, since Threadgill is drawing on both, and Air Lore shows where his long engagement with the past began."],"juan-gabriel-querida":["A long, slow-building ballad in which the singer begs an absent lover to come back. Juan Gabriel starts softly over a bed of strings and keyboards, almost talking the melody, then lets each refrain climb higher, until the final section is a full-voiced plea answered by the orchestra. The song is built from one simple, stepwise phrase repeated with growing intensity, and its pulse sits somewhere between a pop ballad and a slow bolero.","By 1984 Juan Gabriel was the most successful songwriter in Mexico, writing hits for Rocío Dúrcal, José José and many others while recording his own. Querida came out as a single and on the album Recuerdos, Vol. II. Mexican pop at the time was split between rancheras and the polished balada romántica; Juan Gabriel moved between them freely, and Querida is his balada side at its most extravagant.","Listen to how the arrangement withholds the drums for a long time, so that the song seems to start over each time the rhythm section adds another layer. Juan Gabriel's phrasing is deliberately uneven; he stretches some syllables and rushes others, a habit taken from ranchera singing that turns a pop ballad into something closer to a live performance.","The song became one of the biggest hits of his career and one of the most played Spanish-language songs of the decade across Latin America. Critics who dismissed Juan Gabriel as sentimental in the 1980s have largely reversed course; Querida is now usually discussed as evidence of his craft as a melodist, and its emotional excess as a deliberate theatrical style rather than a lapse of taste.","Listen once for the slow build, noticing each point where a new instrument joins. Then find a live performance, where he often stretches the ending into minutes of call and response with the audience. Compare it with Amor eterno, a song of grief rather than longing, to hear how differently he handles a ballad when the subject is final."],"juan-gabriel-hasta-que-te-conoci":["A dramatic ballad about discovering, through a failed love, a kind of pain the singer had never known. It opens with a hushed verse, then breaks into a soaring chorus over sweeping strings and a heavy, deliberate beat. Juan Gabriel sings near the top of his range for much of the song, and the arrangement keeps adding weight, brass and strings answering every phrase, so that the end feels like an outburst rather than a resolution.","The song appeared on the 1986 album Pensamientos, released while Juan Gabriel was in a long dispute with his record company over rights to his songs, a fight that kept him from recording new material for several years afterwards. That background gives the album a valedictory weight in his catalogue. The song was quickly taken up by other singers, and Marc Anthony's salsa version in the early 1990s carried it to a new audience across the Caribbean and the United States.","The verse and chorus are almost the same melody at different heights; what changes is the register and the orchestration, not the tune. Listen for the way he lands just behind the beat at the start of each chorus, a small delay that makes the arrival sound wrenched out of him. Later live versions slow the song down further and turn its ending into a long improvised cry.","It is generally ranked among his finest songs, and the fact that it works equally well as a mariachi number, a salsa and an orchestral pop ballad is often cited as proof of the strength of his writing. Marc Anthony's version prompted a long-running debate among fans about which reading is definitive; most now treat the two as complementary rather than rival.","Hear Juan Gabriel's original, then his performance at the Palacio de Bellas Artes, then Marc Anthony's salsa recording, and listen to how the same melody bends to three rhythmic worlds. Focus on the first chorus each time. Querida, from two years earlier, shows the same gift for slow escalation in a gentler key of feeling."],"juan-gabriel-se-me-olvido-otra-vez":["A short ranchera in which a man keeps returning to the place where he met a lover who has left, having forgotten once more that she is gone. Juan Gabriel sings it with a full mariachi: violins carry the sighing introduction, trumpets punctuate the ends of phrases, and the guitarrón gives the rolling bass. The melody is plain enough to sing after one hearing, and its slow, swaying lilt is the gesture that has made it a cantina standard.","Juan Gabriel, born in Michoacán and raised in poverty in Ciudad Juárez, began his career in the early 1970s as a pop balladeer, then turned to the ranchera, Mexico's national song form. He recorded this song with the Mariachi Vargas de Tecalitlán, the most famous mariachi in the country, and the pairing announced that a young pop writer could claim a place beside José Alfredo Jiménez in the tradition's heartland.","The song never states its sorrow directly; its sadness lies in the gap between the singer's habit and the fact he keeps forgetting, which is why it is gently ironic rather than tragic. Listen to how the violins and trumpets answer the voice between phrases, as if the band were remembering on his behalf.","It became one of the most recorded songs in Mexican music, sung by mariachis, ranchera stars and rock bands; Maná's acoustic version in 1999 introduced it to a younger audience across Latin America. Scholars of Mexican popular music cite it as the point where Juan Gabriel showed he could write in the old form with complete fluency, and it is routinely placed alongside the classic rancheras of the 1950s.","Start with the mariachi original and listen for the trumpets answering the voice. Then try Maná's version and hear how well the tune survives being moved to guitars and a rock rhythm section. José Alfredo Jiménez's El rey makes a good comparison: two ranchera writers, one the master and one the heir."],"stevie-ray-vaughan-pride-and-joy":["A Texas shuffle in which the singer celebrates his sweetheart. Stevie Ray Vaughan plays rhythm and lead at once: a bouncing, heavy-stringed pattern that hits bass notes and chord fragments in the same stroke, broken by bursts of stinging single-note lines. Tommy Shannon's bass and Chris Layton's drums lock into the triplet swing behind him. The opening guitar riff, fat and loping, is recognisable from its first two seconds.","The song was the lead single from Texas Flood, the debut album of Vaughan's trio Double Trouble, recorded quickly in Jackson Browne's Los Angeles studio after Vaughan had impressed audiences at the Montreux festival. In 1983 the blues was commercially marginal, and Epic released the record with modest expectations. Pride and Joy got onto rock radio and MTV, carrying an old-fashioned Texas shuffle to listeners raised on arena rock.","Vaughan tuned his guitar down a half-step and used unusually heavy strings, which is why the riff sounds thicker and darker than the same notes played in standard tuning. Listen to how much of the groove is in his right hand: the muted, percussive strokes between the notes do the work of a second rhythm guitar, so the trio sounds larger than three players.","The single became his signature song and the one most often played by aspiring guitarists. Purists at the time debated whether a young white Texan playing a Jimmy Reed and Albert King-derived style was revival or real blues; the consensus now is that Vaughan was a fluent native speaker of the Texas idiom, and that Pride and Joy reopened the mainstream to the blues.","Listen first to the guitar alone and try to hear the bass notes, chord stabs and muted strokes as separate layers. Then focus on the drums and hear how Layton keeps the shuffle loose. Follow it with Freddie King's Hide Away and Albert King's Born Under a Bad Sign, the two models Vaughan was openly building on."],"stevie-ray-vaughan-soul-to-soul":["The third studio album by Stevie Ray Vaughan and Double Trouble, and the first with Reese Wynans on organ and piano, which turns the trio's lean sound into something fuller and more soulful. It opens with Say What!, a wah-wah instrumental in open homage to Hendrix, and moves through Hank Ballard's Look at Little Sister, the slow minor-key blues Ain't Gone 'n' Give Up on Love, Doyle Bramhall's Change It, and a cool jazz-soul turn on Eddie Harris's Gone Home.","By 1985 Vaughan was a star, touring relentlessly after the success of Texas Flood and Couldn't Stand the Weather, and Epic wanted a third record quickly. The band recorded in Dallas under heavy pressure and growing personal strain. Adding Wynans was partly a practical answer: a keyboard player let the band fill out arrangements on stage and drew on the Texas soul and gospel organ sound that Vaughan had grown up hearing.","Listen for how much the organ changes Vaughan's playing. With Wynans holding chords underneath, he plays fewer rhythm parts and more sustained, vocal lines, especially on Ain't Gone 'n' Give Up on Love, where the guitar sounds closer to a gospel singer than a bluesman. Gone Home shows a jazzier side of his phrasing that rarely appears elsewhere on his studio records.","Reviews were warm but more muted than for the first two albums, and Vaughan himself was later candid about how hard the sessions had been. Over time listeners have revised upward, hearing the record as a bridge between the raw trio and the more mature In Step. Ain't Gone 'n' Give Up on Love in particular is now counted among his finest slow blues performances.","Start with Ain't Gone 'n' Give Up on Love, turned up, and follow how the guitar and organ trade the lead. Then hear Say What! beside Hendrix's wah-wah playing on Electric Ladyland. The live album Live Alive, from the following year, shows how these songs grew on stage with the new quartet line-up."],"bee-gees-saturday-night-fever":["A double album built around the Bee Gees' songs for the film: Stayin' Alive, How Deep Is Your Love, Night Fever, More Than a Woman, Jive Talkin' and You Should Be Dancing, all driven by Barry Gibb's falsetto and a steady, four-on-the-floor kick drum. Around them sit other disco records, among them the Trammps' Disco Inferno, Yvonne Elliman's If I Can't Have You and Walter Murphy's A Fifth of Beethoven, plus David Shire's instrumental cues. The texture throughout is tight rhythm guitar, strings and high harmonies.","Robert Stigwood, the Bee Gees' manager and the head of RSO Records, was producing a film about a Brooklyn dance-floor king played by John Travolta, and asked the brothers for songs. They wrote most of them in France, at the Château d'Hérouville studio, before the film was finished. Disco was then a club culture rooted in Black, Latino and gay New York nightlife; the soundtrack carried it into suburban living rooms around the world almost overnight.","Two tracks reach straight back to the classical concert hall. Walter Murphy's A Fifth of Beethoven sets the opening of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony to a disco beat, and David Shire's Night on Disco Mountain does the same to Mussorgsky's Night on Bald Mountain. Listen too for the Bee Gees' drum sound on Stayin' Alive, a short repeating loop cut from another of their recordings because their drummer was away.","The album became one of the best-selling records ever made and won the Grammy for Album of the Year. Its very success fed the disco backlash of 1979, when the style was mocked and even publicly destroyed, and the Bee Gees' reputation suffered for years. Since the 1990s critics have restored it to its place as a high point of studio craft and of the dance music that later fed house and electronic pop.","Start with Night Fever and listen to how the strings and falsetto float above a beat that never varies. Then play A Fifth of Beethoven right after the first movement of Beethoven's Fifth for a lesson in how pop translates the concert hall. Afterwards hear Chic's Le Freak and Donna Summer's I Feel Love to place it within disco proper."],"bee-gees-to-love-somebody":["A soul ballad in which the singer pleads with someone who cannot see how much he loves them. Barry Gibb takes the lead in a rough, straining tenor quite unlike the falsetto of the brothers' later disco records, and Robin and Maurice join on the chorus. Behind them a strummed acoustic guitar and steady drums are swept up by strings and brass. The rising chorus, which seems to reach for a note just out of range, is what everyone remembers.","The Gibb brothers, raised partly in Australia, had returned to England in 1967 and signed with Robert Stigwood, then working for Brian Epstein's organisation. The song came out on their first international album, Bee Gees' 1st. Barry and Robin are often said to have written it with Otis Redding in mind, hoping he would record it; Redding died in December that year without doing so, and the song remained the brothers' own.","Listen to how the harmonic movement of the chorus comes from Southern soul rather than British pop: the progression and the gospel-tinged vocal climb are closer to Stax than to the Beatles, which is why Black singers took to it so naturally. The orchestral arrangement, typical of London pop in 1967, sits slightly at odds with the raw vocal, and that tension is part of the song's ache.","It was only a modest hit for the Bee Gees, but it became one of their most covered songs. Nina Simone recorded it in 1969, and Janis Joplin made it a centrepiece of her live shows the same year; their versions persuaded many listeners that the young British trio could write real soul. Later critics often name it as the best of their 1960s work.","Hear the Bee Gees' original, then Nina Simone's and Janis Joplin's versions back to back, and listen to how each singer treats the climb of the chorus. Then play Stayin' Alive, written a decade later, to measure how far the same voices travelled. Otis Redding's Try a Little Tenderness shows the kind of singer the song was imagined for."],"grateful-dead-anthem-of-the-sun":["The Grateful Dead's second album is a collage. Each side is a continuous stream in which studio takes and several different live performances are layered and spliced together, so a song can shift from a club recording to a studio overdub in mid-phrase. Side one is the suite That's It for the Other One; side two holds Alligator and the long, accelerating Caution (Do Not Stop on Tracks). Two drummers, feedback, organ and prepared piano make a dense, swirling texture that rarely settles.","Made in 1967 and early 1968 in San Francisco and Los Angeles, the album came out of the Haight-Ashbury scene at its height. Mickey Hart had just joined as second drummer and Tom Constanten contributed prepared piano and electronic sounds. Bassist Phil Lesh had studied composition with Luciano Berio, and he and Jerry Garcia mixed the record like a tape composition, frustrating Warner Bros., which had expected a conventional rock album and watched the costs climb.","Listen to the edges between live and studio passages: sometimes the room sound changes abruptly, and you can hear one performance fade under another. This technique comes straight from musique concrète and the tape works of Stockhausen and Berio rather than from rock. Constanten's prepared piano, with objects placed on the strings in the manner of John Cage, adds metallic clatters that are easy to mistake for guitar.","It sold poorly at first and baffled many reviewers, who heard chaos. Over time it has come to be seen as one of the most adventurous records of the psychedelic era and a crucial document of how the band actually sounded in 1967, with the studio used to approximate a live experience. Fans still argue over the band's own 1971 remix, which many find flatter than the original mix.","Listen to side one in one sitting without skipping, and try to notice each splice where the performance changes. Then hear the band stretch similar material on Live/Dead a year later. Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge and the Beatles' Tomorrow Never Knows are the right neighbours for understanding what the band was attempting."],"grateful-dead-europe-72":["A triple live album drawn from the band's spring 1972 tour of Europe. Much of it introduces new songs, among them Jack Straw, Brown-Eyed Women, Tennessee Jed, Ramble On Rose and the slow, choral He's Gone, played in a relaxed, country-tinged style. The long-form side shows the other band: China Cat Sunflower flowing into I Know You Rider, and Truckin' opening into extended improvisation before the dark ballad Morning Dew. Keith Godchaux's piano gives the whole set a warmer, rolling texture than earlier records.","The tour was a large undertaking, with families and crew travelling together and a multitrack recorder taken along so that every show could be taped. Keith and Donna Jean Godchaux had recently joined; organist and singer Ron McKernan, known as Pigpen, was seriously ill, and this was his last tour before his death the following year. Warner Bros. had grown used to the band's expensive studio habits, and live albums were a way to recoup.","Although billed as live, many vocals were re-recorded in the studio afterwards to fix pitch and harmony problems, a common practice that fans long debated. Listen to the transitions on the jam sides: the band often moves from one song to the next through a quiet passage that seems improvised but follows paths they had worked out over many nights.","It sold well and became one of the band's most loved albums, partly because so many of its songs had never appeared in studio versions. Later the band released the complete recordings of every concert on the tour, and listeners now debate whether the original edited album or the full shows give the truer picture; most agree the 1972 band was at a peak.","Start with He's Gone for the singing, then the China Cat Sunflower and I Know You Rider sequence for the way two songs become one. Listen to the piano rather than the guitars on a second pass. Workingman's Dead and American Beauty show the studio roots of the songwriting, and the Allman Brothers' At Fillmore East is the natural live comparison."],"mariah-carey-butterfly":["Mariah Carey's sixth studio album turns from the big adult-contemporary ballads of her early career toward hip-hop soul. Honey rides a loping, sampled groove; The Roof (Back in Time) builds on the dark bass figure of Mobb Deep's Shook Ones Part II; Breakdown features Krayzie Bone and Wish Bone of Bone Thugs-n-Harmony. Between these sit ballads such as the title song and My All, a Latin-tinged torch song. Her voice is often softer and breathier here, layered into close harmonies instead of belted.","Butterfly was made as Carey's marriage to Tommy Mottola, the head of Sony Music, was ending, and it is widely heard as a declaration of independence from the polished image he had shaped. She worked with hip-hop producers including Sean Combs and Q-Tip as well as her longtime collaborator Walter Afanasieff. In 1997 the meeting of R&B singers and rappers was becoming the dominant sound of American pop, and Carey positioned herself at its centre.","Listen to the backing vocals: Carey stacks many tracks of her own voice into dense, whispered harmonies, a technique she uses more on this album than ever before. The famous high whistle register appears only sparingly, almost as punctuation. On The Roof the sample is used not as a hook but as a mood, the menace of the original turned into nostalgia.","Reviews at the time were divided between those who welcomed the new direction and those who missed the vocal displays. Its reputation has grown steadily, and many critics and fans now regard it as her best album and as one of the records that defined how pop singers and hip-hop producers would work together for the next decade.","Start with The Roof and listen for how the beat and her layered harmonies move against each other. Then hear Mobb Deep's Shook Ones Part II to understand what she borrowed. Compare the hushed singing here with her early hit Vision of Love to hear how much she changed in seven years."],"mariah-carey-we-belong-together":["A ballad about regretting the end of a relationship and wanting the lover back. It is built on almost nothing: a gentle, repeating piano figure, a soft programmed beat and a low bass. Carey sings the verses in a conversational, half-spoken middle register, packing in many syllables, and saves her full voice for the last minute, where the song rises a key and she finally opens up. The restraint, followed by the late release, is what makes it work.","The song came from The Emancipation of Mimi, released on Island Def Jam in 2005, and was produced with Jermaine Dupri and Manuel Seal. Carey's career had stalled after the commercial failure of the film Glitter and its soundtrack, and the album was framed as a comeback. The song refers to hearing records by Bobby Womack and Babyface on the radio, and the writers of those songs received credits on the track.","Listen to the rhythm of the verses: Carey phrases them almost like a rapper, with quick runs of words placed against the beat, a habit she learned from her work with hip-hop producers. The key change near the end is classic pop drama, but notice how late it comes; most of the song has stayed deliberately small to make it count.","It spent fourteen weeks at number one on the Billboard Hot 100 and was later named the most successful song of the decade by Billboard. Critics praised its restraint after years of complaints about vocal excess. It is now widely seen as the model for the minimal, piano-led R&B ballad that dominated the following years, and as the clearest proof that her comeback was real.","Listen for the moment, about two-thirds through, when the key lifts and her voice opens out. Then go back and notice how carefully she held back before it. Compare it with Vision of Love, her first single, where the voice is on full display from the start, and with Toni Braxton's Un-Break My Heart for another kind of breakup ballad."],"allman-brothers-band-the-allman-brothers-band":["The debut is a lean, dark record of seven songs, most of them by Gregg Allman, sung in his weary, older-than-his-years voice over his own Hammond organ. Duane Allman and Dickey Betts play lead guitar side by side, often in harmony, while two drummers lock together underneath. It opens with an instrumental cover, runs through Muddy Waters's Trouble No More and the slow, modal Dreams, and closes with Whipping Post, a riff in an odd, lurching metre that would become the band's great vehicle for improvisation.","The band came together in Jacksonville, Florida, in 1969 around Duane Allman, then a sought-after session guitarist at Muscle Shoals, and settled in Macon, Georgia, where Phil Walden was building Capricorn Records. The lineup was unusual for the Deep South of the time: a racially integrated group, with the drummer Jaimoe beside Butch Trucks. The album was cut quickly in New York, largely as a document of a set the band had already been playing night after night on the road.","Listen to the way the drums are split. Trucks plays a straight, driving pulse while Jaimoe, who came from soul and jazz, decorates around it, and the two parts interlock rather than double. On Dreams, the guitar solo leans on a single slowly turning scale, an idea Duane Allman openly took from Miles Davis and John Coltrane, which is why this blues band could stretch a song for twenty minutes without running out of road.","On release it sold modestly and critics heard it mostly as a strong blues-rock debut among many. Its reputation grew backwards from the live records: once At Fillmore East had shown what Whipping Post and Dreams could become, listeners returned to the studio originals as the blueprints. Some fans still find the debut a little stiff and the recorded sound thin; others prize it precisely for its compression, the songs before the jams swallowed them.","Start with Dreams and listen for the moment the slide guitar rises out of the organ's haze near the end. Then play Whipping Post here and the live version back to back, and notice how the five-minute studio cut is really a map for the later journey. A good remastered edition helps, as the original pressings sound muddy."],"allman-brothers-band-idlewild-south":["The second album is brighter and more varied than the first. It opens with Revival, a jangling, almost gospel celebration by Dickey Betts, and includes Gregg Allman's Midnight Rider, a spare outlaw ballad that became one of his most covered songs. The centrepiece is In Memory of Elizabeth Reed, a Betts instrumental in which the twin guitars state a long, winding melody in harmony before the band opens out into improvisation. Berry Oakley sings a rough reading of Hoochie Coochie Man.","The title comes from the band's name for a farmhouse they rented outside Macon, where they rehearsed and lived for stretches between endless touring. Tom Dowd, the Atlantic engineer and producer who had worked with Ray Charles and Aretha Franklin, now produced them, and he was also involved with Duane Allman's sessions for Derek and the Dominos around the same time. The band was still poor and largely unknown outside the South and its live circuit.","Elizabeth Reed was not a lover. Betts took the name from a gravestone in Rose Hill Cemetery in Macon, where the band members spent time among the old Southern headstones. Listen too to how the instrumental's opening melody is built for two guitars from the start: the second line is not an echo but a counter-voice, an approach closer to a horn section in a jazz arrangement than to rock guitar.","Like the debut it was not a big seller at first, and it is sometimes treated as a transitional record between the debut's blues and the live breakthrough. Yet its best songs became core repertoire, and Elizabeth Reed in particular is now regarded as one of the great rock instrumentals. Dowd's warmer, more open sound is usually preferred to the debut's, though some find the album short and uneven.","Hear the studio In Memory of Elizabeth Reed first, about seven minutes long, and memorise its twin-guitar theme. Then find the long live version on At Fillmore East and listen for that theme returning after the solos, like a refrain in a jazz head arrangement. Midnight Rider, by contrast, is best heard quietly, as a song rather than a jam."],"allman-brothers-band-brothers-and-sisters":["Without Duane Allman, the band rebuilt its sound around Dickey Betts's lighter, country-flavoured guitar and the rolling piano of the newcomer Chuck Leavell. The record has two famous faces. Ramblin' Man is a breezy country-rock song about a restless wanderer, with a long harmonised guitar coda. Jessica is a bright seven-minute instrumental in which guitar and piano trade the lead over a galloping rhythm. Around them sit slower blues numbers sung by Gregg Allman, and the acoustic Pony Boy.","The album was made in mourning. Duane Allman died in a motorcycle accident in Macon in October 1971, and the bassist Berry Oakley died in a similar crash a short distance away about a year later, during the sessions; Lamar Williams took over the bass. Rather than replace Duane with another slide guitarist, the band added a piano. The record came out as the Southern rock wave was cresting, and it became the band's biggest commercial success, topping the American album chart.","Betts said he wrote Jessica with Django Reinhardt in mind, trying to make a tune that could be played with only two fingers, as the Gypsy guitarist had to after a fire injured his hand. Listen for how the melody sits in a narrow span of notes and moves by short hops. The piano solo in the middle is the moment the new lineup announced itself: a whole new lead voice where a second guitar used to be.","It was a huge hit and made the band household names, but some critics and longtime fans felt the shift toward country and pop diluted the band they had known. That argument has softened: Jessica became a staple of radio and, much later, the theme music of a British motoring programme, and Ramblin' Man is a fixture of American classic rock. Most now hear it as a different band, not a lesser one.","Put on Jessica and follow the hand-off from guitar to piano and back, listening for the moment when the whole band rises into the main theme again. Then play Ramblin' Man and notice how its coda borrows the twin-guitar habit from the Duane years. Compare it with Eat a Peach to hear the band in transition."],"black-flag-jealous-again":["Five songs in barely seven minutes: the second Black Flag record is a 12-inch EP that sounds like a band trying to fit as much pressure as possible into each second. The title track lurches between a stop-start riff and a sudden sprint, and Greg Ginn's guitar is thin, harsh and atonal at the edges, already more like a saw than a rock lead. The singer here is Ron Reyes, who shouts with a shrill, almost hysterical urgency, very different from the later Henry Rollins.","Black Flag formed in Hermosa Beach, south of Los Angeles, in the late 1970s, and Ginn founded SST Records to release their music when no label would. By 1980 the band was banned from many venues, followed by police at shows and spreading word by flyers stuck on every telephone pole. The punk scene in southern California was hardening into something faster and more suburban than the Hollywood punk before it, the style soon called hardcore.","The cover image, by Ginn's brother Raymond Pettibon, is a stark ink drawing, and the Pettibon visual world was as much a part of the record as the sound. Musically, listen to Chuck Dukowski's bass: it often carries the riff on its own while the guitar sprays noise above, which is why the songs still hold together when Ginn wanders far from the key.","Small pressings meant few heard it at first outside the scene, and the reputation came later through compilations such as The First Four Years. Some fans consider the Reyes period the band at its rawest and fastest; others hear it as a rough draft for Damaged. Its importance now rests on being one of the records that fixed hardcore's form: very short, very fast, abrupt endings.","Play it loud and all the way through, since it is shorter than most single songs. Listen first for the title track's tempo changes, then for how each song simply stops instead of ending. Compare it with the Nervous Breakdown EP before it and Damaged after, and hear one band redraw its outline twice in three years."],"black-flag-rise-above":["The opening track of Damaged is the band's anthem. A charging, almost marching riff in the guitars and bass sets off Henry Rollins, who barks a song about defying the people and pressures that try to keep you down. The chorus is a shouted group chant, built for a room full of people to yell back. Underneath, Robo's drums keep a fast, steady pulse rather than the frantic blur of earlier Black Flag, so the song feels heavy and purposeful.","Rollins, a young fan from Washington, D.C., had joined in 1981 after jumping on stage at a New York show, and Damaged was his first album with the band. The song reflects the band's everyday conditions in Los Angeles: club bans, police harassment of punk gigs and a media panic about punk violence. Its distribution was itself a battle, as the album's original distributor dropped it, and SST had to fight to get it into shops.","Listen to the second guitar. Dez Cadena, who had been the singer before Rollins, moved to rhythm guitar, and his steady chords let Greg Ginn's lead slide away into squealing, out-of-key lines without the song losing its footing. The gang chant is also deliberately ragged; it was meant to sound like the crowd, not a polished backing choir.","It was never a hit in any chart sense, but it travelled through tape trading, skate videos and covers, and it has become one of the most covered hardcore songs. Later bands treated it as a statement of independence and self-reliance as much as a song. Critics generally rank it among the essential American punk tracks, and its message has been borrowed by causes far from the Los Angeles scene.","Wait for the first chorus and count how quickly the full band falls in behind the chant. Then listen just to the bass and drums, which barely change all song: the energy comes from the voice and the second guitar. Hear it as the opening of Damaged and let the whole album follow."],"bad-brains-rock-for-light":["Bad Brains' second album is a mix of blistering hardcore and deep, unhurried reggae. Many fast songs are re-recordings of numbers from the earlier cassette, now played even quicker and cleaner, with Dr. Know's guitar firing off bursts of fast, melodic lead between H.R.'s rapid, high, almost acrobatic vocals. Then the band drops into slow reggae tracks such as I and I Survive, where the bass becomes the melody and the guitar plays the offbeat chop. The whirl from one world to the other is the record's signature.","The four musicians, H.R., his brother Earl Hudson on drums, Dr. Know and Darryl Jenifer on bass, had started as a jazz-fusion group in Washington, D.C., before discovering punk and then Rastafarian faith. After trouble with D.C. clubs they moved to New York. The album was produced by Ric Ocasek of the Cars, an unusual pairing of a new wave hitmaker with a hardcore band, and it came out on a small independent label.","Behind the music sits the band's creed of positive mental attitude, a phrase they took from a self-help book and shouted from stages, which runs through the lyrics as a counterweight to punk nihilism. Listen for how the fast songs use real chord changes and quick key shifts that betray the musicians' fusion background; this is hardcore played by people who had been studying Return to Forever and Mahavishnu-style chops.","Many fans initially preferred the rawness of the earlier cassette and found Ocasek's production too bright. A remixed reissue some years later changed the sound again, and the arguments about which version is best continue. Its standing as a key hardcore record has never been in doubt, and the reggae tracks are now heard as a crucial bridge between punk and Jamaican music in America.","Start with a fast track, then go straight to I and I Survive and feel the floor drop to half speed. Listen to Jenifer's bass in both; it is the same player, doing opposite jobs. Then compare it with Bob Marley's Exodus for the reggae roots, and with Minor Threat for the D.C. hardcore it inspired."],"bad-brains-banned-in-dc":["Just over two minutes long, Banned in D.C. opens with a tight, chopping guitar figure and then explodes into hardcore at a tempo few bands could hold. H.R. sings about being shut out of their hometown's clubs and taking the music somewhere new, his voice veering from rapid patter to high, sustained cries. The middle of the song shifts gear with a rhythmic break where the bass and drums drive alone, before Dr. Know's lead guitar tears back in.","The song appears on the band's first full release, a cassette issued by the New York label ROIR in 1982. In the early 1980s Bad Brains had been barred from a string of Washington venues, partly because of the wild crowds at their shows, and they relocated to New York, where they became fixtures at clubs such as CBGB. Their D.C. shows had already inspired the local teenagers who would found the Dischord label scene.","The band's precision is the hidden marvel. Listen to the start-stop accents: all four players hit them together at a speed where most punk bands simply smeared. Earl Hudson's drumming has a jazz drummer's lightness on the cymbals even at this tempo, and that crispness is what made the record a model for so many later hardcore drummers.","Never released as a single in its time, it travelled through the cassette and later reissues and became a rallying cry for the D.C. scene the band had left behind. The title was borrowed for a well-known book of punk photography. Critics routinely place the song near the top of lists of essential hardcore tracks, as concentrated proof of the band's technical edge.","Listen once for sheer speed, then again for the break in the middle, where the music suddenly gives you room to breathe. Follow the bass through it. Then compare it with Minor Threat's Straight Edge, from the D.C. kids Bad Brains inspired, and hear how much tighter the teachers were."],"bad-brains-sailin-on":["The opening track of the 1982 cassette is something unexpected in hardcore: a love song with a catchy, singable tune. The band plays at breakneck speed, but the guitar riff is melodic and bright, almost a pop hook, and H.R. sings a clear, rising melody about a relationship that has run its course and the decision to move on. The whole thing lasts about two minutes, with a sharp bridge and a burst of soloing before it slams shut.","Bad Brains came out of the Washington, D.C., area, four Black musicians in a punk scene that was overwhelmingly white, and they had arrived at punk from jazz fusion. The ROIR cassette, recorded after their move toward New York, was the first album most people heard by them, sold by mail and in independent shops on a label that specialised in cassette-only releases. Sailin' On set its tone from the first seconds.","Listen to the harmony under the melody: the chord changes move more like a soul or pop song than a three-chord punk number, which is why it sticks in the head. H.R.'s voice also does something characteristic here, jumping into a high register for the hook, a habit that marks him off from the shouting style that became standard in hardcore.","The song has been widely covered, and later melodic hardcore and pop-punk bands have pointed to it as proof that speed and tunefulness could live together. Critics often cite it alongside Banned in D.C. as the cassette's highlight. Its love-song subject was itself a quiet challenge to the idea that hardcore had to be only about anger.","Try humming the chorus after one listen; you probably can. Then listen to how fast the drums are moving underneath that tune. Compare it with the Descendents' Milo Goes to College, which took the same idea of short, melodic, very fast songs about relationships in a different direction."],"joanna-newsom-emily":["The opening song of Ys runs about twelve minutes. Newsom's concert harp and her bright, unusual voice carry a long, unfolding ballad addressed to her younger sister, full of images of the night sky, meteors and the difference between a meteor, a meteoroid and a meteorite. Around the harp, an orchestra arranged by Van Dyke Parks enters and withdraws, adding woodwind birdsong, swelling strings and pizzicato flickers, so the song feels like a story told in many scenes rather than verse and chorus.","Newsom grew up in Nevada City, California, studied composition and played harp from childhood, and her debut had been a spare record of harp and voice. For Ys she went much bigger: the harp and voice were recorded by Steve Albini in Chicago, Parks wrote orchestral arrangements in Los Angeles, and Jim O'Rourke mixed the album. The record, on the independent label Drag City, appeared at the height of the so-called freak-folk moment, though it fitted no scene easily.","The sister is real: Emily Newsom studied the sciences, and the song quietly turns the astronomy she taught Joanna into a language for family love and distance. Listen for the harp ostinato that returns throughout, a patterned figure in Newsom's right hand that anchors the whole twelve minutes while the melody wanders. Parks's orchestra often comments on the words like a film score, picturing a phrase a moment after she sings it.","Ys received wide critical acclaim, and Emily, as its opening statement, was often singled out. Some listeners were put off by Newsom's voice, which is high, cracked in places and unconventional, and by the length and density of the words; others found exactly those things the point. The pairing with Parks, whose own Song Cycle had once been a commercial flop, gave his orchestral style a new audience.","Give it full attention, ideally with the words in front of you, and let the length unfold like a short story. Listen first just to the harp pattern, then to the moments the orchestra enters. Afterwards, try a live solo harp version to hear the song stripped back, and set it beside Van Dyke Parks's Song Cycle."],"lil-wayne-dedication-2":["A long mixtape hosted by DJ Drama, whose shouted drops interrupt the tracks, this is Lil Wayne rapping almost without stopping over a mix of original and borrowed beats. The pleasure is in the flow: he pours out strings of puns, sudden left turns, odd voices and private jokes, often sounding as if he is inventing each line on the spot. Most tracks drop the song form entirely; they are verses that run as long as his ideas last, then cut away.","By 2006 Wayne had already had hits on Cash Money, but mixtapes, given away or sold informally and passed around online, let him record far faster than any album schedule allowed. DJ Drama's Gangsta Grillz series was the leading brand in Southern mixtapes. The tape appeared in the year after Hurricane Katrina had devastated Wayne's home city of New Orleans, and the storm and the government's response shadow parts of it.","The most discussed track turns sharply political, with Wayne voicing anger at the federal handling of Katrina; it is a striking moment from a rapper usually heard as playful. Listen also for how he treats someone else's beat: rather than just copying the original's cadence, he often rides against it, landing his rhymes just behind or ahead of the pulse.","Critics and fans have come to see Dedication 2 as the start of the run in which Wayne's mixtapes outshone most official albums by anyone, building the reputation that Tha Carter III cashed in. It also became part of the debate about mixtapes and copyright, as DJ Drama's studio was raided in early 2007 over unlicensed mixtape sales. Some find the tapes overlong and uneven; that sprawl is also their character.","Do not try to take it all in at once. Pick three or four tracks, and listen to how a single verse keeps changing direction. Pay attention to the rhythm of his voice as much as the words. Then compare with Tha Carter II before it and Da Drought 3 after to hear the momentum build."],"lil-wayne-da-drought-3":["A two-disc mixtape of nearly two hours, made mostly of Lil Wayne rapping over other artists' popular beats of the moment. Each track is a kind of challenge: take a famous instrumental, ignore the original song, and out-rap it. His voice, raspy and elastic, slides into croaks, giggles and sung asides, and the wordplay piles up in long chains of associations. There are almost no hooks; the form is the extended verse, a stream of rhymes over a familiar groove.","Released in April 2007, the tape spread online and through street sales during a year when Wayne was recording at a famous rate, appearing as a guest on dozens of other artists' records and calling himself the best rapper alive. Tha Carter III had been delayed, and leaks of its material were circulating. Mixtapes like this filled the gap, and for many listeners they were the main way to hear him.","The game is comparison. If you know the original songs behind these beats, the tape becomes a running commentary on 2007's charts, with Wayne bending each producer's track to his own rhythm. Listen for how often he abandons the expected rhyme at the end of a line for a pun that resets the whole thought, a technique later rappers widely imitated.","Many critics consider Da Drought 3 one of the best rap mixtapes ever made, and some at the time ranked it among the year's best hip-hop releases of any kind, a striking claim for a free tape of borrowed beats. Skeptics find it exhausting and repetitive at this length. Its influence on how rappers released music, constantly and online, is not in dispute.","Treat it like a set of variations. Choose a few tracks over beats you already know, and listen to what he does differently from the original rapper. Take a break between discs. Then hear Tha Carter III, the official album this momentum produced, to compare loose and polished Wayne."],"three-6-mafia-chapter-1-the-end":["The group's second album extends the eerie sound of Mystic Stylez. DJ Paul and Juicy J's production is built from booming drum-machine kicks, rattling hi-hats and thin, ghostly synthesizer melodies, often in minor keys and moving at a slow, menacing crawl. Over this, a rotating cast of rappers trade verses in styles ranging from rapid, chopped triplets to chanted, hypnotic hooks. The overall atmosphere is of a horror film soundtrack played through car speakers.","Three 6 Mafia came out of Memphis, Tennessee, where a local underground of cassette tapes had developed its own lo-fi, dark style through the early 1990s. The core members, DJ Paul, Juicy J and Lord Infamous, worked with Gangsta Boo, Koopsta Knicca and Crunchy Black, and released this album independently through the label they were involved with, before major-label distribution arrived. The group's occult imagery was partly theatre, partly a provocation aimed at the conservative South around them.","Listen for the triplet flow that runs through the album: three quick syllables to each beat, rapped in clipped bursts. That cadence, honed in Memphis tape culture, later spread throughout Southern hip-hop and then into trap music worldwide. The production's sparseness also hides a cleverness with space; the drums often drop out for a bar so the synthesizer line hangs alone.","Outside the South it was little noticed by national critics at the time, who largely ignored Memphis rap. Its reputation has grown with the recognition that the Memphis sound shaped later trap and the internet-era revival of eerie, distorted rap. Some listeners find the album overlong and the dark imagery heavy-handed; collectors prize it as a document of the city's underground at its peak.","Listen on speakers with real bass, and focus first on the beats: the kick drum, the hissing hats, the single repeating synth line. Then follow how the rappers' flows lock into that grid. Compare it with Mystic Stylez and with 8Ball and MJG's Comin' Out Hard to hear two sides of Memphis."],"three-6-mafia-chapter-2-world-domination":["The third album is bigger and more forceful than what came before. The trademark ingredients remain, deep drum-machine bass, ticking hi-hats and spooky minor-key synths, but the sound is cleaner and louder, and the hooks are chanted by the whole crew like a stadium crowd. It includes a re-recorded version of their local hit Tear da Club Up, a pounding party track, alongside darker, slower songs that keep the horror-movie mood.","By 1997 the group had built an audience across the South through relentless touring and tape sales, and this album was their first with national distribution through a larger label partnership. The South was becoming a commercial force in hip-hop, with Master P's No Limit and Cash Money in New Orleans and OutKast in Atlanta drawing attention away from the coasts. Memphis, with its own lo-fi tradition, was part of that rise.","Listen for the call-and-response chants, which owe something to the crunk party chants of Memphis clubs and to gospel and drill-team traditions of the region. These chanted hooks, repeated in a crowd voice, were a direct ancestor of crunk, the shouting party style that Lil Jon would popularise in Atlanta a few years later.","It became the group's first record to reach a wide national audience and is often treated by fans as the classic lineup's peak. Critics outside the South still tended to dismiss it at the time. Later assessments place it as a key bridge between the Memphis underground and the commercial Southern rap that dominated the 2000s, though some find its length and repetition a strain.","Start with the version of Tear da Club Up and feel how the chant works like a sports-arena call. Then move to the slower, darker tracks to hear the contrast. Follow it with Most Known Unknown to hear how the same production ideas sounded after a decade of refinement."],"three-6-mafia-most-known-unknown":["The group's commercial peak trims the lineup to DJ Paul, Juicy J and Crunchy Black and polishes the old Memphis sound for radio. The beats are still built on huge 808 bass and minor-key synthesizer lines, but the hooks are catchier and the production brighter. It includes Stay Fly, built around a sped-up soul sample and a slick, swaggering chorus, and Poppin' My Collar, a strutting party track. Guest verses from other Southern rappers spread the album's net wide.","By 2005 Southern hip-hop dominated the charts and the airwaves, and Three 6 Mafia's earlier underground innovations had been absorbed into the mainstream through crunk and trap. Signed through Columbia, the group finally reached the audience their influence had long implied. The following year they won an Academy Award for best original song, for a track from the film Hustle and Flow, becoming the first hip-hop group to win that award.","Listen for how the production quotes their own past: the eerie synth tones and triplet flows of the mid-1990s tapes are still there, cleaned up but recognisable. The group had spent a decade watching other producers borrow their sound, and here they reclaim it. The soul-sample approach of Stay Fly is also a nod to the East Coast style, absorbed rather than imitated.","It became the group's best-selling album and was well received as a confident mainstream statement. Some longtime fans missed the raw menace of the early tapes and felt the polish dulled the group's edge. Its legacy is tied to the moment Southern rap fully took over the mainstream, and to the group's overdue recognition as founders of a sound.","Start with Stay Fly, then go back to Mystic Stylez and hear how much of its DNA is still present. Notice the bass, which is designed for car stereos, and the way the chorus repeats until it sticks. Compare it with Gucci Mane's Trap House from the same year."],"bad-bunny-el-ultimo-tour-del-mundo":["Bad Bunny's third album of 2020 is moodier and more inward than the party-centred YHLQMDLG. It moves between reggaeton, trap and, unexpectedly, rock: Yo Visto Así swings on distorted guitars, and Te Deseo lo Mejor glides on dreamy synth pop. The best-known track, Dákiti, with Jhay Cortez, is a hypnotic, minimal reggaeton cut with a floating synth line. Much of the album concerns heartbreak, isolation and fame, sung in his low, conversational voice.","The album was made during the Covid-19 pandemic, after Bad Bunny's planned world tour had been cancelled, which gives the title its bittersweet irony. Released in November 2020 on Rimas Entertainment, it became the first entirely Spanish-language album to reach number one on the American Billboard album chart, a symbolic milestone for Latin music in the United States. Bad Bunny, from Vega Baja in Puerto Rico, was by then the world's most-streamed artist.","Listen to how the rock songs are not a pose: Bad Bunny grew up on a mix of salsa, reggaeton and American rock, and he had spoken about wanting to make rock sound natural inside a Latin pop album. Dákiti, meanwhile, avoids the classic dembow drum pattern almost entirely, using a lighter, ambient groove, a sign of how far reggaeton's boundaries were being pushed.","Reviews were warm, with critics praising its range and emotional honesty, while some felt it less cohesive than YHLQMDLG. Its chart achievement was widely discussed as a turning point in the American music industry's view of Spanish-language music. It is now often heard as the bridge between his early party records and the more ambitious Un Verano Sin Ti.","Begin with Dákiti and listen to how little is actually going on in the beat. Then jump to Yo Visto Así and hear the guitars. Follow the album in order to feel its arc from restless to melancholy, and compare it with Rosalía's Motomami for another stretching of Latin pop."],"styne-gentlemen-prefer-blondes":["A brassy musical comedy following Lorelei Lee, a showgirl from Little Rock who sails to Paris in the 1920s with an eye on wealthy admirers. Jule Styne's score, with lyrics by Leo Robin, is a showcase of jazz-age pastiche: Charlestons, ballads and big production numbers. Its most famous song, Diamonds Are a Girl's Best Friend, is a mock-sentimental showstopper in which Lorelei explains her practical view of romance; A Little Girl from Little Rock introduces her with brash self-mockery.","Based on Anita Loos's 1925 comic novel, with a book by Loos and Joseph Fields, the show opened on Broadway at the end of 1949. It made a star of Carol Channing, whose wide-eyed, breathy Lorelei became her defining role. Styne, a former Chicago bandleader and Hollywood songwriter, was in his first years on Broadway after hits like High Button Shoes. The 1953 film, with Marilyn Monroe, used some of the songs and made Diamonds world-famous.","Listen for how Styne writes period pastiche: the 1920s setting lets him parody the Charleston and the torch song with a postwar wink, so the score is both nostalgic and knowing. Diamonds itself is built as a patter verse leading into a slow, swaying refrain, a structure that lets the singer play the joke deadpan before the melody blossoms.","The show was a solid hit and ran for more than seven hundred performances. Its reputation now rests heavily on one song and on the Monroe film, which many know better than the stage original. Revivals have been rare, partly because its book feels dated and its humour depends on a style of comedy few performers can carry. Channing's performance is remembered as a landmark of musical-comedy acting.","Hear Carol Channing's original cast Diamonds first, then Marilyn Monroe's film version, and compare the two very different comic personas. Then listen to the rest of the score for its sparkle and 1920s parody. Set it beside Gypsy, a decade later, to hear how much deeper Styne's theatre writing would go."],"compay-segundo-yo-vengo-aqui":["A full album of son and guaracha led by an old man's low, dry voice and the bright, chiming sound of his own instrument, the armónico. The songs are mostly short, unhurried pieces in the classic Santiago style: a sung verse, then a looser section where voices trade short calls and answers over a steady bass and the soft click of claves. Clarinet lines wind around the singing, and the second-voice harmonies, Compay's speciality, sit just beneath the melody rather than above it.","Francisco Repilado was nearly ninety when he made this record, after decades in which he had worked as a cigar roller and played only occasionally. In the 1940s and 1950s he had sung the low harmony part in the duo Los Compadres, which gave him his stage name, the second compadre. A Spanish label's interest in older Cuban musicians in the mid-1990s brought him back into a studio, a year before the Buena Vista Social Club sessions made him a world celebrity.","Listen for the armónico, the guitar Compay designed himself, with seven strings and a doubled G string that gives his chords a ringing, almost harp-like halo. It sits between the guitar and the Cuban tres in sound. Notice too that he sings the lower harmony even on his own record: decades of being the second voice shaped how he hears a melody.","It reached a modest audience on release, mainly in Spain, and was overshadowed within a year by the global success of Buena Vista Social Club. Later listeners have come to value it as the purer Compay statement, closer to how he actually sounded with his own band, without the international production polish. Some critics prefer it to the later solo albums, which grew more lavish as his fame rose.","Start by listening to the clarinet and voice together: they move like two conversation partners rather than soloist and accompanist. Then focus on the armónico's chords under the singing. Follow with Compay's Chan Chan as heard on Buena Vista Social Club to hear the same voice in a larger ensemble, and with Arsenio Rodríguez for the older, harder-driving son he grew up beside."],"compay-segundo-lo-mejor-de-la-vida":["Compay Segundo's first album after Buena Vista Social Club made him famous, a set of sones, boleros and guarachas that alternates gentle love songs with quick, playful dance numbers. His band, Compay Segundo y sus Muchachos, plays with a light touch: the armónico chimes, a second guitar fills in the rhythm, the bass walks the familiar anticipated pulse of son, and a clarinet threads countermelodies. Several songs feature his son Salvador on bass and the voices of younger band members answering the old man's lead.","By 1998 Compay was past ninety and touring the world, a sudden headliner after a lifetime of near obscurity. The record capitalised on that fame, but it remained rooted in the eastern Cuban repertoire he had played since his youth in Santiago, when he worked as a clarinettist with Miguel Matamoros's conjunto. Cuba in the late 1990s was emerging from the economic crisis of the Special Period, and foreign interest in its older music became an unexpected source of income for veteran performers.","Notice how Compay places his voice slightly behind the beat, as if deliberately relaxing against the band's pulse. It is a habit of the old trova singers of Santiago, and younger Cuban bands rarely do it. Also listen for the way boleros and fast sones sit side by side without a break in mood: in his tradition they are two faces of one music.","Critics outside Cuba received it warmly as a document of a vanishing style, and it helped sustain the late-1990s boom in Cuban son. Some Cuban musicians grumbled that foreign audiences now treated a narrow, nostalgic repertoire as the whole of Cuban music while ignoring timba and newer forms. That debate, between preservation and the living present, still shapes how the Buena Vista generation is discussed.","Begin with a fast son to catch the band's lilt, then go to a bolero and hear how the same instruments soften. Listen for the call-and-response choruses where Compay and his musicians trade phrases. For comparison, play Benny Moré for the big-band side of the same tradition and Los Van Van to hear what Cuban dance music had become by the 1990s."],"dean-martin-memories-are-made-of-this":["A brief, easygoing love song that lists the small pleasures making up a happy romance. Instead of the lush orchestra usual for a crooner of the day, Dean Martin sings over a spare, folkish backing: a strummed acoustic guitar, a plucked bass, and a small male vocal group answering each of his lines with a bright, clipped echo. His voice stays relaxed and warm throughout, never pushing, and the whole record is over in a little more than two minutes.","The song was written by Terry Gilkyson with Richard Dehr and Frank Miller, who as the Easy Riders also sang the backing parts and played guitar on Martin's Capitol recording. In 1955 Martin was still best known as half of the comedy team with Jerry Lewis, a partnership that would end the following year. Rock and roll was beginning to unsettle the pop charts, and Capitol was experimenting with lighter, guitar-based settings for its established singers.","Pay attention to the backing voices: they are not just harmony but a running commentary, each short phrase answering Martin like a chorus in a folk song. That structure comes from the folk revival world Gilkyson worked in, and it gives the record a homespun lightness quite unlike Martin's orchestral records. The arrangement is so bare that you can hear the guitar strings buzz.","It became one of the biggest hits of Martin's career and is often cited as proof that he could thrive outside the comedy partnership. Some critics of the time thought it slight; later writers hear it as a clever bridge between crooning and the coming folk boom. It remains a staple of oldies radio and of films seeking a cosy 1950s mood.","Listen first to the call and answer between Martin and the group; then notice how little else is there. Compare it with That's Amore, recorded two years earlier with a full orchestra, to hear how much the change in setting alters his persona. Sinatra's Songs for Swingin' Lovers, from the same label and era, shows the big-band alternative."],"benny-more-como-fue":["A slow bolero in which a lover wonders how and when he fell so completely in love. Benny Moré sings over a gently rocking rhythm, the bongos and maracas keeping a soft, steady pulse while saxophones lay down velvety sustained chords and muted brass add touches of colour. His tenor voice glides between notes, swelling to a full open sound at the climaxes and then dropping to an intimate half-voice. The form is simple, two verses and a return, but every phrase is shaped.","The song was written by the Cuban composer and bandleader Ernesto Duarte, and Moré's recording with Duarte's orchestra was made for RCA Victor in Havana in the early 1950s, as Moré was returning from years in Mexico and becoming the biggest star on Cuban radio. Havana's nightclubs, casinos and radio stations made it one of the busiest musical cities in the world, and the bolero was the romantic heart of that scene.","Listen to how freely Moré treats the tempo. He hangs back on some words and rushes others, while the band holds its pulse beneath him, so his line seems to float. Moré could not read music, yet he directed his own band by ear and gesture; that instinctive rhythmic freedom is exactly what written-out arrangements could not capture.","It became one of the defining boleros of the century and Moré's most covered ballad, recorded by singers across Latin America and Spain. Critics often name it as the best proof that Moré, famous for raucous mambos, was equally a great romantic singer. Later versions by Spanish and Mexican artists kept the song alive for new generations.","Start by listening only to his voice: note where he slides between pitches and where he lets a word hang. Then hear the saxophones underneath, moving like a slow tide. Pair it with Bonito y sabroso to hear Moré's dance side, and with Agustín Lara's Solamente una vez for the Mexican bolero he absorbed in his years there."],"chavela-vargas-macorina":["A song of longing and sensual invitation addressed to a woman named Macorina. Chavela Vargas sings it with only two guitars, one strumming slowly and one picking out bass lines and short fills. Her voice is low and rough, with a smoky edge, and she stretches the phrases until they almost stop, then lets a single word burst out at full strength. The song moves at a slow, swaying tempo, closer to a Cuban son than to a Mexican ranchera.","The words come from a poem by the Spanish writer Alfonso Camín, inspired by a famous Havana woman of the 1920s. Vargas, born in Costa Rica and living in Mexico City, made the song her signature in the late 1950s and early 1960s, when she sang in bohemian bars and was befriended by José Alfredo Jiménez. She performed in men's clothes and openly addressed love songs to women, at a time when Mexican society tolerated neither.","Listen for what Vargas does not change: she sings a song written from a man's point of view to a woman without altering a single pronoun. That simple choice, which her audiences understood perfectly, turned a period piece into a quiet act of defiance. Notice too the long silences she leaves between phrases, letting the guitars carry the line alone.","For decades the song was linked so closely to Vargas that many listeners assumed she wrote it. Her career faded in the 1970s through alcoholism, and her later revival, championed by Pedro Almodóvar in Spain in the 1990s, brought Macorina to a new audience. Writers now treat her performances as an early landmark of open lesbian expression in Latin American popular music.","Hear the opening lines and notice how slowly she lets them unfold; then wait for the moment she suddenly raises her voice. Compare an early studio recording with a late live one from her Carnegie Hall concert, where her voice is rougher and the pauses longer. La llorona makes a natural companion for her darker, mourning side."],"kitty-wells-paying-for-that-back-street-affair":["A woman tells the man who drew her into an illicit relationship that she is the one now carrying the shame. Kitty Wells sings it in a plain, level voice over a classic honky-tonk band: a steel guitar sliding between phrases, a fiddle playing short fills, rhythm guitar and bass keeping a steady two-beat. The melody closely follows that of the hit it answers, so listeners instantly recognised the reply. It is short, unadorned and direct, with no instrumental flourishes.","In 1952 Webb Pierce had a big country hit with Back Street Affair, sung from the man's side. Answer songs were a common commercial strategy in Nashville, and Wells had already broken through with one, It Wasn't God Who Made Honky Tonk Angels. Decca issued this reply the following year, keeping its new star in a role she would make her own: the woman's voice responding to the honky-tonk man's world.","The melody is borrowed almost entirely from Pierce's record, so the real change is in the point of view. Listen to how Wells sings the same contours more slowly and with less swagger; the music hardly differs, but the meaning reverses. Notice how the steel guitar answers her too, a second responder inside a song that is itself an answer.","It became another hit for Wells and confirmed that her first answer song had not been a fluke. Some critics have dismissed answer records as gimmicks, but historians of women in country music see Wells's run of them as a sustained argument, giving women a public voice about double standards. She is often credited with opening the door for Loretta Lynn and other later singers.","Play Webb Pierce's Back Street Affair first, if you can find it, then this; the pairing makes the point instantly. Then listen to It Wasn't God Who Made Honky Tonk Angels for the first, more famous answer. Loretta Lynn's Don't Come Home A-Drinkin' shows where this frank woman's perspective led a decade later."],"carmen-mcrae-the-great-american-songbook":["A live double album of standards recorded at a small Los Angeles club, Carmen McRae fronting a quartet with Jimmy Rowles on piano and Joe Pass on guitar. The set ranges from ballads to brisk swingers, and McRae talks to the audience between songs, joking and teasing. Her voice is lower and darker than in her youth, with a crisp, almost spoken attack on consonants. The intimacy of the room is everywhere: you hear glasses, laughter, and the band listening closely to her.","McRae made the record at Donte's, a jazz club in North Hollywood, at a time when the market for jazz singers had shrunk badly under the weight of rock and soul. Atlantic, by then primarily a rock and soul label, still recorded a few jazz veterans. Rowles, a pianist much admired by singers for his harmonic subtlety, and Pass, about to become a star of solo guitar, were working Los Angeles musicians who suited her conversational style.","Listen to how McRae phrases behind the beat and then catches up in a single word, a trick learned partly from Billie Holiday, whom she revered. Notice also the duets with Pass's guitar alone, where the rhythm section drops out and you hear her pitch held with complete security. Her stage banter is part of the art: she sets up each lyric's mood before singing.","It was well reviewed but sold modestly. Over time it has become one of the most loved records among jazz singers themselves, often recommended as the best single document of McRae in her mature live form. Some prefer her earlier, lighter studio records of the 1950s; others hold that this is where her ironic intelligence came fully into focus.","Begin with a ballad and listen to how she bends single syllables; then try an up-tempo number to hear her swing against Rowles. Pay attention to her spoken introductions. Compare with her earlier Billie Holiday tribute to hear the influence absorbed and transformed, and with Sarah Vaughan's records for a contrasting, more operatic voice."],"elmer-bernstein-to-kill-a-mockingbird":["A film score for a small orchestra that often sounds like a child playing alone. The main title opens with a solo piano picking out a simple tune one note at a time, then a flute, harp and accordion join, and finally the strings swell into a broad, warm melody. Through the film the score stays mostly quiet and transparent, using the children's theme in varied forms and saving darker, more dissonant music for moments of menace and the trial's aftermath.","Bernstein scored Robert Mulligan's film of Harper Lee's novel for producer Alan J. Pakula, with Gregory Peck as Atticus Finch. Hollywood scoring at the time still leaned toward the big symphonic sound, but Bernstein reportedly struggled to find an approach until he realised the story is told through a child's eyes. The film appeared amid the civil rights struggle, which gave its courtroom drama about racial injustice a pressing contemporary weight.","Listen to the opening piano: the notes are spaced as if by small fingers testing the keys, and the melody is built from a few tones a child could pick out. Bernstein's insight was to score the film from Scout's point of view rather than the adults', so even moments of danger are heard as a child would feel them, through simple, music-box sounds.","The score was nominated for an Academy Award and has become one of the most admired American film scores, regularly placed near the top of critics' lists. Some writers note how it broke with the heavy orchestral habits of the time; others praise its restraint, arguing that it never tells the audience how to feel about the trial. It is still widely performed in concert suites.","Start with the main title and listen for the moment the strings finally arrive after the piano, flute and harp. Then watch the opening credits sequence, where the music plays over a box of children's treasures. Compare with The Magnificent Seven, written two years earlier, to hear Bernstein's bold, open style, and with Copland's Quiet City for its roots."],"dinah-washington-this-bitter-earth":["A short, solemn ballad in which the singer asks whether a world so harsh holds any reward for a love that has gone wrong. Dinah Washington sings it slowly over swelling strings and a gentle rhythm section, her voice bright and cutting, with a slight edge of blues in the bent notes. The song barely lasts two and a half minutes and has almost no instrumental interlude; its weight comes from the long, sustained lines and the contrast between her hard tone and the soft orchestra.","The song was written by Clyde Otis, a producer and songwriter at Mercury Records, and Washington recorded it at the peak of her crossover period, after What a Diff'rence a Day Makes had brought her to a mainstream pop audience. Mercury dressed her in string arrangements to reach white listeners, a move some jazz fans resented. It became a sizeable rhythm-and-blues hit in 1960.","Listen for the way she clips the ends of phrases short while the strings keep sustaining; the effect is that she refuses to be consoled by the lush orchestra around her. Decades later the record found a second life: Charles Burnett used it in his film Killer of Sheep, and Max Richter blended it with his piece On the Nature of Daylight for Martin Scorsese's Shutter Island.","At the time, purists regretted the strings, hearing a great blues and jazz singer smoothed for the pop market. That judgment has largely turned around. The song is now one of Washington's most admired recordings, valued precisely for the tension between her uncompromising voice and the sweet setting. Its use in film has introduced it to listeners who know nothing else of hers.","Listen first for the contrast between her voice and the strings, and notice how she never softens to match them. Then play the scene from Killer of Sheep, where a couple dances to it in silence. Finally hear the Richter mashup from Shutter Island to understand how naturally her line fits over his slow string elegy."],"mancini-the-pink-panther-theme":["Under three minutes of tiptoeing menace played strictly for laughs. A bass line creeps upward in chromatic half-steps, the drummer brushes a soft swing, and then a breathy tenor saxophone slinks in with the tune, every phrase landing a fraction late as if the player were peering round a door. Brass punch in sudden stabs and a flute or vibraphone answers, but the sax carries the whole thing. The opening creep is recognisable from its first two notes.","Blake Edwards commissioned the theme for The Pink Panther (1963), a jewel-theft comedy starring David Niven and Peter Sellers as the bumbling Inspector Clouseau. Mancini and Edwards had already made Peter Gunn and Breakfast at Tiffany's together, and Mancini was now Hollywood's most bankable composer. The tune was first written for the animated opening titles, where a cartoon panther strolls through the credits; the cartoon proved so popular that it spun off its own series, carrying Mancini's music with it for years.","The melody was written with a specific sound in mind: the session saxophonist Plas Johnson, whose airy, behind-the-beat tone is the joke. Listen to how the band leaves holes for him and how the arrangement drops to almost nothing before each phrase, so the tune seems to sneak rather than march. The rising chromatic line underneath is a classic suspense device from crime films, here slowed until it becomes comic.","The score was nominated for an Academy Award and the soundtrack album sold enormously for RCA Victor, cementing Mancini's style of jazz-inflected film writing. Critics then treated it as clever light music; it now stands as one of the most recognised instrumental themes of the century, used in countless parodies and advertisements. Some jazz writers still regard Mancini as a populariser rather than an innovator, but few arrangers matched his ear for exactly the right soloist.","Hear the original soundtrack recording rather than a later remake, because Plas Johnson's tone is the point. Count how many times the bass creeps up before the sax arrives. Then play Peter Gunn's riff straight afterwards: the same composer, the same crime-film vocabulary, one played menacing and one played as a wink."],"delerue-le-mepris":["A film score built almost entirely from one idea. Strings play a slow, aching melody, later known as Camille's Theme, which falls step by step through a long arch and then rises to begin again, each return richer and more anguished. A handful of smaller cues surround it, but the theme dominates, heard again and again across the film. The sound is lush, late-Romantic and frankly tragic, closer to Brahms or Mahler's slow movements than to jazz or pop.","Jean-Luc Godard's Le Mépris (Contempt), shot in Rome and Capri in 1963 with Brigitte Bardot and Michel Piccoli, adapts an Alberto Moravia novel about a screenwriter whose marriage collapses while he works on a film of the Odyssey. Delerue, by then the busiest composer of the French New Wave, was asked for music that could carry classical weight. The film's Italian release replaced his score with music by Piero Piccioni, an alteration Godard disowned.","Godard does not let the music play as background. He cuts the theme off mid-phrase, restarts it from the beginning, and lays it over scenes of argument where it seems to comment rather than accompany. Listen to how often a cue begins exactly as before: the repetition is the film's subject, a marriage circling the same wound. Martin Scorsese later reused the theme in Casino (1995), where its grief suddenly frames Las Vegas.","On release the score was admired but little discussed beside Godard's provocations. Its reputation grew steadily, and Camille's Theme is now among the most anthologised pieces of French film music, often cited as proof that New Wave cinema could hold an unashamedly Romantic score. Some critics hear the repetition as Godard using Delerue against his own lyricism; others say the music is the film's one sincere voice.","Start with Camille's Theme on its own and listen for where the melody turns upward again: that is the hinge of every return. Then watch the opening scene on the rooftop terrace and notice how the music stops dead. Compare Herrmann's Vertigo, another score about obsession that circles a single romantic idea."],"delerue-jules-et-jim":["A score of quick-changing moods for a modest orchestra. Bright waltzes and skipping dance tunes carry the early scenes of friendship in Paris before 1914; slower string themes with a folk-song simplicity take over as the story darkens. Woodwinds and piano trade light, transparent lines, and the music often sounds like a café band remembering a ball. The recognisable gesture is a lilting waltz that keeps reappearing, slightly sadder each time the three lovers regroup.","François Truffaut's third feature (1962), from Henri-Pierre Roché's novel, follows two friends, one German and one French, and the woman they both love, Catherine, played by Jeanne Moreau, across twenty years and a world war. Delerue had worked with Truffaut on Shoot the Piano Player in 1960 and became his regular composer. The New Wave shot cheaply and quickly, and Delerue's ability to write fast for small forces suited the method.","The film's best-known song, Le Tourbillon, which Moreau sings on screen, is not by Delerue at all but by Serge Rezvani, credited as Bassiak; Delerue's orchestral waltzes deliberately share its turning, circular motion. Listen for how the score's waltz themes speed up and slow down with the story's moods, so that the dance rhythm itself becomes a picture of the three characters whirling around one another.","Jules et Jim was a critical triumph and made Delerue's name internationally; his partnership with Truffaut lasted until the director's death. Critics have long praised the score's lightness against the film's tragedy, and it is often cited as a model of how New Wave directors used music: emotionally direct, never heavy. Some find it sentimental beside Godard's harder-edged soundtracks, but that warmth is exactly what Truffaut asked for.","Listen for the main waltz in the opening titles, then track its returns through the film. Watch the bicycle and countryside sequences, where the music does the storytelling with no dialogue. Pair it with Delerue's Le Mépris from a year later to hear the same composer writing for joy and for despair."],"marty-robbins-a-white-sport-coat":["A two-and-a-half-minute teenage lament sung by a grown man with a voice like warm honey. The song is about a boy dressed up for a dance whose date has gone with someone else. A gentle shuffle, soft backing voices and a light, almost pop orchestral setting frame Robbins's tenor, which glides upward on long held notes. The recognisable gesture is the title phrase itself, sung slowly and clearly, with a little catch of disappointment at its end.","Robbins wrote the song himself and recorded it for Columbia in 1957, at the moment rock and roll was pushing country singers off the radio. Rather than fight the teenage market he wrote for it, with an arrangement shaped by the pop producer Ray Conniff's orchestra. The record crossed over strongly from the country chart to the pop chart, proving that a Nashville singer could reach the teenagers who were buying Elvis Presley and the Everly Brothers.","The song uses almost nothing from country instrumentation: no fiddle, barely any steel guitar. What keeps it country is Robbins's phrasing, the slight sob and the drawn-out vowels learned from Western balladry. Listen to the backing chorus, which sings in the smooth, close style of 1950s pop vocal groups; that blend of a country voice and a pop frame is the formula later called the Nashville Sound.","It became one of the biggest records of 1957 and Robbins's first major crossover. Country purists of the time saw it as a capitulation to pop, and it has sometimes been dismissed as a novelty. Historians now hear it as an early, deliberate move in the commercial reinvention of country music, and as evidence of Robbins's range two years before his gunfighter ballads made him a Western storyteller.","Listen for how little is going on behind the voice and how much Robbins does with a single held note. Then put on El Paso from Gunfighter Ballads to hear the same voice telling a story with a gun in it. For contrast, play Elvis's Heartbreak Hotel from the year before: two very different answers to teenage heartbreak."],"mel-torme-swings-shubert-alley":["Twelve Broadway show tunes, swung hard and fast. Tormé sings in front of a compact, brassy group arranged by Marty Paich: trumpets, trombone, French horn, tuba, saxophones and a light-footed rhythm section. The charts are tight and contrapuntal, with horn lines weaving around the vocal rather than simply backing it. Tormé's voice is soft-grained, the sound that earned him the nickname The Velvet Fog, but his timing is a drummer's, snapping onto the beat and sliding off it.","Tormé and Paich had made a celebrated album for Bethlehem in 1956; this reunion came after Tormé moved to Verve, Norman Granz's label, home also to Ella Fitzgerald's songbook series. The title refers to the street in New York's theatre district, and the songs come from hit musicals of the previous decades, by Rodgers, Loesser, Lerner and Loewe and others. Show albums were a staple of the late-1950s LP market; Tormé's twist was to treat them as jazz material.","Paich's writing borrows its sound from Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool nonet, with French horn and tuba giving the ensemble a mellow, rounded bottom unusual in vocal records. Listen for the moments where the horns state a counter-melody exactly as Tormé is singing: Tormé was a trained arranger and drummer himself, and he places his phrases to lock with Paich's lines rather than float above them.","The album was well reviewed and has stayed in print, often paired in critical guides with the 1956 Dek-Tette record as the high point of Tormé's career on disc. Some listeners prefer the earlier, looser session; others find this one sharper. Its standing has risen as Paich's arranging has been reassessed as some of the most inventive small-band writing on the West Coast.","Start with the up-tempo tracks to hear how the horns and voice interlock, then a ballad to hear the velvet tone. Listen especially to the tuba and French horn underneath. Compare Sinatra's Songs for Swingin' Lovers, where Nelson Riddle's big strings set a very different frame for the same kind of repertoire."],"mel-torme-an-evening-with-george-shearing":["A live set of standards stripped to almost nothing: Tormé's voice, George Shearing's piano and a double bass. With no drums, the singer and pianist share the job of keeping time, and the music breathes more freely than on Tormé's big-band records. Shearing's playing is crisp and full of quotation, his famous locked-hands chords appearing in brief flashes. Tormé, in his fifties, sings with a darker, more burnished voice and scats with a precision few singers ever had.","By the late 1970s Tormé's recording career had faded; rock had pushed vocal jazz to the margins. Concord Jazz, a California label devoted to mainstream jazz veterans, gave him and Shearing a new home. This concert recording, made in San Francisco, began a run of duo and trio albums with Shearing through the 1980s and brought Tormé a Grammy for best male jazz vocal performance, the start of a late revival.","Shearing was blind from birth and Tormé relied entirely on listening, not sight lines, to coordinate endings and tempo changes; the precision of their stops is a matter of pure ear. Listen for the moments when Shearing slips a fragment of another tune, sometimes a classical one, into an accompaniment line and Tormé answers it in his next phrase: the two were famous for these musical in-jokes.","The album was greeted as a revelation, proof that the singer once dismissed as a crooner was one of the finest jazz vocalists alive. The Grammy and the follow-up records restored Tormé's reputation among critics. Some reviewers find the Concord sound too polite, but most count the Shearing collaborations as the summit of his mature work.","Listen first to how the absence of drums changes everything: the time is carried by voice and piano together. Follow Shearing's right hand when Tormé is singing. Then go back to the 1956 Dek-Tette album to hear the same singer, young and bright, in a crowded, swinging ensemble."],"rl-burnside-a-ass-pocket-of-whiskey":["A loud, ragged collision between a Mississippi bluesman and a New York punk-blues trio. Burnside's guitar drones on one hypnotic riff for minutes at a time while the Jon Spencer Blues Explosion pile on distorted guitar and crashing, primitive drums. Songs stretch, stop, restart; Burnside talks, laughs and tells off-colour stories between the music. The texture is raw and often deliberately sloppy, but the hill-country trance underneath, a single chord pounded until it lifts off, never lets go.","Fat Possum, a small Oxford, Mississippi, label, had been recording Burnside and Junior Kimbrough in their sixties, and was looking for ways to reach a young rock audience. Jon Spencer's band had toured with Burnside and idolised him. The resulting record, released in partnership with the indie label Matador, was cut quickly and loosely, and sold Burnside to college radio and alternative-rock listeners who had never bought a blues record.","Behind the noise, the riffs are old: several songs are versions of pieces Burnside had played for decades in North Mississippi juke joints, learned from neighbours such as Mississippi Fred McDowell. Listen past the distortion to Burnside's own guitar, which barely changes chord at all; the excitement comes from the younger players reacting to his drone, not from any harmonic movement. The album's crude title and spoken stories are part of the same juke-joint tradition, kept unpolished on purpose.","Blues purists were appalled, and some called the record an exploitation of an elderly musician. Rock critics loved it, and it opened the door to the garage-blues revival of the next decade. Burnside himself seemed delighted with the attention and the income. Opinion today remains split between those who hear a genuine meeting and those who prefer his unadorned solo recordings.","Play it loud and let the repetition work; the point is the trance, not the chord changes. Then put on Too Bad Jim to hear the same riffs with only Burnside's band, and Junior Kimbrough's All Night Long for the deeper, slower version of the style. The White Stripes' early records follow directly from here."],"tony-bennett-because-of-you":["A three-minute love ballad sung with open-throated sincerity over a lush bed of strings. The song is about a lover who credits a partner for every good thing in life. Bennett, only in his mid-twenties, sings with a slightly rough, Italian-American tenor that pushes into the high notes rather than gliding; the orchestra swells beneath him in sweeping arcs. The moment you recognise is the final climb, where his voice opens out fully and the strings rise with it.","The song was written in 1940 by Arthur Hammerstein and Dudley Wilkinson. Bennett had been signed to Columbia by Mitch Miller, the label's powerful pop chief, and this recording, with an orchestra led by Percy Faith, was his breakthrough: his first number-one record. It belongs to the last years before rock and roll, when the big-voiced ballad singer with an orchestra was the centre of American popular music.","Listen to how Bennett phrases like a jazz singer even inside a commercial ballad: he leans late on certain words and lets others fall short, small rhythmic liberties he learned from listening to instrumentalists. That instinct would later lead him to the Basie band and the Bill Evans duets. The orchestral arrangement, by contrast, is pure early-1950s Columbia gloss, and the tension between the two is the record's charm.","A huge hit that made Bennett a star almost overnight. Critics of the period treated it as standard pop fare; later writers see it as the beginning of a seventy-year career and a document of the pre-rock ballad style at its most confident. Some find the arrangement dated, but Bennett's vocal is still admired for its directness.","Listen for the final climb and how Bennett holds back until it arrives. Then jump forward eleven years to I Left My Heart in San Francisco: the same voice, more controlled, more knowing. Pair it with Sinatra's Songs for Young Lovers to hear another young singer working out the ballad in the same years."],"galt-macdermot-two-gentlemen-of-verona":["Shakespeare's early comedy turned into a rock musical full of Latin rhythms, soul ballads, calypso and gospel. MacDermot's band mixes electric guitars and organ with horns and congas; the score moves from rowdy ensemble numbers to tender solo songs in quick succession. Where Hair was a loose collage, this show follows a story: two friends, two women, a journey from Verona to Milan. The sound is bright, sunny and dance-driven, with a deliberately multi-ethnic cast and vocal range.","Joseph Papp's New York Shakespeare Festival commissioned the adaptation for its free summer performances in Central Park in 1971, with lyrics by John Guare and a book by Guare and director Mel Shapiro. The production's success moved it to Broadway that December. Papp's theatre aimed to make Shakespeare belong to all New Yorkers, and the casting and Puerto Rican and Black musical idioms reflected the city's streets rather than Elizabethan England.","MacDermot, a Canadian who had studied music in South Africa, had a deep feel for African and Caribbean rhythm, and the score's grooves owe as much to that as to American rock. Listen for how the songs for the lovers often sit over Latin percussion patterns while the comic numbers lean toward rhythm and blues: the musical style marks who is who, much as Shakespeare's verse and prose do.","The show won the Tony Award for Best Musical in 1972, beating Sondheim's Follies, a result still argued over. It ran successfully but has been revived far less than Hair, and is now remembered mainly by theatre historians and fans of MacDermot's grooves. Recent revivals in the park have found it joyful if loose, and the multicultural casting looks ahead of its time.","Start with the original Broadway cast album and listen for the percussion section, which drives almost every number. Then compare Hair, to hear MacDermot moving from collage to storytelling. For the contrast the Tony voters faced, listen to Follies the same evening and weigh the two."],"galt-macdermot-shapes-of-rhythm":["Short instrumental tracks built on tight, funky drum patterns and repeated bass lines, with MacDermot's piano laying down blocky chords and simple, catchy riffs. Horns punch in with brief melodies and then step back. Nothing is long or showy; the album is about grooves, each track settling into a pattern and riding it. The sound sits between soul jazz and early funk, dry and close-miked, with the drums unusually prominent for a jazz record of its year.","MacDermot recorded the album in New York shortly before he wrote Hair, when he was a little-known pianist and composer earning money from commercial work. Released on a small scale, it sold little at the time. His fascination with rhythm came from years spent in South Africa and from listening to American rhythm and blues, and the record is effectively a sketchbook of the grooves he would soon bring to Broadway.","The record's real life came decades later: its crisp, isolated drum breaks and bass lines made it a favourite of hip-hop crate-diggers in the 1990s, and MacDermot's tracks became a quiet source for sampling producers. Listen for the passages where everything drops away except drums and bass; those few bars are exactly what samplers looked for, and they explain the album's cult status.","Ignored on release, the album was rediscovered by record collectors and reissued, and is now treated as a key document of MacDermot's non-theatrical music. Jazz critics rate it modestly as composition; its admirers argue that its value lies precisely in its economy and groove, the qualities that made it so useful to a later generation of producers.","Listen to the drums first; they are mixed as the lead instrument. Then hear Hair's overture with the same ears and notice the shared grooves. For the afterlife, follow with J Dilla's Donuts or Madlib's beat tapes to hear how producers turned records like this into new music."],"vince-guaraldi-cast-your-fate-to-the-wind":["A gentle, rolling piano piece in waltz-like triple time that gradually builds into a gospel-tinged, foot-tapping groove. Guaraldi opens with a simple, singable melody over rocking left-hand chords; bass and brushed drums ease in, the tempo firms up, and the middle section opens into bluesy, repeated chords with a churchy feel before the tune returns. The recognisable gesture is the opening phrase, a few falling notes over a swaying accompaniment, as hummable as a folk song.","Guaraldi, a San Francisco pianist, recorded the tune for Fantasy Records on Jazz Impressions of Black Orpheus, an album otherwise devoted to music from the Brazilian film Black Orpheus. Released as a B-side to one of the bossa nova numbers, it was picked up by radio programmers who preferred it to the A-side, and it became an unexpected hit. Its success brought Guaraldi to the attention of the producer who soon hired him for the Peanuts television specials.","The tune is in waltz time but rarely feels like a waltz; Guaraldi blurs the beat so the music seems to float. Listen for the shift in the middle where the harmony settles into repeated, almost hymn-like chords: that gospel inflection, borrowed from soul-jazz pianists, is what lifts it beyond a pleasant melody. The same rocking left hand would soon underpin his Charlie Brown music.","It won the Grammy Award for Best Original Jazz Composition and became a standard covered in pop and easy-listening versions. Jazz purists sometimes treat it as lightweight, but many pianists have kept it in their repertoire, and it is now recognised as the bridge between Guaraldi the club pianist and Guaraldi the composer of the most-heard jazz in American homes.","Listen to the opening alone and then wait for the moment the drums firm up the beat. Notice the gospel chords in the middle. Then put on Linus and Lucy from the Charlie Brown albums to hear the same left hand at work, and Brubeck's Take Five for another jazz single that crossed into pop."],"buck-owens-ive-got-a-tiger-by-the-tail":["Two minutes and change of bright, driving Bakersfield country. A trebly Telecaster riff kicks it off, the drums hit a hard, almost rock-and-roll backbeat, and Owens sings in his high, slightly nasal tenor with Don Rich's harmony locked right on top. The song is a comic complaint about a partner too wild to handle. The recognisable sound is the twang: sharp, clean electric guitars with no strings or choir anywhere, the opposite of the plush Nashville production of the time.","Owens wrote the song with Harlan Howard and recorded it with his band the Buckaroos for Capitol, and it was a hit in 1965. Bakersfield, California, a town of oil workers and migrants from Oklahoma and Texas, had developed its own honky-tonk scene, louder and rawer than Nashville. Owens had become its biggest star, and this record came at the height of his run of country number ones.","The secret weapon is Don Rich, the Buckaroos' guitarist and fiddler, whose harmony vocal sits so close to Owens's lead that the two voices almost fuse. Listen also to the recording itself: Owens reportedly mixed his records with car radios and jukeboxes in mind, pushing the treble so the record cut through the noise. The comic song is built on a driving shuffle that rock bands were already copying.","It was one of Owens's biggest hits and became a signature tune, even giving its name to an album. Nashville insiders saw Bakersfield as crude; later critics hear it as the honest, guitar-centred alternative that fed country rock and the 1980s neotraditionalists. Owens's later television fame on Hee Haw made some forget how sharp these records were.","Turn it up and listen to the guitar tone and the two-voice harmony. Then play Act Naturally to hear the same band a year earlier, and Dwight Yoakam's Streets of Bakersfield, his duet with Owens, for the inheritance. For contrast, try a Patsy Cline ballad from Nashville with strings and chorus."],"cy-coleman-on-the-twentieth-century":["A two-act musical comedy set almost entirely aboard the Twentieth Century Limited, the luxury express from Chicago to New York, where a bankrupt theatre impresario tries to win back the film star he once made famous. Coleman, best known for brassy jazz-inflected scores, wrote this one as mock operetta: patter trios, coloratura showpieces for the leading lady, a chugging chorus that imitates the train itself, and long ensemble finales that build in the manner of Offenbach and Gilbert and Sullivan.","The book and lyrics are by Betty Comden and Adolph Green, adapting the Hecht and MacArthur stage comedy that Howard Hawks had filmed in 1934. It opened on Broadway in 1978 with Madeline Kahn, John Cullum and a young Kevin Kline, at a moment when the American musical was turning dark and conceptual under Sondheim and Prince. Coleman chose to look backwards instead, toward the champagne operetta of the early century, and the gamble suited Comden and Green's love of screwball pace.","The overture is a little tone poem of locomotion: listen to the orchestra begin slowly, as if a steam engine were shunting out of the station, then pick up speed until the rhythm locks into a steady rail beat that returns throughout the show. Coleman also gives the heroine a mock-sacred number about a religious drama she is persuaded to star in, a parody of oratorio that lets the soprano show her top notes.","Critics admired the score's wit and the design, which turned the train into a gleaming art deco set piece, and the show won Tony Awards for its score, book and two of its actors. It never became a popular hit, partly because its operatic demands narrow the field of possible leading ladies. Judy Kaye made her name replacing Kahn, and a 2015 Roundabout revival with Kristin Chenoweth renewed the argument that this is Coleman's finest score.","Start with the opening sequence and the title song to hear how the orchestra becomes the train, then jump to the heroine's big vanity aria to hear Coleman writing for a true legit soprano. Compare it with Bernstein's Candide, the other great Broadway score that borrows operetta's clothes for comedy. The original cast album with Kahn and Cullum is the place to begin."],"junior-kimbrough-most-things-havent-worked-out":["Kimbrough's third album for Fat Possum, and the last released in his lifetime, is a set of long, hypnotic grooves built on one or two chords that circle for minutes at a time. His electric guitar plays a droning, interlocking riff, the bass doubles it, and the drums push a loose trance beat underneath while his voice floats over the top in a weary, half-spoken moan. The title song is a plain account of a life of disappointments, and the music refuses any release from that mood.","Kimbrough played these songs for decades at his own juke joint in Chulahoma, in the hill country of north Mississippi, where dancers would ride a single riff deep into the night. The Oxford, Mississippi label Fat Possum brought him to records in the 1990s, alongside R. L. Burnside, at a time when rock critics were discovering that this regional style sounded closer to the Velvet Underground than to Chicago blues. Kimbrough died in January 1998, shortly after this record appeared.","Listen to what never happens: there is almost no move to a second chord, the twelve-bar pattern that governs most blues is simply absent, and the songs end when the trance is spent rather than when a verse runs out. Kimbrough often shifts the riff by a beat or stretches a phrase, so the groove seems to limp and then right itself. That irregular pulse is what the dancers at Chulahoma were following.","The album was received as a confirmation of Kimbrough's late discovery, praised for its raw, almost unproduced sound. After his death his reputation grew among rock musicians: the Black Keys devoted an EP to his songs and named it after his hometown, and his name became shorthand for hill country trance blues. Some blues purists still find the style monotonous; its admirers answer that the repetition is the point.","Play the title track loud and do not wait for a chorus; let the riff settle in your body the way a minimalist piece by Steve Reich or Terry Riley does. Then go back to All Night Long to hear the earlier, rawer sound, and on to R. L. Burnside for the more raucous side of the same scene. Put on headphones to follow the bass against the guitar."],"ismael-rivera-incomprendido":["A slow-burning salsa track in which the singer insists he is a misunderstood man whom the world judges without knowing. Rivera's band, Los Cachimbos, sets a medium tempo with trumpets in tight block chords, a rolling piano montuno and a dense layer of Puerto Rican percussion. After the written verses the song opens into the improvised section, where the chorus repeats a short refrain and Rivera answers each time with a new phrase, bending rhythm and pitch like a jazz horn.","The song was written by the Puerto Rican composer Bobby Capó, but listeners took it as autobiography. Rivera had been the voice of Rafael Cortijo's combo in the 1950s, the band that brought Afro-Puerto Rican bomba and plena onto radio and television, before a prison sentence on drug charges interrupted his career in the 1960s. On his return he led his own group in New York for the Tico label, in the years when Fania and salsa were turning Caribbean dance music into a Latino urban identity.","Pay attention to how Rivera sings behind the beat in the improvised section, placing his phrases late and then catching up in a rush of syllables; the habit is why fellow musicians called him the Sonero Mayor, the greatest of improvising salsa singers. The trumpets are voiced so close together that they rub slightly, giving the band a raw edge that differs from the smoother Fania arrangements of the same years.","The record became one of the songs most closely linked to Rivera's public image, and the idea of the misunderstood man has followed him into every later account of his life. Critics of salsa history single out his work with Los Cachimbos as proof that the street sound of Puerto Rico could hold its own against New York's studio polish. Later soneros, from Héctor Lavoe onward, acknowledged his phrasing as their model.","Wait for the moment the written song gives way to the call and response with the chorus, and count how many different answers Rivera finds for the same refrain. Then listen to Las caras lindas for his more joyful side, and to Héctor Lavoe's El cantante for a singer who learned from him. Seek a remastered Tico compilation; early vinyl transfers can be muddy."],"jerry-herman-mame":["A two-act musical comedy about Mame Dennis, a flamboyant Manhattan society woman who takes in her orphaned nephew in 1928 and raises him through the Depression on the principle that life is a banquet. Herman's score is all bright march rhythms and big, singable tunes: a rousing title number in which a whole Southern hunting party salutes her, the cheerful holiday song that has since become a Christmas standard, a waspish comic duet for two old friends, and one aching ballad sung by Mame about the boy she fears she has lost.","Herman wrote both music and lyrics, two years after Hello, Dolly! made him Broadway's most reliable hitmaker; the book is by Jerome Lawrence and Robert E. Lee, adapting their own play from Patrick Dennis's novel Auntie Mame. The title role made a musical star of Angela Lansbury, previously known as a screen character actress, with Bea Arthur as her friend Vera. It opened in 1966, when rock had already pushed show tunes off the radio, and became one of the last great hits of the old Broadway mould.","The title song is built exactly like the title song of Hello, Dolly!: a chorus of admirers greets the heroine with a strutting march and a repeated toast of her name. Herman knew the formula and used it deliberately. Listen, though, to how the ballad If He Walked Into My Life turns the same open, optimistic melodic style inward, with the brass silenced and the strings carrying a doubt the rest of the score never admits.","Lansbury and Arthur won Tony Awards and the show ran for years, though some critics found it a calculated sequel to Dolly. The 1974 film with Lucille Ball was poorly received and damaged the property's reputation. Since then the songs have outlived the show: the Christmas number is everywhere each December, the friends' duet is a cabaret staple, and Lansbury's original cast album is regarded as a definitive musical comedy performance.","Start with the original Broadway cast recording and play the title song next to Hello, Dolly! to hear Herman's showstopper method side by side. Then listen to the ballad for the other Herman, the sentimental melodist, and the duet for two great comic actresses timing every line. Compare Styne's Gypsy for another musical built around a larger-than-life woman."],"schifrin-gillespiana":["A five-movement suite written for Dizzy Gillespie as soloist with an enlarged brass orchestra: Prelude, Blues, Panamericana, Africana and Toccata. There are no saxophones; instead French horns and tuba join the trumpets and trombones, giving a dark, burnished sound, while congas and other Latin percussion drive the rhythm. Each movement sets Gillespie's trumpet against blazing brass chords, moving from a slow, stately opening through Latin dance rhythms to a fast, virtuosic finale.","Lalo Schifrin, a classically trained pianist from Buenos Aires, met Gillespie when the trumpeter toured Argentina in the 1950s and showed him an extended composition. Schifrin moved to New York, joined Gillespie's quintet as pianist, and wrote this suite for him; it was recorded for Verve in 1960 and soon performed at Carnegie Hall. It extended the Afro-Cuban jazz that Gillespie had pioneered in the 1940s into the concert form of a multi-movement suite.","The title of the last movement is a clue: a toccata is a Baroque keyboard showpiece, and Schifrin gives the brass a driving, motoric pattern straight out of that tradition before Gillespie bursts through it. In Panamericana, listen for how the percussion layers rhythms from different Latin American traditions rather than one, an Argentine composer's view of the whole hemisphere rather than just the Caribbean.","The record was praised as one of the most ambitious settings ever made for Gillespie, and it launched Schifrin's career as a composer in the United States, leading within a few years to Hollywood and the theme for Mission: Impossible. Some jazz critics thought the orchestra overwhelms the soloist; others hear it as a peer of the Gil Evans and Miles Davis collaborations of the same years.","Start with the Blues movement, where Gillespie plays with a burnished, unhurried tone against the low horns, then hear the Toccata for the fireworks. Compare it with Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain, the other great trumpet-and-orchestra record of 1960, and go back to Manteca and Cubana Be / Cubana Bop to hear where the Latin rhythms began."],"lola-beltran-paloma-negra":["A ranchera lament in a slow, swaying waltz time, in which the singer begs a lover who has gone off to a life of pleasure to come back or to let her go. The mariachi opens with weeping violins and a single trumpet line, then drops back to a soft strum of guitars and the deep thump of the guitarrón while Beltrán sings. Her voice climbs to long, held notes at the end of each phrase, swelling with a sob and a cry that are the expressive core of the style.","The song was written by Tomás Méndez, the composer of Cucurrucucú paloma, and became one of the best-loved rancheras of the century. Beltrán, from Sinaloa, rose through Mexican radio and film in the 1950s and became the great female voice of ranchera, the genre that Mexican cinema had made a symbol of the nation. In an era when ranchera singing was strongly associated with male stars, she gave its anguished songs of love and abandonment a woman's authority.","Listen to the way Beltrán ends lines: rather than letting a note die away, she holds it at full power and then breaks it with a small catch, a vocal device ranchera singers learn as carefully as opera singers learn a trill. The mariachi violins answer each of her phrases with a short descending figure, as if the band were sighing back at her.","Beltrán's recording helped fix the song in the repertoire, and it was later reinterpreted by many voices, most notably Chavela Vargas, whose stark, broken delivery offers an opposite reading. Mexican critics regard Beltrán's version as the classic statement: grand, controlled and dramatic, in the tradition of the golden age of Mexican cinema. Debate centres on which approach, operatic grandeur or raw confession, is truer to the song.","Listen first for the sustained note at the end of the chorus and how she colours it with the catch in her voice. Then play Chavela Vargas's version to hear the same song stripped to a bare guitar and a speaking voice. Follow with Cucurrucucú paloma, by the same composer, to hear how Beltrán shapes another of Méndez's dove songs."],"lola-beltran-gritenme-piedras-del-campo":["A ranchera of heartbreak in which a lover, abandoned and alone, calls on the stones of the field to cry out the pain they cannot express. Beltrán sings it over a mariachi in slow tempo: trumpets in parallel thirds announce the melody, violins sweep in behind them, and the rhythm section holds a gentle, rocking pulse. The tune is long-breathed and climbs towards a climax on the refrain, where she lets her voice ring out at full volume across the band.","The song is by Cuco Sánchez, one of the most important ranchera songwriters of the mid-century, who wrote many of his songs for the singers who dominated Mexican radio, records and films. Beltrán's association with his material helped make her the leading female ranchera singer of her generation. The repertoire belonged to a national culture shaped by Mexican cinema's golden age, when songs about the countryside and wounded love spoke for a nation becoming rapidly urban.","The image at the heart of the song, a landscape that speaks when people cannot, connects ranchera to older Mexican poetry and folk song, in which mountains, birds and rivers carry human feeling. Listen to how the trumpets and violins never play together at the moment of greatest intensity: the band thins out and leaves Beltrán's voice alone to carry the climax.","The song has been recorded by many ranchera singers and remains a standard of the mariachi repertoire. Beltrán's performance is often held up as an example of her ability to combine dramatic projection with careful phrasing, which earned her the nickname Lola la Grande. Later critics and singers have debated how much her grand style owed to the cinema, and how much to the older traditions of Mexican regional song.","Listen for the trumpets' opening statement and then for the moment the band falls away under her voice on the refrain. Pair it with Paloma negra to hear two sides of her artistry, and with José Alfredo Jiménez's El Rey for the male counterpart of the same tradition. A good compilation of her recordings with full mariachi is the best way in."],"charley-pride-is-anybody-goin-to-san-antone":["A brisk country shuffle about a drifter standing by the highway, eager to get out of town and away from the memory of a woman, asking any passer-by for a ride to San Antonio. The band plays with a bouncing two-beat rhythm, bright acoustic guitar and a crying steel guitar answering each line. Pride's rich baritone sits right in the middle of the mix, relaxed and warm, never pushing, with a hint of a smile even as he describes heartbreak.","Written by Dave Kirby and Glenn Martin, the song became one of the string of number-one country hits that made Pride RCA's best-selling artist since Elvis Presley in that period. Pride, the son of Mississippi sharecroppers and a former Negro League baseball player, was the first Black star of mainstream country music, a remarkable position in the genre and in the American South at the turn of the 1970s. The Nashville industry had at first released his records without photographs.","Under the cheerful tempo, the song's real subject is escape and loneliness, and Pride's light delivery deliberately undersells it. Listen to the steel guitar fills: they play a slightly sadder melody than the singer, offering a second voice that admits what the narrator will not. The arrangement is far leaner than much of the lush Nashville Sound around it.","The single was a major hit and helped confirm Pride as one of country's dominant figures of the early 1970s. Critics have often noted that his records avoided the countrypolitan strings of the time in favour of honky-tonk instrumentation, linking him to Hank Williams and Ernest Tubb. Later writers have explored how his success challenged, and sometimes masked, the racial politics of country music.","Listen to the interplay of Pride's voice and the steel guitar, and to how relaxed his phrasing stays over the bouncy beat. Then play Kiss an Angel Good Mornin' for his gentler ballad side, and Bob Wills's New San Antonio Rose for an earlier Texas road song. A compilation of his RCA singles gives the clearest sound."],"eddie-harris-exodus-to-jazz":["Eddie Harris's debut album, a small-group set built around his reading of Ernest Gold's theme from the film Exodus. Harris plays the melody on tenor saxophone in a high, light, almost vocal register, with a long, slow introduction before the rhythm section settles into a gentle, swinging groove. The rest of the record mixes standards and originals in a relaxed hard bop and soul jazz style, with Harris's tone noticeably cooler and more lyrical than the big, gruff tenor sound common in Chicago.","Harris, a Chicago musician who had studied music formally, recorded the album for the Chicago label Vee-Jay, better known for rhythm and blues and later for the first American Beatles releases. Gold's film score for Exodus, about the founding of Israel, was one of the biggest movie themes of 1960, and many musicians covered it. Harris's version crossed over onto the pop charts, an unusual achievement for a jazz instrumental at the start of the 1960s.","Notice how high Harris plays: much of the melody sits in the top register of the tenor, where most saxophonists sound strained, and he makes it sound easy and sweet. That ability came from his unusual technical work on the horn, which later led him to experiment with electric saxophones and with brass mouthpieces on reed instruments.","The album's commercial success made Harris an instant name but also typecast him as a pop-jazz player, a label he spent years resisting. Some jazz critics dismissed the record as lightweight. Later assessments tend to see it as an early example of the soul jazz crossovers that would follow from labels such as Blue Note and Prestige.","Start with the title track and listen to the introduction, then the moment the band enters behind his high, singing tone. Compare it with Cannonball Adderley's soul jazz records and with Harris's own later The Electrifying Eddie Harris to hear how much he changed. Many reissues pair this set with other Vee-Jay recordings."],"eddie-harris-the-in-sound":["Harris's first album for Atlantic, an acoustic quartet record that opens with his own composition Freedom Jazz Dance, a twisting, chromatic melody over a funky, open groove. The rest mixes standards, including a ballad treatment of the film song The Shadow of Your Smile, with originals. The sound is crisp and modern: Harris's light, agile tenor, a busy pianist, a supple bass and a drummer who can move between swing and the even-eighth rhythms that would soon dominate jazz-funk.","Recorded in the mid-1960s, after Harris had moved from Vee-Jay to Atlantic, the record arrived at a moment when jazz was split between the avant-garde of Coltrane and Ornette Coleman and the soul jazz of the organ combos. Atlantic was known for rhythm and blues and for pushing jazz towards a wider audience. Harris sought a middle way: harmonically adventurous writing that still grooved.","The melody of Freedom Jazz Dance moves almost entirely by small steps, yet constantly changes direction, and it fits over a static vamp rather than chord changes: that combination freed soloists to play outside conventional harmony while keeping a dance beat. Miles Davis heard it as a modern framework and recorded it the following year on Miles Smiles, where Wayne Shorter and Herbie Hancock stretched it further.","The album was well received and Freedom Jazz Dance quickly became a jazz standard, one of the few tunes of its era to be widely taught and played. Critics have since credited Harris as a composer of real importance, though his reputation has sometimes been overshadowed by his electric experiments and pop hits. The record is now seen as a link between hard bop and jazz-funk.","Start with Freedom Jazz Dance and listen to how the band keeps a steady groove under the restless melody. Then hear Miles Davis's version on Miles Smiles to compare two approaches. Follow with Herbie Hancock's Maiden Voyage, from the same year, for another vision of modern jazz rooted in a groove."],"stanley-turrentine-blue-hour":["A set of slow blues and ballads by Stanley Turrentine with the piano trio The Three Sounds, led by Gene Harris. Nothing hurries: the tempos are slow and walking, the trio lays down a soft, churchy cushion, and Turrentine's big, breathy tenor tone fills the space with long, unhurried phrases. The record includes standards such as Willow Weep for Me and Since I Fell for You, and it sustains a single late-night mood from first track to last.","Turrentine, from Pittsburgh, had come up in rhythm and blues bands and with Max Roach before signing with Blue Note at the start of the 1960s. The Three Sounds were one of the label's best-selling groups, known for a bluesy, accessible trio sound. Alfred Lion's label was then building a catalogue of soulful hard bop recorded by Rudy Van Gelder, and this session paired two of its most popular voices.","Listen to Turrentine's tone, not just his notes: he lets a little air and grit into the sound, and at the ends of phrases he often adds a vocal-style cry drawn from gospel and rhythm and blues. Gene Harris answers with bluesy piano tremolos and chords that recall church organ, so the whole record has the feel of a quiet Sunday service after a long Saturday night.","The album was popular on release and has remained one of Turrentine's most loved records, frequently cited as a perfect late-night jazz album. Critics praised its restraint and consistency, though some at the time thought it too comfortable compared with the more adventurous hard bop of the day. Its reputation has grown with listeners who value mood and tone over innovation.","Play it late at night and listen to the first long ballad all the way through, following how Turrentine shapes each phrase. Then compare it with Sugar, his later, funkier hit, and with Jimmy Smith's The Sermon! for another bluesy Blue Note session. The Rudy Van Gelder remaster is the best edition."],"stanley-turrentine-up-at-mintons":["A live recording, issued in two volumes, of Turrentine's quintet with Horace Parlan on piano and Grant Green on guitar, playing standards and blues at length in a Harlem club. The performances stretch out, with long solos from Turrentine, Green and Parlan over a relaxed but firmly swinging rhythm section. The sound is warm and immediate, with room noise and applause, and Turrentine's tenor moves from tender ballad playing to driving, bluesy shouts as the night builds.","Minton's Playhouse in Harlem was the club where, in the early 1940s, Thelonious Monk, Charlie Christian, Kenny Clarke and others helped invent bebop in after-hours jam sessions. Recording there in the early 1960s, Blue Note connected its new soul jazz stars to that history. Turrentine and Green were both rising members of the label's roster, and live recordings were a way to capture the energy of club performance.","Listen for Grant Green's guitar: his single-note lines, played with few chords and a horn-like phrasing, owe much to Charlie Christian, who had jammed in the same room twenty years earlier. Parlan, whose right hand was partly disabled by polio, developed a distinctive left-hand-heavy piano style that you can hear in his dark, punchy chords behind the soloists.","The records were well received and have become valued documents of the early 1960s Harlem club scene. Critics single out the chemistry between Turrentine and Green, which led to further collaborations. Some find the long performances loose, but most listeners treat that looseness as the point of a live set.","Choose one of the longer blues tracks and follow how each soloist builds; notice the crowd responding to Turrentine's most bluesy phrases. Then play Grant Green's Idle Moments and Turrentine's Blue Hour to hear both players in the studio. Look for the complete edition that collects both volumes."],"grant-green-green-street":["Green Street is a guitar trio record with nowhere to hide: Grant Green, a bassist and a drummer, no piano or organ to fill the harmony behind him. Across five tracks, two of his own blues-rooted originals, the Monk ballad 'Round Midnight and the standard Alone Together among them, he plays long single-note lines that sound more like a horn player than a guitarist. The sound you remember is that clean, slightly dry tone and the relentless, conversational swing of phrase after phrase.","Green had arrived in New York from St. Louis only a year or so earlier, brought to Blue Note by Alfred Lion after the saxophonist Lou Donaldson heard him play. Lion recorded him constantly, as leader and as sideman on organ and hard-bop dates, and Green Street was among his earliest albums under his own name. In 1961 the jazz guitar was still a minority voice, overshadowed by horns and keyboards, and a trio record put the instrument squarely at the centre.","Listen to how rarely Green plays chords. Where most guitarists in a trio would strum the harmony behind themselves, he lets the bass outline the changes and keeps his own part almost entirely melodic, the way Charlie Parker or a tenor player would. His habit of seizing on a short figure and repeating it, nagging at it until the rhythm section has to respond, is already fully formed here and becomes his signature through the decade.","The record was respected rather than celebrated on release, one of many Blue Note sessions in a crowded year. Its standing rose with the reissue boom and the later revival of Green's whole catalogue, when listeners began to hear the trio dates as the purest statement of his style. Some critics still prefer his warmer quartet and organ albums, finding the trio format austere; admirers answer that the bareness is exactly the point.","Start with the title track and follow only the guitar line, noticing how long Green can keep a phrase going without stopping for breath. Then hear his reading of 'Round Midnight, where the lack of piano makes the ballad sound spacious and lonely. Compare it with Idle Moments for Green in a larger, softer setting, and with a Charlie Christian recording for his lineage."],"johnny-mathis-misty":["Mathis's Misty is a slow ballad sung over a lush bed of strings, a song about being helplessly, dizzily in love. Its melody belongs to the pianist Erroll Garner, who first recorded it as an instrumental; Johnny Burke later added words. Mathis takes it unhurried, almost floating, his light tenor gliding up into a soft head voice on the high phrases and then settling back down. The recognisable gesture is that weightless ascent, sung with no strain at all.","By 1959 Mathis was Columbia's great romantic balladeer, a young singer from San Francisco whose albums sold steadily to an adult audience that rock and roll had not claimed. Misty came from his album Heavenly and was released as a single. It arrived at the tail end of the classic orchestral pop era, when big string arrangements, careful microphone technique and the long-playing record made intimate singing sound grand.","Garner wrote the tune at the piano, and its shape still betrays that origin: the melody leaps in ways a singer would not naturally choose and that a pianist's hand finds easily. Listen for how Mathis smooths those leaps, sliding between registers so the wide intervals feel like a sigh rather than a jump. His control of the very soft high notes is the technical marvel hiding inside the easy-listening surface.","The record became one of Mathis's signature songs and fixed Misty in the popular repertoire, helping turn a jazz pianist's instrumental into a standard that Sarah Vaughan, Ella Fitzgerald and countless lounge singers took up. Critics who dismissed Mathis as soft-centred missed the vocal craft; later reassessments of traditional pop have placed him among the most technically refined singers of his generation, even if the arrangements now sound period.","Listen once just for the high phrases and how quietly Mathis reaches them; then listen for the strings swelling around him at the climax. Afterwards play Erroll Garner's own instrumental recording to hear where the melody came from, and Sarah Vaughan's version for a jazz singer's darker approach. Pair it with Chances Are for the full Mathis ballad style."],"ramsey-lewis-wade-in-the-water":["Ramsey Lewis takes an old African American spiritual, a song about crossing water toward deliverance, and turns it into a strutting instrumental. The piano states the minor-key melody over a heavy, swaying backbeat; horns punch out answering phrases; the band builds the tune through repetition rather than improvised invention. The sound you recognise at once is that churchy piano riff locked to a groove that feels closer to a soul record than to a jazz club.","After the success of The In Crowd in 1965, Lewis's original trio broke up, and he formed a new one with bassist Cleveland Eaton and drummer Maurice White, who would later found Earth, Wind & Fire. Recording for Chess's jazz imprint Cadet in Chicago, Lewis was now chasing the crossover audience that his hit had uncovered, and Wade in the Water was released as a single as well as the title track of an album.","The spiritual itself is often linked, in later retellings, to coded instructions on the Underground Railroad, though historians treat that story with caution. Musically, notice how little the piano departs from the melody: the drama comes from dynamics and layering, the horns arriving and receding, the drummer leaning ever harder on the backbeat. That approach, groove first and solo second, points straight at the funk and soul-jazz records of the following decade.","The single was a substantial hit and kept Lewis on pop radio, but jazz critics of the time were often dismissive, hearing commercial simplification rather than art. That verdict has softened. Hip-hop producers and soul-jazz collectors embraced Lewis's Cadet records, and his gospel-rooted crossover sound is now read as part of a living Black musical continuum rather than a betrayal of jazz.","Put it on loud and listen to the drums first, the way the backbeat sits slightly heavy, then follow how the horns frame the piano. Compare a traditional sung version of the spiritual by a gospel choir to hear what Lewis kept and what he changed. Follow with Sun Goddess, which Maurice White produced years later, to hear where the groove led."],"eddie-palmieri-vamonos-pal-monte":["Vámonos pa'l monte is New York salsa at its most adventurous. The title track, a song about escaping the city for the countryside, runs past the length of a radio single into an extended montuno where Palmieri's piano breaks into dissonant, hammered clusters and an organ solo, unusual in the idiom, swirls over the percussion. Ismael Quintana sings lead; brass and trombones punch in and out. What you remember is the tension between a dance groove that never stops and a piano that keeps threatening to tear it apart.","By 1971 Palmieri had spent a decade leading bands in the Latin dance halls of New York, first with his trombone-heavy La Perfecta, and was pushing the music toward jazz, soul and political consciousness. Salsa was becoming a commercial identity for Puerto Rican and Cuban communities in the city, while Palmieri himself was listening to McCoy Tyner and modal jazz. The album came out on Tico during the same restless period as his Harlem River Drive project.","The organ on the title track is played by Eddie's older brother Charlie Palmieri, himself a celebrated bandleader; the two rarely recorded together in this way, and the solo is a family duel as much as an arrangement. Listen too for how the piano voicings in the montuno owe more to modal jazz than to Cuban tradition, piling up fourths and clashing notes over a perfectly traditional clave.","The album was admired by musicians and dancers alike and is often cited as one of Palmieri's finest records, though his experiments divided purists who wanted the classic conjunto sound. Over time it has come to represent the moment salsa absorbed jazz harmony without losing the dance floor, and the title track has become a standard in Latin bands' repertoire.","Start with the title track and wait for the long instrumental stretch: first the organ, then the piano, then the brass hitting back. Keep track of the clave pattern underneath, which never wavers whatever the soloists do. Compare it with Azúcar pa' ti for Palmieri's earlier, more classic sound, and with Willie Colón's El Malo for the rougher street side of the same scene."],"glen-campbell-by-the-time-i-get-to-phoenix":["A man leaves a woman before dawn and imagines, town by town, the moments she will realise he has gone. Campbell sings it in a calm, unhurried voice over a gently picked acoustic guitar, with strings slowly gathering behind him. There is no chorus in the usual sense: the song moves like a journey, each section a new place and a new hour. The memorable gesture is how understated the delivery stays, even as the orchestra swells.","The song was written by the young Jimmy Webb and had already been recorded by Johnny Rivers before Campbell cut it for Capitol in Los Angeles, with Al De Lory producing. Campbell was then a top session guitarist turning himself into a solo star after the success of Gentle on My Mind. The record launched the partnership with Webb that produced Wichita Lineman and Galveston and helped define the polished, orchestral country-pop of the late sixties.","The geography is the structure: Phoenix, Albuquerque and Oklahoma mark the verses, and each one advances the clock as well as the map, so the song is really about time zones of grief. Webb avoids a hook that repeats. Listen to how the melody keeps rising to a slightly different peak in each section, so the song never quite resolves, just as the man never quite turns back.","The record was a major hit on both pop and country lists and won Campbell Grammy awards, establishing him as a crossover star. Nashville traditionalists sometimes saw the string-laden sound as a dilution of country; pop listeners heard sophistication. Webb's songwriting has since been reassessed as among the finest of its era, and Frank Sinatra's description of the song as one of the greatest torch songs is often repeated.","Follow the place names and notice how each verse moves the clock forward. Listen for the moment the strings enter fully and how Campbell refuses to push his voice to match them. Then hear Wichita Lineman, the next Webb collaboration, which compresses the same loneliness into an even stranger arrangement, and compare a classic honky-tonk cheating song for the opposite emotional temperature."],"harold-budd-the-moon-and-the-melodies":["The Moon and the Melodies is a collaboration between Harold Budd and the three members of Cocteau Twins: Elizabeth Fraser, Robin Guthrie and Simon Raymonde. Budd's slow, softly pedalled piano drifts through clouds of chorused, reverberant guitar; on several tracks Fraser's wordless or near-wordless voice floats above. Instrumental pieces such as Memory Gongs sit beside songs like Sea, Swallow Me. The recognisable sound is shimmer: everything blurred at the edges, nothing hard-attacked.","By the mid-eighties Budd had made his name through ambient collaborations with Brian Eno, while Cocteau Twins had become the defining band of the British label 4AD, admired for their dense, dreamlike textures. 4AD released the album in 1986, credited to all four musicians rather than to the band's name. The meeting brought a Californian minimalist composer into the world of British indie rock and its new studio-effects vocabulary.","Notice who leads. On the instrumental tracks Budd's piano carries the melody and the guitars are weather around it; on the vocal tracks the balance flips and he becomes a quiet accompanist. That shifting centre is what makes the record sound like a true collaboration. Listen also for the way Guthrie's guitar effects imitate the sustain of Budd's pedal, so the two instruments sometimes become hard to tell apart.","Reviews were warm but some fans of the band found it too slow and too sparse, and Cocteau Twins themselves have spoken ambivalently about it. Over time it has been rediscovered as a bridge between ambient music and dream pop, prized by listeners who came to Budd through the band. Budd went on to work further with Guthrie for decades, which suggests how much the meeting mattered to him.","Begin with Sea, Swallow Me to hear Fraser and Budd together, then go to an instrumental track and focus only on the piano. Play it quietly and let it sit in the room. Compare it with The Pearl for Budd with Eno, and with Treasure for Cocteau Twins at full density; this record sits exactly between them."],"hermeto-pascoal-festa-dos-deuses":["Festa dos Deuses gathers pieces by Hermeto Pascoal and his longtime group in which recorded speech becomes melody. A spoken voice is played back and the band doubles it exactly, note for note, harmonising the natural rise and fall of the sentence as if it were a written tune. Around these experiments sit the group's dense, fast, Brazilian-rooted ensemble pieces. The unforgettable effect is hearing an ordinary voice suddenly become music.","Pascoal, born in rural Alagoas and largely self-taught, had become famous in Brazil and abroad as a multi-instrumentalist who could make music from kettles, animals and anything at hand. Through the eighties and early nineties he developed what he called the som da aura, the sound of the aura: the idea that every spoken voice already contains a melody waiting to be found. This album is one of the fullest presentations of that idea.","One of the voices treated in this way is, by most accounts, a public speech by the then president, Fernando Collor, whose government was mired in scandal. Hermeto's harmonisation lends the politician's cadences a curious, almost comic seriousness, and Brazilian listeners heard a sly commentary in it. Listen to how precisely the instruments track the speech's pitch, including the little upward hooks at the ends of phrases.","The speech-melody technique fascinated musicians and composers, who compared it with Steve Reich's speech-based pieces and with the work of Leoš Janáček, who had transcribed speech melodies a century earlier. Some listeners found the idea more interesting than the result; others hear it as among Hermeto's most original contributions. The album remains a cult object, discussed more often than it is heard.","Start with one of the speech pieces and listen twice: once for the voice alone, once for how the band shadows it. Then turn to the full ensemble tracks for the rhythmic drive of Brazilian northeastern music played at jazz speed. Compare it with Slaves Mass for Hermeto's earlier, wilder sound, and with Steve Reich's Different Trains for a parallel idea."],"baden-powell-berimbau":["Berimbau, written by Baden Powell with the poet Vinicius de Moraes, is a song about the hard lessons of life, named after the single-stringed bow used in Bahian capoeira. The guitar imitates the berimbau itself: a repeated, twanging figure on low strings that sets a hypnotic pulse, over which a minor-mode melody rises and falls almost like a chant. The recognisable moment is that opening guitar ostinato, instantly percussive and unlike the gentle strum of bossa nova.","In the early sixties Baden Powell, a virtuoso guitarist steeped in choro and classical technique, began working with Vinicius, already famous as Jobim's lyricist. Both were drawn to the Afro-Brazilian music of Bahia, its candomblé chants and capoeira rhythms, which bossa nova's Rio elegance had largely left aside. Berimbau was one of the first fruits of that interest and foreshadowed the Afro-Sambas album that followed.","The real berimbau can produce only two or three pitches, and Baden's guitar figure respects that limit, circling a tiny set of notes so that the instrument's percussive buzz is suggested rather than played. Listen to how the melody also stays close to a narrow, chant-like range before breaking out. The song is a translation of an instrument into a guitar technique, not just a song named after one.","Berimbau became one of the most performed songs in Brazilian music, recorded by Astrud Gilberto, Nara Leão, Sérgio Mendes and many jazz musicians. It is regularly cited as a turning point where bossa nova composers opened toward Afro-Brazilian sources, a move later developed by the Tropicália and MPB generations. Some purists questioned the middle-class appropriation of Bahian religion; others see a sincere homage.","Listen first for the guitar figure alone and try tapping its pulse; then notice the melody gradually widening. Seek out Baden's own instrumental performance to hear the guitar fully in charge, and compare it with a recording of a real capoeira berimbau. Follow with Os Afro-Sambas, where the idea becomes a whole album."],"baden-powell-tristeza-on-guitar":["Tristeza on Guitar is an instrumental album in which Baden Powell's guitar carries everything: sambas played at speed, slow lyrical pieces, and Afro-Brazilian material drawn from his songwriting with Vinicius de Moraes. A small rhythm section supports him, but the guitar is melody, harmony and percussion at once. The title track, a carnival samba, opens with a rush of strummed and plucked figures; what you remember is the sheer physical attack of his right hand.","The album was made for the German label SABA, later known as MPS, under the producer and critic Joachim-Ernst Berendt, who championed Brazilian music in Europe. Baden spent long periods in Europe from the mid-sixties, and his records there reached an audience that admired him as a guitarist first and a songwriter second. It caught him at the peak of the energy that also produced Os Afro-Sambas.","Listen to the thumb. Beneath the fast melodies, Baden's thumb keeps a steady bass line on the low strings, like the surdo drum of a samba school, while the fingers play everything else. On the slower tracks, notice his classical touches: rolled chords, tremolo and harmonics that recall his training in the concert guitar repertoire, so that the samba and the salon sit in the same pair of hands.","The record was well received by European jazz critics and became one of Baden's best-loved albums abroad, regularly reissued. Guitarists continue to study it as a model of Brazilian technique. Some Brazilian commentators prefer his song albums, where the collaboration with Vinicius gives the music words and context; others regard the instrumental albums as his purest legacy.","Start with the title track and try to hear how many separate parts the guitar is playing at once. Then choose one of the slow pieces and listen for the classical colours. Compare the album with João Gilberto's quieter guitar for the opposite approach to the same tradition, and with Paco de Lucía for another virtuoso folk guitarist of the period."],"magic-sam-all-your-love":["All Your Love was Magic Sam's first single, a song about longing for a lover's whole devotion. It opens with a quivering, tremolo-drenched guitar figure, and then Sam's voice arrives: high, plaintive, slipping easily into falsetto. The band keeps a medium, rolling groove, with a section that briefly shifts into a faster shuffle before falling back. The recognisable sound is that shimmering, wavering guitar, as distinctive as a signature.","Sam Maghett, born in Mississippi, came to Chicago's West Side as a teenager. He recorded the song for Cobra, a small label run by Eli Toscano that was cultivating a younger, more intense style of blues alongside Otis Rush and Buddy Guy. These players, with more gospel and rhythm and blues in their sound than the Delta-rooted South Side generation, became known as the West Side school.","The tremolo effect came from an amplifier that pulsed the volume of the guitar, and Sam used it not as decoration but as part of the phrasing, letting held notes quiver like a singer's vibrato. Listen also for the change of tempo mid-song, unusual in a blues single of the period and a hint of the rhythmic restlessness that later shaped Sam's live performances.","The single was a regional success and made Sam's name in Chicago, though a stint in the army and personal setbacks slowed his career soon after. He re-recorded the song on West Side Soul in 1967. Today the Cobra original is regarded as a founding record of the West Side sound, and blues guitarists cite its tremolo tone as a defining voice of the era.","Listen to the first bars for the tremolo guitar alone, then follow how Sam's voice rises into falsetto. Notice the shift in tempo and how the band handles it. Compare the 1957 single with the version on West Side Soul to hear how he matured, and with Otis Rush's Double Trouble for the other great Cobra voice of those years."],"bill-withers-live-at-carnegie-hall":["A double live album that catches Bill Withers at the height of his brief, astonishing first run. A small, tight band plays almost nothing that is not needed: a dry snare, a round bass, a little piano, an electric guitar that answers the voice rather than decorating it. The songs from Just As I Am and Still Bill stretch out here, some of them doubling in length, and between them Withers talks to the hall in a slow, wry drawl. The sound you remember is space, and a voice filling it.","Withers had been a Navy aircraft mechanic and then a factory worker making toilet seats for airliners before Sussex Records released his first album in 1971, when he was already in his thirties. Within two years he had two enormous hits and was playing Carnegie Hall, the most formal room in American music, to a crowd that clearly knew every word. The concert was recorded in New York in the autumn of 1972 and issued the following year, while the soul of the period was growing lusher, more orchestral and more produced.","Listen to the spoken passages as part of the music. Before several songs Withers tells the story behind them, about his grandmother, about the town in West Virginia where he grew up, and the stories reset the tempo of the evening. The long vamp near the end, where the band rides a single groove and he improvises on top, shows how much of his writing grew out of repetition rather than verse and chorus: he is as close to a blues singer here as anywhere on record.","It sold well and was respected at the time, but it was long treated as a stopgap between studio albums. Its standing has risen steadily, and many listeners now rank it alongside Donny Hathaway's live album as the great soul concert record of the early seventies. The debate, if there is one, is whether it is better than the studio originals; a fair answer is that it shows a different man, looser, funnier and more dangerous.","Start with the long performance of Harlem, which builds from a quiet strum to a full-band stampede, and notice how the drummer holds back until the very end. Then go to Grandma's Hands and hear how the hall laughs and cheers along. Compare it with the studio version of Still Bill to hear what the stage added. Any decent remaster will do; the original recording is clean and close."],"freddie-hubbard-hub-tones":["A Blue Note quintet date that shows Freddie Hubbard, then twenty-four, as the brightest trumpet voice of his generation. James Spaulding plays alto saxophone and flute, Herbie Hancock is on piano, Reggie Workman on bass and Clifford Jarvis on drums. Five tracks move from a fast, biting reading of the ballad You're My Everything through the title tune's quick, angular blues to the slow, grieving Lament for Booker. The sound to recognise is Hubbard's tone: fat in the low register, ringing at the top, and never short of breath.","Hubbard had arrived in New York from Indianapolis in 1958 and within four years had recorded with Ornette Coleman on Free Jazz, with Eric Dolphy and Oliver Nelson, and had joined Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers. Blue Note gave him a run of leader dates alongside that work, and Hub-Tones came at the peak of the label's hard bop years, cut at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Englewood Cliffs. Hancock was a newcomer too, months away from his own first album for the label.","Lament for Booker is a memorial to the trumpeter Booker Little, a friend and rival who died in 1961 at twenty-three. Hubbard plays it with a restraint that he rarely chose elsewhere, and Spaulding's flute shadows him like a second voice. Listen also to Hancock's comping on the title track: the chords arrive a fraction early or late, pushing against the soloist, a habit he would refine with Miles Davis a year later.","Reviewers at the time heard a technically dazzling player still finding his own manner, and some found his speed more impressive than moving. Later judgement has been kinder: Hub-Tones is now usually counted among his best Blue Note albums, often paired with Ready for Freddie and Open Sesame. The perennial argument about Hubbard, that he could play anything and therefore sometimes played everything, is answered here by the ballad.","Begin with Lament for Booker for the tone and the feeling, then go back to the title track to hear what that tone does at speed. Listen for the moment Spaulding switches to flute and the whole texture lightens. Afterwards put on Hubbard's playing on Hancock's Maiden Voyage a few years later, and hear how much more space he learned to leave. The Rudy Van Gelder remaster is the one to find."],"gordon-lightfoot-canadian-railroad-trilogy":["A seven-minute ballad about the building of the Canadian Pacific Railway, from the dream of a line across the continent to the men who blasted it through rock and forest. It is built in three parts, as the title says: a slow, wide-open opening, a fast, driving middle section that imitates the rhythm of labour and of a train gathering speed, and a return to the opening pace. Lightfoot's twelve-string guitar carries it alone for long stretches, and the switch in tempo is the moment everyone remembers.","The song was commissioned by the Canadian Broadcasting Corporation for a television special marking Canada's centennial, and was first heard on that broadcast at the very start of 1967. Lightfoot was by then a successful songwriter in Toronto's Yorkville folk scene, with songs recorded by Ian and Sylvia, Peter, Paul and Mary and Marty Robbins. He recorded it for his second album, The Way I Feel, released the same year, as the country was celebrating itself with new confidence.","Notice what the song refuses to do. A centennial commission could easily have been a hymn of triumph, but the middle section gives its voice to the navvies, many of them immigrants, who did the work and often died doing it, and the closing section looks back with as much unease as pride. The tempo structure is the argument: the grand vision is slow and abstract, while the human cost arrives at a sprint.","It was admired from the start and has become something close to a national song in English Canada, taught in schools and played on anniversaries. Some later listeners have pointed out that it says little about the Indigenous peoples whose land the railway crossed, and Lightfoot himself spoke in later life about revisiting parts of his early writing. It remains his most ambitious single composition.","Listen through once for the shape: wait for the guitar to suddenly double its pace and feel how the song turns into a work song. Then listen again for the guitar alone, which holds a steady pulse under a melody that keeps stretching its lines. Compare it with Woody Guthrie's Columbia River songs, a similar commission across the border. The 1967 studio recording is the one; a later re-recording exists too."],"shirley-caesar-dont-drive-your-mama-away":["A gospel sermonette: half sung, half preached. It tells the story of a mother who sacrifices everything for her sons, and of one son who later turns her out of his house at his wife's urging. Shirley Caesar delivers most of it as a spoken narrative over a slow, rolling organ and rhythm section, the backing singers humming beneath her, and then rises into song as the story reaches its turn. The texture you recognise it by is the speaking voice over a held church chord, gathering pitch until it breaks into melody.","Caesar had come up as a teenage evangelist in Durham, North Carolina, and spent most of the 1960s singing with Albertina Walker's Caravans, the most important women's group in gospel. Around 1966 she left to form her own group, the Caesar Singers, and quickly built a solo career on the sermonette, a form that brought the storytelling of the Black pulpit into the song. This record, from that early solo period, became one of her signature performances on church radio and on the road.","The sermonette is a musical form as much as a preacher's habit. Listen to how the band follows her speech: the organist changes chord only when her sentence lands, and the drummer adds weight as her phrases shorten. The story is told in the cadence of a Sunday sermon, rising and repeating, so that by the time she sings the congregation has been brought to the edge already.","It was enormously popular in Black churches and helped establish Caesar as gospel's leading storyteller, a reputation later sealed by No Charge. Gospel critics have sometimes been cool on sermonettes, which can trade music for melodrama, but Caesar's admirers point to her timing as the musical skill in question. The record is still requested at her concerts and imitated by younger singers.","Do not wait for the song; the speaking is the performance. Follow the story first, then listen a second time only to the organ and notice how it rises with her. Compare it with No Charge, which uses the same method more compactly, and with Mahalia Jackson for the older, purely sung tradition. Live recordings from her concerts show how a room responds in real time."],"larry-young-into-somethin":["Larry Young's first album for Blue Note, a quartet with Sam Rivers on tenor saxophone, Grant Green on guitar and Elvin Jones on drums, and no bassist: Young's left hand and the organ pedals carry the bottom. Five tracks run from the dark, insistent Tyrone and the Spanish-tinged Plaza de Toros to the lilting Paris Eyes. What you hear is an organ record without the usual grease: the chords are open and suspended, closer to McCoy Tyner's piano than to Jimmy Smith, and Jones keeps the rhythm churning.","Young came from Newark, New Jersey, and had made soul-jazz records for Prestige in the early 1960s in the shadow of Jimmy Smith, the Hammond's reigning star. Grant Green, already established at Blue Note, effectively brought him to the label, and this session was recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in 1964. Elvin Jones was then the drummer in John Coltrane's quartet, and Coltrane's modal sound was exactly what Young was trying to carry over to the organ.","The bassless lineup is the key. Without a bass player fixing the root of each chord, Young can let his harmonies hang ambiguously, and you hear him sliding whole stacks of notes in parallel, a technique taken from Tyner and from Coltrane's modal tunes. Listen to how Sam Rivers, a player who would soon move into free jazz, pushes his solos out further than Green does, as if testing how far the organ will follow.","The album drew less attention on release than Young's later Unity, and was long known mainly to organ specialists. Reappraisal has made it a favourite: many now hear it as the moment the Hammond left the chitlin circuit and entered the jazz avant-garde. Some still prefer Green's more rooted playing on the date to Young's harmonic adventures, but the two together are much of its charm.","Start with Tyrone and listen for the organ pedals in place of a bass, then notice Elvin Jones's cymbals filling the space. Compare it with Jimmy Smith's The Sermon to hear how differently the same instrument can sound, and then move on to Young's Unity, two years later, where the experiment is completed. The Blue Note Van Gelder edition has the clearest organ sound."],"larry-young-of-love-and-peace":["Young's most turbulent Blue Note album: a septet with Eddie Gale on trumpet, James Spaulding on alto saxophone, a second saxophonist, a guitarist and two drummers. Four long tracks swing between dense collective improvisation and the organ's huge sustained chords. Pavanne is a reworking of a light orchestral piece by Morton Gould; the title track opens into near-free playing; Seven Steps to Heaven, the tune associated with Miles Davis, is taken at a pelting pace. The sound is thick, loud and restless.","Recorded in 1966, months after Unity, the album shows Young moving towards the free jazz of the mid-sixties, the music of Coltrane's Ascension period and of the Newark and New York loft scenes. Eddie Gale had played with Cecil Taylor and Sun Ra. The organ was rarely heard in this company: it was associated with bars, dancing and soul jazz, and Young was making an argument that it could hold its own in the avant-garde.","The use of a Morton Gould pavane, a piece from the American light-classical repertoire of the 1930s, is a deliberate jolt: Young treats a polite orchestral melody as raw material for modal improvisation, much as Coltrane had treated My Favorite Things. Listen to the two drummers, who sometimes play different pulses at once, so that the organ seems to float between them.","It sold poorly and puzzled many listeners who had liked Unity's poise, and it was out of print for long periods. Critics have since divided between those who find it diffuse and those who hear it as the missing link between Young's Blue Note work and his explosive playing in Tony Williams's Lifetime a few years later. Its reputation has grown steadily with collectors of spiritual and free jazz.","Begin with Pavanne, where the theme is easiest to follow, and listen for the moment the organ swells behind the horns. Then try the title track at volume and let the two drums wash over you. Follow it with Tony Williams's Emergency! to hear where this sound went next, and with Unity for the calmer version of the same musician."],"lonnie-liston-smith-astral-traveling":["The first album by Lonnie Liston Smith and the Cosmic Echoes, released on Flying Dutchman. It sits between the spiritual jazz Smith had been playing as a sideman and the smoother jazz-funk he would soon make his name with. Long, slow tracks are built on a shimmering Fender Rhodes electric piano, hand percussion and a patient bass line, with saxophone floating over the top. The title piece is the one you recognise: rippling keyboard figures repeated like breathing, and a melody that seems to hang in the air.","Smith had worked with Art Blakey, Roland Kirk and Gato Barbieri, and spent the late 1960s and early 1970s with Pharoah Sanders, whose ecstatic albums for Impulse! defined spiritual jazz, before a spell in Miles Davis's electric band around On the Corner. Bob Thiele's Flying Dutchman label, set up after he left Impulse!, gave him his own group. The electric piano was newly everywhere in jazz, and Smith was one of its most careful listeners.","Smith wrote Astral Traveling for Pharoah Sanders, and it first appeared on Sanders's 1971 album Thembi with Smith himself introducing it on electric piano. Here he reclaims his own tune and slows it further, letting the electric piano's natural tremolo and decay do much of the musical work. That small change, from a horn leader's record to a keyboard player's, is where his later sound begins.","It was received quietly and overshadowed by the commercial success of Expansions two years later. Over time it has become a cult record, prized by DJs and by listeners rediscovering spiritual jazz, and it is often placed alongside Alice Coltrane's albums of the same years. Purists still debate whether Smith's softening of the Sanders sound was a refinement or a dilution.","Play the title track first, ideally at low volume in a dark room, and listen to the Rhodes's wobble and fade. Then play Sanders's version on Thembi to hear the same melody in a wilder setting, and finish with Expansions to hear where Smith took it. A clean vinyl reissue or good remaster keeps the electric piano's warmth."],"lonnie-liston-smith-cosmic-funk":["The second Cosmic Echoes album, and the record on which Lonnie Liston Smith found the formula he is remembered for. The title track sets a funky bass line and crisp drums under layers of electric piano and early synthesizer, with saxophone solos and a gently chanted vocal. Elsewhere there are slow, hymn-like pieces and softer ballads sung by his brother Donald Smith. The texture is warm and cushioned, with every instrument sitting inside the sound rather than on top of it.","By 1974 jazz was being remade by electric instruments and funk rhythms, from Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters to Donald Byrd's work with the Mizell brothers. Smith, recording again for Bob Thiele's Flying Dutchman label, kept the spiritual vocabulary of his years with Pharoah Sanders, its devotional titles and modal harmony, but put it over grooves that people could dance to. Synthesizers were becoming affordable and portable, and he used them sparingly, as colour.","Listen for how little the harmony moves. Like a Sanders piece, many tracks sit on one or two chords for minutes, and the motion comes from rhythm and from the slowly shifting keyboard layers. The funk is real, but underneath it is the same meditative stillness as Astral Traveling, which is why the record works both on a dance floor and as music for listening alone.","It sold better than its predecessor and set up Expansions the following year. Jazz critics of the time were often impatient with records like this, seeing them as a sell-out of the spiritual movement; later generations of DJs, hip-hop producers and jazz-funk fans have embraced it. The arguments about commercial jazz in the seventies are, with hindsight, gentler than they once were.","Start with the title track and follow the bass line, which never changes and never tires. Then listen for the synthesizer entering as a thin, high glow above the electric piano. Pair it with Hancock's Head Hunters for a harder-edged take on the same idea, and with Expansions for the polished sequel."],"roy-ayers-searching":["A mid-tempo song from Roy Ayers Ubiquity's album Vibrations, about the search for something true in a confusing world. Over a rolling bass line and soft electric piano, unison voices sing a short, almost chant-like refrain, and Ayers's vibraphone weaves around them, its metal bars ringing and the motor giving each note a gentle pulse. The groove is relaxed but insistent, and the vibraphone solo near the end is the stretch most listeners remember.","Ayers had been a respected hard bop and soul-jazz vibraphonist, best known for his years with the flautist Herbie Mann, before forming Ubiquity in the early 1970s and moving towards funk, soul and what would be called jazz-funk. Vibrations followed Everybody Loves the Sunshine on Polydor. The mid-seventies were a time when jazz musicians were finding big new audiences in soul radio and in the clubs, and Ayers was one of the most successful at it.","The vibraphone carries the song's mood as much as the voices do. Listen to how Ayers uses the instrument's motor-driven vibrato, the slight wavering in each sustained note, to make the chords sound hazy and unresolved, which suits a song about searching. The refrain hardly changes, and the slow buildup of percussion underneath supplies all the momentum.","It was never a big chart record in the United States, but it became a staple in British jazz-funk and rare-groove clubs and has been sampled and covered many times since. Critics now tend to hear Vibrations as one of Ayers's most consistent albums, and this track as the reason why. It remains one of the touchstones of the acid jazz movement of the late 1980s.","Listen first for the vibraphone, then for the bass line under it, which never stops moving. Notice how the voices sing together rather than as lead and backing. Then put on Everybody Loves the Sunshine to hear the same band at a slower, dreamier tempo, and Lonnie Liston Smith's Expansions for its closest relation."],"roy-ayers-running-away":["The opening track of Roy Ayers Ubiquity's album Lifeline: a fast, bubbling dance number about leaving a love that has gone wrong. A bright bass line and busy hand percussion drive it, the electric piano and vibraphone answer each other in short phrases, and male and female voices trade the refrain in call-and-response. It runs for seven minutes, long enough to build in waves, and the vibraphone breaks are pure exuberance.","By 1977 disco was reshaping American dance music, and Ayers, who had already crossed from jazz into soul and funk, moved with it. Lifeline was released on Polydor and captured Ubiquity at its most club-ready. Ayers was also beginning to work in Nigeria, where he would tour with Fela Kuti a couple of years later, and the percussion-heavy rhythms of his late-seventies records show that wider world.","Listen to the hand percussion: it is not a disco four-to-the-floor pattern but a looser, cross-rhythmic weave closer to Latin and West African dance music, which keeps the track moving even when the voices drop out. The extended instrumental breaks, built for DJs, are where the vibraphone and electric piano play off each other most freely, and where the jazz musicians underneath become audible.","It was an enduring favourite in the clubs, especially in New York and later in Britain, and its long breaks have been sampled by hip-hop and house producers. Critics who once lamented Ayers's move from jazz towards disco now often single out this record as proof that the move cost him nothing in musicianship. It still fills dance floors at soul nights.","Play it loud and let the opening percussion settle before the voices arrive. Listen for the moment the vibraphone takes over and the singers step back. Compare it with Searching, a year earlier, to hear the band speeding up, and with Fela Kuti's records of the same years to hear where some of the rhythm came from."],"vicente-fernandez-mujeres-divinas":["A ranchera written by Martín Urieta, in which a man drinking with friends talks about the women who have caused him grief and concludes that he cannot do without them. Vicente Fernández sings it over a full mariachi, violins in lush unison, trumpets punctuating each line, guitar, vihuela and the deep guitarrón keeping the swaying rhythm. His voice moves from conversational near-speech into enormous held notes, and the song's slow, rolling pulse invites a whole bar or stadium to sing along.","By the late 1980s Fernández was the undisputed king of ranchera, filling arenas across Mexico and the United States and starring in films that kept the charro image alive long after the golden age of Mexican cinema. Urieta, a songwriter from Guerrero, supplied him with several of his biggest songs in this decade. The song arrived when the mariachi tradition was being kept alive mainly by Fernández and a few others as pop and banda grew around it.","Listen to how Fernández shapes the tempo. The mariachi plays a steady slow pulse, but he lingers behind it, stretching certain words and catching up in the next phrase, which gives the song its conversational, slightly rueful tone. The trumpets answer him almost like friends at the table responding to a story, and the closing note is held long past comfort, which is part of the ritual.","It became one of the most beloved songs in his repertoire and a standard in cantinas and at family gatherings across Mexico and the Mexican diaspora. Some listeners hear its view of women as dated; many others hear the song as a self-mocking portrait of a man undone by his own feelings. Its popularity has only grown since his death in 2021.","Listen first for the conversation between voice and trumpets, then for the guitarrón's deep plucked notes holding the dance rhythm. Notice how Fernández almost speaks the opening before swelling into full voice. Compare it with Volver, volver for his most famous anthem and with José Alfredo Jiménez's El Rey for the older tradition. Live recordings show the audience singing every word."],"chucho-valdes-bele-bele-en-la-habana":["Chucho Valdés's first album for Blue Note, a small-group record recorded in Havana that puts his piano front and centre after decades leading the big electric band Irakere. The music moves between Cuban dance forms, son, danzón and the drumming of Afro-Cuban religious tradition, and jazz harmony. Valdés plays with enormous hands and a wide dynamic range, from delicate, almost classical passages to crashing octave runs over the percussion. The sound to recognise is two traditions at once: a jazz trio and a Cuban rumba.","Valdés had founded Irakere in the early 1970s and for twenty-five years had been the most important figure in Cuban jazz, largely inside Cuba because of the American embargo. In the late 1990s, with the success of the Buena Vista Social Club and a wider international curiosity about Cuban music, he began recording for major Western labels. He was also the son of Bebo Valdés, the great pianist and bandleader who had left Cuba in 1960.","Behind the jazz surfaces are the rhythms of Santería and Abakuá ceremony, and Valdés often lets the piano become a drum, repeating a short cell against the percussion until the two lock together. Listen for the moments where his touch turns suddenly light and Romantic, a reminder of his conservatory training and of Cuban pianists such as Ernesto Lecuona.","It was widely praised outside Cuba as an introduction to Valdés the pianist rather than Valdés the bandleader, and it began the series of acclaimed records that brought him international awards in the 2000s. Some admirers of Irakere missed the electric band's energy, but most critics heard a major pianist finally given space. It remains a standard entry point to his work.","Listen first to how the piano and the percussion talk to each other: sometimes the piano leads, sometimes it becomes another drum. Then focus on the slow passages for his classical touch. Compare it with Irakere's records for his big-band side, and with Lecuona's piano music for the older Cuban tradition he draws on."],"bobbie-gentry-fancy":["Fancy is a five-minute story-song told in the first person by a woman looking back on how she escaped a shack in Louisiana. Gentry sings it low and level, almost conversational, over a groove that keeps tightening: a plucked guitar figure, a bass that walks steadily, then brass stabs and strings that swell as the narrator's fortunes rise. There is no chorus in the usual sense, just the title name returning like a refrain. The sound you remember is that relentless, slightly menacing build.","By 1969 Gentry was two years past the enormous success of Ode to Billie Joe and determined not to be a one-song writer. She took this material to FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, where producer Rick Hall and the studio's house players brought a Southern soul punch to what is at heart a country narrative. The song tells of a girl whose desperate mother dresses her up and sends her out to survive by attaching herself to wealthy men. It became the title track of her 1970 album.","Listen to how little the melody changes while the arrangement transforms around it. The horns arrive almost exactly as the narrator's material world improves, so the orchestration itself tells the story of social climbing. Gentry also refuses to give the character a moment of shame: the final section is delivered with more confidence, not less, and the song ends on defiance rather than redemption. That refusal of the expected moral is the real twist, and it is entirely in the singing.","On release the record was a moderate hit and some radio programmers were uneasy with its subject. Its reputation grew steadily, and Reba McEntire's 1990 cover made it a country standard for a new generation and a staple of drag and queer performance. Critics now read it as one of the sharpest feminist songs in country music, a portrait of poverty and survival that Gentry herself defended in interviews as being about women's self-determination.","Hear Gentry's original before any cover, and pay attention to the first thirty seconds: the guitar pattern sets a nervous pulse that never relaxes. Follow the arrangement as it thickens, noting when the strings first appear. Then compare the eerie stillness of Ode to Billie Joe, another Gentry narrative told without judgment, and Dolly Parton's Coat of Many Colors for a gentler account of growing up poor."],"taj-mahal-the-natchl-blues":["Taj Mahal's second album moves easily between rural acoustic blues and Memphis-style soul. Songs such as Good Morning Miss Brown and Corinna sit on loping, country-blues rhythms with harmonica and fingerpicked guitar, while his reading of William Bell's You Don't Miss Your Water slows into a full soul ballad. His voice is the centre: grainy, playful, capable of a growl or a croon. The sound is relaxed and warm rather than loud, a band playing old forms with obvious affection.","Released by Columbia late in 1968, the record followed his self-titled debut from earlier the same year. Taj Mahal, born Henry St. Claire Fredericks in New York and raised in Springfield, Massachusetts, had studied agriculture and immersed himself in the music of the rural South before settling in Los Angeles. There he worked with the guitarist Jesse Ed Davis. At a moment when white rock bands were turning the blues into amplified spectacle, he went back to its roots in dance music and storytelling.","Notice how deliberately the record treats blues and soul as one family. The Stax ballad is not a detour; Taj Mahal sings it with the same phrasing he uses on the country blues, showing the line that runs from Mississippi porches to Memphis studios. Listen too to the guitars, where Davis's slide and the acoustic picking trade small figures behind the voice instead of taking long solos, a restraint unusual for 1968.","The album sold modestly but was admired by musicians, and it established Taj Mahal as one of the first artists of his generation to treat the older blues as a living, flexible language rather than a museum exhibit. Later critics have tended to rank it with his debut as his best early work, and it is often cited as a forerunner of the roots-music revivals that followed.","Start with Corinna for the lilting acoustic side, then go straight to You Don't Miss Your Water to hear how the same voice opens into soul. Listen for the harmonica as a second voice answering the singer. Afterwards play Mississippi John Hurt for the tradition he draws on, and Otis Redding's Otis Blue for the Memphis side of the family."],"tim-maia-gostava-tanto-de-voce":["Gostava Tanto de Você is a slow soul ballad: a soft electric piano and bass, gentle drums, and strings that rise behind Tim Maia's enormous voice. He starts almost tenderly, holding back, then lets the sound broaden into a full-throated plea in the later choruses. The melody is simple and long-breathed, built for singing along. It is the Brazilian answer to the great American soul ballads, recognisable from the first lines by the warmth and weight of the voice.","The song was written by Edson Trindade, a friend from Maia's early years, and recorded for his 1973 album. Maia had lived in the United States in the early 1960s, absorbed doo-wop and soul, and come home to build a Brazilian version of that sound in Rio de Janeiro. By the early 1970s he was a star. The song is about longing for a love that has ended, a theme it treats with plain-spoken sincerity rather than melodrama.","Listen to how the arrangement holds back the full band. The first half leaves wide spaces around the voice, and the strings enter only once the emotion has been established, so the climax feels earned rather than imposed. Notice too Maia's habit of bending the end of a phrase slightly behind the beat, a mannerism learned from American soul singers that gives the Portuguese words a loose, conversational swing.","It became one of the defining Brazilian love songs of the decade and remains among the most covered and requested songs in his catalogue, sung at weddings and in karaoke bars across the country. Critics regard it as proof that Maia could do more than groove: it shows him as a ballad singer of real depth, and it helped make soul a permanent part of Brazilian popular music.","Play it loud enough to feel the bass, and listen first to the restraint of the opening verse. Then wait for the strings and the moment Maia lets his voice open fully. Compare Al Green's Let's Stay Together for the American soul ballad behind it, and Jorge Ben's records from the same years for the other side of Brazil's black music in the 1970s."],"george-benson-beyond-the-blue-horizon":["A compact small-group record built around Benson's guitar, with organ, bass, drums and hand percussion. It opens with a brisk reading of Miles Davis's So What and moves through Luiz Bonfá's bossa nova The Gentle Rain and Benson's own tunes, including the long, airy Ode to a Kudu. The sound is crisp and spacious: long, fluid guitar lines with a round tone, fast runs played with perfect clarity, and a rhythm section that swings hard without crowding him.","This was Benson's first album for Creed Taylor's CTI label, recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in New Jersey. Benson had come up in Pittsburgh, played in Jack McDuff's organ group and been hailed as the heir to Wes Montgomery, whom Taylor had also produced. CTI was known for lush orchestral packaging, but this date is stripped back, with Ron Carter on bass and Jack DeJohnette on drums giving it a modern, loose feel.","Listen to the way Benson's lines are shaped like a horn player's, with breaths between phrases rather than an unbroken stream of notes. On So What he treats Davis's famous bass-line theme as a launch pad and quickly leaves its calm behind. DeJohnette's drumming is worth following on its own: busy, conversational, constantly nudging the guitarist, a sign of how this record sits between organ soul jazz and the freer jazz of its moment.","Jazz critics welcomed it as Benson's most purely improvisational album, and many still name it as the strongest proof of his guitar playing before he became a pop vocalist. After the huge commercial success of Breezin' some purists pointed back to this record as what had been lost; others argue that the melodic clarity heard here is exactly what made the later crossover possible.","Begin with So What and listen for how far from Davis's original he moves within a few choruses. Then put on The Gentle Rain for his lyrical side. Pay attention to the organ's sustained chords under the guitar. Compare Kind of Blue for the source, and Freddie Hubbard's Red Clay for another CTI date of the same years."],"george-benson-give-me-the-night":["A polished R&B record in which Benson is above all a singer. The title track rides a sleek, mid-tempo dance groove with chiming guitar and soft synthesiser pads; Love x Love is brighter and bouncier; Off Broadway is an instrumental showcase for his guitar; Moody's Mood revives the vocalese tradition of setting words to a jazz solo. Throughout, the signature sound is Benson scatting in unison with his own guitar lines, voice and strings moving together note for note.","The album was produced by Quincy Jones, fresh from Michael Jackson's Off the Wall, and released in 1980 on Jones's Qwest label through Warner Bros. Jones brought his writer Rod Temperton, who wrote the title song, and his Los Angeles session circle. Disco had crashed commercially, and producers were reshaping dance music into the smoother, more song-based style later called boogie. Benson, already a crossover star after Breezin', was a natural vehicle for that new sound.","Listen to the title track's bass and keyboard parts, which lock into a pattern so even and unhurried that it feels like walking rather than dancing; that restraint is Temperton's trademark, heard also on Off the Wall. On Moody's Mood Benson is singing a melody that began as an improvised saxophone solo by James Moody, so a jazz invention from decades earlier is hiding inside a glossy 1980 production.","It was a major commercial success, and the album and its songs collected several Grammy awards. Jazz writers were divided: some regretted the guitarist's retreat behind the singer, others admired the craft of the arrangements. Over time it has come to be seen as a model of early-1980s studio R&B, and the title track is a fixture of soul and boogie playlists.","Start with the title track and notice how few elements there are, each perfectly placed. Then play Off Broadway to hear the guitarist at full stretch, and Moody's Mood for the jazz singer underneath the pop star. Pair it with Michael Jackson's Off the Wall, made by much the same team, and with Breezin' for Benson's earlier crossover."],"gloria-gaynor-i-am-what-i-am":["Gaynor's version takes a Broadway showstopper and puts it on the dance floor. It begins almost like a theatre number, with the voice exposed and measured, then the beat arrives: a driving, pulsing synthesiser bass and steady four-on-the-floor drums at a quick tempo. Her voice climbs through the song with increasing force, and the long extended mix gives the dancers room. The sound you recognise is that switch from stage declaration to relentless club pulse.","The song was written by Jerry Herman for La Cage aux Folles, which opened on Broadway in 1983, where it closes the first act as the drag performer Albin's defiant statement of identity. Gaynor recorded it the same year, five years after I Will Survive, at a time when disco had faded from the charts but survived in gay clubs, where a faster electronic style called Hi-NRG flourished. The AIDS crisis was beginning to devastate those same communities.","Listen for how the arrangement saves the full dance groove until after the opening statement, keeping a trace of the theatre staging in a club record. The machine-driven bass, sequenced rather than played, places this firmly in the Hi-NRG world rather than the orchestral disco of her 1970s hits. Gaynor sings it with the same steady, unshowy authority she brought to I Will Survive, which is why the two songs are so often heard back to back.","The single became a club hit, especially in Britain and Europe, and it was quickly adopted as a gay anthem, played at Pride events ever since. Some theatre lovers preferred the original stage version's dramatic context, while club audiences embraced Gaynor's as definitive for the dance floor. Today it is often credited with extending Gaynor's career beyond the disco era and with helping to define the sound of 1980s gay club culture.","Hear Herman's original stage version first, then Gaynor's: notice what the drum machine adds and what the theatrical pacing loses. Listen for the moment the beat enters and the voice changes from statement to celebration. Follow it with I Will Survive and Sylvester's You Make Me Feel (Mighty Real) to hear the clubs that made it an anthem."],"grover-washington-jr-inner-city-blues":["Washington's debut album sets his tenor and alto saxophones against a funky rhythm section and Bob James's orchestral arrangements. The title track stretches Marvin Gaye's song into a long, simmering groove; there are also versions of Bill Withers's Ain't No Sunshine and Gaye's Mercy Mercy Me, alongside other material. The defining sound is Washington's big, vocal saxophone tone, singing the melodies almost as a soul vocalist would, floating over bass and drums and gentle string cushions.","The album was made for Kudu, a soul-oriented offshoot of Creed Taylor's CTI label, in 1971. According to the familiar story, Washington, then a little-known saxophonist working in Philadelphia, got the session because the intended leader, Hank Crawford, was unavailable. Taylor's formula of jazz soloists, contemporary soul hits and lush arrangements was aimed at radio, and the source material was brand new: Gaye's What's Going On and Withers's debut had both come out that year.","Listen to how Washington treats the melodies of these protest and heartbreak songs: rather than improvising away from them, he decorates them with the bends, cries and slurs of a gospel singer, so the meaning of the original words is carried by tone alone. Bob James's arrangements leave the rhythm section remarkably bare in the grooves, holding the strings back for transitions, a pattern he would refine on Washington's later Kudu albums.","The record sold well and launched Washington's career; within a few years he was among the most popular instrumentalists in America. Jazz critics of the time were often wary of the CTI and Kudu sound as too commercial. Later writers, and hip-hop producers who sampled Kudu records, have been kinder, and the album is now seen as a founding document of jazz-funk and, eventually, smooth jazz.","Start with the title track and give it time; the groove builds slowly. Listen for the saxophone's tone more than its notes. Then hear Marvin Gaye's What's Going On to compare the originals, and move on to Mister Magic to hear how Washington and James developed this sound into something more confident and polished."],"janis-joplin-i-got-dem-ol-kozmic-blues-again-mama":["Joplin's first album without Big Brother trades psychedelic guitars for a soul revue: a horn section, organ and a tight rhythm section. It opens with the rousing Try (Just a Little Bit Harder) and includes her version of the Bee Gees' To Love Somebody, Rodgers and Hart's Little Girl Blue, the slow-building Kozmic Blues and the gospel-flavoured Work Me, Lord. Her voice is the whole show, swinging from a cracked whisper to a raw scream within a phrase.","Joplin left Big Brother and the Holding Company late in 1968, at the height of their success with Cheap Thrills, to form a band built on the Stax and Memphis soul she loved. The Kozmic Blues Band was assembled quickly, and the album, produced by Gabriel Mekler, was released by Columbia in 1969. She performed at Woodstock that summer with this group, and was widely watched as rock's first great female star making her own way.","Listen to Little Girl Blue, a Broadway song from 1935 that Nina Simone had made her own a decade earlier: Joplin keeps the melody almost intact and lets her voice break on purpose, turning a show tune into a confessional. On Kozmic Blues itself, notice how the horns enter only after the verse has laid out her loneliness, mirroring the way Otis Redding's records hold the brass back for the emotional peak.","On release the album drew a mixed response; some reviewers in the rock press thought the new band stiff and missed Big Brother's chaotic energy. Joplin was hurt by the criticism. Later assessments have been warmer, hearing it as a bold leap into soul and recognising Try and Kozmic Blues as among her finest performances. It is now seen as the bridge between Cheap Thrills and Pearl.","Start with Try to hear the horns and her soul phrasing, then Little Girl Blue for the most intimate singing she recorded. Listen for the moments where she pushes past the beat and the band follows. Compare Big Brother's Cheap Thrills for what she left, and Pearl for where she was headed; Nina Simone's Little Girl Blue makes a revealing companion."],"janis-joplin-me-and-bobby-mcgee":["Joplin's recording starts as a gentle country song, her voice relaxed over a strummed acoustic guitar, and gradually turns into a full-band soul romp. Each verse adds weight: bass and drums, then organ, then electric guitar. By the end she is improvising wordless shouts and repeated cries over a rolling groove. The shape is a long, steady crescendo from folk ballad to gospel-soul climax, and you recognise it by that easy, almost conversational opening that hides how far the song will go.","The song was written by Kris Kristofferson and the producer Fred Foster and first recorded by Roger Miller in 1969. Joplin cut it in 1970 with her Full Tilt Boogie Band and producer Paul Rothchild for the album Pearl; she died that October, before the album was finished. Released early in 1971, it went to number one in the United States, a posthumous hit. The song is about two drifters travelling together and the freedom and loss of their parting.","Listen to how the song modulates upward late on, lifting the key so Joplin's voice has to push harder, a classic soul trick that turns a country song into something closer to a gospel testimony. Notice too how much of the final minute is not the written song at all: she sings her own variations on the melody and the band keeps vamping, the kind of looseness rarely heard on a hit single.","The single was the biggest hit of Joplin's career, and its success, coming after her death, gave it a particular poignancy. Kristofferson has spoken of first hearing her version after she died. Critics generally regard it as the definitive recording of the song, though some prefer Kristofferson's own understated reading, and it remains one of the most played records of the classic-rock era.","Listen first for the quiet opening and count how many new instruments arrive in each verse. Then focus on the last minute, where the written song gives way to improvisation. Compare Kristofferson's own 1970 recording for the writer's laconic version, and the Kozmic Blues album to hear the soul approach she had been developing."],"pablo-milanes-yo-pisare-las-calles-nuevamente":["A song for voice and guitar with a marching, hopeful rhythm. Milanés strums steadily and sings a long, rising melody with his warm, clear tenor, the phrases climbing as the song moves toward its refrain. It is written as a promise: the singer imagines walking again through the streets of Santiago de Chile once the city has been freed. The tone is not mournful but resolute, a song meant to be sung by crowds, and its broad, anthem-like chorus is what most listeners remember.","The song responds to the military coup in Chile in September 1973, which overthrew Salvador Allende and led to the killing of the singer Víctor Jara and the exile of many Chilean musicians. Milanés, a founder of Cuba's nueva trova movement alongside Silvio Rodríguez, wrote it in solidarity with Chileans living under dictatorship and in exile. Across Latin America and Europe, it became one of the songs of the solidarity movement that grew up around exiled Chilean artists.","Listen to the way the harmony turns briefly dark in the verses before brightening on the refrain: the music enacts the move from grief to hope that the words describe. The rhythm owes something to the Chilean nueva canción tradition of Jara and Inti-Illimani, a deliberate gesture of kinship. It is also notable that the song names real places in Santiago, which gave it great power for exiles hearing it far from home.","The song became a fixture at solidarity concerts and has been recorded by many singers. When democracy returned to Chile in 1990, it took on new meaning, and Milanés performed it in Santiago to large crowds. Some listeners have regarded it as overly declamatory compared with his love songs; most see it as one of the defining political songs of the Latin American 1970s.","Listen first to the guitar pattern, which gives the song its walking pulse. Follow the melody as it climbs toward the refrain. Then hear Víctor Jara's Te recuerdo Amanda for the Chilean world the song mourns, and Silvio Rodríguez's Ojalá for his nueva trova comrade. A live recording conveys how the song is meant to be sung back by an audience."],"pablo-milanes-el-breve-espacio-en-que-no-estas":["One of Milanés's most loved love songs, built on a gently rocking guitar pattern and a long, arching melody. His voice is intimate and unhurried, moving from a near-whisper in the low register to a fuller sound in the refrain. The harmony shifts with subtlety, borrowing from the Cuban bolero and the filin tradition of jazz-influenced song. The title names the song's subject: the brief time spent apart from a beloved, and how much that short absence fills the singer's thoughts.","Milanés was a central figure in Cuba's nueva trova, the movement of politically engaged singer-songwriters that emerged after the revolution. Yet much of his most lasting work is love songs, which drew on his early grounding in filin, the Havana style of the 1950s that blended bolero with jazz harmony. This song comes from the later 1970s, when his recordings were finding audiences across Latin America and Spain.","Listen to the guitar's inner voices, which move by small steps under the melody, an inheritance from filin's chromatic harmony rather than the simpler chords of protest song. Notice how the melody often begins a phrase just after the downbeat, giving the singing a hesitant, thinking-aloud quality that fits the subject. The refrain lands on the title phrase with a brief rise, then relaxes back, like a sigh.","The song became a standard across the Spanish-speaking world, recorded by many singers and frequently performed in Milanés's concerts until the end of his life. Critics often point to songs like this one as evidence that nueva trova was never only political music, and that its love songs could carry as much craft and feeling as its anthems.","Listen first just to the guitar to feel its rocking rhythm, then follow the voice as it rises in the refrain. Notice how quietly Milanés sings for most of the song. Pair it with Yolanda, his other great love song, and with Silvio Rodríguez's Ojalá to hear how the two nueva trova founders handled intimacy differently."],"barry-white-youre-the-first-the-last-my-everything":["The record begins with Barry White speaking in his deep bass voice over a hushed orchestral introduction, then bursts into a bright, galloping disco groove: chattering rhythm guitar, a busy bass line, sweeping strings and punching horns. White sings with joyful force, his low voice riding over the orchestra. It is faster and more exuberant than his slow seduction songs, and the sound everyone knows is that switch from the murmured preamble to the full orchestra kicking in.","White was at the height of his success in 1974, writing, producing and arranging lush orchestral soul for himself, the vocal trio Love Unlimited and the Love Unlimited Orchestra, often working with arranger Gene Page. The song was released on 20th Century Records and grew out of an old country song co-written by Peter Radcliffe, which White and Tony Sepe reworked. It is a declaration of total devotion, sung with uncomplicated happiness.","Listen to the bass line, which moves constantly underneath while the strings hold long notes above: that contrast of busy bottom and floating top is the engine of the Barry White sound and a direct ancestor of disco orchestration. The spoken introduction is not just a gimmick; it establishes his voice as an instrument before he sings a note. The country origins are completely hidden, though the simple, circular melody betrays a folk-song simplicity.","The single was a big international hit, reaching the top of the British chart and close to the top in the United States. Critics at the time sometimes dismissed White's work as formula; later writers have recognised his skill as an arranger and his influence on disco and on the orchestral soul of Philadelphia. The song remains a perennial of weddings and dance floors.","Listen to the opening carefully and wait for the moment the full orchestra enters. Then follow the bass line, which never stops moving. Compare Love's Theme, the instrumental hit he wrote for the Love Unlimited Orchestra, and Can't Get Enough of Your Love, Babe, to hear how he varied the formula. Donna Summer's disco records show where this orchestral approach led."],"johnny-winter-johnny-winter-and":["A twin-guitar rock record rather than a blues album: Johnny Winter's slide and lead lines are now answered by a second guitarist, Rick Derringer, and the songs are tighter, brighter and built for radio. Riffs replace twelve-bar shuffles, harmonies appear in the choruses, and the long improvised solos of his first records are cut down to bursts. The track everyone knows is Derringer's Rock and Roll, Hoochie Koo, a strutting celebration of the music itself.","By 1970 Winter was a Columbia star after a famously large signing, but his brother Edgar had left to lead his own band. Johnny teamed up with Derringer and bassist Randy Jo Hobbs, both veterans of the McCoys, the Indiana group behind Hang On Sloopy. The partnership pulled a Texas blues purist toward the hard-rock arena circuit of the early seventies, the world of Grand Funk and the Allman Brothers, where volume and stamina sold tickets.","Listen to how the two guitars divide the work. Derringer plays clean, melodic pop-rock parts with neat chord voicings, while Winter attacks with thin, stinging tone and fast trills; the contrast is the whole design. Rock and Roll, Hoochie Koo was recorded here before Derringer had his own solo hit with it in 1973, so this is the song's first appearance, slightly looser and more guitar-heavy.","Blues loyalists heard a sell-out and missed the raw trio of Second Winter; rock listeners heard a welcome turn toward songs. The band proved its worth more fully on the following live album, and many critics still rate the studio set as uneven, a collection of strong rockers and filler. Its standing has risen as the Derringer collaboration came to be seen as a key step in the American guitar-rock sound of the decade.","Start with Rock and Roll, Hoochie Koo and follow the two guitars in each ear. Then compare it with anything on Johnny Winter (1969) to hear how far the man moved from Delta and Texas blues in a year. Follow it with the Allman Brothers at Fillmore East for another two-guitar conversation of the same moment."],"hamlisch-the-sting":["A film score made almost entirely of someone else's music. Marvin Hamlisch took the piano rags of Scott Joplin, written around 1900, and arranged them for solo piano and a small band of clarinet, cornet, trombone, tuba, banjo and strings. The Entertainer serves as the main theme, with Solace, Pine Apple Rag and The Easy Winners among others; the tone is jaunty, sly and wistful by turns, matching a film about con men pulling an elaborate swindle.","George Roy Hill's film reunited Paul Newman and Robert Redford in a caper set in 1930s Chicago. Joplin had died in obscurity in 1917, but Joshua Rifkin's 1970 Nonesuch recordings of his rags had started a revival among classical listeners. Hamlisch, a young Broadway rehearsal pianist and songwriter, brought that revival to a mass audience, and the score won him one of his three Academy Awards in a single night in 1974.","Ragtime is about thirty years out of date for a story set in 1936, and the anachronism is deliberate: the music signals an affectionate, storybook past rather than documentary realism. Listen to how Hamlisch slows Solace into something close to a tango-tinged lament, letting the left hand breathe. The piano touch throughout is looser and more theatrical than a classical recitalist's, closer to a silent-film pianist.","The score made Joplin a household name and sent The Entertainer onto pop radio and into every piano lesson book, which delighted ragtime scholars and also irritated some of them: Joplin insisted his rags should never be played fast, and many later imitators ignored that. Purists also noted the period mismatch. Few denied the larger effect, which was to restore an American composer to the canon.","Play Hamlisch's The Entertainer, then Joplin's own written version at the stately tempo the composer marked, then Rifkin's recording. Notice what the band adds: the tuba's bounce and the clarinet's curling counter-melodies. Then hear Solace as Hamlisch's slow centrepiece and ask whether it sounds more like 1909 or 1973."],"hamlisch-the-way-we-were":["The title song of Sydney Pollack's film, sung by Barbra Streisand with words by Alan and Marilyn Bergman. It is a song about looking back on a love affair and choosing what to keep from it. The music is a long, arching ballad for voice and lush orchestra, beginning almost in a whisper and building to a full-throated climax before falling back. Its melody moves in small, hesitant rising steps that suit a mind turning over the past.","Pollack's film cast Streisand and Robert Redford as mismatched lovers across the 1930s to the 1950s, from campus politics to the Hollywood blacklist. Hamlisch, not yet thirty, wrote both song and score. The ceremony of 1974 gave him Oscars for the song, the dramatic score and his Joplin adaptation for The Sting, an unusual sweep. The record became one of Streisand's signature performances and a staple of easy-listening radio.","The score is built almost entirely on the song's melody, which returns in many disguises through the film: slowed on piano, swept up by strings, fragmented under dialogue. So the song is the theme rather than an end-credits add-on. Listen too to how Streisand begins softly, holding back vibrato, and how the arrangement keeps its biggest orchestral swell for the final phrases rather than spending it early.","Critics who found the film uneven usually exempted the song. Some have called it schmaltz, a lavish product of the early-seventies middle of the road, while others hear a craftsmanlike art song in the tradition of Gershwin and Arlen. It has been recorded by Gladys Knight and the Pips and countless lounge singers, and it remains a test of breath control and restraint for any vocalist.","Listen once for Streisand alone: where she breathes, where she holds a note just past comfort, where she pulls the volume down. Then listen to the orchestra underneath and notice how little it does at first. Compare Hamlisch's later Nobody Does It Better to hear the same composer in a lighter, more confident mood."],"mike-post-the-rockford-files-theme":["Mike Post and his partner Pete Carpenter wrote this theme for the James Garner detective series. A chugging rock-funk rhythm section sets up a bluesy riff, then a buzzing synthesizer takes the tune, answered by harmonica and electric guitar. It sounds less like orchestral television music than like an instrumental hit single, which it became, and its swagger perfectly matches a laid-back private investigator who lives in a trailer by the beach.","The Rockford Files, created by Roy Huggins and Stephen J. Cannell, ran on NBC from 1974 to 1980. Post and Carpenter were the busiest theme team in American television, and the synthesizer had only just become portable and affordable enough for a studio session. Where earlier detective themes, like Lalo Schifrin's Mission: Impossible, used big band jazz, this one reached for the sound of pop radio and AM rock.","Every episode opened before the theme with a message left on Rockford's answering machine, a different comic voice each week, so the music arrives as a punchline to the gag. Listen for the moment the synthesizer melody is doubled and smeared by harmonica: the blend makes the electronic tone sound earthy rather than futuristic, a trick that made the synth feel at home on a cop show.","The theme was released as a single and won wide radio play, which was rare for television music, and it is often ranked among the best detective themes ever written. Some musicians at the time dismissed TV themes as commercial jingles; time has reversed that, and producers and DJs now treat Post's seventies work as a source of breaks and synth sounds worth studying.","Start with the opening riff and count how quickly the full band locks in. Then follow the hand-off of the melody between synthesizer, harmonica and guitar. Compare it with Isaac Hayes's Theme from Shaft and Schifrin's Mission: Impossible, then with Post's later Hill Street Blues theme, which turns from swagger to melancholy piano."],"bob-seger-live-bullet":["A double live album recorded at Cobo Hall in Detroit with the Silver Bullet Band, in front of a home crowd that already knew every word. The sound is muscular bar-band rock with a soul engine: pounding piano, a wailing saxophone, a rhythm section that drives rather than swings. Its emotional peak is Turn the Page, a slow song about the loneliness of life on the road, carried by a mournful tenor saxophone line.","Seger had been a Detroit star for a decade, with regional hits and national indifference. Capitol released Live Bullet in 1976 as a last attempt to capture what Midwestern fans saw in his concerts, and it worked: the record finally broke him across the country months before Night Moves arrived. It belongs with Frampton Comes Alive and Kiss Alive, the mid-seventies moment when the double live album was a career-making format.","The set reaches back to Seger's sixties singles, including Heavy Music, and folds in Ike and Tina Turner's Nutbush City Limits, so the record works as a compressed history of a regional career. Listen to the crowd during Turn the Page: they cheer the first notes of a song that had never been a hit, proof that his reputation had been built entirely in clubs and halls rather than on the radio.","Reviewers outside Michigan were surprised, and the album kept selling for years, long after its release. Critics have praised it as a truer portrait of Seger than his polished studio work, and Turn the Page found a second life when Metallica recorded it in 1998. Some find it too long; few dispute its energy.","Begin with Turn the Page and follow the saxophone, which plays the role of a second singer. Then jump to the Travelin' Man and Beautiful Loser medley for the band at full gallop. Compare it with Springsteen's Born to Run, the same year's other great statement of working-class rock romance."],"bob-seger-stranger-in-town":["Seger's follow-up to Night Moves, split between the Silver Bullet Band and the Muscle Shoals Rhythm Section from Alabama. It mixes surging rock with mid-tempo reflection: Hollywood Nights rushes like a car at night, Still the Same is a wry portrait of a gambler who never changes, We've Got Tonite is a slow plea for one night's comfort, and Old Time Rock and Roll is a stomping piano anthem about loving the old records best.","Night Moves had made Seger a national name at thirty-one, and Capitol wanted a quick sequel. He split sessions between Detroit and Muscle Shoals, where the house band had played behind Aretha Franklin and Wilson Pickett. The album arrived at the height of disco and punk, and its plain-spoken rock found an enormous audience among listeners who felt neither movement was for them.","Old Time Rock and Roll was written by Muscle Shoals songwriters George Jackson and Thomas Jones; Seger reworked much of the lyric but did not take a writing credit. The song lay dormant until the 1983 film Risky Business used it for a famous scene, and it became far bigger than it had been in 1978.","Reviewers praised the craft but heard less risk than on Night Moves, and the record is often treated as the moment Seger settled into a dependable formula. Others argue that Hollywood Nights is among his finest recordings. Old Time Rock and Roll has divided listeners ever since, beloved at weddings and named by some critics among the most overplayed songs ever made.","Start with Hollywood Nights and notice how the drums and piano keep pushing forward like a story told without stopping for breath. Then hear Still the Same for Seger's storytelling at a quieter pace. Follow with Tom Petty's Damn the Torpedoes to hear the same heartland sound a year later."],"carly-simon-nobody-does-it-better":["The theme from the James Bond film The Spy Who Loved Me, music by Marvin Hamlisch and words by Carole Bayer Sager, sung by Carly Simon. It is a song of adoration for a lover who outshines everyone. It opens with a famous rolling solo-piano introduction, then Simon enters low and confiding before the strings lift the chorus into a widescreen ballad. Its warmth sets it apart from the darker, brassier Bond themes before it.","The tenth Bond film was Roger Moore's third, and the producers wanted a song that could live on the radio. Hamlisch, fresh from his Oscar sweep, scored the film; Simon had worked with producer Richard Perry on No Secrets, and Perry produced this record too. It was the first Bond song whose title differed from the film's name, though the film's title appears in the lyric.","Listen to the piano introduction: it sounds like a solo ballad, and nothing in it hints at spy-movie glamour. The Bond flavour enters only through the swelling strings and a few dramatic harmonic turns in the bridge. The song is really a soft-rock love ballad wearing a tuxedo, and that is why it outlived the film's other music on the radio.","The song was nominated for an Academy Award and became one of Simon's biggest hits. Bond fans and critics regularly rank it among the best themes of the series, sometimes as the very best, admired for its melody and Simon's unforced singing. Its simple piano opening has made it a favourite of cover singers across genres.","Start with the piano and listen for how long the arrangement holds back before the full orchestra arrives. Then compare it with John Barry's Goldfinger to hear two opposite ideas of a Bond song, brass and menace against tenderness. Hamlisch's The Way We Were makes another revealing companion."],"gal-costa-india":["Gal Costa's 1973 album moves away from the noisy psychedelia of her tropicália years toward something more inward and sensual. The title track, a Paraguayan guarânia about longing for an Indigenous woman, opens with a slow, swaying lilt and lush strings, and her voice moves from a near-whisper to a clear, ringing high register. Around it sit Brazilian folk material and new songs, arranged for acoustic guitars, soft percussion and spare electric colour.","Brazil was under its harshest military rule, Caetano Veloso and Gilberto Gil had only recently returned from exile in London, and censors scrutinised every record. The cover, a close-up of Costa's lower body in a red bikini, was considered indecent and the album was sold in an opaque sleeve. Costa had become the voice of the tropicalista circle while the songwriters were abroad, and this record reasserts her as an interpreter.","Índia itself was a sentimental old guarânia by José Asunción Flores, known in Brazil from country-style duos. Recording it with strings and complete sincerity was a quietly radical act for a former psychedelic rebel, embracing the unfashionable music of the provinces. Listen to how her vibrato thickens on long held notes, then clears; the control is closer to a bel canto singer than a rock vocalist.","The censored cover drew most of the attention at first, and some fans missed the raw energy of Fa-Tal. Over time critics came to hear Índia as a hinge in her career, the moment she joined the experimental spirit of tropicália to the grand song tradition of Brazilian radio singers. It is now often counted among her finest albums.","Begin with the title track and let its slow swing set the pace, noticing how the strings frame rather than smother her voice. Then hear the acoustic folk numbers. Compare Fa-Tal for the wild live Gal two years earlier, and Caetano Veloso's Transa for the exile-era mood these artists shared."],"dave-holland-conference-of-the-birds":["Dave Holland's first album as a leader, a quartet with Sam Rivers and Anthony Braxton on saxophones and flutes and Barry Altschul on drums and percussion. The six pieces balance freedom and form: tunes with strong, singable bass lines and clear structures that open into collective improvisation. The title track is the gentle centre, with flutes twittering over a repeating bass figure and marimba, while Four Winds and Interception race with sharp, angular themes.","Holland, an English bassist, had been hired by Miles Davis in 1968 and played on In a Silent Way and Bitches Brew, then left to form the co-operative group Circle with Chick Corea, Braxton and Altschul. Circle broke up when Corea left, and Holland recorded this session in New York for Manfred Eicher's young Munich label ECM, which was building a reputation for clear, spacious sound.","Holland said the title track grew from listening to birds singing in the garden of his London home at dawn; the title also echoes the medieval Persian Sufi poem by Attar about a flock of birds seeking their king. Listen for how the two horn players never simply alternate solos: they weave countermelodies around each other while the bass holds the shape together.","The record was hailed as proof that free jazz did not have to abandon melody or groove, and it has stayed in print and on lists of essential avant-garde jazz ever since. Some free-jazz purists found it too composed; others hear it as the bridge between the Chicago avant-garde and the post-bop of the following decades. Holland's later bands all trace back to it.","Start with the title track for its gentle bird-like flutes, then go straight to Four Winds and follow Holland's bass line, which is a melody in its own right. Compare it with Ornette Coleman's Shape of Jazz to Come, and then with Holland's own Prime Directive, a quarter-century later."],"linda-ronstadt-canciones-de-mi-padre":["Linda Ronstadt sings traditional Mexican rancheras and huapangos with a full mariachi band, led by the celebrated Mariachi Vargas de Tecalitlán. Violins sing in close harmony, trumpets blaze, the deep guitarrón and the strummed vihuela drive the rhythm. Ronstadt sings in Spanish with a big, open voice and, in the huapangos, the sudden leaps into falsetto that are the style's hallmark. The songs are mostly about love, longing and the land.","Ronstadt grew up in Tucson, Arizona, in a family of Mexican and German descent; her father sang these songs at home. The title comes from a 1946 songbook compiled by her aunt, Luisa Espinel. After hit country-rock records and an album of standards with Nelson Riddle, she insisted on this project against record-company doubts, and it became a bestseller and won a Grammy in the Mexican-American category.","Ronstadt had sung in Spanish on earlier albums only in passing; here she retrains her voice for the mariachi style, which demands a sobbing break between registers that rock singing avoids. Listen to the violins: they are often doubled in octaves and play the countermelody that answers each sung line, a call-and-response at the heart of the music.","It was a commercial surprise and is often described as the best-selling non-English-language album in American history. Mexican and Mexican-American audiences largely embraced it as a gesture of respect, and the stage show that followed toured widely. Some critics questioned whether a rock star belonged in the genre; her diction and the band's authority answered most of them.","Pick any huapango and listen for the moment the voice flips into falsetto. Then follow the trumpets and violins answering her. Compare Lola Beltrán's Cucurrucucú paloma and Chavela Vargas's La llorona for two other women's approaches to the same tradition, and Ronstadt's own Heart Like a Wheel for the distance travelled."],"maria-bethania-rosa-dos-ventos":["A live recording of a theatre show in which Maria Bethânia strings songs together with spoken poetry, so the evening plays like a single dramatic arc rather than a concert. She moves from sambas and northeastern songs to new compositions by her brother Caetano Veloso and others, with a small band that drops away for passages of recitation. Her deep, dark contralto and her actress's sense of timing are the whole show.","Bethânia had first become famous on stage, in the protest revue Opinião in 1965, and theatre remained her natural medium. In 1971 Brazil was under military rule and Caetano was in exile in London. Working with the director Fauzi Arap, she built shows that combined music, literature and staging, a form she would keep developing for decades, and Philips recorded this one in performance.","Listen to the joins. The spoken passages are not introductions but part of the music: she often begins a song mid-sentence, or lets a poem's rhythm carry straight into the band's first beat. Choosing her brother's songs while he was abroad also kept his voice present in Brazil at a time when his own presence was impossible, a quiet political act.","Brazilian critics hailed the show as a new kind of musical theatre, and it established her reputation as the great dramatic interpreter of MPB, distinct from Elis Regina's virtuosity or Gal Costa's experimentation. Some listeners find recordings of her shows stagey without the visual element. Others consider them the truest documents of her art.","Listen in one sitting without skipping, as the show was meant to be heard. Notice how her voice changes between speaking and singing, almost no gap at all. Then compare Carcará for her younger, fiercer protest voice, and Álibi for the studio star she became later in the decade."],"original-dixieland-jazz-band-livery-stable-blues":["Five men and under three minutes of twelve-bar blues taken at a brisk, almost frantic clip. There are no solos in the later sense: the three horns improvise simultaneously, cornet carrying the tune down the middle, clarinet curling above it, trombone sliding underneath, while piano and drums keep a stiff two-beat. What everybody remembers is the breaks, where the clarinet whinnies like a horse, the cornet crows like a rooster and the trombone lows like a cow.","Recorded for Victor in New York on 26 February 1917 by a white band that had come north from New Orleans by way of Chicago and was packing Reisenweber's Café near Columbus Circle. Columbia had recorded them a few weeks earlier and shelved the result as too strange to sell. Victor gambled, and the disc sold in quantities nobody in the industry had expected from a novelty dance record.","Listen closer: the barnyard effects were the selling point and also the lawsuit. Alcide Nunez, a clarinettist the band had dropped, sued over authorship of the tune, and a Chicago court ruled that nobody could claim it — the melody had been common property among New Orleans musicians for years. The label spelled the music jass on the earliest pressings, before the word settled into its modern form.","It made a word and a market, and it handed a white band the credit for a Black New Orleans invention — the founding injustice of the music's commercial history. Freddie Keppard is said to have turned down an earlier chance to record for fear of imitators. Nick LaRocca spent his later decades insisting whites had created jazz, a claim no serious historian has ever accepted.","Play it, then immediately play King Oliver's Dippermouth Blues from six years later, made by musicians who had been playing this music in New Orleans all along. The gap between them is the argument. Notice that nobody steps forward to solo; the interest is in three lines happening at once. Seek a transfer from a clean original pressing, not an over-filtered remaster."],"kid-ory-orys-creole-trombone":["A brisk multi-strain piece in the shape of a rag, with Ory's trombone stepping out of the ensemble to take the melody rather than staying underneath it. Mutt Carey's cornet leads the front line; Ed Garland's bass gives the band a rounder bottom than the tuba-and-banjo sound of most records from these years. The trombone's smears and slides are the whole point, and the title says so.","Cut in Los Angeles, where Ory had moved in 1919 and where a Black audience of recent Southern migrants kept his band busy. The record was made for the Spikes brothers, Black music-shop owners who financed it themselves and issued it on a tiny label, because no major company was recording Black jazz bands. It circulated regionally and was reissued under other names before anyone recognised what it was.","Listen closer: the front line is already doing something the Chicago records would make famous a year later — the horns break off together for two-bar solo breaks and then rejoin, exactly the device Jelly Roll Morton would build whole arrangements around. The band is also playing slightly behind the beat in a way the Original Dixieland Jazz Band never managed, which is where the swing comes from.","For decades the disc was a collector's rumour; its status as the first record by a Black New Orleans jazz band was established only when copies surfaced and the labels were untangled. Its sound quality is poor and its historical weight is greater than its musical one, but it undercuts the tidy story in which jazz begins in a New York studio in 1917.","Listen for the trombone's entrance in the second strain, and hear how different the instrument's job is here from anything in European music — rhythm and smear rather than melody. Compare the ensemble balance with Oliver's Creole Jazz Band the following year. Any modern transfer will be noisy; accept the surface noise and follow the trombone."],"king-oliver-dippermouth-blues":["A twelve-bar blues played by seven musicians at a medium stomping tempo, with Oliver and the young Louis Armstrong on cornets, Johnny Dodds on clarinet, Honoré Dutrey on trombone, Lil Hardin on piano and Baby Dodds on drums. After ensemble choruses and two from Dodds comes the famous passage: three blues choruses from Oliver on cornet with a plunger mute, bending and choking each note until the horn seems to be speaking words.","Made in 1923, first at Gennett's studio in Richmond, Indiana and later for Okeh, at a moment when Chicago's South Side was filling with New Orleans musicians and the migrants who wanted to hear them. Oliver had summoned Armstrong north the year before to play second cornet. Gennett's recording room was beside a railway line; sessions stopped when trains passed, and the band had to stand at carefully judged distances from a single horn.","Listen closer: just before the final ensemble chorus, banjoist Bill Johnson shouts \"Oh, play that thing!\" — he was covering a missed cue, and the shout stayed in. Oliver's three mute choruses were transcribed, memorised and requoted by cornet players for thirty years; when Fletcher Henderson's band recorded the tune as Sugar Foot Stomp, the solo was played note for note as a written arrangement.","These 1923 sides are the earliest recorded evidence of what New Orleans ensemble jazz actually sounded like, and the standard against which every claim about early jazz is checked. Critics have long argued about whether Armstrong was held back in the second-cornet chair or learning discipline there; his own account was affectionate. The primitive acoustic recording flattens the rhythm section almost to inaudibility.","Go straight to Oliver's mute solo about two-thirds through and play it three times; everything the trumpet would do in jazz for the next forty years is implied there. Then listen for Armstrong underneath the ensemble, harmonising rather than leading. Compare the Gennett and Okeh versions — the band plays the same solos, which tells you how fixed these performances were."],"king-oliver-snake-rag":["A multi-strain piece in ragtime shape, played by the Creole Jazz Band with the same collective weave as Dippermouth Blues but more tune and less blues. The thing that stops the listener is the breaks: twice in each chorus the ensemble drops out and the two cornets play a snaking two-bar figure together, in perfect harmony, apparently made up on the spot.","One of the band's 1923 Chicago-period recordings, made at the height of its residency at the Lincoln Gardens on the South Side, where dancers packed a hall lit by a revolving mirror ball and white Chicago teenagers stood by the bandstand taking notes. Among them were the young men who would become the Austin High Gang; Bix Beiderbecke came to listen too. The band worked six nights a week and played these arrangements from memory.","Listen closer: those two-cornet breaks were not improvised on the night. Armstrong later explained that during the preceding chorus Oliver would finger the break silently on his valves, and Armstrong would read his fingers and work out the harmony in time to play it. The audience assumed telepathy. It is the clearest surviving evidence of how much New Orleans improvisation was prepared in plain sight.","Snake Rag is the record collectors reach for when they want to demonstrate what made the Creole Jazz Band different from its imitators — not solos but a shared rhythmic understanding. Two versions survive from different sessions; comparing them shows how much of the performance was fixed. Modern revival bands routinely play the breaks from the record, which rather proves Armstrong's point.","Count the choruses and wait for the breaks; once you know they are coming, listen to how the two cornets lock. Then hear the whole texture as three independent melodies rather than a tune with backing. Follow with Dippermouth Blues from the same band, and with the Original Dixieland Jazz Band for the contrast in rhythm."],"new-orleans-rhythm-kings-tin-roof-blues":["A slow twelve-bar blues, unusually relaxed for a 1923 record: Paul Mares's cornet states a simple, almost mournful theme, George Brunies answers on trombone, and Leon Roppolo plays a clarinet chorus of real tenderness, low in the instrument's range and full of bent notes. The band holds back rather than racing, which was the whole difference between it and its rivals.","Recorded at Gennett in Richmond, Indiana, by a band of young white New Orleanians resident at the Friars Inn in Chicago. They had heard the real thing at home and in the South Side halls, and unlike the Original Dixieland Jazz Band they were frank about their debts. Within months they went back to the same studio with Jelly Roll Morton at the piano, making some of the first recordings by a racially mixed group.","Listen closer: Roppolo's chorus became one of the most copied solos of the decade, but the tune itself had a long second life. Fifty years later it was still a standard, and its melody had been fitted with new words and retitled more than once for the country market. The ensemble also uses a string bass rather than a tuba, giving it a spring that most bands of 1923 lack.","The Rhythm Kings are the awkward case in the argument about race and early jazz: plainly derivative, plainly good, and the direct teachers of the Chicago white players who followed. Roppolo's mental collapse at twenty-two ended the band. Historians now treat these sides as the bridge between the New Orleans originals and the Chicago style of Beiderbecke and his circle.","Start with Roppolo's clarinet chorus and notice how little he plays. Then compare the tempo with any Original Dixieland Jazz Band side: the restraint is the lesson these players had learned. Follow it with Bix Beiderbecke's Davenport Blues to hear the next link in the chain. Transfers from Gennett originals vary wildly; find a well-regarded reissue."],"jelly-roll-morton-king-porter-stomp":["Three minutes of solo piano in the multi-strain shape Morton inherited from ragtime, but played with a swing and a rhythmic looseness ragtime never had. Each strain is a self-contained sixteen bars; the last one, the trio, is the famous part, a rising riff that repeats and builds. The left hand alternates bass note and chord while the right breaks the beat into triplets and delayed accents.","Morton recorded it as a piano solo in 1923, during the burst of sessions that followed his move to Chicago. He had named the piece years earlier after Porter King, a pianist he admired in Florida, and had been playing it since his years working the Gulf Coast and the West. In 1923 a Black pianist recording his own compositions unaccompanied was still close to unheard of.","Listen closer: the trio strain is the seed of the swing era. Fletcher Henderson's arrangement of it, later taken over by Benny Goodman, turned that riff into a big-band chart played by thousands of bands through the 1930s — arguably the single most performed arrangement of the era. Morton, who had sold the publishing cheaply, received almost nothing from any of it.","The 1923 solo is the version scholars use to argue that Morton was a composer rather than an improviser who happened to write things down: the structure is fixed, the strains are balanced, and the later recordings of the same piece keep the same plan. Critics have argued for decades over how much Morton's habanera left-hand bass was genuinely Caribbean and how much was theatrical self-mythology.","Wait for the trio strain and then try to unhear the swing bands that would be playing it fifteen years later. Notice the left hand's small hesitations — this is not the metronomic ragtime bass. Compare with James P. Johnson's Carolina Shout from two years earlier for the difference between New Orleans and Harlem piano."],"jelly-roll-morton-black-bottom-stomp":["Three minutes that contain more incident than most records twice the length. Morton's Red Hot Peppers — George Mitchell on cornet, Omer Simeon on clarinet, Kid Ory on trombone, Johnny St. Cyr on banjo — move through two strains and a string of contrasting choruses, each with a different texture: full ensemble, stop-time cornet, banjo alone, piano alone, everyone together for the ride-out. Nothing repeats without being changed.","Recorded for Victor in Chicago in September 1926, with a rehearsal discipline unusual in jazz at the time. Morton hired New Orleans players he knew, drilled them for hours, told each man where to improvise and where to stay out, and then paid for a second session when he was not satisfied. Victor's electrical recording process, new that year, finally let a rhythm section be heard properly.","Listen closer: the breaks are the architecture. Morton drops the whole band out for two bars at a time, roughly a dozen times, handing the gap to a different instrument each time, so the record's momentum comes from silences rather than volume. Notice too the shift into a Charleston rhythm behind the last choruses, a dance figure then only three years old.","This is the record that settles whether jazz could be composed. Writers from the 1930s onwards used it to argue that arrangement and improvisation need not be enemies, and Gunther Schuller's analysis of it became a standard piece of jazz scholarship. Morton's reputation collapsed in the swing years and was rebuilt largely on these Red Hot Peppers sides.","Count the choruses on a second hearing and name the featured instrument in each; the variety is the point and it is easy to miss at speed. Listen for the two-bar holes. Then play Ellington's later suites to hear where composed jazz went next. Any decent transfer of the Victor original will do; the electrical sound is surprisingly full."],"jelly-roll-morton-dead-man-blues":["A slow blues that opens with a spoken exchange between two of the musicians, joking about a funeral, over a trombone playing Chopin's funeral march. The joke over, the band settles into a medium blues: cornet lead, a trombone chorus, and then the passage everyone remembers, three clarinets in close harmony winding around each other above the rhythm like a small woodwind choir, before the ensemble takes it out.","Cut for Victor in Chicago in 1926 at the same run of Red Hot Peppers sessions as Black Bottom Stomp. Morton was recreating, for a record-buying public far from Louisiana, the New Orleans funeral procession: dirge on the way to the cemetery, stomp on the way back. He had grown up hearing those bands. The spoken introduction is part vaudeville and part documentary.","Listen closer: that three-clarinet trio is written, not improvised, and it is the most obviously composed thing on any Morton record — a texture borrowed from the concert band rather than from jazz. Morton hired two extra clarinettists purely for those choruses. The Chopin quotation at the start is played straight, which is what makes the transition into the blues so abrupt.","For some listeners the comic opening dates the record badly; for others it is the only surviving trace of how a New Orleans band actually addressed an audience. The trio chorus has been the piece's defence since the 1930s revival: it demonstrates Morton's ear for orchestration on a scale nobody else in jazz was attempting in 1926.","Get past the spoken gag and wait for the clarinets; play that chorus again on its own. Then notice how the cornet stays deliberately plain so the harmony behind it can be heard. Compare with Black Bottom Stomp for the other side of Morton — manic variety there, sustained mood here. A good Victor transfer keeps the three clarinets separate."],"jelly-roll-morton-library-of-congress-recordings":["Eight hours of one man at a piano in an empty auditorium, talking and playing. Morton narrates his life and the life of New Orleans, demonstrating at the keyboard as he goes: how a quadrille turned into a stomp, how ragtime differed from jazz, how three different pianists he had known would have played the same tune. He sings, swears, boasts, and plays some of the finest solo piano of his career.","Recorded in 1938 in the Coolidge Auditorium at the Library of Congress by Alan Lomax, who was then building the Archive of American Folk Song and had found Morton running a shabby club in Washington, forgotten by the music business. Lomax expected a folk informant and got a professional with a thesis. The discs were acetates, made for an archive rather than for sale.","Listen closer: Morton keeps returning to what he calls the Spanish tinge, the habanera lilt in the left hand that he insisted was essential to jazz — and then demonstrates the same tune with and without it, which is the clearest lesson in the whole archive. He also names Buddy Bolden and describes his playing; this is close to the only testimony of that kind we have.","Historians have spent eighty years separating Morton's documentary value from his self-mythology, beginning with his claim to have invented jazz in 1902. Lomax's own book drawn from the sessions was criticised for shaping the material into a narrative. Yet no other early jazz musician left anything comparable, and the piano playing alone would justify the set.","Do not try to hear it whole. Start with the quadrille-into-jazz demonstration and the Spanish-tinge passages, then follow with any of the extended solo performances. Play the 1923 King Porter Stomp beside its 1938 counterpart to hear fifteen years of thinking. The complete Rounder edition restores the sequence and the profanity that earlier issues cut."],"armstrong-heebie-jeebies":["A cheerful medium-tempo novelty tune played by the Hot Five — Armstrong's cornet, Johnny Dodds's clarinet, Kid Ory's trombone, Lil Hardin's piano, Johnny St. Cyr's banjo — with one extraordinary event in the middle. Armstrong sings a verse of lyrics, and then, for a full chorus, sings nonsense syllables instead of words, phrasing them exactly as he would phrase a cornet solo.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in February 1926, at the second Hot Five session. The Hot Five never played a live engagement; it existed only in the studio, assembled from musicians working elsewhere around the city. Okeh marketed the discs to Black buyers on its race records list, and this one sold in numbers that made Armstrong a name well beyond the South Side.","Listen closer: the wordless chorus was not the first scat singing ever recorded, whatever the legend says, and the story that Armstrong dropped the lyric sheet mid-take is almost certainly a sales department's invention. What matters is the phrasing — he treats the voice as a brass instrument, accenting off the beat and sliding between notes in ways no popular singer of 1926 attempted.","Contemporary buyers treated it as a comedy record; critics later treated it as the moment jazz singing began. Both readings survive. Armstrong's vocal style, imitated first by Cab Calloway and Bing Crosby and eventually by everyone, arguably changed popular music more than his trumpet playing did, and this three-minute side is where the public first heard it.","Ignore the tune and listen only to the voice, then play any Armstrong cornet solo from the same year and hear the same rhythmic habits. Notice how the band stiffens slightly behind him — they are still playing 1923 while he is playing something newer. Compare with Ella Fitzgerald's scat singing twenty years on."],"armstrong-potato-head-blues":["The Hot Seven, the Hot Five plus tuba and drums, playing a bright multi-strain tune. Ensemble choruses and a clarinet solo lead to the chorus the record exists for: Armstrong improvising over stop-time, where the band hits a single chord every two bars and then falls silent, leaving him alone in the gaps. He builds a line that carries across those holes as if the beat were still sounding.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in May 1927, in the week-long burst of sessions that produced the Hot Seven sides. Armstrong was by then playing nightly in Chicago theatre orchestras and had switched, or was about to switch, from cornet to trumpet. Okeh brought in the extra players to give the records a fuller dance-band bottom; the tuba's presence dates the sound instantly.","Listen closer: the stop-time chorus works because Armstrong is not filling the silences — he plays across them, starting phrases before the band's chord and finishing after it, so the listener supplies the missing beats. Count along and you will find yourself carrying the pulse for him. This displacement of accent, more than any particular note, is what his contemporaries meant by swing.","The stop-time chorus has been transcribed and analysed more often than almost anything else in early jazz, and it is routinely cited as the first fully formed extended improvisation on record. Its fame reached beyond music when Woody Allen's Manhattan named it among the things that make life worth living, which sent a generation of filmgoers to the Hot Seven.","Skip to the stop-time chorus and tap the beat out loud; the whole effect depends on the pulse you keep in your head. Then go back and hear how ordinary the tune itself is. Follow with West End Blues from the next year. The 1927 Okeh sound is dry and close — any well-mastered Hot Five and Sevens set works."],"armstrong-struttin-with-some-barbecue":["A buoyant medium-fast tune for the Hot Five, built on a simple rising figure that sticks after one hearing. Kid Ory's trombone and Johnny Dodds's clarinet take their turns, and Armstrong closes with a chorus that starts low and conversational and climbs, chorus-long, to a held high note over the final ensemble. The band is small and dry-sounding; there is nowhere to hide.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in December 1927, at the last of the Hot Five sessions with the original New Orleans personnel. The tune is credited to Lil Hardin Armstrong, the pianist on the date and Armstrong's wife, who had pushed him to leave King Oliver's band and go out under his own name. Their marriage was by then coming apart; the credit was later disputed for years.","Listen closer: Armstrong's final chorus is not a new melody but a paraphrase — he keeps the tune's shape while displacing nearly every note away from where the beat expects it. Trumpeters have used this chorus as a teaching text ever since, and it turns up almost verbatim, harmonised for a full brass section, in later big-band arrangements of the tune.","Long treated as a lighter companion to Potato Head Blues, the record has risen in critical standing as scholars have taken Lil Hardin Armstrong's role seriously; the authorship question is now part of a wider re-examination of the women written out of early jazz. The tune itself became a Dixieland-revival staple, played faster and more crudely than the original almost every time.","Learn the tune in the first chorus, then follow Armstrong's last chorus against it and hear him bend it out of shape without ever losing it. Notice how little the rhythm section does. Compare a 1950s revival-band version to hear what gets lost when the tempo goes up. Seek a transfer that keeps the banjo audible."],"armstrong-west-end-blues":["Three minutes and fifteen seconds of twelve-bar blues that begin with a shock: an unaccompanied trumpet cadenza, twelve seconds of fanfare and cascade, before any beat arrives. Then a slow blues. Armstrong plays the theme, the trombone takes a chorus, Armstrong hums wordlessly in dialogue with the clarinet, Earl Hines plays a solo chorus of glittering right-hand runs, and the trumpet returns to hold one high note for four bars before spilling downward.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in June 1928 by a reconstituted Hot Five built around Earl Hines, a pianist as advanced as Armstrong himself. The tune was King Oliver's, named for a resort on Lake Pontchartrain, and recording his old teacher's blues was a deliberate gesture. Electrical recording, two years old, could now capture the full range of a trumpet without distortion — which is why the opening works at all.","Listen closer: the wordless vocal chorus is a duet, Armstrong answering Jimmy Strong's clarinet phrase for phrase with the same inflections, so that voice and instrument become indistinguishable. And the famous held note near the end is not held for effect alone — it sets up a descending run that resolves the whole record, which is why the chorus feels finished rather than merely loud.","Musicians treated the opening cadenza as a gauntlet from the moment it appeared; it was memorised, transcribed and quoted for decades. Critics from Gunther Schuller onwards have called this the record on which jazz proves it can carry the weight of serious music, and it is among the most frequently named recordings in any list of the century's essential performances.","Play the first fifteen seconds twice before hearing the rest. Then follow the record as a sequence of four voices — trumpet, trombone, voice with clarinet, piano — and notice how each raises the stakes. Compare Oliver's own version of the tune. Any of the standard Hot Five and Sevens reissues carry it in good sound."],"armstrong-weather-bird":["Two musicians, no rhythm section, no safety net: Armstrong on trumpet and Earl Hines on piano running through an old three-strain rag at speed. They trade the lead, interrupt each other, leave gaps the other has to fill, and repeatedly arrive at the same beat from different directions. The final exchange, a sequence of short breaks traded back and forth, ends on an unresolved joke of a cadence.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in December 1928, at the end of the year in which Armstrong and Hines had been working together nightly. The tune was King Oliver's Weather Bird Rag, which Armstrong had recorded with the Creole Jazz Band five years earlier as a seven-piece; stripping it to two instruments was a private demonstration of how far both men had travelled since.","Listen closer: what makes it dizzying is that neither player keeps time for the other. Hines abandons the stride left hand for long stretches, so the pulse exists only as an agreement between them; both repeatedly displace their phrases by a beat and then meet again exactly where they should. Near the end Hines simply stops, daring Armstrong to hang in the air, and he does.","Critics have reached for counterpoint and for the word conversation ever since, and the record is regularly described as the first jazz duet of real equality between horn and piano. Some listeners find it wilfully tricky next to the gravity of West End Blues from six months earlier. Both descriptions are fair; it is the sound of two players showing off to each other.","Listen once for pleasure and once following only the piano; Hines is doing at least as much as the trumpet. Try to keep the beat yourself through the final exchanges. Then hear the 1923 Creole Jazz Band version of the same rag for the distance travelled. Transfers vary; look for one that does not smother the piano."],"armstrong-st-james-infirmary":["A slow minor-key lament taken at funeral pace. Armstrong sings the old ballad of a man who goes down to the infirmary and finds his lover dead on a table, in a voice by turns conversational and raw, then plays a trumpet chorus that keeps the same shape as the sung melody but darkens it. Earl Hines's piano fills the spaces with unsettled chords rather than comfort.","Recorded for Okeh in Chicago in December 1928. The song is old and its ancestry is tangled — a British broadside about a soldier dying of disease, reshaped in America and claimed as a copyright by a publisher writing under a pseudonym. Armstrong's version fixed it in the repertoire, and it has been recorded by hundreds of singers since, in almost every idiom.","Listen closer: the band plays in a minor key, which early jazz rarely did, and the harmony under the vocal keeps refusing to settle — that instability is what gives the record its chill rather than any lyric detail. Note also how Armstrong sings the word table: he stretches it across the bar line, the same displacement he uses on the trumpet.","It became one of the two or three songs an Armstrong audience always demanded, and he sang it for forty years. Critics who preferred the instrumental Hot Fives long treated his vocal records as a step towards showbusiness; later writers have argued the opposite, that this is where his sense of a song's drama first shows fully. The copyright history remains murky.","Hear it next to Kurt Weill's Berlin ballads of the same year — two very different cities telling stories about corpses over a dance beat. Listen for the piano's refusal to resolve. Then compare a later Armstrong concert version, slower and more theatrical. The 1928 Okeh original has a bleakness the live versions trade away."],"earl-hines-57-varieties":["Three minutes of solo piano that keep changing their mind. Hines states a tune, abandons it, plays octave runs in the right hand like a trumpet line, drops into shimmering tremolos, and repeatedly lets the left-hand beat disappear for a bar or two before catching it again. The title is a joke about the brands of pickle, and the piece really does present that many ideas without settling on one.","Recorded in Chicago in December 1928 for the QRS company, better known for piano rolls, during the same extraordinary year in which Hines made the Armstrong sides and opened at the Grand Terrace ballroom with his own big band. He was twenty-five. Solo piano records had been dominated by the Harlem stride players; a Pittsburgh-born pianist working in Chicago was proposing something else.","Listen closer: the trick everyone borrowed is the right-hand octave tremolo. Hines plays a melody note doubled an octave above and shakes it, which on an acoustic piano imitates a trumpeter's vibrato and lets a single line cut through a loud band. It is why his style was called trumpet piano, and why it worked on a bandstand where stride would have vanished.","Critics have always found Hines's solo records harder to love than his work with Armstrong — the deliberate collapses of the beat can sound like showing off rather than shaping. Later pianists disagreed with their hands: Teddy Wilson, Nat King Cole and Bud Powell all worked from this vocabulary, and the line from here to modern jazz piano is direct.","Listen for the moments when the left hand stops keeping time and notice how uneasy it makes you feel; that is the innovation. Then play a Fats Waller solo from the same year for the steady alternative. Hines's own later solo recordings, made in the 1960s and 1970s in better sound, take the same ideas further."],"jimmie-noone-apex-blues":["A slow blues by a five-piece band with an unusual front line: no brass at all, just Noone's clarinet and Joe Poston's alto saxophone, the saxophone holding the melody steady while the clarinet ornaments it from above and below. Earl Hines is at the piano. The texture is light, almost chamber-like, and Noone's tone stays smooth and even whether he is at the bottom of the instrument or the top.","Recorded in Chicago in 1928 by the band Noone led at the Apex Club, a South Side cabaret that stayed open until the early hours and drew musicians after their own jobs finished. The two-reed format was a practical solution to a small bandstand and became the group's signature. Noone had studied with Lorenzo Tio and taken lessons from Sidney Bechet as a teenager in New Orleans.","Listen closer: the division of labour between the two reeds is the whole design. Poston plays the tune almost without decoration, freeing Noone to treat the melody as a fixed object to circle. It is an arranging idea, not an improvising one, and it anticipates how swing bands would set a soloist against a written line. Hines, meanwhile, is already breaking the beat behind them.","Maurice Ravel, in Chicago on his American tour in 1928, is reported to have gone to the Apex Club to hear Noone, and admirers have made much of the story. Benny Goodman's debt is better documented and less disputed. Critics have sometimes called the band too polite; the counterargument is that this polish is exactly what the clarinet needed to survive into the swing era.","Follow the saxophone rather than the clarinet on the first hearing, then swap; the pleasure is in the two lines fitting. Notice Noone's evenness across the registers, a classical virtue rarely prized in 1928. Then play a Benny Goodman small-group side from the 1930s and hear where it went."],"bechet-wild-cat-blues":["Despite the title this is not a blues but a fast multi-strain rag, and it is really a soprano saxophone record with a band attached. Bechet plays with a vibrato so wide and a tone so large that the cornet beside him sounds like an accompanist. He takes the melody, ornaments it, and drives the ensemble from the top rather than from the middle, which is not how a New Orleans front line is supposed to work.","Recorded in New York in 1923 with Clarence Williams's Blue Five, a studio group assembled by a Black publisher and entrepreneur who used records to sell his sheet music. The tune was his, written with the young Fats Waller. Bechet had spent 1919 to 1922 in Europe, where he bought the straight soprano saxophone in a London shop and where the conductor Ernest Ansermet heard him.","Listen closer: Ansermet had written in 1919 that this young clarinettist's road might turn out to be the whole road music would follow — the first time a European classical musician had said anything of the kind about a jazz player, and four years before this record was made. Listen for the moments when Bechet simply plays over the ensemble rather than with it; the soloist's future is audible there.","The Blue Five sides are where the jazz solo begins to detach itself from the collective, three years before Armstrong's Hot Fives. Bechet's dominance has been read both as a breakthrough and as bad manners; New Orleans purists have never quite forgiven him for refusing to blend. Clarence Williams's role as producer and publisher has been reassessed as central rather than incidental.","Hear how much space Bechet takes and ask who is accompanying whom. Then play Armstrong's Heebie Jeebies from three years later to see the same idea arrive by another route. Bechet's soprano is unmistakable within two notes; once you know the sound you will find it everywhere in the 1920s."],"bechet-summertime":["Gershwin's lullaby from Porgy and Bess, taken slowly by a quintet with Bechet's soprano saxophone alone in front. He barely states the tune before beginning to bend it, each phrase entering late and swelling through that enormous vibrato. There is a piano chorus, and then the saxophone returns and stays, climbing over the rhythm section for the last minute without ever raising the tempo.","Recorded in New York in June 1939 for Blue Note, a label founded months earlier by Alfred Lion, a German émigré who had arrived with a passion for the music and very little money. The session became the young company's first real success. Bechet was fifty-two, out of fashion in a country that wanted big bands, and recording an opera aria only four years old.","Listen closer: Bechet takes a melody written to be sung gently and plays it with the intensity of a blues shout, which reverses the whole point of the original. And Gershwin's tune was itself modelled on the Black American sacred and blues idiom Bechet grew up inside — so this is not a jazz musician borrowing from opera but a New Orleans Creole taking something home.","The record's success persuaded Alfred Lion that a jazz label could survive, and Blue Note went on to define recorded jazz for the next three decades; in that sense this side has consequences out of all proportion to its length. Critics of the period, deep in the argument between swing modernists and New Orleans revivalists, used Bechet as a weapon on the traditionalist side.","Play Gershwin's sung Summertime first, then this, and listen for every place where Bechet arrives late. Notice that he never plays loudly and never plays fast; the force is all in the vibrato. Then hear his 1940s clarinet blues for the other half of his art. Blue Note's own reissues have the best transfers."],"bechet-blue-horizon":["Six choruses of slow blues on the clarinet, and nothing else happens. Bechet begins in the lowest register of the instrument, almost muttering, and climbs one chorus at a time until the last is played high, loud and ragged at the edges. The band behind him plays chords and keeps out of the way. There is no theme to speak of, no arrangement, no second soloist.","Recorded in New York in 1944 for Blue Note, in the middle of a war that had emptied the bandstands and during a period when the New Orleans revival had given Bechet a second career. He was playing clarinet rather than his usual soprano saxophone, returning to the instrument he had learned as a child in New Orleans thirty-five years before the session.","Listen closer: the whole performance is a single deliberate climb, and Bechet marks it by changing register rather than volume — each chorus sits a little higher on the instrument than the last, so the emotional rise is built into the physical act of playing. In the final chorus his tone breaks slightly on the top notes. He left it in.","It is routinely named the finest blues clarinet solo on record, and it is unusual among Bechet's sides for its complete absence of display. Writers who find his soprano playing domineering tend to make an exception here. The record also helped fix the idea, central to the revival, that early jazz was at bottom a blues music rather than a ragtime one.","Do not do anything else while it plays; the piece asks for five undistracted minutes. Track the rise chorus by chorus and notice how late the first loud note arrives. Then play a Johnny Dodds blues from the 1920s for the older clarinet manner. Blue Note transfers are clean and close-miked."],"bix-beiderbecke-davenport-blues":["Beiderbecke's own tune, and not really a blues: a wistful sixteen-bar melody with an unexpected turn in its harmony, played by a pick-up band that includes the young Tommy Dorsey on trombone. Bix's cornet enters with the soft, rounded tone that made him famous — no vibrato to speak of, notes struck cleanly in the middle register, phrases that end where you do not expect them to.","Recorded at Gennett in Richmond, Indiana in January 1925, under the name Bix and his Rhythm Jugglers, by a group of young white musicians drifting between dance-band jobs. Beiderbecke was twenty-one, had been thrown out of a military academy, and was named for a river town in Iowa where he had first heard jazz from the riverboats coming up from New Orleans.","Listen closer: the tune keeps sliding into chords that ragtime and blues would not use, borrowed from the impressionist piano music Bix played at home. He never learned to read music well and worked these harmonies out by ear at the keyboard, then wrote them for horns. Listen for the moment the melody resolves a bar later than it should — that hesitation is his signature.","The record was barely noticed on release and has grown steadily in reputation as the first documented Beiderbecke composition. Arguments about him tend to circle the same question: whether his reputation rests on the playing or on the early death and the novel and film that followed it. This side, made before the legend, is the usual evidence for the defence.","Listen for the tone first — compare thirty seconds of Bix with thirty seconds of Armstrong from the same period and the difference is immediate: bell-like and inward against blazing and outward. Then hear In a Mist to find the same harmony on the piano. Gennett originals are noisy; a good needle-drop reissue is worth seeking."],"bix-beiderbecke-singin-the-blues":["A medium-slow popular song turned into two solos and very little else. Frankie Trumbauer plays a chorus on the C-melody saxophone, light and curling; then Beiderbecke's cornet takes a chorus that has been memorised by trumpeters ever since, built from long shapely phrases that carry across the bar lines rather than sitting inside them. Eddie Lang's guitar fills the corners. Nobody raises their voice.","Recorded for Okeh in New York in February 1927 and issued under Trumbauer's name, with Beiderbecke as a sideman; both men would shortly join Paul Whiteman's enormous and enormously popular orchestra. The song was three years old. Making a ballad-tempo record with no vocal and no dance function was commercially odd in 1927, and the record sold anyway.","Listen closer: Bix's chorus is a composition, not a decoration — it abandons the song's melody almost entirely and builds a new one that could stand alone. Fletcher Henderson's band later had the solo harmonised for a whole saxophone section and played it as a written arrangement, which is the surest measure of how completely other musicians had learned it by heart.","This is the record usually credited with inventing the jazz ballad: improvisation as lyrical melody rather than rhythmic display, the line that runs through Lester Young to Miles Davis. Trumbauer's own reputation rests largely on it, and Lester Young named him as his model. The perennial argument is whether Bix's Whiteman years afterwards were a sell-out or a reasonable living.","Play Bix's chorus, then play Armstrong's Potato Head Blues chorus from three months later; the two poles of jazz improvisation are right there, one lyrical, one rhythmic. Listen for how few notes Bix uses. Then hear the Fletcher Henderson harmonised version if you can find it."],"bix-beiderbecke-in-a-mist":["Three minutes of solo piano by a cornet player, and not a jazz performance in any usual sense: no steady beat to swing against, no improvisation over changes. Instead a sequence of episodes built from whole-tone scales and unresolved chords, with a syncopated middle section that briefly remembers ragtime before drifting back into haze. The title is exact — the harmony never quite settles on a home key.","Recorded for Okeh in New York in September 1927, while Beiderbecke was earning good money with Paul Whiteman's orchestra and spending his evenings at the piano. He had absorbed Debussy, Ravel and the American composer Eastwood Lane by ear from records and sheet music, could not notate properly, and needed help from an arranger to get the piece onto paper for publication.","Listen closer: the whole-tone scale, which divides the octave into six equal steps and so belongs to no key at all, is what produces the drifting quality — the same device Debussy used to make water and fog. Bix plays it with a jazz musician's touch rather than a pianist's, hitting chords slightly unevenly, which is why the piece sounds improvised even where it is fixed.","Sceptics have called it a party piece by a man out of his depth in composition, and it is undeniably short of development. Its importance is as a first: a jazz musician openly using impressionist harmony as material, two decades before the cool school did the same thing systematically. Pianists still record it, usually more correctly and less atmospherically than its author.","Play Debussy's Préludes or Ravel's Jeux d'eau first and the vocabulary will be unmistakable. Then listen for the ragtime fragment in the middle and hear how uncomfortably the two worlds sit together — that discomfort is the interesting part. Bix's own recording is the one to hear; later pianists tidy it."],"james-p-johnson-carolina-shout":["Three minutes of solo piano at a hard dancing tempo, in the multi-strain shape of a rag but doing something ragtime never did. The left hand strides between a low bass note and a mid-range chord, faster and further than any ragtime bass; the right hand answers it with call-and-response figures, as if two voices were trading. The last strain is a sequence of hand-crossing tricks that sound impossible.","Recorded in New York in October 1921, three years after Johnson had cut the piece as a piano roll. The shout of the title is a ring dance from the Sea Islands of the Carolinas and Georgia, brought north by migrants; Johnson had watched them dance to it at Harlem rent parties, where pianists competed all night and a man's reputation rested on what his left hand could do.","Listen closer: the right-hand figures are imitating the dancers' hand-clapping and the shouted responses of the ring shout, which is why the piece feels like a crowd rather than a soloist. The young George Gershwin learned it by slowing the piano roll and following the depressed keys with his fingers, and he was not the only one; it was the test piece every Harlem pianist had to pass.","It is the founding document of stride, the point where ragtime's fixed notation gives way to a swinging, improvised piano style, and it carried directly into Fats Waller, Duke Ellington's early playing and eventually Art Tatum. Johnson's later concert works have never found an audience, and the standard complaint is that jazz history remembers him for three minutes of a long career.","Listen to the left hand alone for a chorus — the distance it covers at that speed is the whole difficulty. Then hear Fats Waller's Handful of Keys for what a pupil did with it, and Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue for where it leaked into the concert hall. Johnson's own 1921 recording is rhythmically sharper than his later remakes."],"james-p-johnson-charleston":["A song of almost brutal rhythmic simplicity: a short phrase, a hole where the next beat should be, then the answer landing early. That displaced accent is the whole tune, and it is enough to make a room move. The melody itself barely rises above five notes, and the lyric, by Cecil Mack, is mostly an invitation to dance. Almost every version adds hand-claps or stop-time breaks.","Written for the Broadway revue Runnin' Wild, which opened in New York in 1923, one of the Black musical comedies that followed the success of Shuffle Along two years earlier. Johnson said the rhythm came from dockworkers from Charleston, South Carolina, whom he had accompanied at a Harlem dance hall. The dance it named spread from the show to the whole Western world within two years.","Listen closer: the rhythm is not a dance-band invention but a survival. The pattern Johnson wrote down had been in Black American and West African music long before 1923, and it turns up in New Orleans records of the same decade under no particular name. Once you know the figure you hear it everywhere in the 1920s, including in the last choruses of Jelly Roll Morton's Black Bottom Stomp.","It became the sound of an entire decade and remains its shorthand in every film about the 1920s, which has made it hard to hear as music. Johnson earned well from it and was thereafter treated as a songwriter rather than the serious composer he wanted to be. Recent scholarship has restored his Broadway work to a central place in the history of Black musical theatre.","Clap the rhythm before listening: long, short, rest, short. Then play Johnson's own piano version and notice how much space he leaves. Compare it with Weill and Krenek's Berlin attempts to use the same rhythm a few years later, where the figure arrives second-hand and slightly stiff."],"james-p-johnson-yamekraw":["A single-movement rhapsody of roughly a quarter of an hour for piano and orchestra, stitched together from blues strains, a spiritual-like slow section, a stride episode for the soloist alone and a stomping finale. The piano is never far from the front, and the writing for it is Johnson's own Harlem manner rather than a concert pianist's. The seams between sections are frank rather than disguised.","Written in 1927 and named for Yamacraw, a Black district of Savannah, Georgia. Johnson wrote the piano score; William Grant Still made the orchestration. It was performed at Carnegie Hall in 1928 with Fats Waller, Johnson's own pupil, at the piano, in a concert of Black American music. Johnson had wanted for years to be taken seriously as a composer and had been told repeatedly that stride pianists did not write symphonies.","Listen closer: the slow middle section is built on the harmonic language of the spiritual rather than the blues, and Still's orchestration gives it to the strings in a way that sounds deliberately like a hymn heard from outside a church. Note too that the piano writing is genuinely idiomatic stride — the left hand does what it would do at a rent party, not what a concerto would ask.","It was overshadowed from the start by Rhapsody in Blue, written three years earlier by a composer who had learned stride from Johnson's piano rolls, and the comparison has followed it ever since. A short film version was made in 1930. Performances remain rare, the orchestral parts have a complicated history, and Johnson's concert music as a whole is only now being recorded properly.","Play Rhapsody in Blue first and then this, and consider who was borrowing from whom. Listen for the stride episode where the orchestra drops out — that is Johnson writing in his own voice. Then hear Still's Afro-American Symphony from a few years later for the orchestral idiom fully worked out. Recordings are scarce; take whichever you can find."],"fats-waller-handful-of-keys":["Stride piano at its most athletic and most relaxed at once. Waller states a bright tune and then plays variation after variation over a left hand that never falters — bass note, chord, bass note, chord, at a tempo that leaves no room for error, while the right hand spins runs, broken chords and sudden two-handed flourishes. The title is a boast, and it is earned in the first twenty seconds.","Recorded for Victor in New York in March 1929, the year Waller broke through as a recording star. He had learned his trade from James P. Johnson, played organ in a Harlem cinema as a teenager, and by 1929 was writing shows, cutting piano solos and drinking heavily. Victor's electrical recording captured a piano better than any previous process, which mattered for music this fast.","Listen closer: the left hand is doing something physically brutal that the recording makes sound easy — the leaps span more than an octave and land on every beat for three minutes without a stumble. Notice how Waller varies the bass pattern to keep it interesting, occasionally walking up in single notes, and how the right hand's phrases end a fraction late, which is where the relaxation comes from.","Among pianists this is the stride showpiece, the one against which technique is measured, and it stayed in Waller's repertoire all his life. Critics who wrote him off as an entertainer because of his comic vocal records have always had to make an exception for the solo piano sides, where no joke intervenes. Art Tatum was listening, and said so.","Listen once to the right hand and once to the left; they are almost independent performances. Play James P. Johnson's Carolina Shout beside it for the teacher's version of the same style. Then hear Waller's Ain't Misbehavin' from the same year to see him hide this technique behind a song."],"fats-waller-aint-misbehavin":["A medium-tempo song whose melody climbs a small ladder and falls back, over harmony that keeps stepping sideways rather than resolving. Waller recorded it in 1929 both as a solo piano performance and with a band; in the solo version the left hand strides steadily while the right treats the tune as raw material. The lyric, by Andy Razaf, is a promise of fidelity delivered with a straight face.","Written for Connie's Hot Chocolates, a Harlem revue that moved to Broadway in 1929. Louis Armstrong played it from the pit and then from the stage, and the song made both men money. Waller had a reputation for selling songs outright for cash when he needed it; this one he kept. The Harlem Renaissance was at its height and downtown audiences were paying to come uptown.","Listen closer: the harmony under the first phrase keeps shifting a half step at a time, a trick from Waller's church-organ training rather than from ragtime, and it gives a cheerful tune a persistent undertow. Razaf's lyric was probably read by its first audiences as domestic comedy; it has also been heard, plausibly, as the boast of someone with nowhere else to go.","It became one of the most recorded American songs of the century and the title of a long-running Broadway revue built from Waller's catalogue. Critics have argued for eighty years about whether Waller's clowning served the songs or buried them; the solo piano recordings are usually produced as evidence that he knew exactly what he was doing.","Hear Waller's piano solo version and his vocal version in that order, then Armstrong's. The song survives all three treatments, which is the test of a standard. Listen for the sideways harmony in the first eight bars, and then for the release in the middle section, where everything suddenly settles."],"fats-waller-jitterbug-waltz":["A jazz piece in three-four time, which in 1942 was close to unheard of, played by Waller on a Hammond organ rather than a piano. The tune descends in a long, lazy curve — reportedly taken from an exercise his son was practising — and the organ's sustained tone lets the line float in a way no piano could manage. A small band shuffles underneath, keeping the waltz light rather than grand.","Recorded in New York in 1942, late in Waller's short life, when he was touring constantly and in poor health. He had played organ since his teens in a Harlem cinema and never lost his affection for the instrument; the Hammond, an electric imitation of a pipe organ introduced in the 1930s, gave him something portable that could hold a note indefinitely.","Listen closer: the waltz was a European dance that jazz had almost entirely refused, associating it with the parlour and the concert hall. Waller does not swing it in the usual sense — he lets the three beats stay even and puts the swing into the melody's phrasing instead. The Hammond's slight electrical waver, audible on sustained notes, is part of the sound.","It became a standard, taken up by flute and vibraphone players in the 1950s who liked its gentleness and by many since, and it is now among the most covered pieces Waller wrote. Critics have treated it as the proof that he was a composer of real subtlety rather than a manufacturer of novelty songs, and it is the piece most often cited against his clownish reputation.","Notice the descending shape of the melody and how it seems to keep falling without ever landing. Play Ravel's La Valse afterwards for the opposite attitude to three-four time — one dance dissolving, the other quietly reinvented. Then hear a 1950s flute version for how portable the tune turned out to be."],"willie-the-lion-smith-echoes-of-spring":["Stride piano with the aggression removed. The left hand keeps a gentle alternating pattern rather than a stomping one, and the right plays a delicate, almost fluttering figure that circles over shifting harmony without ever landing hard. There is no showpiece chorus and no climax; the piece simply turns over on itself for three minutes and stops. It sounds more like a character piece than a dance.","Recorded in New York in 1939 during a session of solo piano pieces of his own, made for a small label run by enthusiasts at a moment when the older jazz was being rediscovered by collectors. Smith was in his mid-forties, a fixture of Harlem since the rent-party days, a decorated veteran of the Western Front, and by his own account a synagogue cantor as well as a nightclub pianist.","Listen closer: the harmony is impressionist rather than bluesy — chords that colour rather than move, resolutions delayed or simply avoided — and Smith was open about having taken it from the French piano music he admired. Duke Ellington, who called him a major influence and wrote a portrait of him, took the same delicacy of touch into his own playing; you can hear the debt in Ellington's introductions.","The 1939 solos rescued Smith's reputation from anecdote. He had been known chiefly as a talker, a cigar and a derby hat, and these recordings showed a composer with a recognisable harmonic world. Critics have since placed him as the third of the great stride pianists, less powerful than Johnson and less famous than Waller, and the only one who was genuinely a miniaturist.","Play Debussy's Clair de lune first and the family resemblance will be obvious, then notice that Smith's left hand is still, unmistakably, stride. Listen for how rarely a chord resolves where you expect. Follow with Ellington's solo piano recordings to hear the inheritance passed on."],"ellington-mood-indigo":["Three minutes for a small Ellington unit rather than the full band: muted trumpet on top, muted trombone in the middle, clarinet underneath in its low register, the reverse of the usual order. That inversion, plus the close spacing, produces a soft blurred haze that sounds like one instrument rather than three. The tune is a short, slow, blues-tinged strain, stated, embellished and returned to, with Ellington's piano placing chords like punctuation and no solo ever raising its voice.","Recorded in New York in October 1930, when Ellington's band held the stand at the Cotton Club and radio wires carried it nationwide. It first appeared as 'Dreamy Blues'; Barney Bigard, the New Orleans clarinettist, brought in the melodic idea, and Irving Mills, Ellington's publisher and manager, attached a lyric and his own name to the credit. Ellington remembered letters arriving by the hundred asking for it. The band was still small, around a dozen players, and the record made his name beyond dancers.","Listen to the very bottom of the chord. Most dance-band scoring of 1930 put the clarinet on top, bright and agile; Ellington buried it below the brass, where its lowest notes are breathy and almost pitchless. The trumpet and trombone are both muted so their edges soften, and microphone placement did the rest. This voicing barely works in a hall. It works on a record, and Ellington was writing for the studio before most bandleaders knew they had one.","The record sold, and the tune became a standard Ellington re-orchestrated for decades, later for the full band with the saxophones added. Bigard's share of the authorship has been argued over ever since; most scholars now treat the theme as his, the setting as Ellington's, and Mills's name as the price of publication. Critics who wanted jazz hot heard a lullaby. Later writers heard the first jazz orchestration that could not simply be handed to another band.","Play the 1930 original before any later version and keep the volume low, because the blend depends on it. Try to pick out the clarinet; if you cannot, the effect is working. Then hear Ellington's 1950s remake with the full reed section to feel what he gained and lost. Ravel's Pavane is a good companion for the same soft, bruised colour."],"ellington-it-dont-mean-a-thing":["Three minutes built around a slogan. Ivie Anderson sings the title line and then scats the doo-wah answer the brass had been playing with plunger mutes for years; Joe 'Tricky Sam' Nanton takes a growling trombone chorus, the reeds answer, and the band rides out. The song's opening strain leans minor and the answering one brightens, so the record keeps tipping between shade and light. The tempo is brisk and absolutely even, four beats to the bar with no let-up.","Recorded in New York in February 1932, three years before the word swing became a marketing category. Ellington said the phrase came from Bubber Miley, the trumpeter who had built the band's growling sound in the 1920s and who was dying of tuberculosis that year. The Depression had gutted the record business; the Cotton Club broadcasts kept the band working. In a dance number, Ellington was making an argument: rhythm, not repertoire, is the measure of the music.","Ivie Anderson's scat is not decoration. The syllables she sings are a transcription of a brass technique, the plunger mute opening and closing over a note, turned back into a human voice, which is where it came from in the first place. Notice too that the band never plays the title phrase as a written riff. It is only ever sung, delivered by the one voice on the bandstand that cannot be muted.","It handed the swing era its motto three years early, and the title has been quoted ever since by people who have never heard the record. Ellington was later accused of reducing his art to a catchphrase; he pointed out that he had been playing that rhythm since the Cotton Club. Anderson, the band's first regular singer, is now recognised as a major stylist who was overshadowed by the soloists surrounding her.","Count along in four and notice how little the rhythm section does: an even pulse with almost no fills to help you. Then follow Nanton's trombone, which talks rather than sings. Afterwards, Shostakovich's Jazz Suite waltz from the same decade shows what Europe imagined swing sounded like, and how much it was missing."],"ellington-ko-ko":["Under three minutes, and the densest thing Ellington ever recorded. A minor blues, seven choruses deep: baritone saxophone and tom-toms lay the ground, Juan Tizol's valve trombone states the theme over sustained reeds, Tricky Sam Nanton takes two plunger-muted choruses, the saxophones climb in sliding blocks, and Jimmy Blanton's bass steps out of the rhythm section to answer the whole orchestra. Ellington interrupts with two bars of clashing piano. Then it stops, abruptly, with no ritard and no fade.","Recorded in Chicago in March 1940, at the first session of what collectors call the Blanton-Webster band, with Jimmy Blanton newly on bass and Ben Webster newly on tenor. Ellington had just signed back to Victor and was writing at a rate he never matched again. He had been planning a stage work about Black American history called Boola; Ko-Ko survived from it, and its subject, he said, was the slave dances held in Congo Square, New Orleans.","Blanton is the key. Before 1940 the string bass kept time; here it plays two-bar melodic answers in the gaps, in tune and in the clear, because Victor's engineers could finally capture it. Notice too that the saxophone section never plays a tune. It plays chords sliding chromatically upward beneath the brass, a device Ellington took from nobody. And the piano entry that sounds like a mistake is the only place the harmony openly cracks.","It sold modestly on release and is now routinely named the finest single record of the big-band era. Gunther Schuller's analysis in the 1960s argued that its construction bears comparison with composed concert music, which irritated writers who wanted jazz kept in its own category. The argument over whether Ko-Ko is composition or arrangement has never really ended. Ellington seems to have regarded the distinction as somebody else's problem.","Three minutes, so play it three times. First follow Nanton's muted trombone; second, follow only the bass; third, hear the saxophones as a single sliding mass. Then go to Stravinsky's Rite of Spring for the same trick of building enormous pressure from short repeated figures. Take the 1940 Victor original over any later concert version."],"ellington-concerto-for-cootie":["A concerto in everything but scale. Cootie Williams plays the whole record, changing mutes as he goes: a plunger-muted opening that speaks in half-words, a middle section open and high, then back behind the mute for the close. The orchestra does not accompany so much as frame him, holding long reed chords and answering in clipped brass punctuations. There is no improvised solo in the usual sense. The trumpet part is a worked-out role that only one player in the world could fill.","Recorded in Chicago in March 1940, weeks after Ko-Ko, for Victor. Williams had been with Ellington since 1929, having replaced Bubber Miley, and had spent a decade learning plunger technique alongside Tricky Sam Nanton. Within the year he left to join Benny Goodman, a departure notable enough that Raymond Scott wrote a lament called 'When Cootie Left the Duke'. Ellington's practice of writing for named individuals rather than for chairs in a section has no real parallel in band music.","The melody outlived the record. In 1943 Bob Russell fitted words to it and it became the ballad 'Do Nothin' Till You Hear from Me', a hit most listeners never connect with this instrumental. Listen for the point where Williams drops the mute: the open sound is the event, more than anything happening in the harmony. Ellington builds the piece out of timbre the way another composer builds one out of modulation.","The French critic Andre Hodeir devoted a celebrated chapter to it, arguing that every note is placed and that the piece answers the question of whether jazz can be composed. Some musicians objected that the analysis mistook a worked-out routine for a score. What nobody disputes is that Williams is irreplaceable: later Ellington trumpeters played the part, and it was never quite the same piece again.","Follow the mute. Notice how much articulation Williams gets from a rubber plunger held against the bell, almost consonants. Then hear the same man on 'Do Nothin' Till You Hear from Me' to feel how a lyric domesticates the tune. Ravel's Bolero is the obvious companion: one idea, carried entirely by changing colour."],"ellington-take-the-a-train":["Three minutes, and the most recognisable opening in big-band music: a saxophone riff over a rocking piano vamp, rising in one clean arc. Ray Nance takes the trumpet solo, muted first, then open for a shouting second chorus, and the band answers in stacked brass. The form is a thirty-two-bar song in the standard shape, at an easy jogging tempo that never pushes. Nothing about it is complicated. Everything about it is placed exactly where it needs to be.","Billy Strayhorn wrote it, not Ellington. Strayhorn had joined the organisation in 1939, and the title came from the directions Ellington gave him for reaching his Harlem apartment by the new A subway line. In January 1941 a dispute between the broadcasters and the publishing society ASCAP barred Ellington's own catalogue from the airwaves, so the band urgently needed theme music it could legally play. They recorded Strayhorn's tune in February and used it for the next thirty years.","The melody contains a note that does not belong to its chord, a raised fourth early in the phrase, and that is why the tune sounds modern rather than merely cheerful. Strayhorn nearly threw the sketch away for sounding too much like Fletcher Henderson. Listen also to Ray Nance: this was his first recorded solo with the band, and every later Ellington trumpeter learned it note for note, as though it had been written down.","It became the band's signature and, for many listeners, the sound of the swing era itself, which has made it easy to underrate. Strayhorn's contribution to Ellington's output was obscured for decades by contracts and by Ellington's public persona; scholarship since the 1990s, and David Hajdu's biography in particular, has reset the credit. Collectors generally prefer the compact 1941 studio take to the long later concert versions with their extended Nance features.","Start with the 1941 Victor record, then hear a 1960s live version to see how far the band stretched it. Listen for the piano introduction: Ellington plays a few bars nobody would miss if they were cut, and the record would be poorer without them. Follow Nance's second chorus for the moment the mute comes off."],"basie-one-oclock-jump":["A head arrangement, nothing written down, running about three minutes and consisting of riffs stacked over the blues. Basie opens alone at the piano, spare and boogie-flavoured, then the rhythm section settles into an even four: Freddie Green's guitar on every beat, Walter Page's walking bass, Jo Jones keeping time on the hi-hat rather than the bass drum. Solos pass from tenor to trombone to trumpet, the key changes partway through, and brass and reeds trade riffs to the end.","Recorded for Decca in New York in July 1937, a year after John Hammond heard the band on a late-night radio wire from the Reno Club in Kansas City and brought it east. The tune had an unbroadcastable working title and an announcer renamed it for the hour. Basie's men had come up in the territory bands of the southwest, where a night's work meant spinning riffs for hours; the record is that practice compressed. It stayed the band's theme for half a century.","Listen to what the drummer is not doing. Jo Jones moved the pulse off the bass drum and onto the hi-hat, so the beat floats instead of thudding, the single change that separates Kansas City rhythm from everything before it and seeds everything after. Underneath, Freddie Green plays four chords to the bar and is never amplified and never given a solo. The band's swing is a rhythm-section invention that no arranger could have written.","It sold steadily rather than spectacularly and became a standard almost at once, recorded by Goodman, Shaw and Harry James. Basie's Decca contract paid a flat fee with no royalties, a piece of exploitation now inseparable from the record's story. Critics have argued ever since about whether a head arrangement counts as composition. The Basie band's answer, for the next fifteen years, was to keep not writing things down.","Ignore the soloists on the first pass and listen only to guitar, bass and drums; the record is a rhythm-section demonstration with decoration on top. Then follow Lester Young's tenor, light and behind the beat. Compare it with Ellington's Ko-Ko from three years later to hear two opposite ideas of what a big band is for."],"basie-jumpin-at-the-woodside":["Three minutes at a fast but unhurried clip. The riff is a few notes long and never develops; the record's life is in the solos and in the way the ensemble riffs behind them, adding layers until the last chorus is three separate figures going at once. Lester Young's tenor chorus is the centre, dry and light-toned, phrasing across the barlines as though the bar were only a suggestion. The rhythm section does not vary its pulse for a single beat.","Recorded for Decca in New York in August 1938. The Woodside Hotel in Harlem was where the Basie band stayed and rehearsed when they came to New York, in rooms too small to hold the whole orchestra. By then the band was a fixture at the Famous Door on 52nd Street, where an air-conditioning unit paid for by John Hammond kept the club open through the summer and a radio wire carried Basie to the rest of the country.","Count the choruses and notice that the arrangement, such as it is, works purely by addition: each time a soloist finishes, another riff is stacked behind the next one, and nothing is ever taken away again. That is a Kansas City device, learned in cutting contests where a band had to keep raising the temperature for dancers. Lester Young's entrance is the moment most tenor players of the following decade set out to copy.","One of the records that convinced New York that Kansas City had something it did not. Musicians treated it as a textbook; Charlie Parker, still a teenager back in Kansas City, is said to have learned Young's solo by heart. Critics who valued written arrangement thought the Basie band crude and said so in print. The judgment reversed completely inside twenty years and has not moved since.","Play it loud and try to hear the final chorus as three tunes at once: saxophone riff, brass riff, and soloist over both. Then isolate Lester Young's solo and sing it, which is how a generation learned it. Basie's later Roulette records show the same band with the riffs written down, polished and a little tamed."],"basie-atomic-mr-basie":["Eleven tracks, around forty minutes, all arranged by Neal Hefti for the band collectors call the New Testament Basie. The sound is enormous and exact: brass that enters like a door slamming, saxophone sections in perfect unison, dynamics that drop to a whisper and climb back inside four bars. 'Li'l Darlin'' is the famous one, taken slower than any band had dared, the melody almost falling over. Basie himself plays less than anyone on the record, a few notes, placed.","Recorded for Roulette in New York in 1958. Basie had broken up the band in 1950 and rebuilt it in 1952 around written arrangements instead of riffs, hiring Hefti, Ernie Wilkins and Frank Foster to supply them. The LP was now the unit of jazz, high fidelity was the selling point, and the sleeve carried a photograph of a nuclear explosion, a piece of 1958 taste that later pressings quietly dropped along with the title.","Hefti's trick is space. Listen to how often the whole band stops dead for a beat before a brass entry; the silence is what makes the chord land. On 'Li'l Darlin'' the tempo is so slow that the players cannot ride the beat and must place each note deliberately, which means the entire band has to agree on exactly how late to be. Basie's piano fills are the joke: three notes where a lesser bandleader would play thirty.","It sold, won the band a new audience, and fixed the public image of Basie as master of the big-band shout chorus. Older listeners who preferred the loose 1930s band heard the Hefti charts as slick, and the argument between Old Testament and New Testament Basie is still live among collectors. The engineering, unusually clean for 1958, is part of why the record became a hi-fi demonstration disc.","Start with 'Li'l Darlin'' and resist the urge to speed it up in your head. Then 'Whirly-Bird' for the opposite extreme. Listen for the endings: Basie's band stops together in a way almost no other ensemble manages. Bernstein's Symphonic Dances, from the same year, shows a classical composer chasing the same brass sound."],"goodman-after-youve-gone":["Three musicians and no arrangement: Benny Goodman's clarinet, Teddy Wilson's piano and Gene Krupa's drums, mostly brushes, on a song from 1918 taken at a gallop. After the big bands the texture is startlingly transparent; you hear every note of the piano's left hand and every consonant of the clarinet's tonguing. Goodman plays a long, fluent chorus, Wilson answers with the even running lines that made him the model for a generation, and the three of them scramble to the finish together.","Recorded in New York in July 1935, weeks before the Palomar Ballroom engagement that started the swing craze. John Hammond had put Goodman and Wilson together at a private party first. The trio existed partly because a record could be made and sold when the same group could not appear on a stage in most of America with a Black pianist in it. Goodman began using the trio in public the following year and refused to drop Wilson; Lionel Hampton made it a quartet in 1936.","There is no bass. Wilson's left hand carries the entire harmonic floor, which is why the record sounds so clear and why it needs a pianist of exactly his precision to work at all. Krupa plays brushes on a snare for most of it, almost inaudible until suddenly he is not. The absence is deliberate: Goodman wanted a chamber group inside a dance-band career, and this is the format he kept returning to for fifty years.","Its importance is usually stated in social terms, as one of the first widely sold records by a racially mixed jazz group, and Goodman's willingness to tour with it mattered more than the disc itself. Musically, listeners at the time were startled by how little sound three players needed. Goodman's own reputation has fluctuated since: praised as organiser and technician, doubted as an improviser, and defended by clarinettists who have tried to play this chorus.","Listen to Wilson's left hand and ask yourself where the bass went. Then follow Goodman's articulation, every note started cleanly at that speed, which is the thing clarinettists mention first. Copland's Clarinet Concerto, written for Goodman a decade later, is the natural next step, and the Quintette du Hot Club is the European answer to a jazz group with no drums or horns."],"goodman-sing-sing-sing":["Eight and a half minutes spread across both sides of a twelve-inch record, at a time when three minutes was the limit. It opens with Gene Krupa's tom-toms alone, a rolling figure that never stops for the whole performance; Harry James takes a trumpet chorus, Goodman a long clarinet one, and the band shouts riffs in between. Louis Prima's tune is barely a tune, a repeated minor phrase, and the record is really a drum feature with an orchestra attached to it.","Recorded for Victor in Hollywood in 1937, at the height of the Goodman band's fame, when the King of Swing was a radio brand and teenagers danced in cinema aisles. Jimmy Mundy's arrangement stitched Prima's tune to other material and let the band stretch out past every commercial convention. Krupa had become the first drummer to be a star in his own right, a development older bandleaders regarded as a category error and dancers regarded as the whole point.","Partway through, the band plays a riff that is not from this tune at all: it is 'Christopher Columbus', a Fletcher Henderson band hit from the year before, spliced in by the arranger. Listen also to Krupa's tom-tom figure. He is not keeping time so much as playing a melody on unpitched drums. The famous quiet piano solo is not on this record; that happened at Carnegie Hall six months later and is a different performance.","The biggest instrumental hit of the swing era and, almost immediately, the thing serious critics used as evidence against it: loud, long, and built on a single riff. Krupa's showmanship divided musicians, and he left the band within a year after a public row with Goodman. Later writers have been kinder, hearing a genuinely radical piece of formal risk, since nobody else was making eight-minute dance records for a mass audience.","Play it at volume and follow only the drums; the rest is scenery. Then listen for the quiet passage before the final chorus, where Goodman plays almost alone over the toms, the record's best moment and the one people forget. Afterwards, the 1938 Carnegie Hall version, which is longer, wilder and has the piano solo."],"goodman-carnegie-hall-1938":["Close to two hours of a live concert, released twelve years after the night it happened. The programme moves from a sketched history of jazz through the Goodman band's hits to a jam session on 'Honeysuckle Rose' with players borrowed from the Ellington and Basie orchestras, and ends with 'Sing, Sing, Sing' stretched past twelve minutes. The sound is one microphone's worth, thin and boxy with the audience close, and the playing is faster and rougher than any studio version.","16 January 1938, in a hall built for symphony orchestras that had never presented a jazz band. The promoter's idea was half publicity stunt; Goodman reportedly asked how long an intermission they wanted and was told the same as Toscanini's. Johnny Hodges, Harry Carney and Cootie Williams came over from Ellington's band, Lester Young and Buck Clayton from Basie's, sharing a Carnegie stage in a segregated country. Acetate discs of the evening sat in a cupboard until 1950.","Jess Stacy's piano solo on the closing 'Sing, Sing, Sing' was not planned. Goodman simply pointed at him when the arrangement ran out, and Stacy played two minutes of quiet, almost impressionist music that has nearly nothing to do with the number surrounding it. He was never asked to repeat it. Listen for the moment the band drops away and a single piano is left alone in a hall holding three thousand people.","It was received at the time as the night jazz entered the concert hall, a framing that irritated musicians who had been playing this music for twenty years without anyone's permission. The 1950 release was among the first double LPs and sold enormously. Modern transfers from the original acetates have restored missing announcements and corrected pitch; collectors argue over which one is least processed.","Do not start at the beginning. Go to 'Sing, Sing, Sing' and wait for Stacy, then go back for the 'Honeysuckle Rose' jam to hear Lester Young and Johnny Hodges in the same room. Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue at Aeolian Hall in 1924 was the first attempt at this argument; this is the night that settled it."],"fletcher-henderson-king-porter-stomp":["Three minutes containing the blueprint for the next fifteen years of American dance music. Jelly Roll Morton's old piano rag is rebuilt as a conversation between two teams: the saxophones play a smooth phrase, the brass answer with a stabbing one, back and forth, until the whole band joins for a final shout chorus over a rocking trombone figure. Coleman Hawkins takes a tenor chorus. The rhythm section keeps an even four with no ornament whatsoever.","Recorded in New York in 1933, one of several versions Henderson made of the tune across a decade. He was a chemistry graduate from Georgia who had come north for postgraduate study, found the laboratories shut to him, and ended up leading the best band in Harlem at the Roseland Ballroom, with Louis Armstrong passing through it in the 1920s. By 1933 his business was collapsing; two years later he was selling arrangements to Benny Goodman, whose version of this one helped start the swing era.","The idea that changed everything here is not a melody but a layout: set reeds and brass in opposition and write for the sections rather than for the players. Don Redman had begun it in this band in the 1920s and Henderson perfected it. Listen for the trombone figure under the last chorus, a riff repeated without variation while everything above it climbs. Goodman's 1935 hit is essentially this chart played tighter and recorded better.","Henderson's own records sold poorly and his band was famously undisciplined; the money and most of the credit went to Goodman, who at least said publicly where his arrangements came from. Whether Henderson was exploited or rescued is still argued. What is not argued is the authorship of the method: historians of the big band trace almost every swing chart back through him, and through Redman, to this pattern.","Play Henderson's version and Goodman's 1935 one back to back, the same chart in two different worlds. Listen for the call and response and try to hear the band as two choirs rather than one ensemble. Then Morton's original piano version, which sounds like it comes from another century, because it does."],"fletcher-henderson-wrappin-it-up":["Under three minutes, and the most polished thing Henderson wrote for himself. The reeds open with a long, smoothly harmonised line that moves in even notes rather than riffs, the brass answer with clipped figures, and the two exchange places across the chorus with an ease that makes the writing sound improvised. There are short solos, but the interest lies in the scoring: four saxophones moving together as one flexible voice, breathing in the same places.","Recorded in New York in September 1934, near the end of Henderson's time as a bandleader. His orchestra had lost Louis Armstrong years earlier and Coleman Hawkins that spring, to Europe, and the payroll was failing. Within months he would be writing for Goodman rather than for himself. The subtitle, 'The Lindy Glide', names the dance it was made for: the Savoy Ballroom crowd set the tempos that the whole industry then followed.","Count the saxophone line at the start and notice that it does not repeat. Most swing writing is built from riffs, short figures said over and over; here Henderson writes a long melody for the section instead, the way an orchestrator writes for strings. It is much the harder thing to do, and the reason arrangers still study this chart. The brass never once play the same rhythm as the reeds.","Barely noticed on release and never a hit, it survives as a teaching piece: arrangers from the 1940s onward cite it as the point where section writing became supple. Henderson's reputation has been rebuilt slowly, partly by scholars working out which of 'his' charts were actually written by his brother Horace or by Benny Carter. On this one the hand is generally agreed to be his own.","Listen to the saxophones alone and ignore everything else; the record is a demonstration of what four reeds can do when they breathe together. Then compare Milhaud's La creation du monde, a European composer writing jazz down a decade earlier, to hear what notation captures and what it cannot."],"chick-webb-stompin-at-the-savoy":["Three minutes at a comfortable dancing tempo, with a melody so well made that it stopped sounding like a band number and became a standard. Edgar Sampson, the band's alto player, wrote and arranged it; the reeds carry the tune, the brass punch the corners, and Webb drives from behind with a snap on the rim and a rolling press on the toms. Nobody shows off. It is a working dance chart played by the best band in Harlem.","Recorded in New York in 1934 by the house band of the Savoy Ballroom on Lenox Avenue, the largest integrated dance floor in America, where two bandstands meant the music never stopped. Webb, left small and in pain by childhood spinal tuberculosis, played a kit built to reach him and had held the room since 1931, beating every visiting orchestra that came to challenge him. The tune is named after the building it was made in.","The credit reads Sampson, Goodman and Webb, with a lyric by Andy Razaf, but the writing is Sampson's; the other names arrived with the publishing deal. Listen to Webb's bass drum, used for accents rather than for time, the opposite of every white swing drummer of the period, and to the way he leaves the last beat of a phrase empty so the band falls into the next one. Krupa said openly that he had copied him.","It became one of the most recorded tunes of the era and is now better known through Goodman's and Ella Fitzgerald's versions than through Webb's own. Webb died in 1939 at thirty-four, before the LP could preserve him properly, so his reputation rests on a handful of Decca sides and the testimony of other drummers. Critics rediscovering him almost always arrive by way of Krupa and Buddy Rich.","Listen for the drums, mixed low and doing everything. Then hear Goodman's version for the tune played faster and cleaner, and Ella's for what a singer finds in it. Webb's 'Harlem Congo' shows the same band with the leash off, which is the other half of the picture."],"chick-webb-harlem-congo":["Under three minutes at a hard, fast tempo, and the best surviving evidence of what Chick Webb's band sounded like when it was trying to destroy an opponent. The brass play in tight stacked harmony, the saxophones answer, and every few bars Webb fills the gap with a drum break that is short, exact and placed so the band lands on the other side without a wobble. The last chorus is a shouted riff over drumming that never once loses the beat.","Recorded for Decca in New York in late 1937, months after Webb's band and Benny Goodman's played a public battle at the Savoy Ballroom in front of a crowd that filled the building and spilled into Lenox Avenue. Webb was the house drummer in a room where bands were judged by dancers rather than by critics, and where losing meant losing the job. He was already seriously ill. The record was made to sell a reputation earned live.","Listen to how Webb ends his breaks. A drum break is easy to start and very hard to finish; he consistently places the last stroke a fraction early, so the band's re-entry feels like a door opening rather than a rescue. Because of his size he played a kit built to reach him, with custom pedals. What sounds like sheer power is almost entirely placement.","Never a hit, and rarely anthologised beside the ballads that made the band's name, it is the track drummers name when asked why Webb mattered. The Goodman battle has hardened into folklore with both camps claiming victory; the contemporary Black press mostly gave it to Webb. His early death and thin recorded legacy mean the whole argument rests on a few sides like this one.","Follow the breaks and keep counting the beats through them; the tempo never moves. Then hear Krupa on 'Sing, Sing, Sing' from the same year for the showier, slower and far more famous version of the same idea. Any clean Decca transfer will do, since the drums are what the remastering is for."],"artie-shaw-begin-the-beguine":["Three minutes of a Cole Porter show tune that nobody had been able to make work. Jerry Gray's arrangement strips the long, wandering melody back to its skeleton and sets it over a soft Latin-tinged pulse; the saxophones state it, Shaw's clarinet floats above the section in its clear upper register, and muted brass answer before the clarinet takes it home. Nothing is fast and nothing is loud, and the song's extraordinary length passes completely unnoticed.","Recorded for Bluebird in New York in July 1938. Shaw had just rebuilt his band after abandoning an earlier group built around a string quartet, and this was meant to be the throwaway side; 'Indian Love Call' on the flip was expected to be the hit. Porter's song had flopped in the 1935 show Jubilee and was considered unsingable. The record sold in the millions and made Shaw, overnight, the only real rival Goodman had.","Porter's melody runs to more than a hundred bars, three times the normal length of a popular song, which is precisely why singers stumbled on it. Gray's solution was to stop treating it as a song at all and treat it as an instrumental theme, cutting the words out of the equation. Listen to the rhythm too: it is not a beguine. The Caribbean pattern Porter asked for has been smoothed into a swing a ballroom could actually dance to.","It made Shaw rich and he spent thirty years resenting it, walking out on his band in 1939 and calling his own audience morons in print. Critics then and since have divided over whether the record is a triumph of arrangement or a dilution of Porter; Porter himself was reportedly delighted. It remains one of the best-selling records of the era and the line in every obituary Shaw ever received.","Listen for the clarinet's entrance high above the saxophones, the sound Shaw is remembered for and cooler than Goodman's. Then find a sung version to feel how much melody Gray had to tame. Ella's Cole Porter Songbook, twenty years later, gives the composer's side of the argument back."],"artie-shaw-star-dust":["Three minutes, and among the most admired band records of the era, chiefly for three solos in a row. Billy Butterfield's trumpet states Hoagy Carmichael's melody, warm and unhurried; Shaw's clarinet takes the middle; Jack Jenney plays a trombone chorus that climbs to a held high note musicians still talk about. Behind them sits a string section, unusual in a swing band, and a rhythm section that barely moves. It ends quietly, on a clarinet note suspended over the strings.","Recorded for Victor in Hollywood in October 1940. Shaw had quit the music business the year before, gone to Mexico, and come back with a new orchestra that added strings and a French horn, which his fans regarded as a betrayal and he regarded as the entire point. Carmichael's tune was already a decade old and had been recorded hundreds of times. Shaw's arrangement treats it as a ballad to be listened to rather than a number to dance to.","Jenney's trombone chorus rises to a note near the top of the instrument's range and holds it without a break in the tone, which is why trombonists learn this solo before they learn most others. Listen to what the strings actually do underneath: nothing melodic, only sustained chords, so they function as a cushion rather than a voice. Shaw had learned from the failure of his 1936 string-quartet band that strings in jazz must stay out of the way.","It sold well and is regularly voted the finest version of a song with hundreds of rivals. The strings remain the argument: to jazz purists they are the moment the swing band began softening into the easy-listening orchestras of the 1950s, and Shaw is often blamed for a trend he only accelerated. His own view, stated repeatedly and at length, was that the purists had never understood what he was attempting.","Play it late and quiet. Count the three solos and pick a favourite; most listeners choose Jenney. Then compare Louis Armstrong's 1931 recording of the same song for the opposite approach, and Ravel's Pavane for the same nostalgia treated as a colour rather than a mood."],"hampton-flying-home":["Three minutes, a riff tune at a medium-fast tempo, and one saxophone solo that changed popular music. Lionel Hampton's big band states the theme in unison; then Illinois Jacquet plays two choruses of tenor saxophone that abandon melody altogether for repeated high notes, squeals and honks, building to a shriek the band answers with a shout chorus. Hampton's vibraphone frames the whole thing. The performance is designed to make a room lose control, and it does.","Recorded for Decca in New York in 1942. Hampton had written the tune with Benny Goodman in 1939 for the Goodman Sextet, where Charlie Christian played it on electric guitar; leaving to form his own big band, Hampton took it with him. Jacquet was barely twenty. The record sold steadily for a decade, was played at every dance the band worked, and became the number audiences demanded before it had been announced.","What Jacquet plays is not a jazz solo in the 1942 sense. It has almost no harmonic movement; it repeats one high note in rhythm, over and over, and takes its effect from sheer physical persistence. Every rhythm-and-blues tenor player of the following fifteen years, and through them every rock and roll saxophonist, is copying this chorus. Listen for the moment the band stops arranging and simply riffs behind him.","Jazz critics disliked it and called it exhibitionism; the same solo was, within a few years, the standard by which honking tenor players were judged, and Jacquet spent a career both proud of it and boxed in by it. Historians now treat the record as one of the clearest bridges from swing to rhythm and blues, and Hampton's band as the place where the transition happened first.","Listen for the exact moment the solo stops developing and starts repeating; that is the invention, not a failure of imagination. Then hear the 1939 Goodman Sextet version with Charlie Christian for the same tune as chamber music. Any decent Decca transfer works. The point is the volume."],"billie-holiday-summertime":["Three minutes, a small pick-up group, and the first jazz singer to treat Gershwin's lullaby as a blues. Holiday enters after a short instrumental introduction, sings a single chorus almost without ornament, and stays behind the beat throughout, so the words arrive a fraction later than you expect them. Bunny Berigan's trumpet answers her phrases. There is no drama and no rubato; the whole effect comes from timing, and from a voice with a small range used exactly.","Recorded in New York in July 1936, a year after Porgy and Bess had closed at a loss on Broadway. Holiday was twenty-one, working the clubs of 52nd Street and Harlem, and recording for the jukebox trade under contracts that paid a session fee and nothing afterwards. Songs were handed to the musicians in the studio and read down in a few takes. Artie Shaw, not yet a bandleader of any consequence, played clarinet on the date.","Listen to where the words fall. Gershwin wrote a slow, even melody; Holiday delays almost every phrase and then catches up, which turns a lullaby into something withheld. She also flattens the tune's leaps, staying inside a narrow band of pitches, a limitation she converted into a method. This is often described as the first popular hit the song ever had, two years before it became a standard.","It sold well enough to help establish her, and the small-group sides she made in these years are now regarded as one of the great runs in recorded music. At the time they were disposable jukebox product and she was paid accordingly, with no royalty on any of them. Critics still argue over whether her late, damaged voice or this young light one is the real Holiday; the answer usually depends on the listener's age.","Play it beside a sung version from the opera and hear the difference between a soprano's line and a jazz singer's. Then listen only to her entrances, counting four and noticing that she is always late. Ella's later reading of the same song is brighter and squarer, which tells you exactly what Holiday is doing."],"billie-holiday-strange-fruit":["Three minutes, and for most of them almost nothing happens. A muted trumpet plays a long sour introduction over piano; then Holiday sings twelve lines about a lynching, twice through, with no repeated chorus and no rise in volume anywhere. Frankie Newton's small band holds sustained chords and stays out of the way. She ends on the word 'crop', held and then simply dropped. There is no applause on the record and there was none in the room.","Abel Meeropol, a Jewish schoolteacher in the Bronx who published as Lewis Allan, wrote it as a poem after seeing a photograph of a lynching. Holiday began performing it in 1939 at Cafe Society in Greenwich Village, the first integrated nightclub in New York, where it closed every set: the waiters stopped serving, the lights went down to a single spot on her face, and nothing followed it. Columbia refused to record it, so Milt Gabler issued it on his small Commodore label.","Listen to the introduction, which takes most of the first minute, an extraordinary proportion of a three-minute record and there purely to make the audience wait. The arrangement gives Holiday no instrumental support at the ends of her lines, so the silences belong to her. She never raises her voice or bends a note for effect. The only theatrical decision in the performance is the pause before the final word, and it is the reason the record works.","Banned from many radio stations, it sold steadily anyway and made Holiday a political figure she had not set out to become; the Federal Bureau of Narcotics harassed her for years afterwards, and the song is part of why. Some critics have always found it too explicit to be art, and said the accusation applies to the poem rather than the singing. Time magazine named it the song of the century in 1999.","Listen once without reading the words and once with them in front of you. Notice that she does not act: the restraint is the performance. Then hear her 1944 remake and the late versions, where the voice is rougher and the effect different. Schubert's Erlkonig is the nearest thing in another tradition, one voice, one horror, no rescue."],"billie-holiday-god-bless-the-child":["Three minutes in an unusual shape: a sung passage that states a proverb, then a refrain that keeps circling back to the title phrase. Holiday's voice is at its lightest and most controlled here, the band a small swing group with a gentle two-beat feel, the tempo slow enough to let her sit behind every phrase. The melody moves within a narrow compass and turns on one rising interval on the word 'child'. The solos are short. The record belongs entirely to the singer.","Recorded for Okeh in New York in May 1941. Holiday and the lyricist Arthur Herzog Jr. wrote it together; her account was that it came out of an argument with her mother about money, and that the phrase 'God bless the child that's got his own' was something she had said in anger and then remembered. She was twenty-six, at the height of her technical control, and had just become a headline act rather than somebody's band singer.","The song is not a blues and not quite a standard thirty-two-bar popular song. Its shape, a text stated and then returned to, comes from gospel and ultimately from the sermon. Listen to how Holiday sings the title differently every time it comes round, a little later, a little flatter, never with emphasis. The lyric's bitterness lives entirely in that timing; sung straight, the same words read as a hymn.","A modest seller that grew into one of the most covered songs in American music, taken up by singers from every tradition and often by people who have never heard her version. The authorship has been disputed: Herzog later claimed most of the words, Holiday's ghostwritten autobiography claims the idea, and the likeliest answer is that both are partly right. It is now the Holiday song that people who own no Holiday records can sing.","Follow the title phrase each time it returns and listen for what she changes. Then hear a gospel version to find where the tune's shape came from, and Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige for the same sermon logic written out for an orchestra. Start with the 1941 Okeh original rather than a later live reading."],"ella-fitzgerald-a-tisket-a-tasket":["Two and a half minutes of a nursery rhyme turned into a dance record. Ella Fitzgerald, twenty-one, sings a playground chant about a girl who loses her yellow basket, over Chick Webb's band, with the reeds answering her in the gaps and Webb's drums keeping a light, skipping beat. Near the end she improvises a spoken exchange with the musicians. The voice is clear, quick, perfectly in tune and completely unshadowed by anything that has happened to it.","Recorded for Decca in New York in May 1938. Fitzgerald had won an amateur night at the Apollo in 1934 and been taken on by Webb, who became her guardian. She and the band's arranger Van Alexander built the record out of a rhyme she remembered from her childhood in Yonkers. It sold enormously, became the biggest hit either of them ever had, and made her a star a year before Webb died and left her his orchestra to lead.","The joke is the arrangement's straight face. Webb's band plays it as a proper swing chart, with section writing and a real shout chorus, while the lyric is about a lost basket, and the gap between the two is what made it funny in 1938. Listen for the band members answering her in spoken asides: call and response, the oldest device in African-American music, deployed here on a nursery rhyme.","It made Fitzgerald famous and then followed her around: for a decade she was the singer of novelty numbers, and critics who admired Holiday's weight found her shallow by comparison. The Songbook records of the 1950s reversed that judgment completely. Webb's band has been undervalued by the same story, remembered as her backing group rather than as the finest swing orchestra in Harlem.","Hear it first as a piece of comic timing. Then jump twenty years to her Cole Porter Songbook and hear the same voice with all the sugar taken out. Listen for Webb behind her throughout; the drumming is far better than the song strictly requires."],"ella-fitzgerald-cole-porter-songbook":["Two LPs, thirty-two songs, close to two hours. Buddy Bregman's arrangements are light and mostly orchestral, strings and woodwinds over a rhythm section, and they stay behind the voice; there is very little improvising anywhere on the record. Fitzgerald sings the melodies as written, with the introductory verses most singers cut put back in, from 'Night and Day' and 'Begin the Beguine' to songs nobody had recorded in years. The tone is even from bottom to top and the diction needs no lyric sheet.","Recorded in Los Angeles in 1956, the first release on Norman Granz's new Verve label, which he founded largely in order to record her properly. Fitzgerald had spent twenty years at Decca singing whatever the label put in front of her. Granz's idea was to treat the great American songwriters as a repertoire worth documenting whole, at exactly the moment rock and roll was about to turn that repertoire into history. Porter, still living, is said to have remarked on her diction.","The restored verses are the point of the project. Porter wrote introductory sections for most of his songs that nightclub singers routinely dropped, and hearing them changes the songs, because the verse usually sets up the joke or the regret the refrain then delivers. Listen also to how little Fitzgerald ornaments. She was the most fluent improviser of her generation and here she almost never departs from the written tune, deliberately.","It sold beyond anything else in her career, ran to seven more Songbooks across eight years, and effectively invented the idea of the Great American Songbook as a canon. Jazz critics have complained ever since that the records are too polite and that Bregman's charts are the weakest in the series. The counter-argument is that the project was preservation rather than performance, and that on those terms it succeeded completely.","Do not play it straight through. Take 'Ev'ry Time We Say Goodbye' and 'Night and Day' first, then one song you have never heard. Compare her 'Begin the Beguine' with Artie Shaw's instrumental to hear the words put back in. Schubert's Winterreise is a distant relation: songs treated as a repertoire rather than as a set of hits."],"ella-fitzgerald-mack-the-knife-berlin":["A live set of about forty minutes, recorded in one night with a quartet rather than an orchestra. The famous track comes late: Fitzgerald announces that nobody has recorded 'Mack the Knife' as a girl, then loses the words after a couple of verses, sings about having lost them, invents replacement lyrics naming Louis Armstrong and Bobby Darin, and finishes with a scat impression of Armstrong's voice that brings the hall down. The rest of the set is swinging, unfussy and beautifully sung.","Recorded at the Deutschlandhalle in Berlin in February 1960, on one of Norman Granz's package tours, fifteen years after the war and a year before the Wall. Kurt Weill's song came from The Threepenny Opera of 1928, written a few miles away, and by 1960 it had become an American pop hit by way of Bobby Darin. Fitzgerald was singing a Berlin song back to Berlin, in English, from a memory that turned out to be incomplete.","The mistake is the record. She loses the lyric partway through and never recovers it, and what follows is minutes of improvised text delivered in strict time and perfect tune; she does not drop a single beat while inventing. Listen for the moment she sings that she is making a wreck of the song. The audience laughs, and she uses the laugh as a rhythmic rest. It won a Grammy, for a song she did not know.","It became the best known live jazz vocal record of its era and the standard example of what a great singer does when everything goes wrong. Collectors note that the album has been reissued in expanded and resequenced forms and that the original LP was edited; the 1960 sequence is still what most people mean by it. Weill scholars tend to be less charmed, since almost nothing of Brecht's text survives the encounter.","Go straight to 'Mack the Knife', then back to the start of the set. Listen to the rhythm section, which never flinches through the improvised verses. Then hear the song in German on a Weill recording to find out what the words actually say; the distance between the two versions is the whole point."],"lester-young-shoe-shine-boy":["Just over three minutes by a five-piece group drawn from the Count Basie band, and the record on which a new way of playing the tenor saxophone arrives fully formed. Lester Young takes two choruses: a light, dry tone with almost no vibrato, phrases long enough to cross the barlines, and a rhythmic placement that floats slightly behind the beat. Basie plays sparse piano, Jo Jones brushes the time, Carl Smith takes a trumpet chorus. Two complete takes survive.","Recorded in Chicago in October 1936 for Vocalion under the name Jones-Smith Incorporated, because Basie's band was contractually tied elsewhere and the session had to be disguised. John Hammond organised it after hearing the Basie band on a late-night radio wire out of Kansas City. It was the first time Young had ever been recorded. He was twenty-seven, and had already been pushed out of Fletcher Henderson's band for refusing to sound like Coleman Hawkins.","Compare the two surviving takes. Young improvises entirely different choruses on each, both completely shaped, which is the evidence usually offered that he thought in paragraphs rather than in stored phrases. Listen to his vibrato, or its absence: every tenor player of the 1930s used a wide one, and Young's straight tone was heard at the time as thin and unmanly. Within ten years it was simply the sound of modern jazz.","The session sold nothing and was noticed only by musicians. Its reputation grew through the people it converted, Charlie Parker and Stan Getz and Dexter Gordon and eventually the whole cool school. The old division between Young's school and Hawkins's is one of the few genuine stylistic schisms in jazz, and this three-minute side is where the second side of it begins.","Play both takes back to back; that is the whole lesson. Then put on Hawkins's 'Body and Soul' from three years later to hear the two opposite answers to the same instrument. Listen for how long Young's phrases run before he takes a breath, and notice he never seems short of one."],"lester-young-lester-leaps-in":["Three minutes by a small group drawn from inside the Basie band, built on the chord sequence of Gershwin's 'I Got Rhythm' and on almost nothing else. After a brief riff statement, Young plays chorus after chorus, trading eight-bar phrases with Basie's piano, which answers with two or three notes at a time. The rhythm section never varies. The tune as such lasts a few seconds; the record is a demonstration that a set of chords can serve as a form.","Recorded for Vocalion in New York in September 1939 by a septet billed as Count Basie's Kansas City Seven. Small groups drawn out of big bands were a commercial format, cheaper to record and good for jukeboxes, and they gave soloists room the full orchestra could not. Gershwin's 1930 song had by then become the second most borrowed harmonic template in jazz after the blues, used so routinely that musicians simply called the practice rhythm changes.","Listen to the exchanges with Basie. The pianist answers full saxophone phrases with a handful of notes and a silence, and the silence is the substance: the Basie rhythm section was built to leave holes in exactly this way. Notice too that Young almost never lands on the obvious note at the end of a phrase, stopping a beat early or a beat late, which is why the line sounds like talking. None of it is written down.","It became the standard vehicle for jam sessions and the piece bebop players used a few years later to prove their fluency; Charlie Parker's generation built dozens of new tunes on the same chords. Contemporary critics barely covered it at all. It is now cited as a hinge record, the swing era's finest soloist playing, in effect, the first bebop set of variations on a borrowed ground.","Follow the trade-offs between saxophone and piano and count how few notes Basie plays. Then hear Gershwin's original song to find the chords underneath. After that, any bebop recording on rhythm changes will sound like a direct continuation of this one, because that is exactly what it is."],"hawkins-body-and-soul":["Three minutes in which the melody is played once, in the first few bars, and then never returns. Coleman Hawkins states a fragment of Johnny Green's song and improvises two full choruses over its chords, a big tenor tone with a wide vibrato, accompanied by a quiet rhythm section and, briefly, sustained band chords. The line climbs in long arcs, grows harmonically denser as it goes, and reaches its loudest point in the last eight bars before falling away to nothing.","Recorded for Bluebird in New York in October 1939, two months after Hawkins returned from five years in Europe to find that younger tenor players had taken his place. He had been playing the song nightly at Kelly's Stables on 52nd Street. The session was routine and the take reportedly almost an afterthought, cut because there was time left over. Victor released it expecting nothing; it became a jukebox hit, which for an instrumental with no tune in it was unheard of.","Count how often you hear the melody: once, at the start, and after that only as a shadow. That was the radical act, a popular record in which the song is a starting point rather than the content. Listen to how Hawkins builds density, beginning with long held notes and ending in runs, so the record accelerates without the tempo ever changing. The harmony he outlines in the second chorus is already most of the way to bebop.","Musicians memorised it, saxophone sections played it in harmony from transcriptions, and it remains the most analysed improvisation of the swing era. The debate has been over what it proves: whether Hawkins invented the modern jazz ballad or perfected the older style at the moment it was ending. Lester Young's admirers heard a heavy, ornate approach already going out of fashion. Both readings are defensible and the record outlives them.","Listen once for the tune, fail to find it, then listen again for the shape: quiet to loud, simple to complex, across three minutes. Then Lester Young's 'Shoe Shine Boy' for the other school entirely. Any clean transfer of the 1939 Bluebird will do; avoid the later live remakes, which are longer and much less concentrated."],"hawkins-picasso":["Under three minutes of tenor saxophone entirely alone: no piano, no bass, no drums, no second horn. Hawkins improvises a continuous line, implying the harmony by the notes he chooses and marking the beat by accent alone. The phrases are long, the tone is full, and the tempo drifts a little because there is nothing there to hold it steady. It is the first recording of an unaccompanied jazz saxophone solo, and it sounds exactly as exposed as that suggests.","Recorded in 1948 for The Jazz Scene, a lavish limited-edition album Norman Granz produced as a deluxe art object, with photographs and heavy pressings sold by subscription to people who could afford it. Granz asked the players to attempt something they could not do on an ordinary session. Hawkins, then in his mid-forties and watching bebop go past him, chose to record with nobody at all. The title nods at the painter, whose name in 1948 simply meant modern.","The difficulty is not the notes but the time. With no rhythm section, every implied barline is Hawkins's decision, and he keeps an audible pulse going purely through where he places accents and takes breaths; remove those and the piece becomes formless. Listen for the points where he lands on the harmony's strong beats. That is the skeleton, and there is nothing else holding the record upright.","It was heard at the time as an experiment and a curiosity, buried inside an expensive album few people owned. Its descendants make the case for it: Sonny Rollins's unaccompanied cadenzas, Coltrane's late solo passages, and the whole solo-saxophone recital tradition after 1960. Whether Hawkins meant to open a genre or to make a point about his own standing in a changing music is still unresolved.","Play it twice. The first time it sounds like an exercise, the second like a line. Then Bach's First Cello Suite prelude or the D minor Violin Partita for the same problem solved two hundred years earlier, one melody instrument implying a whole harmony. Try tapping a beat along with it and see how long you can keep it."],"django-minor-swing":["Three minutes by the Quintette du Hot Club de France: two rhythm guitars, a double bass, Stephane Grappelli's violin and Django Reinhardt's lead guitar, with no drums and no horns anywhere. The theme is a descending arpeggio figure over a short minor sequence, stated in unison by violin and guitar, and then everyone solos over the same few bars again and again. The rhythm guitars chop on every beat, and that chopping is the entire percussion section.","Recorded in Paris in November 1937. The Quintette had formed in 1934 around Reinhardt, a Manouche Romani guitarist raised in caravan encampments outside Paris who had lost the use of two fingers on his left hand in a fire at eighteen. The group played American swing with no drums, no piano and no trumpet, which was less a style choice than a description of who was available. It was the first European jazz that American musicians took entirely seriously.","Watch the left hand. Reinhardt fretted with two working fingers and a thumb, using the damaged third and fourth only for chords held across two strings, which is why his lines run in long single-note sweeps and why his chord voicings are so strange: they are the only ones he could physically reach. The famous rapid arpeggios exist because they can be played by sliding one hand position up the neck rather than by stretching.","The Quintette's records made Reinhardt the first non-American jazz musician of undisputed rank, and 'Minor Swing' has become the anthem of the gypsy jazz scene that grew up after his death, played in every Manouche session in Europe. Critics argue over whether that scene preserves him or embalms him. The romanticising of his biography, and the near-erasure of the Romani genocide from accounts of his war years, is a live issue in current writing.","Listen to the rhythm guitars first, because the whole style rests on that even chop. Then follow Reinhardt's solo and try to hear where he shifts position rather than stretching. Liszt's Hungarian Rhapsody and Ravel's Tzigane show what European art music made of Romani playing; this is a Romani musician making his own case, in his own time."],"django-nuages":["Three minutes, slow, and the most famous tune Reinhardt wrote. The melody drifts downward in long notes over shifting harmony, stated first by clarinet, since the wartime quintet had replaced Grappelli's violin with Hubert Rostaing, and then taken up by the guitar, which plays it with a wide singing vibrato and a slow chromatic descent that seems to lose its footing before it resolves. There is no fast playing anywhere on the record and nothing is repeated for effect.","Recorded in Paris in December 1940, six months into the German occupation. Grappelli had stayed in London when war broke out; Reinhardt, a Romani man in occupied France, remained and went on performing, protected in part by German officers who liked the music while the Nazi state was deporting and murdering Roma across Europe. The tune became something close to an unofficial anthem in Paris, requested nightly in rooms full of people who could not say why.","The title means clouds, and the harmony does what the word suggests: the melody holds while the chords underneath slide away from it, so the same notes keep changing meaning. Listen for the point where the tune repeats and the ground beneath it has moved. Reinhardt could not read music and dictated his compositions by playing them; the harmonic subtlety here came from an ear that had absorbed French concert music as much as American jazz.","It is the record that made Reinhardt a national figure in France rather than a specialist enthusiasm, and he recorded it many times afterwards without ever improving on it. Writing about his occupation years has moved away from the old story of a charmed survivor toward something more uncomfortable, acknowledging both the danger he was in and what playing for the occupier involved. The music has not been damaged by the reassessment.","Play it late and let the harmony do the work; try holding the melody in your head while the chords change underneath it. Then compare a Grappelli-era Quintette record to hear how differently the group breathes with a clarinet in place of a violin. Ravel's and Debussy's slow pieces are the nearest relatives it has."],"parker-koko":["Just under three minutes recorded for Savoy in November 1945. A scrambling unison figure for alto and trumpet, a passage of muted trumpet over drums, then Parker plays two choruses on the chord scheme of Ray Noble's 'Cherokee' at a tempo near three hundred beats a minute, phrases spilling over the bar lines. Max Roach takes a drum chorus, the opening frame returns, and it stops. The melody of 'Cherokee' is never stated.","Parker was twenty-five, four years out of Kansas City, and the after-hours experiments at Minton's and Monroe's had hardened into a style the record industry had not yet named. A musicians' union recording ban had kept it off disc until 1944. Savoy ran the date in a small New York studio; Gillespie was there, a teenaged Miles Davis played on other titles from the session, and the three-minute limit of a 78 rpm side shaped everything.","The absent tune is the point: publishers owned 'Cherokee', so stating the melody would have meant paying for it, while the chords were free to anyone. Listen to the introduction — not a head at all but a written frame, alto and trumpet in octaves, that returns at the end like a door closing. Behind Parker's solo the piano is Gillespie's, hands borrowed from the trumpet.","Older players heard chaos, and the American jazz press spent a decade fighting over whether bebop was still jazz. By the 1950s 'Ko-Ko' had become the exhibit for the defence, transcribed note for note in classrooms. Scholars now argue mostly about the session's details — who played what, and whether Davis really stood aside because the tempo was beyond him.","Play it twice: first for the shock of speed, then counting eight bars at a time so you can feel where Parker's phrases refuse to land. Hear 'Cherokee' in a swing band version afterwards and the trick becomes obvious. The Savoy masters have been reissued many times; look for a transfer that keeps the room sound rather than scrubbing it clean."],"parker-parkers-mood":["A slow blues in twelve bars, cut for Savoy in 1948 with John Lewis at the piano, Curley Russell on bass and Max Roach on drums. It opens with an unaccompanied alto call, a rising and falling fanfare answered by the piano, before Parker plays the most vocal choruses of his career: long bent notes, sudden clusters of fast ones, and silences left open. Three minutes, with no theme beyond the blues itself.","1948 found Parker at his most productive and least stable. A breakdown and six months at Camarillo State Hospital lay just behind him, and heroin was already costing him work and reputation. Savoy's studio dates were quick and cheap, three or four titles in an afternoon with little rehearsal. John Lewis, who would go on to found the Modern Jazz Quartet, brought a chamber player's restraint to music built on heat.","Listen for how few notes the final chorus contains. The singer King Pleasure later set words to Parker's solo — 'come with me, if you want to go to Kansas City' — and once you have heard that version the phrasing sounds like speech; after Parker's death in 1955 the record served as an unofficial elegy. The opening call is unaccompanied and never repeated, an announcement that belongs to no chorus.","Critics who accused bebop of being all velocity and no feeling had to account for this. It is now routinely named the finest blues performance Parker left, though reissues have shuffled the surviving takes and there is disagreement over which one the original issue used.","Start here if fast bebop leaves you cold. Follow the alto alone through the first eight bars, then notice Roach's brushes, which never once raise their voice. Compare it with the slow blues of the Kansas City bands Parker grew up hearing, and then with King Pleasure's vocal, which teaches you the phrasing by putting words under it."],"parker-with-strings":["Parker with a small string group — violins, viola, cello, harp and oboe — playing standards at ballad tempo: 'Just Friends', 'Everything Happens to Me', 'April in Paris', 'Summertime'. Jimmy Carroll's arrangements are plush and unhurried. Parker enters over them on 'Just Friends' with an eight-bar phrase that arcs up and does not come down where you expect. The band never swings hard; the alto does all the moving.","Norman Granz produced the sessions from 1949, betting that the most controversial alto player in America could sell records to people who bought light orchestral music. Parker had wanted strings behind him for years and afterwards named these among his own favourites. A ballad date with orchestra was the era's mark of arrival, the gesture Sinatra and Ellington were also making; for a bebop player it doubled as a claim on the concert hall.","Parker's ambitions ran further than this. He sought out Edgard Varèse in New York and asked, apparently seriously, to study composition with him, and he spoke of writing for larger forces. On 'Just Friends' listen to the opening flourish: it is neither the tune nor part of the arrangement but an unmeasured cadenza dropped in front of both, and it is the first thing most listeners remember.","Bebop partisans called it a sell-out and the arrangements syrupy, and the argument has never quite closed. Against it stand Parker's own verdict and the fact that these were his best-selling records by a wide margin. Recent writing treats the date less as compromise than as evidence of where he intended to go next, had he lived past thirty-four.","Ignore the strings for one hearing and follow only the alto line; then listen again for how it refuses to sit inside the arrangement's cushions. 'Just Friends' first. Afterwards try Ravel's Piano Concerto in G, where the traffic runs the other way and written orchestral music borrows jazz phrasing."],"parker-jazz-at-massey-hall":["One night's music from five bebop principals: Parker on alto, Gillespie on trumpet, Bud Powell at the piano, Charles Mingus on bass and Max Roach on drums. Six tunes — 'Perdido', 'Salt Peanuts', 'All the Things You Are', 'Wee', 'Hot House', 'A Night in Tunisia' — taken fast and played competitively, with Gillespie and Parker trading phrases that are half duel, half comedy. Around fifty minutes, audibly live and audibly unrehearsed.","Toronto, 15 May 1953, in a half-empty Massey Hall; a heavyweight title fight on television had kept the crowd away. Parker, under contract elsewhere, was billed as 'Charlie Chan' and played a white plastic Grafton alto, his own horn having been pawned. Mingus recorded the concert himself and issued it on Debut, the label he ran with Roach and one of the first artist-owned jazz labels in America.","The bass you hear was largely added afterwards: unhappy with the hall's balance, Mingus overdubbed his part in a studio, which is why it sits so far forward of everything else. The plastic saxophone is audible too once you know — thinner and more brittle than Parker's usual sound. Gillespie is said to have slipped offstage between solos to check the fight.","It has been sold for seventy years as the greatest jazz concert ever given, a claim the music does not need. The playing is superb and slightly ragged, the recording amateur. What is not in doubt is that these five never played together again, and that this is the fullest surviving live document of bebop's founders at work in front of an audience.","Start with 'Hot House', where the theme's difficulty is the joke. Notice how Powell accompanies — jabbing, irregular, sometimes simply absent. Then go back to the studio 'Ko-Ko' and hear how much a stage changes them. Later masterings have tried to fix the bass balance; the earliest Debut transfers keep the rough honesty of the tape."],"gillespie-shaw-nuff":["Three minutes cut in May 1945 by a Gillespie sextet: trumpet and Parker's alto in a unison head so fast and so exactly matched that it sounds like one instrument with two timbres, over Al Haig's piano, Curley Russell's bass and Sid Catlett's drums. A whirling written introduction, the head, solos, the head again. The theme sits on the chords of 'I Got Rhythm', the common currency of the style.","The 52nd Street clubs had been running this music for two years with nothing on record to show for it. Guild, a tiny label, got Gillespie and Parker into a studio in 1945, and the sides that resulted taught bebop to musicians in cities where nobody had ever heard it played live. The tune is named for Billy Shaw, the booking agent who put the band on the road.","The introduction is the tell. It is written out bar by bar and it is nobody's idea of a jazz riff: a knotted chromatic figure that sounds composed rather than improvised. Bebop is usually described as a soloist's music, yet its calling cards were these arranged frames. Catlett, a swing drummer a generation older than the horns, is the secret of how hard it swings.","Musicians learned these records by needle-drop, slowing the 78s to half speed. Older critics called it noise and younger ones treated it as scripture, splitting the American jazz press into mouldy figs and modernists. Nothing about the record is controversial now except the old question of how much of the style was Parker's and how much Gillespie's.","Play the head three times before going further, until your ear can separate trumpet from alto — most people cannot on a first pass. Then follow Catlett rather than the horns. Compare it with an Ellington side from the same years to hear what bebop gave up, which was the orchestra, and what it gained in return."],"gillespie-a-night-in-tunisia":["The most recognisable tune of the bebop years: an A section riding a bass figure that rocks rather than walks, a bridge that suddenly strides in four, and between them a written four-bar break for the soloist that has frightened saxophonists ever since. Gillespie's own 1946 recordings set his high, bright trumpet over it; Parker's Dial version turns the break into a famous twelve seconds of unaccompanied alto.","Gillespie wrote it around 1942 while with the Earl Hines band, when it went by the name 'Interlude'; Frank Paparelli shares the credit. Caribbean and Cuban dance music was audible all over wartime New York, and it was in Gillespie's ear years before he hired a conga player. By 1946 he was leading a big band and the tune had become his signature and his encore.","The lurch in the A section comes from a bass line that refuses to walk, rocking between two notes instead, which is why the bridge's ordinary four-to-the-bar arrives like sunlight. Listen also to what the break actually is: an unaccompanied solo over nothing at all, written into the arrangement, so that every recording of this tune gets judged on four bars.","It became a standard within five years and a test piece within ten, which is both its fortune and its problem — played so often that its strangeness has worn smooth. Debate continues over the date of composition, the original title, and how much Paparelli contributed beyond the publishing arrangement.","Hear two versions back to back, a Gillespie big-band reading and Parker's Dial take, listening only for the break. Then notice the moment the bass starts walking at the bridge; that switch is the whole design. Blakey's live Birdland version shows what a drummer can do with the same rocking figure underneath."],"gillespie-manteca":["Three minutes for big band, recorded in December 1947. It begins with bass and baritone saxophone locked into one repeated figure, then adds congas, then brass, layer on layer, until the band is playing four things at once; a written bridge in straight swing arrives like a change of weather before the layers rebuild. Chano Pozo's drums and chanted voice run through the whole of it.","Gillespie hired Pozo, a Cuban percussionist newly arrived in New York, for his big band in 1947 on Mario Bauzá's recommendation. Pozo spoke little English and Gillespie no Spanish, so they worked by singing at each other. Pozo brought the opening vamp and the rhythms; Gillespie and the arranger Gil Fuller supplied the bridge and the overall shape. Pozo was shot dead in a Harlem bar a year later.","The two halves do not belong to the same musical system, and that is the design: the vamp sections sit on a clave-based cycle that never resolves, while the bridge is ordinary thirty-two-bar swing. Gillespie said afterwards that Pozo's material had no bridge at all until he wrote one for it. Underneath the horns, listen for Pozo's chanted vocal line.","'Manteca' is usually dated as the start of Afro-Cuban jazz as a recorded style, though Machito's band had been fusing the two in New York for years and the priority argument persists. Its influence is not in doubt: Latin jazz, salsa and a great deal of later funk descend from those stacked, unresolving ostinatos.","Count the layers as they enter — bass, baritone, congas, trumpets — then wait for the bridge to knock the floor out. Follow it with Stravinsky's Rite of Spring for the same trick of piling repeated cells until they grind against each other, and with Milhaud's La création du monde for the opposite traffic, a European composer borrowing from jazz."],"thelonious-monk-round-midnight":["A ballad in a minor key whose melody keeps stepping downward and then correcting itself, harmonised with chords that never settle where a 1940s popular song would settle. Monk's own first recording, made for Blue Note in November 1947, frames it with a written horn introduction and a coda, Art Blakey's brushes underneath. Three minutes, in which the theme matters more than anything the soloists do with it.","Monk was thirty, had been house pianist at Minton's Playhouse in Harlem through the early 1940s, and was almost unrecorded. Alfred Lion signed him to Blue Note in 1947 largely on faith, and the records sold badly for a decade. The tune was older than the session: Cootie Williams recorded it in 1944 and took a composer credit, and Bernie Hanighen later added a lyric.","Nearly every version you have heard begins with an introduction Monk did not write — Gillespie's fanfare, attached to the tune in the late 1940s and now almost inseparable from it. In Monk's original the song simply starts. Notice too the bar where the melody hangs on one repeated note while the harmony slides away underneath, which is the moment the song stops being ordinary.","It is the most recorded composition by any jazz musician, a status that took twenty years to arrive. Critics in 1947 found Monk's playing clumsy and his harmony wilful; by 1964 he was on the cover of Time. The Williams credit is still disputed by scholars who believe Monk had the tune finished years before anyone else touched it.","Hear Monk's own version first, unadorned, then Miles Davis's 1956 reading with the added fanfare, and the difference will teach you what an arrangement does to a song. Listen for the descending melody in the first four bars; it is the whole piece. Monk's later solo recordings of it are slower and stranger still."],"thelonious-monk-brilliant-corners":["Five pieces for a shifting band: Sonny Rollins and Ernie Henry on saxophones, Oscar Pettiford on bass, Max Roach on drums, with Clark Terry and Paul Chambers arriving for the closing 'Bemsha Swing'. The title track alternates two tempos, the second exactly twice the first, so the theme lurches into double time and back. 'Pannonica' adds a celeste; 'Ba-lue Bolivar Ba-lues-are' is a slow blues. Around forty-four minutes.","Riverside had signed Monk in 1955 and eased him in with albums of Ellington tunes and standards; this was the first record of his own music for the label, cut in October 1956 with Orrin Keepnews producing. Monk's New York cabaret card had been revoked after a 1951 drug arrest, keeping him out of the city's clubs for years, so the bands on his records were assembled rather than road-tested.","The title track was never played through successfully. Keepnews spliced the issued performance together out of some twenty-five incomplete takes, and the seams are audible if you listen for sudden shifts in room sound. On 'Pannonica' Monk plays the piano with one hand and a celeste with the other; the tune is named for Baroness Pannonica de Koenigswarter, his patron and friend.","The sessions were notoriously tense, with the title track's written part reportedly defeating the band. Reviewers at the time were baffled; the record then became the standard answer to anyone who thought Monk a primitive. It is now read as the first fully realised Monk album, and the splice is treated as documentary evidence rather than as a scandal.","Start with 'Pannonica' for the tune, then take the title track twice: once for the tempo flip, once counting to confirm that the ratio really is exact. Roach's timpani on 'Bemsha Swing' is easy to miss. If the angles feel arbitrary, hear Bartók's Fourth Quartet and the family resemblance may suddenly click."],"thelonious-monk-monks-music":["A septet recorded in June 1957. It opens with something nobody expects on a jazz record: a Victorian hymn, 'Abide with Me', scored for four horns, lasting under a minute, with no rhythm section at all. Then come five Monk pieces, among them 'Well, You Needn't', 'Ruby, My Dear' and the closing 'Crepuscule with Nellie'. Coleman Hawkins and John Coltrane share the tenor chair. About forty minutes.","Monk's cabaret card was restored in 1957, and the Five Spot residency that followed, with Coltrane in the band, remade his reputation in a season. Hawkins had recorded Monk a decade earlier when almost nobody else would; setting the thirty-year-old Coltrane beside him was a deliberate joining of generations. Riverside were by now willing to let Monk record more or less whatever he proposed.","At one point Monk shouts 'Coltrane, Coltrane!' to cue a solo and the take was kept — listen for it in the middle of 'Well, You Needn't'. The hymn is by William Henry Monk, no relation, and Monk had reportedly loved it since childhood; hearing four horns hold those plain chords before the album's angularity begins reframes everything that follows it.","Critics were kinder to this than to Brilliant Corners, partly because Hawkins's presence certified it as jazz. Coltrane's solos here are now studied as the point where his harmonic thinking broke open under Monk's tutelage. The lasting argument is whether the hymn is a joke, a homage, or a plain statement of where Monk's harmony came from.","Play the first minute, stop, and let the hymn settle. Then go to 'Crepuscule with Nellie', a through-composed piece with no improvisation at all, written for Monk's wife. Compare Hawkins's broad, vibrato-rich tone with Coltrane's narrow driven one in the same tune; they are twenty years apart in every respect."],"thelonious-monk-solo-monk":["Monk alone at the piano: thirteen short performances, around forty minutes. Half are his own pieces, half are old popular songs — 'Dinah', 'I Should Care', 'Sweet and Lovely', 'These Foolish Things'. The left hand often plays outright stride, striding bass note and chord, while the right pulls the melody apart, flattens notes and leaves holes in it. The touch is percussive and deliberately uneven throughout.","Columbia had signed Monk in 1962 and briefly made him a famous man; Teo Macero produced, and the sessions ran across 1964 and 1965. Solo piano was where Monk had begun, in Harlem rent parties and church halls, and these records are full of the stride tradition of James P. Johnson, whom he had known as a neighbour. The cover photograph shows him dressed as a First World War aviator.","Listen to what he does with songs he did not write. In 'I Should Care' he sounds a note and holds the pedal so that its decay bleeds into the next chord — an effect impossible in a band, and the closest jazz piano comes to using silence as material. The wrong-sounding notes recur from take to take; they are not slips, they are the arrangement.","For years this was filed as minor Monk, a label record of standards. Later writers hear it as the clearest statement of his aesthetic: nothing to hide behind, the whole style audible in the touch. The long argument over whether his technique was limited or exactly calibrated is one that solo recordings settle for most listeners.","Begin with 'Dinah' for the stride, then 'Ask Me Now' for the strangeness. Notice the silences and resist the urge to fill them. If you know Satie's Gymnopédies, the shared taste for plainness and space is startling; if you know Liszt's études, this is the opposite ambition pursued with equal seriousness."],"bud-powell-un-poco-loco":["Four and a half minutes for piano trio — Powell, Curly Russell on bass, Max Roach on drums — recorded for Blue Note in May 1951. Roach plays a cowbell figure that runs almost unbroken from first bar to last, an Afro-Cuban pattern rather than a swing beat. Over it Powell states a jagged theme and then improvises in long unbroken single-note lines, hammering the keys, the left hand punching chords at unpredictable moments.","Powell was twenty-six and had already spent long stretches in psychiatric hospitals, where he was given electroconvulsive treatment; a police beating in Philadelphia in 1945 had left lasting damage. Alfred Lion kept recording him through the worst of it. Afro-Cuban rhythm was in the New York air by 1951, four years after Gillespie hired Chano Pozo, and Powell brought the idea into a piano trio, where it had not been.","Blue Note issued three takes together on the same record, which is unusual, and they are not interchangeable: the first two never find the feel, the third finds it and stays inside it. Hearing the failures beside the success is the nearest thing in jazz to reading a composer's crossings-out. Listen to Roach's cowbell, which never once drops the pattern.","Powell's reputation is complicated by the audible struggle in his later records; this one comes from the period everybody agrees about. Critics have called it the finest piano trio performance of the bebop era, and it appears regularly on lists of the century's American music. What remains argued is how far his illness shaped the intensity people hear in it.","Listen to the issued master first, then the two earlier takes, and notice how much depends on Roach settling into the groove. Follow the right hand for one chorus without trying to track the harmony. Afterwards, Gillespie's 'Manteca' shows where the rhythm came from, and Scriabin's hammered études offer a distant cousin in obsessive repetition."],"bud-powell-amazing-bud-powell-vol-1":["A Blue Note album drawn from two sessions. The 1949 date is a quintet — Fats Navarro's trumpet, a nineteen-year-old Sonny Rollins on tenor, Tommy Potter's bass, Roy Haynes's drums — playing Powell originals: 'Bouncing with Bud', 'Wail', 'Dance of the Infidels'. The 1951 date is the trio with Russell and Roach and includes 'Un Poco Loco' and 'Parisian Thoroughfare'. About forty minutes of the fastest, cleanest bebop piano on record.","Powell had no working band and few club engagements; these dates, arranged by Alfred Lion around his hospitalisations, are most of what survives of him at full power. Navarro was dying of tuberculosis and would be dead within a year of the first session. Rollins, still a teenager from Sugar Hill, was handed his first serious recording date by the pianist his whole generation had copied off records.","The 1949 quintet themes are not blowing vehicles but written pieces with bridges and codas, much closer to compositions than the borrowed-chord contrafacts that filled the era's sessions. Listen to 'Dance of the Infidels' for a theme that would stand up without any solos at all. Powell's left hand does almost nothing — a jab here and there — which is why the right hand sounds so exposed.","This is the record on which the case for Powell as the pianist of bebop rests, and it has not been seriously contested since the 1950s. The arguments are all around it: discography, take numbers, reissue sequencing, and how far Blue Note's editing shaped our picture of a man who rarely got to a studio on a good day.","Start with 'Bouncing with Bud' for the sheer flow, then 'Dance of the Infidels' for the writing. Listen for Navarro, warmer and rounder than Gillespie and less often imitated. If Powell's right hand starts to seem relentless, Chopin's Op. 10 études make the same demand of the same fingers for entirely different reasons."],"bud-powell-glass-enclosure":["Two and a half minutes for piano trio, recorded for Blue Note in August 1953 with George Duvivier on bass and Art Taylor on drums. It is not a tune with solos: it is a chain of episodes that follow one another without repeating — a grand fanfare, a scurrying passage, a chord that stalls, a fast run at the end. There is essentially no improvisation. It simply stops.","By 1953 Powell was under the informal guardianship of the man who managed Birdland and was reportedly kept confined to an apartment between engagements; the title is usually read that way. He had been in and out of institutions for eight years. In the same period he was writing pieces rather than heads, an ambition for which the jazz record business of the early 1950s had almost no room.","Count the sections: there are five or six depending how you divide them, and not one returns. Jazz almost never does this, because the form is normally a loop you go round. The ending gives the game away: a downward rush that does not cadence so much as fall off an edge, which is why the piece feels like a door shutting rather than a performance finishing.","Contemporary reviewers barely noticed it, sitting as it did among ordinary trio sides. Later writers took it up as evidence that Powell was a composer of real ambition kept from the forms he needed, and it has since been arranged for larger ensembles more than once. Whether the biographical reading of the title is accurate is not documented anywhere reliable.","Two minutes: play it four times in a row. Try to predict where each section will go and notice how often you are wrong. Then hear Ravel's Gaspard de la nuit for the same taste for brilliant, unstable piano writing that refuses to settle, and Monk's solo records for the opposite solution to the same problem."],"max-roach-we-insist-freedom-now-suite":["Five movements, some thirty-seven minutes, for Abbey Lincoln's voice, Booker Little's trumpet, Julian Priester's trombone, tenor saxophones including Coleman Hawkins, bass, Roach's drums and, in the last movements, African percussion. 'Driva' Man' is a work song in an uneven metre punctuated by a tambourine crack like a whip. 'Triptych' is Lincoln and Roach alone: prayer, then protest, in which she screams, then peace. 'All Africa' names ancestral peoples over drums.","Recorded in 1960 for the small, fearless Candid label with Nat Hentoff producing, it was conceived for the 1963 centenary of the Emancipation Proclamation and overtaken by events: the Greensboro sit-ins had begun that February, and the sleeve photograph shows three Black men at a segregated lunch counter. Oscar Brown Jr. wrote the lyrics. Roach and Lincoln were partners, and both were putting their careers at risk.","'Tears for Johannesburg' responds to the Sharpeville massacre of March 1960, in which South African police killed sixty-nine people — the suite reaches outside America deliberately and early. In 'All Africa' the exchange between Roach's kit and Michael Olatunji's hand drums is not accompaniment but conversation between two drum languages. Lincoln's scream sits in the middle of the record with nothing around it.","Some critics called it propaganda and questioned whether Lincoln could sing at all; one hostile review provoked a famous published round-table in reply. Roach lost bookings and Lincoln's recording career stalled for years afterwards. It is now read as the moment the civil rights movement entered jazz as content rather than atmosphere, and as a direct ancestor of the free jazz that followed.","Do not sample it; play it through in order, because the sequence is the argument. 'Driva' Man' first, with the tambourine. Brace yourself for 'Triptych'. Then hear Rollins's Freedom Suite from two years earlier to measure how fast the temperature rose, and Blakey's Free for All for the same fury without any words."],"max-roach-drums-unlimited":["An Atlantic album that alternates quintet tracks with three pieces for unaccompanied drum kit. 'The Drum Also Waltzes' is in three, a lilting figure built from tom-toms and bass drum that develops like a theme rather than showing off. 'For Big Sid' salutes the swing drummer Sidney Catlett. The solo pieces have shape, contrast and proper endings; they are compositions for a set of drums, not breaks between choruses. About forty minutes.","By the mid-1960s Roach had spent a decade arguing that the drum kit was an instrument of pitch and melody rather than a timekeeper, and that Black American music should be heard as art with a history. He had co-led the Clifford Brown quintet, made the Freedom Now Suite, and taken his politics into print. Atlantic gave him room to put unaccompanied drumming on a commercial LP.","Listen to the tuning. Roach pitches his toms so that the solo pieces move between recognisable high and low intervals, which is why 'The Drum Also Waltzes' has a tune you can hum afterwards. Listen too for the dynamic restraint: much of this is quiet, and the crescendos are structural rather than climactic, arriving where a composer would place them.","Unaccompanied drum records were a hard sell in 1966 and remain a minority taste, but musicians took the point, and almost every subsequent solo percussion record in jazz descends from these tracks. Critics divided between those who heard composition and those who heard a demonstration of technique; the first view has largely won.","Take 'The Drum Also Waltzes' first and count it in three. Notice how few cymbals there are. Then hear Varèse's Ionisation, written thirty years earlier for percussion alone in an entirely different tradition, and the shared problem becomes obvious: how do you make a piece out of sound without fixed pitch?"],"clifford-brown-and-max-roach":["The first album by the quintet Brown and Roach co-led: trumpet, Harold Land's tenor, Richie Powell's piano, George Morrow's bass and Roach's drums, recorded across 1954. Seven tracks including 'Joy Spring' and 'Daahoud', both Brown's, plus Duke Jordan's 'Jordu' and Bud Powell's 'Parisian Thoroughfare'. Brown's tone is round and unhurried at any speed; the arrangements are tight, with worked-out introductions and endings around every solo.","Hard bop is often dated from this band. After bebop's speed and the cool school's restraint, Brown and Roach put the heat back without giving up the writing, and the formula — two horns, strong themes, blues and gospel inflections, a drummer who leads from behind — set the pattern for a decade of Blue Note and Prestige records. Richie Powell was Bud Powell's younger brother.","'Parisian Thoroughfare' opens with the band imitating city traffic, horns and a whistle, before the tune begins — a piece of theatre inherited from Bud Powell's solo version of his own piece. Listen to how Brown begins his solos: almost always mid-phrase, as though he had been playing before the tape started. Land, the least famous man here, is the quiet reason the ensemble blends.","Brown was twenty-three and already spoken of as Gillespie's successor; he neither drank nor used drugs, which mattered enormously to a generation watching Parker die. He and Richie Powell were killed in a car crash on the Pennsylvania Turnpike in June 1956, and the critical literature has never quite escaped the elegiac mode. On the record alone, the ensemble discipline is what impresses.","'Joy Spring' first — the tune is a small model of how to write for two horns. Follow Roach behind the soloists rather than during his own breaks; he is arranging in real time. Then hear the Jazz Messengers live at Birdland from the same year for the rougher, hotter version of the same idea."],"clifford-brown-study-in-brown":["The same quintet a year on, recorded for EmArcy in February 1955 and playing faster and harder. It opens with 'Cherokee' at a tempo that invites comparison with Parker, and includes 'Sandu', a slow blues, 'George's Dilemma' with its Latin undertow, and a closing 'Take the \"A\" Train'. Brown's playing is clean at every speed, full-toned down low, and he ends phrases where a singer would need to breathe.","The band had been touring for a year and sounds like it. EmArcy, Mercury's jazz imprint, was recording them regularly, and hard bop was becoming a commercial proposition rather than a rumour among musicians. Brown's sound — warm, centred, without the flamboyant high notes of the Gillespie school — was being copied by trumpet players across the country before he turned twenty-four.","'Cherokee' is the test and the joke at once: the same chord sequence Parker used for 'Ko-Ko' ten years earlier, now played with the melody restored and at a tempo that makes restoring it much harder. Listen to Brown's articulation in the fast passages — every note is tongued and separate, where most trumpeters slur in order to survive.","For decades this and its companion album were what people meant by hard bop. Reception at the time was strong and it never went out of print; after Brown's death his discography acquired a weight a four-year recording career could not otherwise carry. Trumpeters still argue over whether anyone since has matched the evenness of his attack.","Play 'Cherokee' and then Parker's 'Ko-Ko' back to back: the same chords, two different eras of technique. Then 'Sandu' to hear Brown slow, which is where the tone tells most. Look for EmArcy masterings that leave the trumpet unbrightened; over-equalised reissues damage the very sound he was famous for."],"art-blakey-a-night-at-birdland":["A live quintet date from February 1954 at Birdland: Clifford Brown, Lou Donaldson's alto, Horace Silver's piano, Curly Russell's bass and Blakey's drums, issued by Blue Note across two volumes, around eighty minutes in all. The repertoire is bebop — 'A Night in Tunisia', 'Now's the Time', 'Confirmation' — but the delivery is louder, bluesier and far more physical. Blakey's press roll before a soloist's entry works like a shove in the back.","Blakey was thirty-four, a swing-trained drummer who had come up with Billy Eckstine's band, and the name Jazz Messengers was not yet fixed to the group. Birdland on Broadway was the music's showcase room, and recording live in a nightclub was still unusual and technically awkward. The club's diminutive master of ceremonies, Pee Wee Marquette, introduces the band and his patter was left on the record.","Listen to the audience. You can hear tables, glasses and conversation, and the band plays over and into it, which is precisely the point: this is dance-hall energy applied to bebop's repertoire. Blakey's trademark is audible several times — a rapid closing and reopening of the hi-hat on the second and fourth beat that pushes the band rather than merely keeping time.","Released as two LPs that have never been out of print, it is often cited as the first hard bop record, though Silver's own dates and the Brown–Roach quintet have equal claims. Brown's death two years later gives it the same posthumous charge as his studio work. Later reissues have restored extra takes and Marquette's full announcements.","Start with 'A Night in Tunisia' and hear what Blakey does with the rocking bass figure. Follow Silver's accompaniment, sparser and funkier than a bebop pianist's. Then compare the studio polish of the Brown–Roach records to hear how much a room adds. Seek reissues taken from the original tapes rather than early electronic reprocessing."],"art-blakey-moanin":["Six tracks, forty minutes, recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's Hackensack studio in October 1958 by the Jazz Messengers line-up of Lee Morgan, Benny Golson, Bobby Timmons, Jymie Merritt and Blakey. The title piece opens with Timmons's piano playing a four-bar gospel phrase to which the horns answer 'amen' — call and response lifted straight out of church. 'Blues March' walks in from a parade; 'Along Came Betty' is Golson's long-lined ballad.","Blakey had rebuilt the band around Golson as music director, and the record turned the Jazz Messengers into an institution and a finishing school — Morgan was twenty. Blue Note's engineer Van Gelder had by then perfected the drum sound that defined the label. The gospel feeling was deliberate and commercial: hard bop was reaching back into the Black church at exactly the moment rock and roll was doing the same.","The album was issued untitled, with only the band's name on the cover; it became 'Moanin'' because that is what people asked for in record shops. Listen for Golson's writing across the other tracks — 'The Drum Thunder Suite' is a three-part feature that gives Blakey composed material rather than a solo spot, something almost nobody was doing for drummers in 1958.","It sold, especially in France and Japan, and turned the Messengers into a touring franchise that ran until Blakey's death in 1990. Purists then and since have complained that the gospel turn was a formula; the counter-argument is that the formula produced Morgan, Shorter, Hubbard, Hancock and thirty years of working musicians who could read, write and lead.","The first thirty seconds decide it for most people. Then listen to 'Blues March' for what a drummer trained on parade rudiments does with a backbeat. Follow it with Silver's 'The Preacher' from three years earlier, the same idea in embryo, and with Copland's Simple Gifts for another composer mining a plain hymn tune."],"art-blakey-free-for-all":["Thirty-five minutes recorded in February 1964 by the sextet Messengers: Freddie Hubbard, Curtis Fuller's trombone, Wayne Shorter's tenor, Cedar Walton's piano, Reggie Workman's bass and Blakey. The title track, Shorter's, is played at a pitch of controlled violence — cymbals washing over everything, horns shrieking in the ensembles, soloists pushed rather than supported. 'Pensativa', which closes the record, sounds almost weightless by comparison.","Shorter had been music director for four years and was writing pieces that stretched the band's harmony towards where he would take Miles Davis's quintet the following year. The session falls between Coltrane's most searching Impulse records and the first wave of free jazz; Blakey, twenty years older than his sidemen, was audibly refusing to be left behind. Van Gelder had by then moved the studio to Englewood Cliffs.","Listen to what Blakey does with the ride cymbal on the title track: for long stretches he abandons the swing pattern altogether and simply roars, which is a free jazz gesture sitting inside a hard bop record. Shorter's theme is built from a single interval turned over and over; once you hear it in the head you will find it inside every solo that follows.","Reviewers found it ferocious and some found it ugly. It sat in the shadow of Moanin' for years and is now the Messengers album musicians name first, largely because Shorter's writing here is the missing link between his Blue Note albums and his work with Davis. How close Blakey came to free jazz, and whether he wanted to, is still argued.","Play the title track loud; quiet playback destroys it. Try counting the first sixteen bars, then give up and follow Blakey instead. Afterwards, Roach's Freedom Now Suite shows the same energy given an explicit politics, and Stravinsky's Rite of Spring the same appetite for pounded, unresolved rhythm."],"horace-silver-the-preacher":["Four minutes of quintet blues recorded for Blue Note in February 1955 by Kenny Dorham, Hank Mobley, Silver, Doug Watkins and Art Blakey. The theme is a simple singable riff played in harmony by trumpet and tenor, its shape close to the old song 'Show Me the Way to Go Home', and the band delivers it with a hand-clapping lilt. Silver's piano solo is spare, riff-based and rhythmically stubborn, with almost no fast runs.","The session was billed as Horace Silver and the Jazz Messengers, the co-operative group that would shortly become Blakey's band. Silver was twenty-six, from Norwalk, Connecticut, the son of a Cape Verdean immigrant. Bebop had made jazz a listener's music; Silver was reintroducing the parts a dancer or a churchgoer would recognise, at a moment when rhythm and blues was outselling jazz everywhere in America.","Alfred Lion reportedly thought the tune too old-fashioned to record and had to be talked round, with Blakey usually cast as the advocate in the retellings. Listen to Silver's left hand: it plays almost nothing but one repeated rhythmic figure, which is the whole trick, and the reason pianists ever since have been able to imitate the style badly.","It was a hit by jazz standards and gave a name to what became soul jazz. The condescension was immediate — funky, crowd-pleasing, backward-looking — and never entirely went away, even as the record's descendants filled the Blue Note catalogue for fifteen years. Silver's defenders point out that the simplicity here is composed, not fallen into.","Listen once for the tune, which you will be humming afterwards, then once for the left hand alone. Notice that nobody plays anything technically difficult. Then hear 'Moanin'' from 1958 for the same idea with a church organ's authority, and Monk's Music for the hymn showing at the root of it."],"horace-silver-song-for-my-father":["A Blue Note album whose title track runs six minutes over a bass figure Silver brought home from Brazil: a bossa nova pulse rather than a swing beat, with the piano stating the melody in bare octaves before Carmell Jones's trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor take it up. The rest of the record mixes that band with the previous quintet. About forty minutes, warm and unhurried from end to end.","The cover photograph is of Silver's father, John Tavares Silva, a Cape Verdean who emigrated to Connecticut and played violin, guitar and mandolin in a folk band. Silver had toured Brazil and come back with the rhythm; Cape Verdean music, Brazilian music and the Black American church all sit inside the same tune. Blue Note by this point was selling jazz records to people who bought little else.","The bass vamp had a long afterlife: Steely Dan built 'Rikki Don't Lose That Number' on it a decade later and said so openly. Listen also to the piano's opening statement, played in bare octaves with no chords at all — Silver withholds the harmony for eight bars, so the tune arrives undressed and only afterwards gets its clothes.","It became Blue Note's best-selling album of its era and Silver's signature, which flattened his reputation into that of a groove writer. Later assessments have restored the composer: the harmonic writing across the rest of the record is subtler than the hit suggests. Any argument about hard bop's commercial turn eventually arrives at this record.","Let the title track's first minute pass before deciding anything; the tune only opens out at the trumpet's entry. Then listen to Henderson, the most modern player on the date. Compare it with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras for a different meeting of Brazilian melody with a formal tradition from elsewhere."],"sonny-rollins-saxophone-colossus":["Five tracks, forty minutes, recorded for Prestige in June 1956 with Tommy Flanagan's piano, Doug Watkins's bass and Max Roach's drums — no second horn, so the tenor carries everything. 'St. Thomas' is a calypso; 'Moritat' is the tune from Brecht and Weill's Threepenny Opera; 'Blue 7' is a slow blues, eleven minutes long, built almost entirely out of one three-note shape turned over and over.","Rollins was twenty-five and had just come off a year in Chicago getting clean; he had already recorded with Miles Davis, Monk and the Brown–Roach quintet. Prestige worked fast and cheap — Van Gelder's Hackensack studio, one afternoon, minimal rehearsal, no second takes to speak of. New York tenor playing at that moment was an open contest, and Rollins won it with this record.","'St. Thomas' is not an invention: Rollins learned the tune as a nursery song from his mother, who came from the Virgin Islands, and it has an older life in Caribbean folk music. On 'Blue 7', listen for the small figure he plays in the first chorus and then count how often it returns, inverted, stretched and compressed; the whole solo grows out of it.","Gunther Schuller's 1958 essay on 'Blue 7' argued that Rollins improvised thematically, developing motifs the way a composer would, and made the record an exhibit in the case for jazz as art music. Rollins later said the analysis made him self-conscious for years afterwards. Some writers now suspect Schuller over-read a performance that was at least partly instinct.","'St. Thomas' first for pleasure, then 'Blue 7' with Schuller's claim in mind — try to catch the motif coming back. Compare 'Moritat' with a German cabaret performance of the same song to hear what jazz does to it. Any decent transfer of the Prestige master will serve; the recording is plain and close."],"sonny-rollins-way-out-west":["A trio record — tenor, Ray Brown's bass, Shelly Manne's drums, no piano — made for Contemporary in March 1957. With no chords behind him Rollins has to imply the harmony himself, and the resulting space is enormous. The repertoire is a joke told with a straight face: 'I'm an Old Cowhand' and 'Wagon Wheels', cowboy songs played as hard bop, alongside Ellington's 'Solitude'. About forty minutes.","Rollins was in Los Angeles on tour and the session ran through the early hours after a club date, which is part of why the playing has such a loose, late feel. Contemporary's engineer Roy DuNann got a famously clear sound in a small room. William Claxton's cover photograph shows Rollins in the Mojave in a Stetson and gun belt holding his tenor, a Black New Yorker claiming the iconography of the western.","The piano-less trio was not a novelty but a strategy: with nobody stating the chords, Rollins could stretch or skip them, and the long solos here come as close to thinking aloud as recorded jazz gets. Listen to Manne playing a clip-clop figure on woodblock under 'I'm an Old Cowhand' with a completely straight face; the humour is real and the swing is unaffected by it.","The cover has generated as much commentary as the music, read variously as parody, as a claim on American mythology, and as a record company's idea of a gimmick. Musically it is now standard study for anyone learning to play without a chordal instrument behind them, and DuNann's sound remains a benchmark among audio enthusiasts.","Start with 'I'm an Old Cowhand' and enjoy the absurdity, then stay for the third chorus, when it stops being funny. Listen for how rarely Rollins plays the obvious note at the end of a phrase. Original Contemporary pressings and their careful reissues are worth the search; the sound is part of the record."],"sonny-rollins-freedom-suite":["A Riverside album whose first side is a single nineteen-minute piece for tenor, Oscar Pettiford's bass and Max Roach's drums, again with no piano. It moves through several themes and tempos without a written score's rigidity — a waltz, a march, a long slow section — and returns to its opening idea at the close. The remaining tracks are standards. The suite makes its argument by development, not by words.","Rollins wrote a sleeve note that did the talking: America's culture, he observed, is saturated with the contributions of the Black American, who is nonetheless denied his freedom. In 1958 that was an unusual thing to print on a jazz record. Riverside issued it, then later reissued the album under a different title with the note dropped and the suite buried — a reversal Rollins did not authorise.","The music itself carries no programme and no lyrics, which was deliberate: Rollins wanted the claim to rest on the quality of the playing rather than on illustration. Listen for the return of the opening theme nearly twenty minutes later, slowed and thickened. Pettiford, who died three years afterwards, is doing far more than keeping time; in a piano-less trio the bass is the harmony.","It is now recognised as the first extended jazz statement of the civil rights era, more than a year before Roach's Freedom Now Suite and five before Coltrane's 'Alabama'. At the time the retitling attracted little comment, which is itself part of the story. Critics still debate whether a wordless suite can carry an explicit political claim, or whether the sleeve note does the work.","Give the suite its nineteen minutes in one sitting. Follow Pettiford rather than Rollins for a chorus and you will hear the harmony being invented underneath. Then play Roach's Freedom Now Suite, which answers it with voices, and Copland's Appalachian Spring for the official American pastoral that this record declines to join."],"cannonball-adderley-somethin-else":["A Blue Note quintet date from March 1958: Adderley's alto, Miles Davis's muted trumpet, Hank Jones's piano, Sam Jones's bass and Art Blakey's drums. It opens with 'Autumn Leaves' — a bell-like piano figure, then the trumpet entering alone and slow, one of the most quoted introductions in the music. 'Love for Sale' and the loping blues 'One for Daddy-O' follow. Around forty-five minutes, unhurried and dark-toned.","Adderley had joined Davis's band the year before, and on this date the roles are politely reversed: the album is Adderley's, the presence is Davis's, and Davis was under contract elsewhere, so the sleeve is careful about how it says so. Blue Note rarely got Davis at all in these years. Alfred Lion paid for rehearsal, which is audible in how worked-out the arrangements sound.","At the end of 'One for Daddy-O' a voice asks, 'Is that what you wanted, Alfred?' — Davis addressing Alfred Lion, and the take was kept. Listen also to the contrast the whole record is built on: Adderley's alto bright, fast and blues-drenched, Davis's muted trumpet spare and behind the beat, the tracks sequenced so that the two never quite meet.","It has sold steadily for decades and remains a favourite entry point to jazz, which has made some critics wary of it as dinner-party music. The counter-case is that 'Autumn Leaves' here is a lesson in how much can be drawn out of a standard, and that Blakey's playing is far more attentive than the album's reputation for smoothness suggests.","The first two minutes of 'Autumn Leaves' will decide it for you. Listen for the piano's four-note figure, which never stops turning. Then hear Adderley on his own later records, where the joy is much louder, and Dexter Gordon's Go for the same relaxed authority on a bigger horn."],"cannonball-adderley-mercy-mercy-mercy":["Five minutes of gospel in a rolling six-eight, written by the band's Austrian pianist Joe Zawinul and played on a Wurlitzer electric piano whose reedy, slightly distorted sound was still new in jazz. Nat Adderley's cornet and Cannonball's alto state a plain hymn-like tune; the alto solo builds by repeating phrases rather than running the changes; an audience claps and shouts throughout. It ends on an amen.","Recorded in 1966 in a Los Angeles studio with an invited audience and drinks laid on, then released as a live album from a club that did not exist as billed. Zawinul had been with the Adderleys for five years and would go on to co-found Weather Report; the electric keyboard here was a step in that direction. Soul music was outselling jazz, and this was jazz answering in kind.","The staged audience is the open secret, and it works, because the shouts arrive in the right places — the room knew what it was there for. Listen to Zawinul's left hand, which plays a rocking figure closer to a gospel organist's than a jazz pianist's, and to Cannonball's spoken introduction, which frames the tune as a sermon before a note is played.","It was a genuine pop hit, played on radio stations that never otherwise touched jazz, and covered within months by singers and organ trios. Critics divided over whether this was jazz reaching an audience or leaving the room, the same argument that attached to Silver and to the boogaloo records of the period. Zawinul's later career complicates the easy dismissal.","Listen first for the electric piano's timbre, which is the reason the record sounds like 1966 and nothing else. Then follow the alto solo and notice how little it moves harmonically. Pair it with 'The Preacher' and 'The Sidewinder' to hear a decade of jazz reaching for the same congregation."],"dexter-gordon-go":["A quartet album recorded for Blue Note in August 1962 with Sonny Clark's piano, Butch Warren's bass and Billy Higgins's drums. Gordon's tenor is huge, dry and placed just behind the beat, so that every phrase seems to arrive late and still land right. 'Cheese Cake' opens at a medium swing; 'I Guess I'll Hang My Tears Out to Dry' is a ballad played almost without vibrato. Six tracks, forty minutes, nothing hurried.","Gordon had been among the first tenor players to translate Parker's language onto the bigger horn in the 1940s, then lost most of the 1950s to heroin and prison. Alfred Lion brought him back with a run of Blue Note albums of which this is the best known. Within weeks of the session Gordon left for Europe and settled in Copenhagen for fourteen years, part of a wider expatriation of Black American musicians.","Listen for the quotations. Gordon habitually dropped fragments of other songs into his solos — a nursery tune, a show melody, an anthem — placed so that they comment on the lyric of whatever he is playing. Listen too for how far behind Higgins's cymbal his phrases sit; written down the notes would look early, and the delay is entirely deliberate.","The Blue Note run rebuilt a reputation the 1950s had wrecked, and Go is the album named first. Gordon returned to America in 1976 to a hero's reception, then took the lead role in the film Round Midnight and was nominated for an Academy Award. The persistent critical question is how much of his 1940s priority was later credited to other players.","Start with 'Cheese Cake' and deliberately listen late — try to locate the beat while he plays around it. Then take the ballad, where the tone does everything and technique does nothing. Van Gelder's recording puts the tenor very close; on a good system it is almost too present, which is exactly the point."],"lee-morgan-the-sidewinder":["The title track runs ten minutes over a twenty-four-bar boogaloo: a strutting bass line, Billy Higgins's backbeat, and a riff that Morgan's trumpet and Joe Henderson's tenor play in harmony until it lodges in your head. Barry Harris's piano and Bob Cranshaw's bass hold the groove without ornament. The rest of the album, recorded in December 1963, is straighter hard bop, and none of it sounds like filler.","Morgan was twenty-five and had already been a star with Gillespie's big band at eighteen and with Blakey's Messengers, then lost two years to heroin. Blue Note wanted a strong opening track and got a hit instead. Soul and rhythm and blues were taking jazz's audience away; the boogaloo, a dance rhythm with a backbeat, was the label's answer, and this record turned it into a formula.","The tune is twenty-four bars long, not the blues' twelve or the standard's thirty-two, and that extra length is why the groove seems to keep turning over instead of resolving. Some of the record's sales had nothing to do with jazz at all: the title track was used in a car advertisement broadcast during the 1965 World Series.","It charted, an almost unheard-of thing for a Blue Note album, and the label spent the next four years asking its artists for another sidewinder — a demand several of them resented, Morgan included. Critics have used it ever since as the example of jazz's mid-1960s commercial turn. Morgan was shot dead at a New York club in February 1972, at thirty-three.","Give the title track its full ten minutes; the groove is the composition and it needs the length to work. Listen to Higgins, who never varies and never drags. Then hear 'Song for My Father' and 'Mercy, Mercy, Mercy' alongside it, and Morgan's own Search for the New Land for what he did when nobody was asking for a hit."],"miles-davis-birth-of-the-cool":["The sides gathered here were cut by a nine-piece band: trumpet, trombone, French horn, tuba, alto and baritone saxophone over piano, bass and drums. The horn and the tuba are the point — they give the group a soft, wide middle register no bebop combo had. Tempos sit back, solos are short, and the written passages matter as much as the improvising. Recognise it by the becalmed, hymn-like sound of “Moon Dreams”.","Miles Davis was twenty-two and had just left Charlie Parker's quintet. The band grew out of the arranger Gil Evans's basement flat on West 55th Street in Manhattan, where Evans, Gerry Mulligan, John Lewis and John Carisi argued over voicings. It played two weeks at the Royal Roost in 1948 and then almost nowhere else. Capitol recorded it in three sessions across 1949 and 1950, issued the results as 78s, and only gathered them onto an LP in 1957 with the title that stuck.","Listen for the French horn and tuba doubling the saxophones an octave apart. Evans learned that thickened unison from Claude Thornhill's dance band, where he had arranged before the war; the nonet is essentially Thornhill's orchestra shrunk to the smallest size that keeps the blend. Notice too how rarely the drums announce anything. They shade rather than punctuate, which is half of what the word cool came to mean.","The records sold poorly and were treated as curiosities until the LP appeared and gave a name to a style already spreading from California. Critics have argued ever since about whether cool was Davis's invention or Evans's, and whether these sessions launched West Coast jazz or merely got the credit for it. Mulligan spent years insisting the band had been a collective. The influence itself is not in doubt.","Start with “Boplicity” — Evans's arrangement, credited to Cleo Henry, Davis's mother — and hear how the phrase lengths refuse to land where you expect. Then put on a Parker record from the same years and notice what has been removed: the heat, the extremes of register, the sense of a race. The 1998 Capitol edition adds the surviving Royal Roost broadcasts."],"miles-davis-miles-ahead":["Davis plays flugelhorn throughout — rounder and warmer than his trumpet — in front of nineteen players whose ranks include French horns, tuba, bass clarinet and flutes but no saxophone section to speak of. Gil Evans wrote every note around him and segued the ten pieces together, so the record plays as one unbroken half hour. The textures shift constantly under a single unhurried solo voice; the effect is closer to a concerto than to a big band date.","Davis had signed to Columbia and wanted the nonet sound at full size. He and Evans had not worked together in seven years. The repertoire is deliberately unbookish: a song by Léo Delibes, Kurt Weill's “My Ship”, Dave Brubeck's “The Duke”, Ahmad Jamal's “New Rhumba”. Columbia's engineers assembled the master from many takes, a freedom that magnetic tape had only lately made cheap, and one Evans exploited hard.","The joins. Several of the famous transitions are edits rather than performances, and once you know it you can hear the acoustic of the room shift under the splice. Listen also to Davis's entrances: he almost never begins a phrase on a downbeat, so the orchestra seems to arrive first and he steps in late, a habit that makes a written score sound improvised.","Some critics heard luxury and called it mood music. Others took it as proof that a jazz soloist could carry a large written score without turning into a concerto soloist. The original sleeve, showing a white woman and a child on a sailing boat, angered Davis and was replaced on later pressings. The 1996 reissue restored much of the unedited session material and made the construction audible.","Play it whole and unshuffled — the segues are the argument. “The Maids of Cadiz” makes the case in two minutes: a nineteenth-century French salon song rebuilt for flugelhorn and low brass. Then hear Sketches of Spain, which takes the method further than anyone expected. The mono mix keeps Davis inside the band rather than in front of it."],"miles-davis-ascenseur-pour-lechafaud":["Barely half an hour of music for Louis Malle's thriller, and most of it is one trumpet wandering over a vamp that hardly moves. Davis leads a pick-up group of French players plus the expatriate American drummer Kenny Clarke: tenor saxophone, piano, bass, brushes. There are no themes in the usual sense, only a mood, a walking bass and long silences between phrases. The main title, over Jeanne Moreau walking the boulevards at night, is the sound everything after it copies.","Davis was in Paris in December 1957 at the end of a European tour. Malle, twenty-five and making his first feature, screened the film in the studio and asked the band to play to it. They worked through the night with the images running, using sketched harmonies rather than scores. French audiences already treated Davis as an artist rather than an entertainer, and the film's success at home did much to make jazz the default sound of screen menace.","Listen for how little the harmony moves. Long stretches sit on a single minor chord with the bass rocking between two notes — a year before the modal experiments of Milestones and Kind of Blue. The pictures left no room for chord changes, so you can hear the discovery arriving under duress rather than as a theory. Clarke's brushes never once raise their voice.","The soundtrack was praised at once in France and largely ignored in America, where it appeared piecemeal. It is now read as the hinge between Davis's Gil Evans records and his modal period, though writers disagree about how deliberate the harmonic simplicity was. Later reissues added alternate takes that show the band feeling its way, which some listeners find more revealing than the finished cues.","Watch the opening ten minutes of the film first, then hear the music alone; it loses none of the atmosphere and gains an ache. Follow the drummer rather than the trumpet for one pass. Compare the Gerry Mulligan quartet's pianoless records for another cool sound made largely out of space."],"miles-davis-milestones":["The one studio album by the sextet with both Cannonball Adderley's alto and John Coltrane's tenor beside Davis, over Red Garland, Paul Chambers and Philly Joe Jones. Most of it is fast, hard-swinging bebop. The title track is not: it turns on two scales, one bright and one darker, held for long stretches while the horns play a simple rising figure. Adderley's whoop and Coltrane's crowded runs make the two saxophones instantly distinguishable.","1958 was the year Davis's quintet became a sextet and the band worked almost continuously. Davis had been listening around the edges of jazz — to Ahmad Jamal's use of space, to George Russell's argument that scales could replace chord sequences. Columbia gave him a budget and left him alone. Garland walked out during the sessions after a disagreement, which is why Davis himself plays the piano on one track.","On the title track, count how long each of the two scales lasts: sixteen bars apiece, an eternity by bebop standards, and the reason the soloists sound like they are floating rather than running. Notice that Coltrane and Adderley solve the new problem in opposite ways — Coltrane fills the space with notes, Adderley sings through it and leaves it open.","Reviewers heard a strong hard bop record and mostly missed the significance of the title track; only after Kind of Blue did writers go back and find the method already working here. The name has caused confusion ever since, because Davis had recorded a different tune called “Milestones” in 1947. Arguments persist about how much of the modal idea belongs to Russell rather than to Davis.","Play “Milestones” and then “So What” back to back — the same discovery a year apart, one still restless, one completely calm. Listen for Philly Joe Jones's rim shot on the second beat, the signature that dates the band to the minute. Then try Cannonball Adderley's own records from the same months, with Davis as sideman."],"miles-davis-porgy-and-bess":["Gil Evans took numbers from George Gershwin's opera and rewrote them for flugelhorn, trumpet and a nineteen-piece orchestra thick with flutes, French horns and tuba. There is no singing; Davis carries every vocal line. “Summertime” becomes a short riff tolled behind him like a bell, and “I Loves You, Porgy” is played with so little vibrato that it sounds spoken. “Prayer (Oh Doctor Jesus)” is the wildest thing here, closer to a field holler than to an aria.","The opera was twenty-three years old and freshly contentious: a Hollywood film was in production, and Black critics were increasingly uneasy about a white composer's Catfish Row. Davis and Evans said little about that argument and simply removed the libretto. Columbia's 30th Street Studio, a converted church, gave Evans the reverberant space his low woodwind writing needed. By now the two men were working almost as a single composer.","On “Summertime” Evans hands the orchestra a two-bar figure and repeats it roughly a dozen times without variation while Davis improvises above it — pattern-and-drone thinking a year before Kind of Blue made it explicit. Listen for the moment the figure drops out entirely and the trumpet is left alone in the church acoustic, with nothing underneath at all.","Reviews were strong and it sold well. Later critics have asked harder questions about what is lost when an opera about a Black community loses its words. Others answer that the wordlessness is the point, returning Gershwin's melodies to the blues idiom they were borrowed from in the first place. Evans's scores were long thought lost and have been only partly reconstructed for concert use.","Hear Gershwin's “Summertime” sung first — any great version will do — then this one, and notice that Davis plays almost none of the melody's ornaments. “Gone”, a drum feature for Philly Joe Jones, is the shock in the middle of the record. Seek the stereo remaster; mono flattens Evans's careful separation of flutes and horns."],"miles-davis-kind-of-blue":["Five pieces, two sessions, one sextet: Davis, John Coltrane, Cannonball Adderley, Bill Evans (Wynton Kelly on one track), Paul Chambers and Jimmy Cobb. “So What” alternates two scales a semitone apart. “All Blues” is a slow blues in six over a shivering piano figure. “Blue in Green” is a ten-bar loop that never resolves, and “Flamenco Sketches” gives each soloist a series of scales to move through at will. Nothing hurries; the bass introduces almost everything.","Davis brought sketches rather than parts to Columbia's 30th Street Studio in March and April 1959, and the band read them down, most pieces captured in one or two takes. Bill Evans, recently out of the group, was brought back for the date, and his Debussy-and-Ravel touch is audible in the voicings. Jazz was losing its dance-hall audience to rock and roll and finding a new one in college rooms and hi-fi shops.","Evans's liner note compares the method to Japanese brush painting, where a line cannot be corrected once drawn. Listen closer to “Flamenco Sketches”: the last scale each soloist reaches is the Spanish-sounding one, and each man decides for himself how long to stay there. The original pressings also run very slightly sharp, because one of the three-track machines ran fractionally slow — corrected only in the 1990s.","Praised on release, it became the best-selling jazz album ever made and a shorthand for good taste, which has provoked a backlash of its own: the complaint that it now gets played as furniture. The authorship of “Blue in Green” is disputed. Bill Evans said he wrote it, most scholars agree, and the credit still reads otherwise. Nobody disputes what the record did to everyone who came after it.","Listen to “So What” twice: once for Davis, once for Jimmy Cobb's cymbal, which is the record's pulse and its weather. Then follow Coltrane from here to A Love Supreme and hear where the scales took him. Any pressing from the corrected remaster onward is fine; earlier ones are a shade too bright and too fast."],"miles-davis-sketches-of-spain":["The long centrepiece is the slow movement of Joaquín Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez, with Davis's flugelhorn in the guitar's place and Gil Evans's orchestra — harp, castanets, low woodwind, snare drums — standing in for the strings. Around it sit “Will o' the Wisp” from Falla's El amor brujo, a funeral-procession “Saeta” in which Davis imitates the unaccompanied Andalusian street chant, and “Solea”, a long flamenco lament laid over a march.","Davis heard a recording of the Concierto in 1959 and began buying Spanish folk records by the armful; Evans studied flamenco forms for months before writing a note. The sessions ran long and over budget, with classical session players who found the rhythms hard to place. Spain itself was closed under Franco and neither man had been there, so the record is an act of imagination assembled in a New York studio.","In “Saeta” the snare drums of the procession approach from a distance, halt, and start again: the band is staging a Holy Week street ritual indoors, with Davis as the singer who interrupts it from a balcony. Listen too for how often Evans doubles the trumpet at the octave with a bass clarinet or flute, so the soloist never quite sounds alone even at his most exposed.","Classical critics objected to the liberties taken with Rodrigo, who was reportedly unhappy; jazz critics complained there was hardly any improvising. Both were right and both missed the point, and the album outsold nearly everything Evans ever wrote. Later writers have debated homage against appropriation, and recent scholarship tends to place it in the long line of Spanish tinge running through jazz from its beginnings.","Play Rodrigo's Adagio first, on guitar, then this — the same melody, the same key, an utterly different silence around it. Listen for the moment near the end of “Solea” when the orchestra drops away and the trumpet keeps going. Then hear Falla's El amor brujo for the source of the record's darkest colours."],"gil-evans-out-of-the-cool":["Evans's own band, without Miles Davis in front of it. Five long tracks for around fifteen players, weighted heavily towards low brass and woodwind, with guitar in the rhythm section. “La Nevada” is the heart of it: fifteen minutes built on a single riff that Evans cues and re-cues, letting soloists enter while the texture thickens and thins like weather. The writing leaves large holes on purpose and the band fills them differently each chorus.","Impulse! was a new label with money and ambition, and Evans, then nearing fifty, finally had a record under his own name and his own control. He had spent a decade as the invisible hand behind Davis's orchestral albums and had a reputation as the slowest writer in New York. The programme mixes his own pieces with Kurt Weill's “Bilbao Song” and George Russell's “Stratusphunk” — a fair map of where his ear was pointing.","“La Nevada” was not fully written down. Evans brought in a riff and shaped the performance from the piano as it happened, cueing entrances with a nod, which is why the ensemble sometimes arrives raggedly and why nobody troubled to fix it. Listen for the bass trombone and tuba sustaining under everything: the bottom of an Evans band never stops breathing.","This is the Evans album musicians name when they want to argue that he was a composer rather than an arranger. Critics of the day filed it beside the Davis collaborations; later writers have treated it as the more radical record, because the looseness anticipates the open forms of the decade that followed. The alternate takes released since show how much was decided in the room.","Give “La Nevada” its full length with headphones and follow one instrument — the guitar is the easiest — all the way through. Then go back to Miles Ahead and hear the same hand writing for a soloist instead of an orchestra. The Impulse! stereo mix spreads the winds wide and is worth seeking."],"bill-evans-peace-piece":["Seven minutes of solo piano over a two-chord figure in the left hand that never changes and never stops. The right hand begins with plain, slow melody and drifts steadily further from the key, thickening into clusters and bell-like dissonance before withdrawing again to where it began. There is no theme, no head, no solo in the ordinary sense — only a rocking left hand and a mind wandering above it.","Evans recorded it in December 1958 at the end of a day's work on his second Riverside album. The ostinato came from a vamp he had been playing behind Leonard Bernstein's song “Some Other Time”, which the trio had tried and abandoned. He was twenty-nine, months away from joining Miles Davis's sextet, and his conservatory training — he had played Ravel and Debussy seriously — was audible to everyone who heard him.","The performance is improvised in one take and unedited. Follow the left hand: it is the same two chords from beginning to end, so everything that sounds like harmonic motion is happening above a floor that does not move. Near the middle the right hand reaches notes belonging to no key at all, and the left simply keeps rocking underneath, which is what makes the dissonance feel calm rather than tense.","It was a filler track that became the piece students learn first. Critics have debated whether to call it jazz at all, and Evans rarely played it live, saying it could not be repeated. Its lineage is argued over too: Satie and Debussy are the names usually offered, though the Bernstein vamp is documented and much closer. Minimalist composers have claimed it retrospectively.","Play it in the dark and resist the urge to wait for a tune. Then hear Satie's first Gymnopédie and notice the shared trick: a slow, unvarying accompaniment that makes anything laid over it weightless. From here Kind of Blue is six months away and sounds inevitable."],"bill-evans-portrait-in-jazz":["The first record by the trio of Evans, the bassist Scott LaFaro and the drummer Paul Motian, and the one where the format changes. LaFaro does not walk behind the piano: he plays counter-melodies in the same register, answering phrases and sometimes starting them. Motian uses brushes and mallets more than sticks. The programme is standards — two takes of “Autumn Leaves”, “Someday My Prince Will Come” — plus “Blue in Green”, carried over from Kind of Blue.","Evans had left Miles Davis's band after eight months, exhausted and uneasy in the spotlight. Riverside's Orrin Keepnews gave him a trio date at the very end of 1959. LaFaro was twenty-three and had the technique to play lines rather than roots; Motian was willing to abandon timekeeping altogether. The three had worked the material out in rooms rather than on the stand, which is why a first album sounds like a fifth.","Listen to the two versions of “Autumn Leaves” one after the other: in one LaFaro plays melody under the piano, in the other he stays out of the way. The trio is auditioning its own principle in public. Notice too how often Evans leaves the root out of a chord entirely, trusting the bass to supply it or to decline, which is why the harmony seems to hang in the air.","Reviewers liked it; musicians rebuilt their bands around it. Every piano trio since has had to decide whether to take LaFaro's freedom or refuse it. The lasting argument is whether Evans's introversion was temperament or manner, and whether the classical touch thins the swing — a charge most pianists now consider answered by the playing itself.","Start with “Autumn Leaves” and listen only to the bass for one chorus, then play it again and listen only to the drums. Compare any Red Garland trio record of the fifties for the older division of labour. The Riverside stereo mix separates the three players cleanly, which helps the first time through."],"bill-evans-sunday-at-the-village-vanguard":["Recorded live in a Greenwich Village basement on a Sunday afternoon and evening, with the audience audible throughout — glasses, conversation, the occasional laugh. The selection favours Scott LaFaro: his two compositions, “Gloria's Step” and “Jade Visions”, both appear, and the mix keeps his bass well forward. “Alice in Wonderland” shows the trio's habit of letting the tempo breathe rather than hold. It is a quiet record made in a noisy room.","The trio had been playing together for about eighteen months and had recorded twice in the studio. Orrin Keepnews brought a recording truck to the Village Vanguard on 25 June 1961 and taped five sets. Eleven days later LaFaro was killed in a car crash in upstate New York, aged twenty-five. Evans stopped playing for months, and Riverside drew two albums from the tapes: this one and Waltz for Debby.","“Gloria's Step” is incomplete as recorded, because the tape machine dropped out for a few seconds during the first take; the released version patches the gap. Listen also to the audience. On the quietest numbers the room noticeably drops its voice and then resumes between tunes — the band is not playing over the crowd but pulling it down to its own volume.","Released months after LaFaro's death, it was received as a memorial, and critics have argued ever since about how much of its standing belongs to the story. The consensus holds that both albums from the date are among the finest live jazz recordings made. Complete editions of all five sets appeared later and confirmed how consistent the playing was across the whole day.","Begin with “Gloria's Step” and follow the bass as a lead instrument; it takes a minute to stop hearing it as accompaniment. Then play Waltz for Debby, from the same afternoon. If the music takes hold, the complete Vanguard sessions are worth the length — the between-tune talk is part of it."],"brubeck-time-out":["Seven original pieces by the Dave Brubeck Quartet, every one in a metre jazz had avoided: nine beats to the bar, five, six, three against four. Paul Desmond's alto saxophone is dry and almost vibratoless, Brubeck's piano blocky and chord-heavy, Eugene Wright's bass steady as a metronome, Joe Morello's drums quick and light. The contrast between Desmond's floating lines and Brubeck's hammered chords is the group's whole character, audible within ten seconds.","Brubeck had studied composition with Darius Milhaud at Mills College in California, who told him not to abandon jazz for the concert hall. In 1958 the quartet toured Eurasia for the US State Department, and Brubeck heard Turkish street musicians playing a rhythm of nine beats grouped unevenly. Columbia was nervous: an album of originals in odd metres behind an abstract painting looked like commercial suicide. It became one of the first jazz albums to sell a million copies.","The nine beats of “Blue Rondo à la Turk” are grouped two-two-two-three, the lopsided version Brubeck heard in the street rather than the tidy three-three-three a Western composer would write. Listen also to Wright's bass on the trickiest tracks: he plays almost nothing but roots on the strong beats, the anchor that lets everyone else float. Brubeck cancelled bookings rather than tour the segregated South without him.","Critics of the day accused Brubeck of stiffness, and of being a white musician handed a magazine cover that better players deserved — a charge he accepted with visible discomfort, saying Ellington should have had it first. The metres were dismissed as gimmicks. Sixty years on the record is still in print, still the entry point for new listeners, and the rhythmic argument has been thoroughly won.","Try to clap along to “Blue Rondo à la Turk” and fail, then feel the long beat underneath and stop counting. “Take Five” is the hit, but “Three to Get Ready” is the charmer, alternating waltz and march bar by bar. Compare Bartók's Bulgarian dances for the same lopsided rhythms collected in another tradition."],"brubeck-take-five":["Five beats to the bar, grouped three then two, over a vamp of two chords that lasts the entire piece. Paul Desmond wrote it and plays the tune, a thin dry alto line floating above Brubeck's rocking piano figure. The middle is given over to Joe Morello's drum solo, which keeps the five going while playing across it. The single edit runs well under four minutes; the music never modulates, never resolves, and simply stops.","Desmond wrote the tune at Brubeck's request for something in five, building it from figures Morello had been playing at soundchecks. Columbia released it as a single in 1959 without much hope, and it charted twice, becoming the money that funded the quartet for the rest of its working life. Desmond later left his composer royalties to the American Red Cross, which continues to receive them.","The famous piano vamp is the accompaniment, not the tune. Brubeck plays it unchanged from first bar to last, so the piece has no harmonic motion at all — exactly the discovery Miles Davis was making in the same year. Listen to Morello's solo with the vamp in mind: the piano holds the five while the drums pretend to lose it and never quite do.","It made the quartet famous and left Desmond, who wrote it quickly, faintly embarrassed. Purists called it a novelty, and its long afterlife in films, adverts and lifts has made it hard to hear freshly. Musicians still use it as the first lesson in odd metre, and drummers still argue about whether Morello's solo is played in five or in ten.","Count one-two-three, one-two right through and the strangeness turns into a groove. Notice that Desmond never once raises his volume. Then hear one of the quartet's live versions from the early sixties, which run twice as long and show what the studio take was holding back."],"brubeck-blue-rondo-a-la-turk":["It opens with the piano hammering nine beats to the bar in an uneven pattern — short, short, short, long — at a speed that sounds impossible, with the saxophone doubling it. Then, without warning, the band drops into a slow, ordinary blues in four, and for the rest of the piece the two worlds alternate, the nine swinging back like a door. The improvising all happens in the blues; the nine is the frame around it.","Brubeck heard the rhythm from street musicians in Istanbul during the quartet's 1958 State Department tour and asked where it came from; the answer, as he liked to tell it, was that it was as natural to them as the blues was to him. The title glances at Mozart's Rondo alla Turca, which is neither in nine nor especially Turkish. The piece opens Time Out and announces exactly what the album intends.","The nine divides two-two-two-three, and the joins are where the trick lives: listen for how the band gets out of the nine and into the four, which happens on a single shared beat with no signpost at all. Brubeck also shifts the long unit to different places in the bar in later choruses, so the pattern keeps tilting under the same tempo.","This was the track that convinced sceptics the odd metres were musical rather than arithmetical, and it remains the harder of Time Out's two famous pieces to play. Critics have since noted the irony of a Californian borrowing an Istanbul street rhythm and naming it after Mozart's fantasy of Turkey, though Brubeck's own account of the encounter is warmer than the title suggests.","Listen for the switch into the blues about a minute in — the moment the room relaxes. Then compare Bartók's Romanian and Bulgarian dances, written out of the same kind of field encounter decades earlier, and Mozart's Turkish rondo, which is what this sort of borrowing used to sound like."],"chet-baker-chet-baker-sings":["Baker sings standards in a light, almost boyish tenor with no vibrato, no ornament and very little volume, then plays a trumpet solo that sounds like the same voice with the words removed. “My Funny Valentine”, “But Not for Me”, “Let's Get Lost”, “I Fall in Love Too Easily”. The band behind him — piano, bass, drums — stays quiet enough that you hear breath. Nothing is dramatised; the effect is of someone singing to himself close to a microphone.","Baker was twenty-four, had just left Gerry Mulligan's quartet, and was being sold by Pacific Jazz as the photogenic face of West Coast jazz. Singing was the label's commercial idea and it worked: the record outsold his instrumental albums, was expanded two years later, and fixed his public image for life. Los Angeles studios in the mid-fifties were cheap and close-miked, so the intimacy is partly a technology. This voice could not have filled a room.","Listen to where he breathes. Baker phrases as a trumpeter, holding a line past the point a trained singer would take air, then snatching a breath in an odd place, which is why the delivery sounds unguarded rather than styled. He also keeps almost entirely to one narrow register, transposing songs downward to stay in it, so the melodies come out flattened and conversational.","Singers were scathing about the pitch and the absence of technique; the public did not care, and the record has never been out of print. Queer and feminist readings have since been drawn to the androgyny of the voice. Baker's later life — heroin, prison, a broken jaw, a fatal fall in Amsterdam in 1988 — has coloured every rehearing, sometimes at the music's expense.","Start with “But Not for Me” and hear how the trumpet solo answers the vocal in identical phrasing. Then hear Frank Sinatra sing “My Funny Valentine” and measure the distance between craft and confession. The mono Pacific Jazz mix is the one to look for; later stereo remixes push the voice too far forward."],"chet-baker-my-funny-valentine":["Rodgers and Hart's song played by the Gerry Mulligan Quartet — trumpet, baritone saxophone, bass and drums, and no piano at all. Baker states the melody almost straight, in the middle of the horn, with a tone so unshaded it could be a flugelhorn. Mulligan does not take a solo; he plays a countermelody underneath, so the two horns move like two singers. Without a piano there is no chord to lean on, and the harmony is implied between them.","Mulligan formed the quartet in Los Angeles in 1952, partly because the club he was playing had no piano worth using. Baker, then unknown and barely able to read music, won the trumpet chair at an audition. Pacific Jazz was a new label run out of a record shop, and the West Coast scene it documented was cheap to record, beautifully photographed and very quickly turned into a brand.","Count the bars supported by nothing but bass and brushes: the absent piano is not a subtraction but a structural decision, forcing the two horns to supply the harmony between them. Listen for Mulligan's line in the second half, which is not a harmony part but a separate tune — the arrangement is closer to two-part Baroque counterpoint than to a jazz head.","The record made Baker famous within months and won him magazine polls over trumpeters with far more technique, which caused lasting resentment and the charge that West Coast jazz was a white, photogenic dilution of bebop. Later critics have managed to separate the marketing from the music, and the performance is now taught as the model of what a pianoless group can do.","Listen for the countermelody rather than the tune; once you hear Mulligan's line as an equal, the record changes shape. Then hear Baker sing it two years later, and after that Miles Davis's long, furious live version from 1964, which takes the same song somewhere else entirely."],"gerry-mulligan-walkin-shoes":["A relaxed medium-tempo original for the pianoless quartet: Mulligan's baritone saxophone, Chet Baker's trumpet, bass and brushed drums. The tune ambles, as the title promises. The two horns state it in loose harmony, then trade and overlap so that the accompaniment is always the other horn. Mulligan's baritone is light and agile, played high in its range, nothing like the honking bottom-of-the-band instrument of the swing era.","Mulligan had arrived in California from New York with a reputation as an arranger — several of the Birth of the Cool charts were his — and almost no money. The quartet's residency at the Haig, a small club on Wilshire Boulevard, turned him into a bandleader. Pacific Jazz recorded the group cheaply and often. A narcotics arrest in 1953 broke up the band after barely a year of work.","Listen to how the two horns divide the labour in the final chorus: whoever is not playing the melody is playing a bass line or a riff, so four players cover the work of six. The drummer plays brushes almost throughout and tunes his drums high, which keeps the bottom of the sound clear for the baritone to move around in.","The quartet's records sold enormously and became the emblem of West Coast cool, which later critics treated as a suspiciously comfortable style: polite, white, well recorded. Mulligan spent decades objecting that his band swung hard and that the geography was an accident of where the work was. Heard now, through decent reissues, the playing is springier than the reputation.","Follow the baritone saxophone alone and notice how rarely it descends. Then hear the same quartet's “My Funny Valentine” for the ballad version of the idea, and Mulligan's later Concert Jazz Band for what he did once he had a full brass section again."],"lee-konitz-subconscious-lee":["A new melody written over the chord sequence of Cole Porter's “What Is This Thing Called Love”, played by Konitz's alto and Billy Bauer's guitar in unison, with Lennie Tristano at the piano. The line is long, even and almost without accent — no bebop stabs, no obvious phrase endings — and Konitz's tone is pale and vibratoless, deliberately unlike Charlie Parker's. The rhythm section keeps time and does very little else.","Tristano, a blind pianist from Chicago, ran what amounted to a private conservatory in New York, where students learned to sing solos from records before playing them and were forbidden to lean on familiar phrases. Konitz was his most gifted pupil and the one alto player of the period who could be heard without thinking of Parker. These Prestige sides are the school's manifesto, recorded in the same year as Miles Davis's nonet.","The theme is composed, not improvised, yet it sounds like a solo — which is exactly Tristano's principle, that a written line should be indistinguishable from a good improvisation. Listen for the accents, or rather their absence: Konitz places weight almost nowhere, so the ear cannot locate the bar lines and the melody seems to have started before the record did.","Tristano's circle was admired and disliked in roughly equal measure; the playing was called cerebral, bloodless, a white intellectual's jazz. Konitz outlived the argument by seventy years of playing and is now heard as one of the few genuinely independent voices of his generation. Scholars also note that the group recorded free improvisations in 1949, a decade before Ornette Coleman.","Listen to the unison head twice before following the solos; getting the shape of that line into your ear is the whole lesson. Then hear Parker on the same chord sequence for the heat Konitz was deliberately avoiding. Konitz's album Motion, from 1961, stretches the approach across a whole record."],"stan-getz-focus":["Seven movements for string orchestra by the arranger Eddie Sauter, with Getz's tenor saxophone improvising over them. There is no written solo part at all: Sauter composed the strings complete and left Getz to find his own way through. The writing is nervy and contrapuntal rather than lush — no cushion of sustained chords — so the saxophone has to argue with the strings instead of floating on them. Drums join for one movement only.","Getz had returned from three years in Scandinavia and was between styles, his cool-toned manner out of fashion beside hard bop. Verve's Creed Taylor paired him with Sauter, a swing-era arranger with serious compositional ambitions. Records of a jazz soloist with strings were usually commercial sweetening; this one inverted the formula by making the strings the difficult element and the saxophone the free one.","The movement “I'm Late, I'm Late” is built on a scurrying string figure openly indebted to Bartók's fast night-music, and Getz enters chasing it rather than riding on it. Listen for the passages where he stops playing entirely: the score stands up perfectly well alone, which is why the record feels composed rather than accompanied, and why his silences carry weight.","Getz called it his favourite among his own records, and it is the one critics reach for when defending third stream against the charge of compromise. It sold modestly. Later assessments give more credit to Sauter, whose name is rarely on the spine; the argument now is whether the album belongs to the arranger or the soloist, which is precisely the question the music poses.","Play “I'm Late, I'm Late” first and hear the saxophone catch up. Then put on Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta for the sound world Sauter was working in. Nothing else in Getz's discography sounds like this, so hear it before the bossa nova records rather than after them."],"stan-getz-getz-gilberto":["Eight songs, most of them by Antônio Carlos Jobim, who plays the piano. João Gilberto sings in Portuguese and plays the guitar pattern that defines bossa nova, a syncopated figure that lands almost nowhere you expect. Getz's tenor answers him in long, warm phrases. On two tracks Gilberto's wife Astrud sings the English words in an untrained, almost affectless voice. Everything is quiet, and the quietness is the style rather than the mood.","Recorded in New York in March 1963 and held back a year by Verve. Bossa nova had reached the United States through Getz's earlier Jazz Samba, and Brazil was exporting the music deliberately as the sound of a modern, optimistic country — a year before the 1964 military coup ended that story. Astrud Gilberto had never recorded before; she was in the studio because she spoke English. The single, cut down from the album track, became a worldwide hit.","Listen to Gilberto's right hand. The guitar plays a two-bar pattern that contradicts his own singing, so voice and accompaniment are constantly a fraction apart — the tension that makes bossa nova feel as though it is leaning. Notice too that Getz never plays loudly enough to break the room; his volume is set by the guitar rather than the other way round.","It won the Grammy for album of the year, almost unheard of for a jazz record, and turned bossa nova into a lounge cliché within months. Brazilian critics have long been ambivalent about the version of their music that travelled. Astrud Gilberto was paid a session fee and no royalties, a fact that has shadowed the record ever since; her plain delivery is now widely credited as the reason it worked.","Hear “Corcovado” before “The Girl from Ipanema” — the same recipe without the familiarity. Listen for the moment Getz enters on each track, always after the voice and never over it. Then go to Gilberto's own Brazilian records, where the guitar is louder and the saxophone is not there at all."],"modern-jazz-quartet-django":["John Lewis's elegy for the guitarist Django Reinhardt, who had died the year before. A slow, dark theme in vibraphone and piano, stated in unison, gives way to solos over a different and faster sequence, and the theme returns at the end like a frame closing. Milt Jackson's vibraphone is the singing voice; Lewis's piano is spare, almost prim, placing two notes where another pianist would use ten. The bass carries the funeral tread throughout.","The quartet had grown out of Dizzy Gillespie's big band rhythm section and decided, unusually, to behave like a chamber ensemble: dinner jackets, concert halls, printed programmes, no patter from the stage. Lewis, who had studied music formally in New York, wrote most of the repertoire and set the group's manner; Jackson, a blues player to his fingertips, supplied the heat. The tension between the two men is the band.","The solo section is not the theme's chord sequence. Lewis wrote a separate structure with shifting held bass notes to improvise over, so the piece has two harmonic worlds rather than one — an idea borrowed from composed music and almost unheard of in a jazz standard. Listen for the bass holding a single note under long stretches of the solos while the harmony moves above it.","Musicians adopted it immediately and it became one of the most recorded jazz compositions of the fifties. The quartet's formality drew fire: critics called them stiff, and some heard the concert dress as a bid for respectability on white terms. Lewis argued that jazz deserved the same conditions as any other music, and Jackson's periodic frustration with the discipline is part of the group's history.","Listen for the moment the slow theme returns at the end and reframes everything that happened in between. Then hear the same group's Third Stream Music for where the formality led. A Reinhardt record afterwards makes the dedication land — nothing in the tune imitates him, which is the tact of it."],"modern-jazz-quartet-third-stream-music":["The quartet plays with two guests: the Jimmy Giuffre Three, a drummerless group of reed, guitar and bass, and the Beaux Arts String Quartet. The pieces, by John Lewis, Giuffre and Gunther Schuller, alternate written passages for the combined ensembles with improvised sections for the jazz players. The sound is unusual — vibraphone against violins, clarinet against a jazz rhythm section — and the seams between written and improvised are deliberately left audible.","Schuller had used the phrase third stream in a lecture in 1957 to describe a music that was neither jazz nor classical but fed by both, and by 1960 the idea had enough currency to sell a record. Atlantic backed it. The Modern Jazz Quartet was the obvious vehicle: a jazz group already touring concert halls, already playing fugues, already trusted by classical promoters and resented by some jazz writers for exactly that.","Listen to the string players' rhythm. Classically trained musicians in 1960 rarely swung, and the writing mostly declines to ask them to, giving them sustained lines and counterpoint while the jazz musicians supply the pulse. That division of labour is the honest solution to the problem the album sets itself, and where the two groups do meet rhythmically you can hear the effort it costs.","The reception set the pattern for third stream in general: classical critics found the writing thin, jazz critics found the strings a hindrance, and both accused the project of being a theory rather than a music. Schuller argued the case for the rest of his life. Later listeners, less invested in the border, tend to hear a curious and often lovely record that nobody quite followed up.","Start with the Giuffre pieces, where the blend is easiest, before the string tracks. Listen for Milt Jackson's vibraphone, equally at home in both settings, which is the proof that the experiment was possible at all. Then compare Schuller's Transformation and Getz's Focus for two other answers to the same question."],"john-coltrane-my-favorite-things":["Thirteen minutes on a waltz from a Broadway musical, played by Coltrane on soprano saxophone — an instrument almost nobody in modern jazz had touched — over McCoy Tyner's stacked piano chords and Elvin Jones's rolling drums. The tune appears at the start and at the end. In between, the band alternates between two chords for chorus after chorus, the piano hammering, the soprano circling a small group of notes until they stop sounding like a tune at all.","Coltrane had left Miles Davis's group in 1960 and formed his own quartet, and Atlantic wanted something that would sell. Rodgers and Hammerstein's show had opened on Broadway in 1959, so the song was current and familiar to everyone. The soprano saxophone came to Coltrane partly through his study of Sidney Bechet; its nasal, reedy cry, closer to a shawm than to a saxophone, became his second voice almost overnight.","The waltz never stops. Underneath everything, Jones keeps three beats to the bar while playing patterns that suggest four and six at once, so the music feels rooted and unmoored together. Listen for the switch between the minor and major versions of the same two-chord vamp: Coltrane uses it as a light switch, and the return to minor is the darkest moment each time it happens.","An edited version became a hit and Coltrane's calling card, and he played the piece for the rest of his life in versions that grew steadily longer and wilder until the tune was barely present. Critics who loved this recording often recoiled from the later ones. The soprano revival it set off changed the instrumentation of jazz for a generation.","Listen to the piano's left hand, which plays two chords for ten minutes and never tires of them. Then find a late live version from 1965 or 1966 to hear where the idea went. The original album is worth having whole, but this track is the reason it exists."],"john-coltrane-africa-brass":["Coltrane's first record for Impulse! and his only large-ensemble date of the period: the quartet plus a brass group of French horns, euphonium, trombones and tuba, scored mainly by Eric Dolphy from Coltrane's sketches. “Africa” runs sixteen minutes over a bass pattern played by two bassists at once, the brass entering in slow slabs behind the tenor. “Greensleeves” turns the English tune into a modal waltz, and “Blues Minor” is the short hard closer.","Impulse! signed Coltrane in 1961 with a promise of freedom and a budget, and he spent it immediately on brass. He had been listening to recordings of African drumming and reading about non-Western music, an interest that would dominate his last years and is already organising this record. Dolphy, a close friend, did the scoring and conducted, though the label credited him grudgingly at the time.","The brass writing barely modulates. Dolphy voices the horns in dense, static chords that work as colour rather than as harmony, so the ensemble behaves like an enormous drone — the opposite of a big band's usual job. Listen for the two basses on “Africa”: one holds the pattern while the other departs from it, which is why the floor of the music seems to move.","Reviewers divided between those who heard a new orchestral jazz and those who found it monotonous, a complaint that followed Coltrane from here on. The sessions produced more music than one album could hold, and later editions have shuffled versions of “Africa” and “Greensleeves” in and out. Dolphy's contribution is valued far more highly now than it was in 1961.","Put on “Africa” and stop waiting for a chord change; the interest is in the layering, not the motion. Follow the tuba. Then hear Gil Evans's orchestras for the other way of writing low brass behind a soloist, and A Love Supreme for where this drone eventually goes."],"john-coltrane-ballads":["Eight songs, none longer than five minutes, played by the quartet — Coltrane, McCoy Tyner, Jimmy Garrison, Elvin Jones — almost entirely in the melody. There are solos, but they stay close to the tune and to the middle of the horn. Coltrane's tone here is broad and unhurried, with none of the vertical scrambling of the preceding years. “Say It (Over and Over Again)”, “You Don't Know What Love Is”, “Nancy (With the Laughing Face)”.","Coltrane's live playing in 1961 and 1962 had drawn accusations of anti-jazz from critics who found the long modal explorations shapeless. Impulse!'s Bob Thiele scheduled a run of approachable records in response: this one, an album with Duke Ellington, another with the singer Johnny Hartman. A frequently repeated story holds that a damaged mouthpiece made slow playing easier that season. It is plausible and not firmly documented.","Listen to Elvin Jones, who does not play a straightforward ballad beat anywhere on the record. Under the calmest melodies he keeps a shifting, triplet-heavy undertow going at very low volume, so the quiet is never static. Tyner meanwhile voices his chords in fourths rather than thirds — the sound of the quartet's most radical records, applied to songs your grandparents knew.","It was dismissed at the time as a commercial retreat and has since become one of Coltrane's best-loved albums, the one non-specialists own. The argument about whether it represents rest or capitulation continues, and the mouthpiece story is part of that argument. Nothing in the playing sounds reluctant, which is the strongest evidence against the capitulation reading.","Play it late, quietly and straight through; it is short. Listen for the drummer under the melody rather than the saxophone on top of it. Then go to A Love Supreme, recorded two years later by the same four men, and hear how far one sound can travel."],"john-coltrane-a-love-supreme":["A suite in four parts — “Acknowledgement”, “Resolution”, “Pursuance”, “Psalm” — played by the quartet in about thirty-three minutes. A four-note bass figure opens it and runs through everything; near the end of the first part Coltrane sings the words of the title over that figure, many times over. “Pursuance” is fast and hard, with a long Elvin Jones solo. “Psalm” has no theme and almost no pulse: the saxophone recites.","Recorded in one evening in December 1964 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in Englewood Cliffs, New Jersey. Coltrane had stopped using heroin in 1957 after what he described as a spiritual awakening, and the suite is the thanksgiving he had been promising ever since. He wrote a poem and printed it in the sleeve alongside a signed letter to the listener. The record appeared as the civil rights movement entered its most dangerous years.","“Psalm” is the poem. Coltrane plays it phrase by phrase, one saxophone phrase per line of text, so the movement is a wordless recitation you can follow with the sleeve in your hand, right down to the closing amen. Listen also to the opening bass figure: Coltrane transposes it through key after key in the first part, refusing to let it settle before the voices enter.","It sold in numbers no Coltrane album had reached and remains the record that carries his name to people who own no other jazz. Some critics were uneasy with the explicit religion; others have argued that the devotional frame made the increasingly free playing of his last years legible. A sextet version and a live Antibes performance, both released much later, have changed how scholars read the composition.","Read the poem while “Psalm” plays; one hearing turns the movement inside out. Start with “Acknowledgement” for the bass figure, which is the whole suite in four notes. Then compare Messiaen's Quartet for the End of Time for another sacred piece built on slow, static devotion rather than argument."],"george-russell-all-about-rosie":["Three connected movements for a jazz orchestra of about fifteen players, lasting eleven minutes, all derived from a single short phrase — a Black children's game song from Alabama. The first movement is fast and contrapuntal, the second slow and dissonant, the third a headlong swing over which Bill Evans plays the piano solo that made his name. The writing is dense and the counterpoint real, yet the band never stops sounding like a jazz band.","Brandeis University commissioned six pieces in 1957 for its Festival of the Creative Arts, three from jazz composers and three from concert composers, and had them all recorded by one mixed ensemble. Russell had spent years developing a theory of scales — the Lydian Chromatic Concept — arguing that jazz harmony should be built from modes rather than from chord sequences. Miles Davis knew the theory, and Kind of Blue was two years away.","The theme is only a few notes long. Russell derives every melody, countermelody and bass line across all three movements from it, in the way a symphonic composer would treat a motif, while the rhythm section swings unbothered underneath. Listen for the phrase inverted and slowed in the second movement, where it becomes almost unrecognisable, then hammered out plainly in the third.","The Brandeis commissions were meant to prove that jazz composition could stand in a concert hall, and this is the piece critics agreed had done it. Russell's reputation rests on the theory as much as on the music, and there is a long argument about how much of the modal turn belongs to him rather than to Davis. Bill Evans's solo has been transcribed and studied for decades.","Go straight to the third movement for the Evans solo, then start again from the beginning to hear how the material was assembled. Follow the little phrase; once you have it, the whole piece opens. Then hear Milestones, where the same thinking arrives in a band of six."],"schuller-transformation":["Eight minutes for eleven players. It opens as strict twelve-note chamber music — pointillist, pulseless, single notes passed between winds, vibraphone and piano in the manner of Webern. A drum beat enters almost imperceptibly, then a walking bass, and within two minutes the same material is being swung by a jazz band with Bill Evans at the piano. Near the end the process reverses and the music dissolves back into isolated notes and silence.","Schuller was a French horn player who had recorded with Miles Davis's nonet and played in the Metropolitan Opera orchestra, which made him unusually qualified to argue that the two musics could meet. He coined the term third stream in a lecture at Brandeis University in 1957, the same year the university commissioned this piece for its Festival of the Creative Arts. Serialism was then the prestige language of American academic composition.","The transformation is literal and audible if you follow the drummer. The jazz does not arrive as a new section; it is smuggled in one element at a time — first a cymbal, then a bass note on the beat, then a piano voicing — so there is no bar where the style changes, only a few where you notice that it has. The twelve-note row survives the crossing intact.","It remains the clearest demonstration of what Schuller meant, and the usual exhibit for both sides of the argument: proof of concept to his defenders, a laboratory experiment to critics who thought jazz needed no legitimising. Schuller went on to write a two-volume history of jazz that changed the field's scholarship. The piece is performed rarely, which says more about programming than about the music.","Listen once without knowing when the jazz begins and try to mark the moment; you will be wrong. Then follow the piano through the whole span. Afterwards hear Webern's miniatures for the opening language, and All About Rosie, written for the same festival, for the journey made in the other direction."],"jimmy-giuffre-train-and-the-river":["Five minutes for a trio with no drummer and no piano: Giuffre's clarinet, low and woody in its bottom register, with guitar and bass. The piece alternates two ideas — a rocking blues figure that is the river and a chugging, syncopated one that is the train — and never states a beat outright. The pulse is carried by the guitar's strumming and by implication, which leaves the music unhurried and slightly weightless.","Giuffre had been an arranger for Woody Herman's band before moving to California and stripping his own music down to almost nothing. In the mid-fifties he was preoccupied with folk song, country blues and the idea that a jazz group could dispense with drums entirely, and Atlantic let him try it. The trio's performance at the 1958 Newport Jazz Festival was filmed for Jazz on a Summer's Day, which is how most people first met the piece.","The clarinet stays in its dark bottom octave — the hollow register that classical composers reserve for menace and that swing-era jazz clarinettists almost never visited. Listen for how Giuffre articulates: he tongues very little, letting notes slur into one another, which is a country fiddler's phrasing transferred to a reed instrument.","Critics of the day used it as evidence that West Coast jazz had gone soft, and Giuffre's later career — increasingly abstract, eventually free, and commercially disastrous — was read as a warning. Reassessment came late. His 1961 trio with Paul Bley and Steve Swallow is now treated as a landmark of quiet free improvisation, and this piece is the friendly door into that body of work.","Find the clip from Jazz on a Summer's Day and watch the players listen to each other; the missing drummer becomes visible. Then hear Giuffre's Free Fall from 1962 for the same instincts taken to the edge. Compare Copland's American vein for the folk colour in the writing."],"ahmad-jamal-poinciana":["Eight minutes on an old popular song, recorded live in a Chicago cocktail lounge by a trio of piano, bass and drums. Vernel Fournier plays a repeating figure on tom-toms and closed hi-hat that never varies and never swings in the ordinary way; Israel Crosby's bass holds a two-note pattern. Jamal plays very little, leaving bars of silence, then fills the space suddenly with a cascade. The tension between the unchanging groove and the unpredictable piano is the whole performance.","Jamal had worked Chicago rooms for years with a trio built on discipline, rehearsed relentlessly and instructed to play less rather than more. The album, recorded at the Pershing Lounge in January 1958, sold in enormous numbers and stayed on the charts for years, which was unheard of for a jazz piano trio. Miles Davis had been listening since the early fifties and told his own pianists to study Jamal's use of space.","The drum pattern is the tune's memory. Fournier keeps it going through the piano's silences, so nothing ever collapses when Jamal stops playing — the groove carries the form, not the soloist. Listen for the two or three moments when he plays the melody plainly; they land like arrivals because he has withheld it for so long.","Critics of the fifties often filed Jamal as a cocktail pianist, a judgment that took decades to reverse and that says more about the prestige of density in jazz criticism than about the playing. Davis's public admiration helped, as did the hip-hop producers who sampled his records heavily from the 1990s. He is now routinely named among the most influential pianists of the century.","Listen to the drums alone for the first chorus and nothing else. Then hear Milestones, where the same patience becomes modal jazz in a larger band. If it takes hold, the complete Pershing sets show a trio that could keep this up all night without raising its voice."],"ornette-coleman-the-shape-of-jazz-to-come":["Six Coleman tunes played by a quartet with no piano: Coleman's plastic alto, Don Cherry's pocket cornet, Charlie Haden's bass and Billy Higgins's drums. Each piece opens with a tight, often lopsided theme played in unison, then the solos head off without a fixed chord sequence underneath, following the melody's mood instead. The opener, Lonely Woman, is the track everyone knows: a slow, keening lament from the horns floating above bass and drums that race along at double speed.","Coleman had spent the 1950s in Los Angeles, laughed off bandstands and working as a lift operator while he studied theory books. Contemporary Records gave him two albums; then John Lewis of the Modern Jazz Quartet sent him to the Lenox School of Jazz in summer 1959 and to Atlantic, where Nesuhi Ertegun produced this session in Hollywood that May. The title was a label's boast, and that November the quartet opened at New York's Five Spot to fascinated, furious crowds.","Without a piano nobody is laying down chords, so the bass is free to move with the soloist rather than beneath him. Listen to Haden on Lonely Woman: he strums a repeating figure that almost suggests a key, then abandons it the moment Coleman drifts. The horns bend pitch deliberately, playing slightly sharp or flat for expression, which Coleman argued was a truer intonation for the human voice than the tempered scale.","The Five Spot run split New York: Leonard Bernstein was reported enthusiastic, while trumpeter Roy Eldridge and others suspected a put-on. Critics argued for years over whether Coleman could play. Within a decade the question had dissolved; Lonely Woman became a standard, recorded by the Modern Jazz Quartet among many others. It is now heard less as a revolution than as blues-rooted, singing music whose freedom lies in form, not noise.","Start with Lonely Woman and hold on to the two speeds at once, slow horns over a galloping rhythm section. Then play Congeniality and notice how the theme stops and starts like speech. Compare Miles Davis's Kind of Blue from the same year, which loosened harmony more politely. The original Atlantic stereo mix separates the four players cleanly; any faithful remaster will do."],"ornette-coleman-free-jazz":["One continuous improvisation of about 37 minutes by two quartets playing at once, one in each stereo channel. On the left, Coleman, Don Cherry, Scott LaFaro and Billy Higgins; on the right, Eric Dolphy on bass clarinet, Freddie Hubbard, Charlie Haden and Ed Blackwell. Short written fanfares mark the joins, and each player takes a turn in the spotlight while the other seven respond, argue and comment around him. The texture is dense, busy and surprisingly cheerful.","Recorded in a single New York day in December 1960 and issued the following year, a year after the Five Spot uproar had made Coleman the most debated musician in jazz. Stereo was still a novelty in jazz releases, and the double quartet used it as structure. The gatefold sleeve had a window cut into it showing a Jackson Pollock painting, White Light, making an explicit claim of kinship with Abstract Expressionism.","The notion that nothing was planned is wrong. The ensemble passages between solos are composed, the soloists appear in a set order, and a shorter first take of the same plan, about seventeen minutes long, survives and was later issued on the Atlantic album Twins. Listen for the bassists: LaFaro plays fast and melodic on the left while Haden stays low and grounded on the right.","Down Beat ran two reviews side by side, one maximum rating and one refusing to rate it at all, which neatly summarised the reaction. Its title named a movement, which Coleman came to find limiting. Musicians who dismissed it in 1961 were recording collective blowing sessions by 1965. Later listeners are often surprised by how swinging and good-humoured it is next to the noisier records it inspired.","Use headphones and follow one channel at a time: listen through once to the left quartet, then to the right. Notice how the horns briefly echo a soloist's phrase before scattering. Then hear Coltrane's Ascension, which took the idea to a bigger, hotter band, and Stockhausen's Gruppen, a composed piece for three orchestras that also treats space as form."],"ornette-coleman-at-the-golden-circle":["Two volumes recorded live in a Stockholm club by a trio: Coleman, David Izenzon on bass and Charles Moffett on drums. Most of it is Coleman's alto at its most lyrical, singing long lines over a bass that bows and swoops like a second voice. Then he picks up the violin and trumpet, instruments he had taught himself, and the sound changes completely: violin sawed in rapid, scraping strokes, trumpet in splintered bursts, colour and energy rather than conventional technique.","After 1962 Coleman withdrew from performing, partly in a dispute over fees, and spent the time practising violin and trumpet. He resurfaced in Europe in 1965, where audiences received him more warmly than New York's club owners, and Blue Note, by then open to the avant-garde, recorded the trio over two December nights at the Gyllene Cirkeln in Stockholm. It was his first Blue Note release.","Izenzon was trained in classical bowing and plays arco much of the time, so the bass often sounds like a cello in counterpoint with the saxophone rather than a timekeeper. On the violin tracks, notice that Coleman ignores conventional bowing altogether, sawing in rapid strokes that treat the instrument as a source of texture. Moffett keeps a crisp, almost march-like snare underneath.","Down Beat readers and European critics hailed the return, and the trio is widely held to be the best small group of Coleman's middle years. The violin and trumpet divided opinion: trained players heard incompetence, while others heard a musician refusing to let technique dictate sound. The Blue Note engineering is admired, and the two volumes are among the most approachable ways into his music.","Begin with the alto ballads on the first volume and notice how tuneful they are. Then put on a violin track and hear it as texture, like a painter changing brushes. Faces and Places shows the trio at full running speed. Compare Cecil Taylor's piano records of the same year to hear two very different ideas of freedom."],"ornette-coleman-dancing-in-your-head":["Two long variations on one short, bouncing, folk-like theme, played by Coleman's electric band Prime Time: two electric guitars, electric bass and drums weaving independent lines under his alto. The tune repeats over and over while everything around it shifts, so the effect is part dance record, part mosaic. A short final track, Midnight Sunrise, is something else: Coleman playing with the Master Musicians of Jajouka in the Moroccan hills, reeds and drums in a fierce ritual drone.","The theme comes from Skies of America, Coleman's 1972 piece for his quartet and the London Symphony Orchestra. By the mid-1970s he had been developing a theory he called harmolodics, in which every player may treat the melody as the root of their own line. He had visited Jajouka in 1973 with the writer Robert Palmer, and A&M's jazz imprint Horizon released the album as funk and electric jazz were both commercially strong.","Harmolodics is audible here if you listen to the guitars separately: each is effectively soloing on the theme in a different place at the same moment, rather than one playing chords behind the other. The theme itself never modulates in any conventional sense; what shifts is everything the other players build against it. On Midnight Sunrise the clarinet heard alongside Coleman is Palmer's.","Jazz critics were puzzled by the repetition and the backbeat; rock critics, and later the New York punk and no wave scene, embraced it. It fed directly into James Blood Ulmer's and Ronald Shannon Jackson's bands and the downtown funk of the late 1970s. Debate continues over whether harmolodics is a coherent theory or a poetic name for Coleman's instincts; the record's energy is not in dispute.","Let the theme lodge in your head in the first minute, then stop following Coleman and follow one guitar instead. Play loud. Afterwards try Midnight Sunrise on its own, then Steve Reich's Drumming, which builds different cycles from repetition. The CD reissue keeps both variations intact."],"coltrane-ascension":["A single forty-minute piece for eleven players. Roaring full-ensemble passages, in which horns overlap on a few loose scale shapes until the sound becomes a thick, surging mass, alternate with solos by Coltrane, Freddie Hubbard, Pharoah Sanders, Archie Shepp, Marion Brown, John Tchicai and Dewey Johnson, each backed by McCoy Tyner, bassists Jimmy Garrison and Art Davis and Elvin Jones. It opens with a brief five-note motif, then the dam breaks.","Recorded at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in New Jersey in June 1965, six months after A Love Supreme had made Coltrane jazz's most admired figure. He had been listening closely to the younger free players in New York and brought several of them in, most for the first time on a major label. Impulse! issued it early in 1966; it marks the end of the classic quartet's world, though they are all present.","Two complete takes were made, and both were released under the same title at different times, as Edition I and Edition II, after Coltrane changed his mind about which he preferred; their solo orders differ. Listen to the ensemble sections: the players are not improvising freely but moving through a sequence of agreed chords and scales at their own speed, which is why the mass seems to shift gradually, like weather.","Reactions in 1966 ranged from reverence to accusations that Coltrane had abandoned music for noise. Tyner and Jones, uneasy in the new direction, left within the year. It is now seen as the pivot of Coltrane's late period and a charter for large-group free jazz, from Europe's orchestras to the Jazz Composers Orchestra. Many listeners still find the recording harder to love than to admire.","Listen in one sitting and loud, treating the ensemble roars as landscape and the solos as figures walking through it. Notice how each soloist is framed by Tyner's rolling chords. Then compare Ornette Coleman's Free Jazz, which is sparer, and Penderecki's Threnody, a notated work of the same decade that also turns clusters of sound into drama."],"coltrane-meditations":["A continuous five-part suite, The Father and the Son and the Holy Ghost, Compassion, Love, Consequences and Serenity, for a sextet with two tenors, Coltrane and Pharoah Sanders, and two drummers, Elvin Jones and Rashied Ali. The first section is a howling storm, Sanders shrieking in the upper register; then the music calms into Compassion and a tender bass-led Love before rising again and ending in a quiet, hymn-like Serenity. The arc is an act of devotion.","Recorded in November 1965, the last studio album with McCoy Tyner and Elvin Jones, both of whom left within months. Coltrane was increasingly absorbed in religious reading, Christian, Hindu and Sufi, and in the idea that sound itself could heal. A quartet recording of the same suite, made two months earlier, surfaced years after his death as First Meditations; comparing the two shows what the extra horn and drummer added.","Listen to the drums: Jones plays in pulse and swing while Ali plays in washes of cymbal with no fixed beat, and the two kits argue across the stereo image. Love begins with Jimmy Garrison's bass alone, a calm after the storm, and Tyner's piano here is at its most hymn-like; the suite quietly looks back while moving on.","Many listeners found Sanders's screams unbearable at the time, and some of Coltrane's quartet admirers still do. Later assessments put Meditations beside A Love Supreme as a spiritual statement, but a rawer, more exposed one. The existence of First Meditations has fuelled debate over whether the quartet or the sextet version is the better realisation.","Treat it as one piece and listen through without skipping the opening storm; the peace of Love only lands if you have been through it. Then hear First Meditations for quartet. For contrast with a composed spiritual arc, try Messiaen's Quartet for the End of Time, written in a prison camp and also ending in praise."],"coltrane-live-at-the-village-vanguard-again":["Two long performances from a May 1966 night at the Vanguard by Coltrane's last band: Pharoah Sanders, Alice Coltrane on piano, Jimmy Garrison, Rashied Ali and a percussionist. Side one is Naima, his gentle 1959 ballad, turned into a slow-burning prayer. Side two opens with a long, strummed solo introduction by Garrison, then My Favorite Things, the Rodgers and Hammerstein waltz that had been his hit, stretched and shattered into something ecstatic and nearly unrecognisable.","Five years earlier Coltrane had made his name at this same Greenwich Village club with Eric Dolphy; the title's Again! invites comparison. Tyner and Jones had gone, replaced by his wife Alice and the pulse-free drummer Ali. Coltrane was still filling rooms, but many older fans walked out. Impulse! released the album later that year, one of the few official live records from the final band.","Hear how the old tunes are used: Coltrane states the waltz theme of My Favorite Things on soprano almost exactly as on the 1961 record, then leaves it far behind, so the familiar melody works as a doorway rather than a destination. Garrison's introduction is played with chords and strums like a flamenco guitarist; it is one of the great bass solos on record.","Sanders's screaming contributions divided reviewers, and many listeners still regard this band's recordings as an ordeal. Others hear the most honest version of Coltrane's search, a band that trusted intensity over polish. Its reputation has risen steadily with the posthumous flood of late-period tapes, and it is the most accessible introduction to that period.","Start with the Garrison introduction on side two and let it lead you into My Favorite Things. Then play the 1961 studio My Favorite Things back to back to hear how far the same song travelled in five years. Naima rewards the same comparison with its 1959 original on Giant Steps."],"coltrane-interstellar-space":["Four duets for tenor saxophone and drums, named for planets: Mars, Venus, Jupiter and Saturn. Each starts with Coltrane shaking a set of sleigh bells before he states a short theme and launches into long, torrential improvisation while Rashied Ali plays waves of cymbal and tom with no fixed beat. Venus is gentler; Mars is ferocious. With nobody else present, you hear every breath, overblown shriek and fast rippling run in close focus.","Recorded at Van Gelder's studio in February 1967, five months before Coltrane died of liver cancer at 40. He was playing little in public by then. The tapes stayed in the vault until Impulse! released them in 1974; later reissues added further duets from the session. The saxophone-and-drums format was almost unknown in jazz at the time and became a staple of free improvisation afterwards.","The sleigh bells are the clue to structure: each time they sound, a new section is beginning, so the bells frame improvisations that would otherwise have no audible edges. Listen too for how often Coltrane, amid the storm, returns to a short melodic cell and turns it over, like a preacher repeating a phrase. Ali never plays a steady beat, yet the pulse is always felt.","Released posthumously to a jazz world that had moved toward fusion, it was received as a relic. Its standing has grown since the 1990s; many now hear it as the purest document of late Coltrane, free of any debate about his sidemen. Some listeners still find the undifferentiated intensity exhausting; others call it his last great record.","Start with Venus, the calmest, then take on Mars. Listen to the drums as much as the saxophone. The planet titles invite a comparison with Holst's The Planets; hear Holst's Mars after Coltrane's and notice two utterly different ideas of force. Then compare Peter Brötzmann's duets and trios, which built on this format."],"cecil-taylor-unit-structures":["Four pieces for a septet built around Taylor's piano: Eddie Gale's trumpet, Jimmy Lyons's alto, Ken McIntyre on alto, oboe and bass clarinet, two bassists, Henry Grimes and Alan Silva, and Andrew Cyrille's drums. The music moves in blocks, bursts of composed material handed from player to player, then sprawling improvisation. Taylor's piano is the engine: clusters struck with fists and forearms, fast runs, sudden silences. It sounds less like a band soloing on tunes than an orchestra thinking aloud.","Taylor had studied at the New England Conservatory and admired Bartók, Stravinsky and Ellington as much as Monk. For a decade he had struggled for work in New York, sometimes washing dishes. Blue Note, under Alfred Lion, recorded him in May 1966 at Van Gelder's studio, the first of two albums for the label. Taylor wrote his own liner notes, a dense prose poem about sound and structure.","Notice the two bassists: one bows high and the other plucks low, so they occupy different registers rather than doubling. The title refers to Taylor's method of building pieces from small units, cells passed round the band and recombined, which is why fragments you heard in the opening Steps keep resurfacing in later tracks. The oboe is rare in jazz and gives some passages a chamber-music colour.","Reviewers in 1966 were baffled or awed; LeRoi Jones and a handful of others championed Taylor as the music's most original mind. The record is now regarded as one of the high points of 1960s free jazz and the clearest early statement of Taylor's compositional thinking. Debate lingers over how much of it is written; the answer seems to be more than it sounds.","Try Enter Evening first, the quietest track, with oboe and bowed bass. Then go back to Steps and listen for the moments when the whole band snaps together on a composed figure. Compare Bartók's Fourth String Quartet, whose clusters and cell-building Taylor admired, and Elliott Carter's Double Concerto, a notated cousin in density."],"cecil-taylor-conquistador":["Two side-long pieces, Conquistador and With (Exit), for a sextet: Bill Dixon on trumpet, Jimmy Lyons on alto, basses from Henry Grimes and Alan Silva, and Andrew Cyrille on drums. It is more open and flowing than Unit Structures: long, wave-like build-ups, Taylor's piano rushing in torrents, Lyons threading bebop-derived lines through the storm, and Dixon's trumpet playing soft, smeared, breathy tones. The title track rises and falls in great surges for eighteen minutes.","Taylor's second and last Blue Note album, recorded that October at Van Gelder's studio. Bill Dixon, a trumpeter and organiser, had led the October Revolution in Jazz concerts of 1964 and the short-lived Jazz Composers Guild, efforts to give avant-garde players control of their own work. Blue Note was taking risks on the new music while also scoring hits with Lee Morgan and Horace Silver.","Dixon's trumpet is the odd colour here: he avoids brassy attacks and plays in low, breathy smears, almost like a muted voice, which sets up a very different conversation with the piano. Listen for Lyons, who stayed with Taylor for over twenty years; his alto keeps a thread of Charlie Parker running through music otherwise far from bebop. The CD reissue adds an alternate take of With (Exit).","Less discussed than Unit Structures at first, it has gained ground as the more immediately exciting of the two Blue Notes, with some critics preferring its sweep. Taylor then went years without a steady record deal, working in universities and Europe, so these two albums stood for a long time as his fullest studio statements.","Start at the beginning of the title track and ride the build-ups rather than trying to follow individual notes. Follow Lyons's alto as your guide. Then hear Unit Structures to compare Taylor's tighter and looser modes, and Ornette Coleman's Golden Circle trio from the same year for a different free idiom."],"cecil-taylor-silent-tongues":["A solo piano concert recorded at the Montreux Jazz Festival in July 1974: a five-part suite followed by encores. Taylor plays with astonishing physical force, arpeggios crashing up the keyboard, clusters hammered with the flat of the hand, thunderous bass rumbles, and then passages of hesitant, almost tender single notes. The movements have titles, Abyss, Petals and Filaments, Jitney, Crossing, After All, and the whole plays like one long argument that keeps returning to its opening material.","In the early 1970s Taylor taught at the University of Wisconsin and Antioch College, and his solo concerts had become events in Europe and Japan. Alan Bates's Freedom label, distributed in the United States by Arista, issued the Montreux set in 1975. Solo piano was newly fashionable in jazz thanks to Keith Jarrett's ECM concerts, which offered a very different model: flowing, tonal, meditative.","Listen for short figures from the opening of Abyss: Taylor keeps returning to his opening material, transformed, so the suite coheres like a composed work. Taylor is often called a percussive pianist, but notice his pedalling, which blurs clusters into halos of resonance; the piano rings like a gong in places, recalling Cage's and Cowell's interest in the instrument as a sound source.","Widely considered one of the finest solo records of the 1970s, it won Taylor wide European acclaim. Critics who had dismissed his music as chaotic found the solo format made his logic easier to hear. Comparisons with Jarrett's Köln Concert, recorded six months later, became a standard way to frame two possibilities for solo improvisation.","Try it in one sitting, loud, with no distractions, and listen for the returning material. Then hear the softest encore. For a composed counterweight, hear Scriabin's Fifth Sonata, all ecstatic flights, or Feldman's Triadic Memories, whose extreme quiet makes the opposite case for the piano."],"albert-ayler-spiritual-unity":["A trio of Ayler's tenor, Gary Peacock's bass and Sunny Murray's drums, in four pieces: Ghosts: First Variation, The Wizard, Spirits and Ghosts: Second Variation. Ayler's sound is huge, with a wide, trembling vibrato like an old gospel singer or a brass band at a funeral. He states simple, almost childlike melodies, then tears them apart in screams and honks. Peacock plays fast, free lines and Murray plays no beat at all, just a shimmer of cymbals.","Recorded in New York in July 1964 for Bernard Stollman's new ESP-Disk label, which paid little but let musicians record as they wished. Ayler, from Cleveland, had developed his sound playing in army bands and in Scandinavia, where he found more listeners than at home. The title is a statement of belief; he spoke of his music as spiritual in the most literal sense.","Ghosts is built on a tune that sounds like a hymn or a folk song, and it appears twice, opening and closing the record in two variations, a frame most listeners miss because the middle is so wild. Listen to Murray's drums: the cymbals hiss constantly and the bass drum marks accents rather than time, a sound he developed with Cecil Taylor that removes any sense of a regular beat.","It was barely reviewed at first, but musicians heard it; Coltrane reportedly championed Ayler and asked that he play at his funeral in 1967. The record is now regarded as a landmark of free jazz, some saying the first to fully abandon pulse and chord. Critics still argue about Ayler's intonation and his later turn toward rhythm and blues, but this trio is held in near-universal respect.","Put on Ghosts: First Variation and hum the tune along; it is catchier than its reputation suggests. Then listen to the whole record and let the vibrato wash over you. Compare Bessie Smith's St. Louis Blues and a New Orleans funeral march; Ayler is closer to those than to bebop."],"albert-ayler-love-cry":["Short, march-like and hymn-like tunes, most under five minutes, played by Ayler with his brother Donald on trumpet, Alan Silva on bass, Milford Graves on drums and, on several tracks, Call Cobbs on harpsichord. The harpsichord's tinny jangle is the surprise, making the music sound at times like a Baroque parlour invaded by a brass band. Ayler sings wordlessly on the title track, and revisits old themes, Ghosts and Bells, in compact new versions.","Ayler signed to Impulse! after Coltrane's intervention, and this was his third album for the label, recorded in 1967 and 1968. His music was moving toward shorter, more melodic forms and, on the next records, toward rhythm and blues and rock. He was found drowned in New York's East River in November 1970, aged 34; the circumstances remain unclear.","Notice how Ayler compresses his earlier pieces: the new Ghosts takes the tune of Spiritual Unity and plays it with trumpet in unison, almost like a Salvation Army band. Graves is one of the most original drummers of the era, playing flurries of tom-toms that sound like hand drums. Cobbs's harpsichord gives a church-hall or parlour feel that Ayler clearly wanted.","It sold modestly and was eclipsed in critical attention by his ESP records and by the controversial rock-leaning New Grass that followed. Later listeners often find it the most approachable Ayler, concise and tuneful, while purists prefer the extended trio work. It is a good record for hearing how thoroughly Ayler's music was rooted in melody.","Play Ghosts here and then the version on Spiritual Unity to hear the same tune tightened into a march. Then the title track for Ayler's voice. Compare Ives's Central Park in the Dark, which also layers a street band, parlour music and dissonance."],"sun-ra-jazz-in-silhouette":["A Chicago big band record that sounds, at first, like sophisticated late-1950s hard bop: swinging charts, tight saxophone sections, John Gilmore's muscular tenor. But the textures are strange, full of flutes, percussion, exotic modes and unexpected dissonance. The centrepiece, Ancient Aiethopia, is a long, mysterious piece with chanting, flute and a nearly static pulse, looking toward Egypt and Ethiopia. Enlightenment, a lilting tune Sun Ra kept playing for decades, opens the album.","Sun Ra, born Herman Blount in Alabama, had reinvented himself in Chicago as a visitor from Saturn and built a big band, the Arkestra, which rehearsed obsessively. He and his partner Alton Abraham ran their own label, Saturn, pressing records in small runs and selling them at gigs, one of the first artist-owned jazz labels. This was recorded in 1958 and released around 1959.","Listen to Ancient Aiethopia for the moment the band begins chanting and the trumpet plays a tense, snake-charmer line: this was years before modal or Afrocentric jazz became fashionable. Sun Ra was reading widely in Egyptology and esoteric history, and his spellings of titles were often deliberate. The saxophonists Gilmore, Marshall Allen and Pat Patrick stayed with him for decades, a stability almost unknown in jazz.","Barely distributed at the time, it was known mostly to Chicago audiences. Later reissues brought it wide attention, and it is now often recommended as the best entry point to Sun Ra, proof that the space-age eccentric was a superb arranger in the Ellington and Fletcher Henderson line.","Start with Enlightenment, a tune Sun Ra's bands played for the rest of his life, then Ancient Aiethopia, and notice how the same saxophone section that swings so crisply can turn to incantation. Hear Ellington's Far East Suite afterwards, a later big band travelogue that makes a telling comparison. A modern CD reissue sounds far cleaner than the original Saturn pressings."],"sun-ra-the-magic-city":["Side one is a single 27-minute collective improvisation, The Magic City, for the Arkestra. It begins with Sun Ra's electric keyboard, spacey and warbling, then passes through sections for small combinations of instruments, flutes and piccolos in eerie clusters, saxophones in wild counterpoint, piano runs, near silence. There is no beat or tune to hold on to, but it is carefully shaped, with dynamics rising and falling as if Sun Ra were conducting the band by hand.","Recorded in New York in 1965, where the Arkestra had moved in 1961 and lived communally, rehearsing daily. Sun Ra's hometown, Birmingham, Alabama, was nicknamed the Magic City; in 1963 it had been the scene of the church bombing that killed four girls. Released on Saturn around 1966, the album joined a burst of New York free jazz including the Heliocentric Worlds records on ESP.","Listen for Sun Ra directing: sudden changes of texture happen too cleanly to be accidental, and he is known to have used hand signals to cue sections. The title is ambiguous, part tribute to the city of his birth, part futurist vision of a city yet to be built. The piccolo passages recall Varèse more than anything in jazz.","Initially obscure, like most Saturn releases, it is now regarded as one of the most fully realised of Sun Ra's free recordings, and admired by improvisers for its control of large-group texture. Some listeners prefer the tighter, swinging Arkestra; for others this is the band at its most visionary.","Listen in the dark, in one sitting, and treat it like a film score without a film. Notice how often only three or four players are sounding at once. Then hear Varèse's Arcana or Ligeti's Atmosphères, composed works of shifting orchestral texture, and Coltrane's Ascension, made the same year with a very different density."],"sun-ra-space-is-the-place":["The title track fills side one, over twenty minutes of a simple chanted refrain, space is the place, sung by June Tyson and the Arkestra over a loping rhythm, while saxophones, synthesiser and percussion swirl and erupt around it. It is part gospel call-and-response, part cosmic manifesto, part party. Side two mixes shorter pieces: Discipline 27-II with its chanted invocations, and more conventional Arkestra material, all threaded with Sun Ra's electronic keyboards.","Released in 1973 on Blue Thumb, a rock-oriented label, one of Sun Ra's rare mainstream deals. He had lectured at Berkeley in 1971 on 'The Black Man in the Cosmos', and was shooting a film of the same name, Space Is the Place, released in 1974, in which he lands in Oakland to offer Black Americans passage to another planet. The Arkestra by now performed in costume with dancers and light shows.","Behind the chant, listen for Sun Ra's electronic keyboards: he was among the earliest jazz musicians to use synthesisers, and the burbling electronic sounds are an essential part of the cosmic effect. The lyric is simple but pointed: in the film and the song, space is a metaphor for escape from American racism, the idea later named Afrofuturism.","Sold modestly but became a touchstone for later Afrofuturist artists, from Parliament-Funkadelic's space mythology to hip-hop and electronic musicians. Jazz critics sometimes treated Sun Ra as a novelty; the reappraisal since the 1990s sees him as a serious conceptual artist whose theatre and music are inseparable. The film has become a cult classic.","Let the chant carry you and notice how the horns erupt and subside around it. Then watch the film if you can. For kin, listen to Art Ensemble of Chicago's Les Stances à Sophie and Pharoah Sanders's Karma; for contrast, Stockhausen's Stimmung, a very different ritual chant built on overtones."],"archie-shepp-fire-music":["A mix of originals and standards played by Shepp's sextet with brass and alto. Hambone opens with a raucous, swaggering theme drawn from children's street rhymes; Los Olvidados, the forgotten ones, is a portrait of abandoned city youth. Malcolm, Malcolm – Semper Malcolm, for trio, sets Shepp's own spoken poem over bass and drums, before his tenor wails. Then comes Ellington's Prelude to a Kiss, played with fruity, old-fashioned warmth, and The Girl from Ipanema, the pop hit of the moment, taken apart.","Shepp, a playwright and poet as well as a saxophonist, was one of the most outspoken figures of the new Black music. Coltrane had helped get him signed to Impulse!, and this, recorded in two sessions in February and March 1965, was one of his first albums for the label. Malcolm X was assassinated in New York on 21 February, between the sessions; the elegy was recorded a few weeks later.","Listen to the choice of material. Shepp's sound, breathy, with a wide vibrato like Ben Webster, and his affectionate Ellington show he saw himself inside the jazz tradition, not outside it. The Girl from Ipanema is a political gesture too: the most commercial song of 1964, broken open. The Latin semper, always, in the elegy's title makes the same claim as the poem: that Malcolm endures.","Received as a manifesto, praised by LeRoi Jones and others as an expression of Black nationalist anger in music, and attacked by more conservative critics. It remains Shepp's most celebrated album. The range, from street rhyme to Ellington, is now seen as its point: a claim to the whole of African American music.","Start with Malcolm, Malcolm – Semper Malcolm for the poem and the saxophone cry after it. Then Prelude to a Kiss, to hear how lovingly Shepp plays a ballad. Compare Ellington's own version of the tune and Schoenberg's A Survivor from Warsaw, another spoken testimony over music."],"archie-shepp-attica-blues":["A big band record with strings, singers and a funky rhythm section, moving from the pounding soul of the title track through gospel, blues, ballads and a spoken invocation. Steam is a lament for a young cousin of Shepp's killed in street violence; Blues for Brother George Jackson mourns the prisoner and author shot at San Quentin; Quiet Dawn is sung by a child. It is closer to Marvin Gaye's What's Going On than to Fire Music, but free-jazz horn cries break through.","In September 1971 prisoners at Attica Correctional Facility in New York rebelled; the state's assault to retake the prison left dozens dead, prisoners and hostages. Shepp, by then teaching as well as performing, recorded this response in early 1972 with a large ensemble including strings. Soul, funk and protest music were at their commercial height, and Impulse! was releasing ambitious, political records.","The spoken Invocation is delivered by William Kunstler, the radical lawyer who had tried to negotiate at Attica. Listen for how Shepp uses the strings: sometimes lush like a soul record, sometimes as a sombre chorale. The album tracks a single emotional arc, from rage through mourning to the quiet hope of the final child's song.","Initially seen by some jazz critics as a sell-out to soul, it has since become a favourite of DJs, rare-groove collectors and hip-hop producers, and is now regarded as one of the key protest albums of its time. Shepp revived it in live performances in the 2010s, finding it newly relevant.","Start with the title track and turn it up; notice the free horn solo breaking through the groove. Then listen straight through in order to feel the arc. Compare Shepp's Fire Music to hear how his anger changed form, and Sun Ra's Space Is the Place for another answer to the same America."],"eric-dolphy-out-to-lunch":["Five Dolphy compositions for a quintet with no piano: Freddie Hubbard's trumpet, Bobby Hutcherson's vibraphone, Richard Davis's bass and the teenage Tony Williams on drums. Dolphy plays bass clarinet, flute and alto, all with huge leaps between registers, like speech gone angular. The vibes' ringing chords replace the piano's, and the rhythm section breaks up time, bars stretching and contracting. Hat and Beard, a portrait of Thelonious Monk, lurches in nine; Gazzelloni is a tribute to an Italian flautist.","Recorded at Van Gelder's studio in February 1964. Dolphy had played with Mingus, Coltrane and Ornette Coleman, and also worked with Gunther Schuller's third-stream projects and performed contemporary classical flute music. He was about to leave for Europe with Mingus's band. He died in Berlin that June, aged 36, from undiagnosed diabetes, before the album came out.","Gazzelloni is named after Severino Gazzelloni, the flautist of the Darmstadt avant-garde for whom Berio wrote his Sequenza I, a clue to how closely Dolphy followed new classical music. Listen at the end of the title track for a hesitant, stuttering ensemble figure that deliberately fails to resolve. Tony Williams was eighteen and in Miles Davis's quintet; his ride cymbal floats rather than swings.","Posthumous release lent it the aura of a last testament. It is now considered one of the finest records in the Blue Note catalogue and a model of avant-garde jazz that is composed and structured rather than open-ended. Its blend of freedom and precision has been admired by later composers and improvisers from Anthony Braxton to Steve Coleman.","Start with Hat and Beard, and hear how the vibes and bass clarinet play off each other. Then the title track. Afterwards try Berio's Sequenza III, whose leaping vocal line has kinship with Dolphy's playing, and Monk's own recordings to hear the man in the portrait."],"eric-dolphy-at-the-five-spot":["Live recordings from a residency at New York's Five Spot by a quintet co-led by Dolphy and the young trumpeter Booker Little, with Mal Waldron on piano, Richard Davis on bass and Ed Blackwell on drums. Waldron's Fire Waltz opens with a dark, rolling three-beat groove; Little's Bee Vamp twists through odd accents; Dolphy's The Prophet is long and searching. Dolphy's alto and bass clarinet leap wildly while Little plays lines of unusual lyricism.","The quintet played the Five Spot for two weeks in July 1961, and Prestige recorded one night, issuing it on its New Jazz subsidiary in volumes. The club had become the centre of New York's avant-garde since Ornette Coleman's debut there in 1959. Booker Little, only 23, died of uraemia that October, making the recording a memorial almost immediately.","Listen to Little: he often bends toward dissonant intervals and deliberately sour notes, playing inside the harmony yet stretching it, a counterweight to Dolphy's leaps. Waldron's piano is the anchor, repeating left-hand patterns hypnotically. Notice Blackwell's drums, New Orleans parade rhythms folded into modern time, keeping the long pieces dancing.","Regarded as one of the great live jazz albums, a perfect balance between hard bop's drive and the avant-garde's freedoms. Some later listeners regard the Dolphy-Little quintet as a road not taken, cut short by Little's death; with Dolphy's own death three years later the record has doubled as elegy.","Put on Fire Waltz first and let Waldron's groove settle you in, then listen to how differently Dolphy and Little solo over it. Compare Ornette Coleman's Shape of Jazz to Come, which removed the piano; here the piano stays but the solos slip its grip."],"roscoe-mitchell-sound":["Three pieces by a Chicago sextet led by Roscoe Mitchell with Lester Bowie on trumpet and Malachi Favors on bass. The long title track is quiet and sparse: long silences, single notes, harmonica wheezes, bicycle horns and whistles, recorder squeaks, then sudden explosions of sound. Ornette is a warmer, swinging tribute; The Little Suite is a playful sequence of small textures. What you hear most is space, instruments speaking one at a time, with pauses as important as notes.","Recorded in August 1966, the first album by members of the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians, founded in Chicago in 1965 around Muhal Richard Abrams. Bob Koester's Delmark, a blues and traditional jazz label, took the risk. The AACM insisted on original music, self-organised concerts and education, a response both to the collapse of the club scene and to the civil rights movement.","Listen to how often a sound is followed by silence: Mitchell was thinking about sound as an event in space, closer to Cage or Feldman than to Coltrane's continuous intensity. The small instruments, bells, horns, whistles, which became an AACM trademark, are used for colour and humour. The piece called Ornette sounds nothing like free jazz noise; it is a gentle, affectionate homage.","Initially ignored beyond Chicago, it is now seen as the first document of the AACM aesthetic, a turning point that took free jazz away from fire and fury toward space, colour and structure. Critics like John Litweiler and scholars like George Lewis have established it as one of the key records of 1960s American experimental music.","Listen in a quiet room and do not turn the volume up to compensate for the silences; they are the music. Follow the small instruments as characters. Then compare Cage's 4′33″ and Feldman's Rothko Chapel, and the Art Ensemble of Chicago's People in Sorrow, which grew from this band."],"muhal-richard-abrams-levels-and-degrees-of-light":["Abrams's first album as a leader, three pieces featuring AACM members including Anthony Braxton and violinist Leroy Jenkins. The opening title piece begins with a wordless soprano over shimmering vibraphone, floating and slow, then gradually thickens. My Thoughts Are My Future – Now and Forever is a fierce ensemble workout. The Bird Song sets spoken poetry and bird-like sounds against free improvisation. The whole record mixes the delicate and the ferocious, with Abrams's own piano moving from gospel to cluster.","Abrams had led the Experimental Band in Chicago since 1961, a workshop for new compositions, and in 1965 co-founded the AACM, whose members studied theory, organised concerts and taught in the South Side community. Delmark released this in 1968. The album appeared at a time when AACM members were starting to be heard beyond Chicago; within a year several would move to Paris.","Listen to the use of voice: the soprano Penelope Taylor sings wordlessly, like an instrument, rather than singing a text, a colour almost unknown in jazz at the time. The Bird Song, with its imitations of birds and its poet, is closer to Messiaen's bird-inspired music than to anything in bebop, and shows how widely the AACM's idea of jazz ranged.","Rarely reviewed at the time and hard to find for years, it has since been championed as one of the foundational AACM statements and Abrams as the movement's intellectual centre. George Lewis's history of the AACM, A Power Stronger Than Itself, placed it at the heart of that story. Its mix of composition and improvisation now looks prophetic.","Start with the title track and let the voice and vibes set the mood. Then hear The Bird Song. Compare Oliveros's Sonic Meditations and Messiaen's bird music, and Roscoe Mitchell's Sound for the AACM's other founding sound."],"anthony-braxton-for-alto":["A double album of unaccompanied alto saxophone, eight pieces each dedicated to someone: the composer John Cage, the pianist Cecil Taylor and others, friends and fellow musicians among them. Each piece explores one idea: rapid-fire staccato, long tones, multiphonics that produce two notes at once, squeals, key clicks, fragments of melody. Some are gentle and lyrical, others relentlessly harsh. Without accompaniment every breath and click is exposed, and each piece has the character of a study.","Recorded in Chicago in 1968 and released by Delmark in 1969, when Braxton was 23 and a member of the AACM. An entire album of solo saxophone had essentially no precedent; Coleman Hawkins's short Picasso of 1948 was the famous earlier solo. Braxton, also a chess player and student of philosophy, was developing a system of diagrams and numbered compositions that would govern his whole career.","The dedication to Cage is not incidental: Braxton admired Cage, Stockhausen and Schoenberg as much as Coltrane, and approached solo improvisation as composition, with each piece built on a chosen language of sounds. Listen for how each track isolates one technique. His later titles would be diagrams rather than words, already implicit here.","Initially seen by many as an extreme experiment and by some jazz critics as not jazz at all, it has become a landmark; it established solo saxophone as a genre, followed by Steve Lacy, Evan Parker and others. Braxton's relationship to jazz, which he both belongs to and refuses, remains a lively debate.","Choose one piece, perhaps the Cecil Taylor dedication, and listen to it twice; it gets easier. Then hear the Cage dedication and think about how silence and sound work. Compare Berio's Sequenza III, a composed solo virtuoso piece, and Debussy's Syrinx for unaccompanied flute."],"art-ensemble-of-chicago-people-in-sorrow":["A single piece in two parts, about forty minutes, by Lester Bowie, Roscoe Mitchell, Joseph Jarman and Malachi Favors. It begins almost silently, with bells, gongs and soft flute, and grows slowly over many minutes, like a procession approaching, into a keening lament of horns and then an anguished climax, before fading back into quiet percussion. Every member plays many instruments, including gongs, whistles, sirens and bells, and the sound is lamenting and ceremonial rather than improvised in the bebop sense.","In 1969 the group moved to France, where free jazz had found a keener audience than in the United States, and recorded this piece that summer. The drummer Don Moye had not yet joined, so the four horn players shared the percussion. The group's motto, Great Black Music, Ancient to the Future, captured their aim to embrace the whole African diaspora. The album was reissued by Nessa in the US.","Listen to the first ten minutes very quietly: the members are playing small percussion and silence, and a slow, sustained lament emerges, much closer to ritual than jazz. The group wore face paint and costumes onstage, and the title's sorrow has often been heard as mourning for Black America after the assassinations of 1968. Notice Favors's bowed bass, a steady voice under the chaos.","Widely acclaimed in Europe, it became the group's most admired early work and a key example of AACM compositional thinking. Critics praised its patience and structure, qualities rarely credited to free jazz at the time. For many listeners it remains the group's finest record, and among the quietest, subtlest documents of late-1960s free jazz; some find its slow opening a test of patience.","Listen in one sitting, in quiet surroundings, and wait: the arc takes time. Notice the moment the horns first combine. Compare Feldman's Rothko Chapel, another slow ritual of mourning, and Penderecki's Threnody for the opposite, instant anguish."],"art-ensemble-of-chicago-les-stances-a-sophie":["The soundtrack to a French film, and the Art Ensemble's most accessible record. Thème de Yoyo, the opening, is a thumping funk vamp sung by Fontella Bass, the soul singer of Rescue Me, with lyrics about a woman's body; the horns then erupt into free jazz. The rest of the album moves between atmospheric film cues, a lovely ballad-like Thème de Céline, and freer passages, with drums from Don Moye, now a full member.","Made in Paris in 1970 for Moshé Mizrahi's film Les Stances à Sophie, a feminist-inflected drama from a novel by Christiane Rochefort. The group was living in France, playing regularly and recording for several labels. Bass, then married to Lester Bowie, was singing with them. The soundtrack was issued by Pathé and was hard to find for decades.","Listen to how the groove of Thème de Yoyo is interrupted by free-jazz explosions, then comes back: the collision of dance music and the avant-garde would become a model for later groups. The saxophone solos on the funk vamp are wild, but Malachi Favors's bassline never budges. The film itself is rarely seen now; the soundtrack has outlived it.","Unknown to most jazz fans for years, it was rediscovered by rare-groove DJs and hip-hop crate-diggers; Thème de Yoyo is now one of the group's best-known pieces. Critics count it as a proof that the AACM's freedom could embrace funk and soul, not only abstraction.","Put on Thème de Yoyo loud and move. Then hear the quieter cues. Compare Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head, which made funk and freedom meet from the other side, and Milhaud's Création du monde, a much earlier fusion of jazz and the concert hall."],"alice-coltrane-journey-in-satchidananda":["Five pieces built on drones and repeated bass figures. In the title track, a tanpura hums throughout, Cecil McBee repeats a short, hypnotic bass line, and Alice Coltrane's harp pours over them in rippling glissandi while Pharoah Sanders's soprano saxophone sings long, ornamented lines, and Rashied Ali keeps a gentle pulse. Shiva-Loka and Stopover Bombay continue the mood; Isis and Osiris, recorded live, adds oud and Charlie Haden's bass. The harp is the sound people remember.","Alice Coltrane, pianist and harpist, had been in John Coltrane's last band and was grieving his 1967 death. She became a devotee of Swami Satchidananda, the Indian guru who opened the Woodstock festival, and travelled to India in 1970. This album, recorded that year and released in 1971, made her spiritual practice her music. Impulse! was by then the home of what came to be called spiritual jazz.","The tanpura is played by a musician credited only as Tulsi, and it tunes the whole album: its drone is the tonal centre that every other instrument leans on, just as in Hindustani classical music. Listen to how Alice's harp does not play chords like a pianist but sweeps, a technique partly her own. The title word joins three Sanskrit terms for being, consciousness and bliss.","Received quietly and sometimes condescendingly as the work of Coltrane's widow, it has been thoroughly reappraised, especially since the 2000s, as a founding album of spiritual jazz and a touchstone for ambient, electronic and new-jazz musicians. Her role as composer and bandleader is now fully recognised.","Start with the title track and let the bass figure and tanpura settle in before listening for the harp. Then hear Ravi Shankar's Raga Mala and La Monte Young's Well-Tuned Piano, other drone-based musics that made their way into the West. For the Coltrane lineage, hear Meditations."],"alice-coltrane-universal-consciousness":["Alice Coltrane on harp and Wurlitzer organ with a small string section, bassist Jimmy Garrison and drummers Jack DeJohnette, Rashied Ali and Clifford Jarvis. The organ is the surprise: swirling, bending, almost psychedelic, played with pitch-bending effects. The strings play slow, glowing lines. The six pieces include Battle at Armageddon, a fierce organ and drums duet, the devotional Hare Krishna and Sita Ram, and the title track, where harp, strings and organ build to a kind of cosmic surge.","Recorded in 1971, this marked Alice Coltrane's growing ambition as composer and arranger. She wrote for strings with help from Ornette Coleman, who transcribed her ideas; she had begun to compose by playing on the organ and singing lines. She was deepening her Hindu practice, and would later found a Vedantic centre and ashram in California.","Listen to how the organ pitches slide: she used the instrument in ways drawn from Indian vocal ornament, bending notes rather than hitting them cleanly. The strings are written with Coleman's help, which explains their slightly angular, unexpected voicings. Battle at Armageddon, a savage duet with Ali, shows her as a forceful improviser, not only a devotional one.","Barely noticed at release and long out of print, it has become one of her most admired albums, often cited by later musicians and critics as her finest. It is now seen as a bold synthesis of jazz, Indian devotion and Western art-music orchestration. Some listeners still find the organ's wild pitch-bending hard going; others hear it as the record's boldest idea.","Start with the title track and let the organ swirl. Then hear Battle at Armageddon for the contrast. Compare Messiaen's organ music, especially La Nativité du Seigneur, and Scriabin's Prometheus, both mystic visions in colour and harmony."],"pharoah-sanders-karma":["Almost all of it is The Creator Has a Master Plan, a 32-minute piece that starts with a peaceful, rising theme over a two-chord bass figure, then Leon Thomas sings a simple hymn of love and faith, and his yodelling vocal cries become part of the texture. In the middle Sanders's tenor erupts into a shrieking, overblown storm before the calm returns. French horn, flute and piano add colour. A short second piece, Colors, is gentler.","Sanders had played with John Coltrane from 1965 until Coltrane's death. This, his third album as leader, recorded in February 1969, built on Coltrane's A Love Supreme but made it more direct, more like a gospel or pop song stretched out. Leon Thomas, a singer in Count Basie's orbit, had developed a yodelling technique he linked to African Pygmy song.","Listen to the bass line: it keeps a short, repeating figure for most of the piece, a debt to Coltrane's A Love Supreme and to Indian drones. Thomas's yodel is the most surprising sound on the record; he said it came from hearing Central African music. Notice that the saxophone storm and the peaceful theme are the same piece; the peace frames the chaos.","It became a surprising success, played on FM radio and embraced by the counterculture. It is now regarded as one of the founding records of spiritual jazz and has been sampled and covered widely. Critics once debated whether its accessibility was a betrayal of free jazz; the consensus now celebrates it.","Listen straight through, letting the theme lodge before the storm hits; notice how relieved you feel when it returns. Compare Coltrane's Meditations and Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda. For a classical cousin, hear Terry Riley's In C."],"brotzmann-machine-gun":["An octet with Brötzmann, Evan Parker and Willem Breuker on saxophones, Fred Van Hove on piano, two bassists and two drummers, Han Bennink and Sven-Åke Johansson, blasting at full volume. The opening title track hits like artillery: repeated saxophone attacks, pounding drums, screaming, collectively improvised. Then the din breaks into sections where smaller groups play, with brief written riffs that pull everyone together again. It is loud, dense and relentless, with a surprisingly clear structure.","Recorded in Bremen in May 1968 and released on Brötzmann's own label, later reissued by FMP, the Berlin label he helped found. The title refers to the Vietnam War, and to Don Cherry calling Brötzmann's saxophone a machine gun. The record came out of the student protests and a European generation's search for a jazz that owed nothing to American models or to German post-war conformity.","Listen for the composed parts: short riffs and signals that bring the band together after the chaos, showing that the fury has a plan. The doubled basses and drums echo the double quartet of Ornette's Free Jazz. Notice Van Hove's piano, often inaudible in the roar, but pounding clusters in the quieter sections.","It became the founding document of European free improvisation and is often cited by rock and noise musicians as an influence. Many listeners still find it too much; its supporters consider it one of the most important records of 1960s Europe. It has been reissued repeatedly, often with alternate takes.","Listen at volume in one sitting, and use the composed riffs as landmarks: each time the band snaps together on one, a new section begins. Notice Bennink's drumming, theatrical and funny even at full tilt. Then compare Coltrane's Ascension, its direct ancestor, and Penderecki's Threnody, a notated work that also turned mass sound into protest."],"don-cherry-complete-communion":["Two side-long suites, Complete Communion and Elephantasy, each chaining four themes together, by a quartet of Cherry's cornet, the Argentine Gato Barbieri on tenor, Henry Grimes on bass and Ed Blackwell on drums. Themes appear, are improvised on, and give way to the next, so each side flows like one long song. Cherry's cornet is light and playful; Barbieri's tenor is hoarse and passionate; Blackwell's drums are melodic and dancing, rooted in New Orleans.","Recorded on Christmas Eve 1965 at Van Gelder's studio, Cherry's first album for Blue Note, after years as Ornette Coleman's partner and time in Europe with Albert Ayler and his own groups. Cherry would soon settle in Sweden and move toward what he called organic music, drawing on Indian, African and Turkish music, a pioneer of what would later be called world music.","Listen to how the themes are linked: the suite form, borrowed perhaps from Ellington as much as from Ornette, lets improvisations flow into new tunes without stopping. Blackwell's drumming draws on New Orleans street beats. Cherry's cornet is small and bright, with half-valved smears, a sound he used to shape melodies almost like a singer.","Received well at the time, it is now regarded as one of the most joyful and structured of 1960s free jazz records, an example of free jazz that sings. It is often seen as a bridge between Ornette's quartet and Cherry's later world-music explorations. Barbieri, soon famous for the Last Tango in Paris score, is heard here at his rawest.","Listen to one side straight through and notice when a new theme begins. Compare Ornette's Shape of Jazz to Come, where Cherry began, and Ellington's suites. Then hear Cherry's later records, with their Indian and African elements."],"miles-davis-filles-de-kilimanjaro":["Five long pieces with French titles, played by Miles Davis's second great quintet at the moment it began to plug in. Herbie Hancock and later Chick Corea play Fender Rhodes electric piano, Ron Carter and then Dave Holland play bass, Tony Williams drums, Wayne Shorter tenor. The grooves are slower and straighter than bebop, closer to soul records, and the tunes are built from short riffs repeated until they turn into landscapes. The sound you remember is the soft, bell-like buzz of the electric piano under the trumpet.","Recorded in two sessions in June and September 1968, the album is a hinge: the acoustic quintet that had made Miles Smiles and Nefertiti was breaking up, and Davis was listening hard to James Brown, Sly Stone and Jimi Hendrix. The second session brought in Corea and Holland, the players who would carry him into In a Silent Way. Columbia was pressing him to reach the young audience that was buying rock albums, and Davis wanted that audience too.","The woman on the cover is Betty Mabry, the funk singer who married Davis in 1968 and introduced him to Hendrix and Sly Stone; \"Mademoiselle Mabry\" is named for her, and its opening borrows the chord shape of Hendrix's \"The Wind Cries Mary\", slowed almost to stillness. Listen for the change of personnel between tracks: the bass on the Holland pieces is rounder and more insistent, a first hint of the vamping bass lines to come.","Often overlooked, squeezed between the celebrated acoustic quintet records and the electric revolution of 1969, the album was received politely rather than excitedly. Later listeners have promoted it as the true transition record, the place where the fusion idea is audible before it was a style. Arguments continue over whether it is the last quintet album or the first electric one; the honest answer is both.","Start with \"Mademoiselle Mabry\", sixteen unhurried minutes, and let the rhythm section's patience set the pace. Then play \"Frelon Brun\" for the busier, rock-tinged drumming of Tony Williams. Follow with Nefertiti from the year before to hear what changed, and In a Silent Way from the year after to hear where it went."],"miles-davis-in-a-silent-way":["Two sides, each around twenty minutes. Side one, \"Shhh/Peaceful\", rides a light, ticking hi-hat groove under a cloud of three electric keyboards, with John McLaughlin's guitar picking out single notes. Side two frames the funkier \"It's About That Time\" with Joe Zawinul's hymn-like \"In a Silent Way\", heard at the start and again at the end. Nothing hurries; solos drift in and out of a haze. The sound is of a jazz band holding its breath.","Recorded in a single day in February 1969 in New York, with Hancock, Corea and Zawinul on keyboards and the English guitarist John McLaughlin, who had arrived in America days before. Davis had heard rock musicians building whole pieces from one chord, and he stripped Zawinul's tune of its original changes, leaving a single sustained tonality. Producer Teo Macero then shaped hours of tape into two sides with a razor blade.","The symmetry of side two is an editing trick: Macero copied the \"In a Silent Way\" section and spliced it onto both ends, so the same performance is heard twice. Side one also repeats a large block of music. The album is in part a composition made in the edit suite, like musique concrète, and it quietly pioneered the studio-built jazz record that Bitches Brew would make explicit.","Jazz critics were split: some heard a lazy abandonment of swing and harmony, others a new kind of beauty. Rock critics loved it at once. Its reputation has only grown, and it is now often preferred to the louder Bitches Brew as the most perfectly made of the electric records. The argument over Macero's edits, whether they are sacrilege or authorship, has largely settled in favour of authorship.","Listen in the dark, at moderate volume, from start to finish. Notice how the drums never get louder than a whisper for the first side, and wait for Davis's trumpet to enter the title theme, plain and almost vibrato-free. Then compare Terry Riley's A Rainbow in Curved Air from the same year: another American music made of sustained drones and patient repetition."],"miles-davis-bitches-brew":["A double album of six long pieces for a band twice the normal size: two or three drummers, electric and acoustic bass together, three electric pianos, guitar, soprano saxophone and Bennie Maupin's dark bass clarinet. Grooves churn for twenty minutes at a time, dense and murky, while Davis's trumpet, often drenched in echo, cuts across the top. The title track opens with a stark, spaced call and response before the band boils over. It sounds like jazz heard through the walls of a rock club.","Recorded over three days in August 1969 in New York, the week after Woodstock. Davis gave the musicians sketches, a bass line, a mood, and let the tapes roll; producer Teo Macero then assembled the album from the takes. Columbia marketed it to the rock audience, Davis played the Fillmore halls opening for rock bands, and the record became his first to sell half a million copies, with Mati Klarwein's psychedelic cover painting in every student bedroom.","\"Pharaoh's Dance\" is stitched together from many short fragments, some loops repeated verbatim; the famous stops and restarts are edits, not band cues. Macero also added tape echo to the trumpet after the fact. Listen to the stereo picture: the two drummers and two keyboard players are often split left and right, so the album works almost like two bands arguing in real time.","It split the jazz world. Many older critics and musicians called it a sell-out or a noise; younger listeners heard the future. Its commercial success made fusion a genre and launched the bands of nearly everyone on the sessions: Weather Report, Return to Forever, Mahavishnu Orchestra. Debate now centres less on legitimacy than on credit, since so much of the form belongs to Macero's editing.","Start with \"Spanish Key\" or \"Miles Runs the Voodoo Down\", the most tightly grooved pieces, before tackling the half-hour title track and \"Pharaoh's Dance\". Pick one player and follow them all the way through, the bass clarinet is a good guide. Hear it loud. Then go back to In a Silent Way to hear how much quieter the same idea began."],"miles-davis-a-tribute-to-jack-johnson":["Two sides, \"Right Off\" and \"Yesternow\", each around twenty-five minutes. The first is Davis's most openly rock record: John McLaughlin's guitar chops out a relentless shuffle, Michael Henderson's bass locks with Billy Cobham's drums, and Davis plays one of the longest, most ferocious trumpet solos of his life over the top. The second side slows into a brooding, spacious bass figure and ends with an actor's voice speaking as the boxer.","Made as the soundtrack for Bill Cayton's documentary about Jack Johnson, the first Black heavyweight champion, whose defiance of white America in the 1910s Davis saw as his own. Davis was a keen amateur boxer. The music was drawn chiefly from a session in April 1970 and edited by Teo Macero, with material from other dates folded in. It was the first time Davis worked with the funk bassist Michael Henderson, who had played for Stevie Wonder.","\"Right Off\" reportedly began while Davis was in the control room: McLaughlin started noodling a shuffle in E, the others joined, and Davis ran out and began playing. Herbie Hancock, who happened to drop by, plays a cheap Farfisa organ. The bass figure under much of \"Yesternow\" is taken from James Brown's \"Say It Loud, I'm Black and I'm Proud\". The closing speech is voiced by Brock Peters.","Barely promoted on release, it sold poorly and was overshadowed by Bitches Brew. Its reputation rose steadily among guitarists, who treat McLaughlin's playing here as a founding document of jazz-rock, and among rock critics, who often rank it Davis's best electric album. The 2003 box set of the complete sessions showed how much editing lay behind it and sharpened the debate about what the finished work really is.","Play \"Right Off\" loud and wait: Davis's entry about two minutes in, after a long guitar groove, is one of the great moments of electric jazz. Notice how the band stops and restarts on a signal. Then listen to James Brown's \"Say It Loud\" and hear the bass line come back as a meditation on \"Yesternow\"."],"miles-davis-on-the-corner":["Four sides of dense, repetitive funk: a single bass riff hammered for minutes, several drummers and percussionists interlocking, tabla and electric sitar buzzing inside the rhythm, wah-wah guitar and keyboards stabbing in and out. Davis plays trumpet through a wah-wah pedal, in short bursts, as one voice in the mix rather than the soloist on top. There are almost no tunes; the groove is the composition. The feeling is of a crowded street heard from inside.","Recorded in June and July 1972 in New York, and designed, Davis said, to reach young Black listeners who had abandoned jazz for James Brown and Sly Stone. The English arranger Paul Buckmaster had introduced him to the music of Karlheinz Stockhausen, and the album grew from the idea of layered, looping parts. Columbia released it with cartoon street characters by Corky McCoy on the cover and, at first, no list of the musicians at all.","The Indian instruments are not decoration: Badal Roy's tabla often carries the pulse while the drum kit plays around it, a borrowing of how Hindustani music layers rhythm cycles. Listen to how the tape was edited into repeating blocks, much as Stockhausen and later hip-hop producers built pieces from loops. The absent credits were deliberate: Davis wanted the band heard as one anonymous machine.","It was savaged on release, called monotonous and a cynical grab for a pop audience, and it sold badly. Few jazz records have been rehabilitated so completely: post-punk bands, drum and bass producers and hip-hop beatmakers adopted it, and it is now heard as a prophecy of loop-based music. Some jazz critics still regard it as a dead end; many more now call it Davis's boldest record.","Start with the opening medley and give it ten minutes; the point arrives only once the repetition stops sounding repetitive. Follow the tabla, then the bass, then Davis's wah-wah trumpet as just another colour. Compare it with Steve Reich's Drumming from the year before, another American work of interlocking patterns, and with Stockhausen's Mantra."],"miles-davis-agharta":["A double album recorded at an afternoon concert: four sides of continuous, roaring electric music with no gaps between numbers. Pete Cosey's guitar, fed through fuzz, wah and synthesisers, howls and squeals; Reggie Lucas plays tight rhythm guitar; Michael Henderson and Al Foster keep a heavy funk pulse; James Mtume's percussion never stops. Sonny Fortune adds sax and flute. Davis plays wah-wah trumpet and stabs at an organ, directing the band with hand signals. The sound is closer to Hendrix than to bebop.","Recorded on 1 February 1975 at Festival Hall in Osaka during a Japanese tour; the evening concert that day became the companion album Pangaea. Davis was in poor health, in pain from a hip condition and ulcers, and playing through it. Within months he stopped performing altogether and did not record again until 1980. The album was released first in Japan, where his electric music had a loyal audience.","Listen for how the music changes gear: Davis signals shifts with a raised hand or an organ chord, and the whole band swerves at once, a live version of the edits Teo Macero once made with tape. Cosey's guitar often sounds like electronic music, a whole wall of noise rather than notes; he used a bank of effects pedals and an EMS synthesiser. The title names a mythical underground city.","American critics largely dismissed it as chaotic noise from a declining star. Its standing changed in the 1990s, when noise-rock and experimental guitarists championed Cosey and when the loops of electronic dance music made its repetition sound modern. Many now rank Agharta and Pangaea as the summit of Davis's electric bands; others hear the sound of a musician burning out.","Put on the first side, \"Prelude\", and let the first twenty minutes settle the scale of the thing. Track Cosey's guitar solo as it moves from blues to pure texture. Then compare the dense layers of On the Corner, built in the studio, with the same idea here done live, without a net."],"tony-williams-emergency":["A double album by a trio of drums, guitar and Hammond organ, played at rock volume with jazz speed. Tony Williams drives everything with a flood of cymbals and tom-toms, John McLaughlin plays fast, distorted guitar lines, and Larry Young's organ supplies both bass and swirling chords. Williams sings, tunelessly and bravely, on a few tracks. The pieces range from blistering jams to the eerie, slow \"Vashkar\" by Carla Bley. The recording itself is raw, overloaded and loud.","Recorded in New York in May 1969, three months before Bitches Brew. Williams, still in his early twenties, had spent six years as Miles Davis's drummer and wanted to play the kind of music he heard from Cream and Jimi Hendrix. He left Davis to form Lifetime, taking McLaughlin, who had just arrived from London. Polydor, a label associated with rock, released it as a double LP.","The distortion is not all intentional: the recording was badly engineered, and the band disliked the sound. Yet that grit became part of the record's legend and its influence on later noise and rock musicians. Listen past it to Young's organ bass lines, played with his feet and left hand, which let the trio sound like a full band.","It sold poorly and confused both jazz and rock audiences. Among musicians it had an outsized influence: many regard it, rather than Bitches Brew, as the first true jazz-rock record. McLaughlin's playing here led directly to the Mahavishnu Orchestra. Critics now place it among the essential fusion documents, while still wishing for a better-sounding master.","Begin with the title track and simply hold on. Then focus on Williams's cymbal work: he plays the ride cymbal with the precision of a jazz drummer while hitting the drums like a rock drummer. Compare with Cream's live recordings from 1968 and with Mahavishnu's The Inner Mounting Flame two years later."],"weather-report-mysterious-traveller":["Seven pieces that move from the long, hypnotic \"Nubian Sundance\", with wordless voices and a crowd audible behind the band, to the springy funk of \"Cucumber Slumber\" and the tender duet \"Blackthorn Rose\" for Wayne Shorter's soprano and Joe Zawinul's piano. Zawinul's synthesisers act like an orchestra, painting backdrops and sound effects. The group had become a composed band rather than a jam band: parts are arranged, solos short, and the whole record sounds like a series of short films.","Weather Report was founded in 1970 by Zawinul and Shorter, both veterans of Miles Davis's early electric bands, with a manifesto that everyone solos and no one solos. By 1974 Zawinul was steering the band toward groove and orchestration. New bassist Alphonso Johnson brought a funkier feel. Zawinul had embraced the ARP synthesiser, then a new, expensive instrument, and was using it as a composing tool rather than a novelty.","\"Nubian Sundance\" sounds as if it were recorded live, but the audience noise was reportedly overdubbed onto a studio performance, a trick to capture the energy of the band's concerts. The title track begins with a rushing electronic sound and a single low gong-like note. Listen for how often Shorter plays only a few notes, then disappears: his restraint is the band's secret colour.","Well received at the time and a strong seller for a jazz album, it has lived in the shadow of Heavy Weather. Critics who prefer the band's early, freer records sometimes see it as the moment they became too polished; others hear the best balance of adventure and groove. The turn toward tight, composed grooves set the course for the band's later hits.","Start with \"Cucumber Slumber\" for the groove, then go back to the opening track for the wider picture. Pay attention to Zawinul's synthesiser as orchestration, colours that function like horns or strings. Compare with In a Silent Way, where both Zawinul and Shorter played five years earlier."],"weather-report-heavy-weather":["Eight tracks, opening with \"Birdland\", Joe Zawinul's joyful tribute to the New York jazz club, which builds from a low synthesiser bass line and Jaco Pastorius's bass harmonics to a hummable big-band shout. \"A Remark You Made\" is a slow, aching ballad for Wayne Shorter's tenor; Pastorius's \"Teen Town\" puts his fretless bass up front, playing the melody at speed. The sound is bright and clear, synthesisers and saxophone blending into a single colour.","By 1977 the band had been transformed by Pastorius, a young Florida bassist who told Zawinul he was the greatest bass player in the world and proceeded to prove it. With Alex Acuña on drums and Manolo Badrena on percussion, the band recorded in Los Angeles. Synthesiser technology had jumped forward, and Zawinul used new polyphonic instruments that allowed chords, not just single lines.","In \"Birdland\" Pastorius plays the high melody in bass harmonics, touching the strings lightly so they ring like a bell; many listeners assume it is a keyboard. Pastorius also plays the drums on \"Teen Town\". \"Birdland\" is built like a big-band chart without a big band: several separate tunes stacked up, each one returning, the last a shout chorus worthy of Count Basie.","It became Weather Report's best-selling album and one of the best-selling jazz records of the decade, and \"Birdland\" was quickly covered by Maynard Ferguson and, with words, by the Manhattan Transfer. Some critics grumbled that the band had gone pop, and that Shorter was sidelined. Most now hear the album as fusion at its most melodic and exact, and the Zawinul–Pastorius partnership at its height.","Start with \"Birdland\" and count how many separate themes it has before the final shout; then listen to \"A Remark You Made\" for the tenderness beneath the bravado. Notice the fretless bass, which slides like a voice. Follow with the album Jaco Pastorius from the year before and the Manhattan Transfer's vocal \"Birdland\"."],"hancock-maiden-voyage":["A five-track suite about the sea. The title track floats on a repeated rhythmic figure and hovering chords that never quite settle, while Freddie Hubbard's trumpet and George Coleman's tenor state a calm, open melody. \"The Eye of the Hurricane\" is fast and churning, \"Little One\" a slow meditation, \"Survival of the Fittest\" nearly free, and \"Dolphin Dance\" a gentle, harmonically winding close. Hancock's piano is light-fingered and full of space.","Recorded in March 1965 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio in New Jersey for Blue Note. Hancock, just twenty-four, was the pianist in Miles Davis's quintet, and he brought along Davis's bassist Ron Carter and drummer Tony Williams. Jazz musicians were then exploring modes, scales used in place of chord sequences, after Kind of Blue; Hancock took that idea and made it into a coloured, programmatic album.","The title tune's chords are suspended, the kind that ask a question and leave it unanswered, which is why the music seems to bob on water rather than move forward. Hancock reportedly first wrote the vamp for a television commercial. Listen to Tony Williams on \"Survival of the Fittest\": the drummer shifts from time-keeping to free commentary, the tune dissolving into sound effects of a storm.","Received well at the time, it has become one of the most loved Blue Note albums, and both \"Maiden Voyage\" and \"Dolphin Dance\" are core standards taught in every jazz school. Critics sometimes debate whether it is truly a concept album or a well-sequenced blowing date. Few doubt that it defined the harmonically sophisticated post-bop sound of the mid-1960s.","Begin with the title track and hear the pattern of the bass and piano rocking under the melody. Then \"Dolphin Dance\" for Hancock's way of changing key every few bars without ever sounding lost. Compare with Debussy's La Mer, another portrait of the sea made from colour rather than tune."],"hancock-head-hunters":["Four long tracks of tight, playful funk. \"Chameleon\" opens with a synthesiser bass line, gurgling and fat, that anyone who hears it remembers; the band locks into a groove for fifteen minutes and then changes into a different piece halfway through. \"Watermelon Man\" reinvents Hancock's early hit with bottles, whistles and a slow, loping beat. \"Sly\" salutes Sly Stone; \"Vein Melter\" is dreamy and slow. Clavinet, Rhodes piano and synthesisers replace horns as the main colour.","Recorded in San Francisco in 1973. Hancock had spent several years leading the Mwandishi band, whose spacey experimental music had lost money and audiences. Newly committed to Nichiren Buddhism, he decided to make music that would move bodies and reach people, and he formed a new band of funk-minded players. The newly affordable synthesisers made by ARP gave him the bass sound he wanted.","The opening of \"Watermelon Man\" is percussionist Bill Summers blowing across the top of a beer bottle, imitating the hindewhu, a whistle-and-voice technique of the Ba-Benzélé people of central Africa that Hancock had heard on a field recording. The \"Chameleon\" bass line is played by Hancock himself on a synthesiser, not by the bassist. The album's funk became a source of samples for decades of hip-hop.","It became the best-selling jazz album of its time, crossing into pop and R&B charts. Jazz purists attacked it as a commercial betrayal; funk and soul audiences loved it. The debate softened as its influence on hip-hop, electronic dance music and jazz musicians of every generation became obvious. It is now usually named alongside Bitches Brew as a foundation of fusion.","Start with \"Chameleon\", ride the groove, and notice the moment around the middle when it turns into a new, slower piece with a Rhodes solo. Then listen to the original 1962 \"Watermelon Man\" and hear the tune taken apart. Compare it with James Brown's early seventies records for the rhythm section's discipline."],"hancock-rockit":["A single built from machines: a hard, clapping drum-machine beat, a stuttering synthesiser riff, and the scratching of a vinyl record by the DJ Grand Mixer D.ST, pushed back and forth under the needle so that it becomes a percussion instrument. Hancock's keyboards add hooks and chords. There is no singing and almost no melody in the ordinary sense; the track works by texture, repetition and surprise. The scratch is the thing people remember.","Hancock made Rockit with the New York producers Bill Laswell and Michael Beinhorn of the band Material, who were connected to the downtown hip-hop and art scenes. Hancock, a lifelong gadget enthusiast, was fascinated by early computers and samplers. The song's video by Godley and Creme, full of jerking robot sculptures, ran constantly on the new MTV and won several of its first video awards.","For many listeners outside New York this was the first time they heard scratching, and Grand Mixer D.ST's contribution was placed front and centre, credited on the record: a DJ treated as a featured soloist on a major-label jazz musician's single. Listen to how the scratches answer the synthesiser like a horn in a call-and-response. It also won the Grammy for best R&B instrumental performance.","A hit worldwide, it baffled jazz critics and delighted breakdancers. Hip-hop historians now credit it with spreading turntablism internationally, and generations of DJs cite it as their first inspiration. Some have argued about whether Hancock or Laswell and D.ST deserve the credit for its sound; most agree it was a genuine collaboration and a bridge between jazz, electro and hip-hop.","Listen for the first scratch, and then count how many different sounds D.ST gets from one record. Compare the beat with Afrika Bambaataa's \"Planet Rock\" from the year before, and go back to Head Hunters to hear the same musician's funk ten years earlier. The original extended mix is worth finding."],"corea-now-he-sings-now-he-sobs":["A piano trio record of five pieces, played with crisp attack and sudden changes of direction. Chick Corea's piano is percussive and clear, full of fast runs and chords struck like a drum; Miroslav Vitouš's bass is agile and melodic; Roy Haynes's drums crackle and comment. \"Matrix\" is a bright, angular blues; \"Steps – What Was\" moves from free improvisation into a Spanish-tinged theme; the title track is lyrical and searching. The trio sounds like three equal voices in conversation.","Recorded in New York in March 1968, before Corea joined Miles Davis's band. Corea had played Latin music with Mongo Santamaría and Willie Bobo, and the influence of Bill Evans and McCoy Tyner was in the air, but here he found his own crisp, bright style. The piano trio was being rethought after Bill Evans made the bass and drums equal partners.","The titles come from Corea's reading of a translation of the I Ching, the ancient Chinese book of divination. Listen to \"Steps – What Was\": the Spanish theme near the end prefigures \"Spain\", one of his most famous compositions four years later. Roy Haynes, then in his forties, had played with Charlie Parker; his snare comments sound as fresh as the young players around him.","Not a big seller at first, it became one of the most influential piano trio albums of the late 1960s; pianists study \"Matrix\" and the trio's interplay as a model. Reissues added extra tracks from the sessions, sparking mild debate over the original sequence. Corea himself returned to the trio with Vitouš and Haynes in later decades.","Start with \"Matrix\" and listen to Haynes's snare drum talking back to the piano. Then \"Now He Sings, Now He Sobs\" for a gentler side. Compare with Bartók's Mikrokosmos, whose crisp, percussive piano writing Corea admired, and with Bill Evans's trio records for another model of equal partnership."],"corea-return-to-forever":["Four pieces bathed in light. The title track opens with Flora Purim's wordless voice floating over Chick Corea's Fender Rhodes, then breaks into a Latin groove; \"Crystal Silence\" is a still, bell-like ballad; \"What Game Shall We Play Today\" is a light Brazilian song; \"Sometime Ago – La Fiesta\" moves from a sung reverie into a blazing Spanish-flavoured workout. Joe Farrell's flute weaves through, Stanley Clarke's bass sings, and Airto Moreira's percussion keeps everything shimmering.","Recorded in New York in February 1972 for Manfred Eicher's young German label ECM, and released first in Europe. Corea had just left both Miles Davis and the free-jazz group Circle, and wanted music that communicated directly. The Brazilian musicians Airto and Flora Purim brought samba and bossa nova into the band, part of a wave of Brazilian musicians then working in American jazz.","The band had no name when this was recorded; the album title became the band's name, and Return to Forever went on to become one of the loudest fusion bands of the 1970s, a long way from this gentle beginning. Listen to \"La Fiesta\": its flamenco-style chord sequence and hand-clapped rhythm show Corea's Spanish ancestry and his ear for Spanish classical music.","Warmly received, it became a touchstone for a lyrical, Latin-inflected fusion. \"Crystal Silence\" and \"La Fiesta\" became standards, and the former a favourite duet for Corea with vibraphonist Gary Burton. Critics who prefer the band's acoustic origins sometimes rank this above its later, heavier albums; others find it slight. Its clarity and warmth still win listeners.","Begin with \"La Fiesta\", the most exciting track, then go back to the title track and let the flute and voice carry you. Notice how the Rhodes piano chimes rather than buzzes. Compare with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5, another fusion of Brazilian and European music for a floating soprano voice."],"mahavishnu-orchestra-the-inner-mounting-flame":["Eight tracks of fiercely virtuosic, high-volume music in unusual rhythms. John McLaughlin's electric guitar and Jerry Goodman's electric violin trade phrases at blinding speed; Jan Hammer's keyboards add distortion and colour; Billy Cobham's drums are thunderous and exact. \"Meeting of the Spirits\" opens with a massive, ringing chord pattern; \"A Lotus on Irish Streams\" is a delicate acoustic trio; \"The Dance of Maya\" sets a slow blues shuffle against an odd-metre riff.","McLaughlin, an English guitarist who had played on In a Silent Way and Bitches Brew, formed the band in New York in 1971. He was a disciple of the spiritual teacher Sri Chinmoy, who gave him the name Mahavishnu; the music reflects his study of Indian rhythm and his devotion. Rock audiences were ready for long instrumental virtuosity after Cream and Hendrix, and the band became a concert draw.","\"The Dance of Maya\" runs a riff in ten beats and then, without warning, turns into a plain twelve-bar blues shuffle, so the two rhythms seem to argue: illusion, maya, against the ordinary world. Many rhythms borrow from Indian music's long cycles. \"You Know You Know\", a quiet, spacious track, was later sampled by Massive Attack on their first album.","An immediate success with rock audiences and musicians, it established fusion as music for virtuosos. Jazz critics sometimes found it overheated and short on swing. Its influence on progressive rock and later metal guitarists is enormous; many prog bands cite the band as a model. Opinion now splits between those who hear exhilaration and those who hear excess.","Start with \"Meeting of the Spirits\" and feel the weight of the first chord pattern. Then listen to \"The Dance of Maya\" and count the rhythm until the shuffle arrives. Compare with the machine-like energy of Stravinsky's Rite of Spring, another music of odd, driving metres, and with McLaughlin's acoustic group Shakti."],"mahavishnu-orchestra-birds-of-fire":["Ten tracks, shorter and tighter than the first album. The title track opens with a gong and builds a hard, odd-metre riff on guitar and violin that climbs and climbs. \"Miles Beyond\" is a funky tribute to Miles Davis; \"Thousand Island Park\" is a gentle acoustic piece; \"One Word\" ends with a long drum feature for Billy Cobham. Jan Hammer's newly acquired Moog synthesiser adds sounds that rival the guitar. The band sounds like a single engine.","Recorded in 1972 at the height of the band's fame. It became one of the biggest-selling jazz-rock albums of its era and reached the upper reaches of the American pop album chart, unusual for instrumental music. The band toured relentlessly with rock acts, and internal tensions over credit and composing were already building; the original lineup split later in 1973.","Listen to how the title track's riff is built: guitar and violin play in unison, then split into close harmony, then compete, all in a metre that refuses to settle into four. Hammer's Moog often imitates the guitar's distortion, so it is hard to tell which instrument is playing. The drum solo in \"One Word\" is composed around cues, not simply improvised.","A commercial peak, it was praised at the time for its power and precision. Later critics sometimes find it colder than The Inner Mounting Flame; others consider it the band's best-played album. Its influence on rock guitarists and on the jazz-rock of the late seventies is widely acknowledged, and the title track remains a benchmark for guitar-violin unison.","Start with the title track and listen for the gong opening. Then \"Thousand Island Park\" as relief. Compare its driving metres with Stravinsky's Rite of Spring and its unison fury with Bartók's Miraculous Mandarin."],"return-to-forever-romantic-warrior":["Six instrumental pieces on a medieval-fantasy theme, played by a quartet of virtuosos: Chick Corea on keyboards and synthesisers, Al Di Meola on guitars, Stanley Clarke on bass and Lenny White on drums. \"Medieval Overture\" opens with fanfare-like synthesiser brass; the title track is played on acoustic instruments, a gentle ballad turned into a quest; the closing \"Duel of the Jester and the Tyrant\" is a long, twisting composed showpiece of unison runs and sudden changes of mood.","Recorded in early 1976 at Caribou Ranch in the Colorado mountains, a studio used by rock bands, and released by Columbia. Return to Forever had turned from the soft Latin band of 1972 into a loud electric quartet competing with Mahavishnu and with progressive rock groups such as Yes and Emerson, Lake and Palmer. Polyphonic and portable synthesisers let Corea imitate brass and strings on stage.","Each member contributed writing: Stanley Clarke's \"The Magician\" and Lenny White's \"Sorceress\" sit beside Corea's pieces, which is one reason the album sounds like a suite of characters rather than a string of solos. Listen to how much of \"Duel of the Jester and the Tyrant\" is written out note for note: fusion here behaves like chamber music, with improvisation squeezed into short windows.","It became the band's best-selling album and a favourite of musicians, while many jazz critics mocked its sword-and-sorcery imagery and its closeness to progressive rock. Admirers point to the precision of the writing and playing; detractors hear fusion at its most grandiose. It remains a rite of passage for young guitarists and bassists.","Start with the acoustic title track, then \"Duel of the Jester and the Tyrant\" for the full machine. Follow a single instrument, Clarke's bass is the clearest guide. Compare with the medieval music it imagines, Machaut's songs, and with Stravinsky's Soldier's Tale, another tale of characters told by a small virtuoso band."],"jaco-pastorius-jaco-pastorius":["The debut album of the bassist who rewrote the instrument. It opens with \"Donna Lee\", the bebop line from Charlie Parker's circle, played at speed on fretless electric bass with only Don Alias's congas. \"Portrait of Tracy\" is built almost entirely from harmonics, ringing like a music box. \"Come On, Come Over\" is hard funk with vocals by Sam and Dave; \"Continuum\" is a slow, singing ballad. Herbie Hancock, Wayne Shorter and the Brecker brothers guest.","Pastorius, a young musician from Florida who had played in local R&B and pop bands, was produced by Bobby Colomby of Blood, Sweat and Tears for Epic. He had pulled the frets out of his Fender Jazz bass himself and filled the slots with wood, creating a voice-like sliding tone. The album was released just as he joined Weather Report, which quickly made him famous.","The whole of \"Portrait of Tracy\" uses harmonics, notes produced by lightly touching the string at precise points, so the bass sounds like a bell or a high keyboard. It is written for his first wife, Tracy. On \"Opus Pocus\" Othello Molineaux's steel drums join Wayne Shorter's soprano: a Caribbean colour Pastorius loved from his Florida years.","Received with astonishment by musicians, it transformed the electric bass from a supporting instrument into a solo voice, and bass players still treat it as a textbook. Critics sometimes note that the album is uneven, a showcase jumping between styles. Pastorius's later struggles with illness and his early death in 1987 have given the record a poignant weight.","Start with \"Donna Lee\" and remind yourself the bass is the only melodic instrument. Then \"Portrait of Tracy\" with headphones for the harmonics. Compare with Weather Report's Heavy Weather and Pat Metheny's Bright Size Life, both of which he played on."],"metheny-bright-size-life":["A trio of guitar, fretless bass and drums playing eight light, open, singing pieces. Pat Metheny's guitar has a warm, clean tone and a melodic, almost folk-like way of phrasing. Jaco Pastorius plays counter-melodies rather than bass lines, and Bob Moses's drums are loose and responsive. The title track is bright and springy; \"Unity Village\" rolls; \"Midwestern Nights Dream\" is quiet and wide. It closes with a medley of Ornette Coleman tunes.","Recorded in December 1975 in Ludwigsburg, Germany, for ECM, when Metheny was only twenty-one and teaching at Berklee after playing with vibraphonist Gary Burton. Metheny came from Lee's Summit, Missouri, and wanted a sound that reflected the open landscapes of the Midwest, not New York. ECM's clear, spacious recording style suited that ambition.","Listen to the chords on the title track: Metheny often plays open voicings, with strings ringing together like a folk guitar, which gives the music its brightness. The Ornette Coleman medley at the end is a clue: Metheny admired Coleman's melodic freedom, and would later record Song X with him. Pastorius here plays mostly in the upper register, like a second guitar.","Modestly successful on release, it grew into one of the most admired guitar trio records in jazz, a model for students of melodic improvisation. Some critics saw ECM's sound as too polished; others heard a genuine new pastoral voice in American jazz. It is now routinely listed among the essential guitar albums.","Start with the title track and hum along; the melodies are singable. Then \"Midwestern Nights Dream\" for the landscape. Compare with Copland's Appalachian Spring, another American music of wide open space, and with Jaco Pastorius's own album from the same year."],"metheny-as-falls-wichita-so-falls-wichita-falls":["The first side is a single twenty-minute title piece, a slow-building sound picture of synthesiser drones, voices, distant crowd sounds and a repeating melodic cell that grows into something vast and ominous. The second side holds shorter songs: \"Ozark\" is a bouncing piano piece, \"September Fifteenth\" a slow elegy, \"It's for You\" joyful, and \"Estupenda Graça\" a meditation on \"Amazing Grace\". Naná Vasconcelos's berimbau and voice add colour throughout.","Made by Pat Metheny with his keyboard partner Lyle Mays and the Brazilian percussionist Naná Vasconcelos, and released on ECM in 1981. Polyphonic synthesisers were becoming available to musicians, and Metheny and Mays used them to create textures closer to minimalism and film score than to jazz. The album is credited to Metheny and Mays together.","\"September Fifteenth\" is dedicated to the pianist Bill Evans, who died on 15 September 1980; Mays's piano voicings deliberately echo Evans's style. In the title piece, listen for the long ostinato underneath the drama: like much minimalist music, it holds still while the colours around it shift, which is why its crescendo feels so large.","Something of a cult favourite, it is admired by fans of ambient and minimalist music as well as jazz listeners. Critics have described the title track as a precursor of ambient and post-rock; others find it too close to film music. It has stayed in print and is often cited by musicians outside jazz.","Put on the title track with headphones and do not skip; the build takes time. Then play \"September Fifteenth\" and think of Bill Evans. Compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Philip Glass's Music in Twelve Parts, other slow-building works of the same decade."],"jarrett-belonging":["Six pieces by Keith Jarrett's so-called European Quartet: Jan Garbarek's saxophone, cool and piercing, over Jarrett's piano, Palle Danielsson's bass and Jon Christensen's drums. \"Spiral Dance\" is short and bouncing; \"Blossom\" is spacious and lyrical; \"'Long as You Know You're Living Yours\" is a warm, gospel-tinged groove; the title piece is a brief hymn. The feel is airy, with long notes, open space and a melody-first approach, and Garbarek's tone is the signature.","Recorded in Oslo in April 1974 for ECM, the German label whose producer Manfred Eicher had a vision of clean, spacious sound. Jarrett was also leading an American quartet with Dewey Redman and Charlie Haden at the time, a fiercer, more blues-rooted group. The European Quartet gave him a different outlet: Nordic players who were drawn to folk song and open space.","Listen to how often Christensen plays around the beat rather than on it, with cymbal colour and wash instead of steady time; this is the Scandinavian drum approach that became part of the ECM sound. Jarrett's piano on \"'Long as You Know\" slips into gospel phrasing, a reminder of his American roots even in the most European of settings.","Warmly reviewed at the time, it has become one of the most admired ECM records and a favourite among fans who find the label's sound at its most balanced here. Some critics found the music too cool, too European; others see it as the start of a distinct Nordic jazz tradition. The quartet's later albums, including Nude Ants, have added to its standing.","Start with \"'Long as You Know You're Living Yours\" for the groove, then \"Blossom\" for the space. Notice how Garbarek holds long notes with little vibrato. Compare with Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel, another ECM-era music of stillness, and with The Köln Concert from the next year."],"jarrett-the-koln-concert":["A solo piano concert, entirely improvised, released as four sections across a double album. The first part moves from a simple, rocking figure to long passages over two repeated left-hand chords, with melodies that sound like folk songs or hymns. The later parts range from gospel to rhapsody to hushed lyricism. Jarrett hums, stamps and groans. What is recognisable is the rolling left-hand ostinato under the singing right hand.","Performed on 24 January 1975 at the Cologne Opera House, organised by a seventeen-year-old promoter, Vera Brandes. Jarrett arrived exhausted from travelling, with back pain, to find the wrong instrument: a small, worn piano with weak bass and brittle top notes. He nearly refused to play. The concert was recorded for ECM, which had built a reputation for clear, intimate piano recordings.","The opening notes are said to echo the chime used in the opera house to summon audiences back to their seats, which drew a laugh. Listen to how Jarrett avoids the piano's harsh upper register and leans on the middle and repeated bass figures: much of the famous sound came from working around a bad instrument. For years he declined requests to publish a transcription.","It became the best-selling solo piano recording in jazz and one of ECM's biggest records, running into millions of copies. Some critics dismissed it as new-age wallpaper; others hailed it as a new kind of spontaneous composition. Jarrett himself grew weary of its fame. Scholars now discuss it alongside minimalism and Romantic piano improvisation as well as jazz.","Start with Part I and give it half an hour, following the repeated left hand. Then listen for the moments where a melody seems to arrive from nowhere. Compare with Satie's Gymnopédies for stillness, Glass's Mad Rush for repetition, and Jarrett's own recording of Bach's Goldberg Variations for his classical side."],"shorter-speak-no-evil":["Six compositions, dark and elegant, played by Wayne Shorter's tenor saxophone with Freddie Hubbard's trumpet, Herbie Hancock, Ron Carter and Elvin Jones. \"Witch Hunt\" opens with a stark horn fanfare; \"Fee-Fi-Fo-Fum\" borrows from fairy tale; \"Dance Cadaverous\" is a slow, eerie waltz; \"Infant Eyes\" is a ballad of great tenderness. The tunes are short, harmonically unusual and haunting, and Shorter's saxophone has a dry, slightly veiled tone that makes them sound like riddles.","Recorded on Christmas Eve 1964 at Rudy Van Gelder's studio and released in 1966. Shorter had just joined Miles Davis's quintet after years with Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers, and he was writing a stream of compositions for both. The mid-sixties were full of anxiety, the civil rights struggle and the Vietnam war, and the record's imagery of witches, spells and fairy-tale threats has often been read against that background.","The chord progressions move by unexpected steps, so a tune seems to drift into another key and then slip back; this is why jazz students find Shorter tunes hard to improvise on and so easy to remember. Elvin Jones, John Coltrane's drummer, plays with the Davis rhythm section of Hancock and Carter, joining two of jazz's great bands on one record. The cover shows Shorter's wife at the time.","Admired from the start, it has become one of the most cherished albums in the Blue Note catalogue, and \"Infant Eyes\" a widely played standard. Critics consider it the peak of Shorter's Blue Note period and a model of post-bop composition. Debate turns on whether it is the best of his albums of this time or merely the most famous.","Start with \"Infant Eyes\" and hear how few notes Shorter needs. Then \"Witch Hunt\" for the fanfare. Compare with Hancock's Maiden Voyage, recorded months later with almost the same band, and with Bartók's Sixth String Quartet for another music of dark, questioning harmony."],"shorter-native-dancer":["Nine songs made with the Brazilian singer Milton Nascimento, whose soaring falsetto opens the album on \"Ponta de Areia\". Shorter's soprano saxophone circles the voice; Herbie Hancock plays piano and electric keyboards; Brazilian musicians provide gentle, rocking grooves. The music moves between Nascimento's songs and Shorter's own pieces, such as \"Beauty and the Beast\" and \"Diana\". The overall sound is light, warm and melancholy, closer to song than to fusion's usual volume.","Recorded in Burbank, California, in 1974, while Shorter was also a member of Weather Report. He had fallen in love with Nascimento's music, from the Minas Gerais movement known as the Clube da Esquina, and invited him to America. Brazilian music had been familiar in jazz since bossa nova, but Nascimento's songs were stranger and more hymn-like, and this album introduced him to many American listeners.","\"Ponta de Areia\" is a song about a railway line in Minas Gerais that was shut down, leaving its towns isolated: its sad optimism is political as well as personal. Listen to the way Shorter plays under and around Nascimento's voice rather than above it, a deliberate humility. Some of the album's songs had already appeared on Nascimento's own records in Portuguese.","Little noticed by jazz critics at first, it became one of Shorter's best-loved records and a founding document of Brazilian-jazz fusion. Critics now see it as far ahead of the idea of world music. Its songs have been covered by many singers, and it made Nascimento a name among jazz musicians.","Start with \"Ponta de Areia\" and let the voice carry you. Then \"Beauty and the Beast\" for Shorter's own writing. Compare with Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for another Brazilian voice over light accompaniment, and Esperanza Spalding's Chamber Music Society, which continues the conversation."],"shorter-emanon":["A three-disc album in two parts. The first disc sets Wayne Shorter's quartet, with Danilo Pérez, John Patitucci and Brian Blade, against the Orpheus Chamber Orchestra in four works including \"Pegasus\" and \"Prometheus Unbound\", orchestral colour and improvisation braided together. The other two discs capture the quartet live in London, playing long, shape-shifting pieces where themes appear only in fragments. Shorter's saxophone, now spare and oracular, speaks in short phrases.","Released in 2018, when Shorter was in his mid-eighties, it came packaged with a graphic novel written by Shorter with Monica Sly and illustrated by Randy DuBurke, about a philosopher-hero who travels between worlds. Shorter had long been fascinated by science fiction and comics, and by the idea of music as philosophy. The quartet had played together since 2000, developing a style of collective improvisation without set solos.","Emanon is \"no name\" spelt backwards, a title Dizzy Gillespie had used for a bebop tune in the forties, and Shorter meant it as a statement about a hero with no fixed identity. Listen in the orchestral pieces for how written passages for strings and winds hand over to the quartet mid-phrase, so it is hard to tell where composition ends and improvisation begins.","Widely praised and awarded a Grammy for best jazz instrumental album, it was hailed as a late-life summation. Some critics found the orchestral pieces more successful than the live discs; others the reverse. It stands as one of the most ambitious jazz-orchestra projects of recent years.","Start with the orchestral disc and \"Pegasus\"; listen for the moment the quartet takes over from the strings. Then dip into the live discs. Compare with Ellington's Black, Brown and Beige, Gershwin's Rhapsody in Blue and Kamasi Washington's The Epic."],"mehldau-songs-art-of-the-trio-vol-3":["Ten tracks of a piano trio thinking in counterpoint. Brad Mehldau's left hand is as busy and melodic as his right, so tunes often seem to be played by two pianists. The programme mixes originals such as \"Song-Song\" and \"Unrequited\" with standards like \"Bewitched, Bothered and Bewildered\" and two rock songs: Radiohead's \"Exit Music (For a Film)\" and Nick Drake's \"River Man\". Larry Grenadier's bass and Jorge Rossy's brushes are soft and supple.","Recorded in 1998 for Warner Bros., the third in a series of trio albums that made Mehldau the most influential jazz pianist of his generation. He grew up playing both jazz and classical music, and was open to rock songs at a time when many jazz musicians treated them with suspicion. The album appeared only a year after Radiohead's OK Computer.","Mehldau's two hands often play independent lines, like a Bach invention or a Brahms intermezzo, and he has spoken about the influence of German Romantic piano music; the last track is even called \"Sehnsucht\", longing. Listen to \"Exit Music\": he keeps the song's slow build and dark harmony but lets the left hand carry a counter-melody the original never had.","Widely praised, it made Mehldau's version of \"Exit Music\" a new standard and helped legitimise rock songs as jazz material. Some critics found his style too introspective or Romantic; many more regarded it as a fresh direction for the piano trio. The album is now a staple for pianists studying independence of hands.","Start with \"Exit Music (For a Film)\", then compare it with Radiohead's original. Listen to the left hand. Follow with Brahms's Intermezzo Op. 118 No. 2 and Schumann's Kreisleriana for the Romantic roots, and The Köln Concert for Jarrett's different kind of piano storytelling."],"kamasi-washington-the-epic":["Three discs and nearly three hours: a ten-piece band with two drummers, two basses and keyboards, plus a string orchestra and a choir. Kamasi Washington's tenor saxophone plays long, spiritual, searching solos over big grooves and wordless choral swells. The record ranges from the soaring \"Change of the Guard\" to a lush arrangement of Debussy's \"Clair de Lune\" and \"Malcolm's Theme\", which includes Ossie Davis's eulogy for Malcolm X. The sound is huge, warm and ecstatic.","Washington and his West Coast Get Down collective of Los Angeles musicians had played together since childhood. He had just played on Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly, and the album was released by Flying Lotus's electronic label Brainfeeder. Both records appeared the same spring, at a time of protests over police killings, and they helped bring jazz back to a young audience.","Listen to the \"Clair de Lune\" arrangement: Debussy's piano piece is rescored for sax, choir and band, and the famous melody appears in a slow, swaying groove that nods to 1970s soul. The two bassists, one acoustic and one electric (Thundercat), and the two drummers are spread across the stereo field. The spirit of Alice and John Coltrane runs underneath.","Hailed as a landmark of jazz's revival and a crossover success, it topped many year-end lists. Some critics found its length and grandeur excessive, and argued that it looked back to 1970s spiritual jazz rather than forward. Its influence on the new wave of jazz festivals and audiences is widely acknowledged.","Start with \"Change of the Guard\" and ride the build. Then \"Clair de Lune\", comparing it with Debussy's piano original. Don't try to take in all three discs at once; treat it like a trilogy. Follow with Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly for the hip-hop side of the same scene."],"esperanza-spalding-chamber-music-society":["Eleven tracks mixing jazz trio, string trio and voice. Esperanza Spalding sings and plays double bass, often both at once, in melodies that leap and bend. The opening \"Little Fly\" sets William Blake's poem to a spare string accompaniment; \"Chacarera\" draws on Argentine folk rhythm; \"Apple Blossom\" features Milton Nascimento. The sound is intimate, like a salon concert, with strings used for colour and counterpoint rather than lush backing.","Released in 2010, it was Spalding's third album and part of a planned pair with the later Radio Music Society. She grew up in Portland, Oregon, trained on violin as a child, and studied and taught at Berklee. The album's title points to the European chamber tradition, and she arranged the string parts with a sense of classical intimacy.","\"Little Fly\" sets Blake's \"The Fly\" from Songs of Experience, and the strings imitate the fluttering insect. Listen for how often Spalding's bass line and vocal line diverge, a feat of coordination most musicians cannot manage. The Nascimento duet links her to the Brazilian thread that runs back through Wayne Shorter's Native Dancer.","Well received, it became famous when Spalding won the Grammy for Best New Artist in 2011, the first jazz musician to do so, beating Justin Bieber and prompting a storm of online comment. Some critics found the album too gentle; others praised its independence from both pop and jazz formulas.","Start with \"Little Fly\" and read Blake's poem first. Then \"Chacarera\" for the rhythm. Compare with Ravel's String Quartet for the string colours and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for voice and strings, and with Native Dancer for the Nascimento link."],"robert-glasper-black-radio":["Twelve tracks where jazz musicians play R&B and hip-hop as their own music. The Robert Glasper Experiment, with Casey Benjamin on sax and vocoder, Derrick Hodge on bass and Chris Dave on drums, backs a series of singers and rappers: Erykah Badu on a slow \"Afro Blue\", Lalah Hathaway on Sade's \"Cherish the Day\", Yasiin Bey on the title track, and a vocoder version of Nirvana's \"Smells Like Teen Spirit\". The drums play like a sampled beat, subtly off the grid.","Released on Blue Note in 2012, it grew from Glasper's double life as a jazz pianist and as a sideman for hip-hop and neo-soul artists. Chris Dave's drumming reflects the influence of J Dilla's loose, dragging beats, played by hand. The album came at a moment when young jazz musicians grew up with hip-hop as their first music, and it announced a new mainstream.","The title, from Yasiin Bey, refers to an airliner's black box: the thing that survives the crash, as Black American music survives. Listen to the drums: Chris Dave places beats slightly early or late, imitating producers who shifted samples by hand off the grid. \"Afro Blue\" was a Mongo Santamaría tune later sung by Abbey Lincoln and played by Coltrane.","It won the Grammy for Best R&B Album, which surprised jazz and R&B listeners alike, and sparked debate over whether it was jazz at all. Glasper argued that the category mattered less than the conversation. It is now seen as a turning point in the merging of jazz and hip-hop.","Start with \"Afro Blue\" with Erykah Badu, then \"Cherish the Day\". Notice Chris Dave's drumming. Compare with Head Hunters for an earlier jazz-funk crossover, and Kamasi Washington's The Epic for another side of the same generation."],"gogo-penguin-v2-0":["An acoustic piano trio that plays like electronic music. Chris Illingworth's piano repeats short, crystalline patterns; Nick Blacka's double bass locks into tight, bowed or plucked riffs; Rob Turner's drums imitate the skittering breakbeats of drum and bass. Pieces such as \"Murmuration\", \"Garden Dog Barbecue\" and \"Hopopono\" build by accumulation, like dance tracks, with melancholy chords floating over furious rhythm. The sound is clean, precise and cinematic.","Released by the Manchester label Gondwana in 2014, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize, rare for a jazz trio. The three musicians grew up on electronic artists such as Aphex Twin and Squarepusher as much as jazz, and studied at Manchester's music colleges. Their approach reflects a generation for whom programmed beats were as natural as swing.","Listen to Turner's drums on \"Garden Dog Barbecue\": he plays patterns designed to sound like edited, looped breakbeats, humanly reproducing a machine. The piano parts owe more to Steve Reich and Philip Glass than to bebop, and the trio later wrote and performed their own live score for Godfrey Reggio's film Koyaanisqatsi, replacing Glass's.","Warmly reviewed and lifted by the Mercury nomination, it brought new listeners to jazz. Some critics argued the music had little to do with jazz; others praised its synthesis of minimalism, electronica and the piano trio. It helped define a new British jazz scene alongside groups in London and Manchester.","Start with \"Hopopono\" and follow the drums. Compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians and Philip Glass's Glassworks for the pattern-building, and with Brad Mehldau for another modern trio. Listen loud, like a dance record."],"charley-patton-pony-blues":["Three minutes of one man doing the work of a band. Patton sets a hard, uneven stomp on the low strings, slaps the guitar body between phrases, and sings in a cracked bark so far back in the throat that half the words arrive as sound rather than language. The verses run on the usual twelve-bar frame but he stretches or clips bars at will, so the beat keeps stumbling and recovering. Between vocal lines the guitar answers in short bent figures, as though two people were talking.","Recorded in June 1929 at Paramount's session in Richmond, Indiana, after the Jackson record dealer H. C. Speir sent Patton north. He was already the best-known musician on the Dockery Farms circuit in the Mississippi Delta, playing plantation dances, picnics and juke joints for a mixed Black and white audience. Paramount was chasing the race-record boom Bessie Smith had opened six years earlier, and had discovered that a single man with a guitar cost far less to record than a band.","Listen for how little of the rhythm comes from strumming. Patton hits the strings, damps them, then knocks the top of the instrument, so the pulse is partly percussion on wood. The song is also a template rather than a text: the same melody and stomp carry a dozen other Patton titles, and several lines here turn up, barely altered, in records made by Howlin' Wolf twenty-five years later.","Paramount's shellac was notoriously poor and surviving copies hiss badly, which kept Patton's reputation behind Robert Johnson's for decades; the 1929 sides only became widely audible with post-war reissues and modern transfers. Scholars now treat him as the earliest documented Delta stylist rather than an inventor working alone, since he clearly inherited a local tradition. Arguments continue over how much of the murk is Paramount's fault and how much is Patton's deliberate roughness.","Play it twice: once for the words, which you will not entirely catch, and once ignoring them, following only the guitar's replies. Then put on Son House's 'My Black Mama' from a year later and hear the same engine driven harder. For a way in, find a remastered transfer rather than a raw disc rip — the difference in what you can hear is large."],"charley-patton-high-water-everywhere":["A two-part record, one song split across both sides of a disc, and the nearest thing the country blues has to reportage. Patton names Delta towns one after another — Sumner, Leland, Greenville, Rosedale — as the water takes them, and the guitar hammers a single insistent figure underneath, broken by slapped percussion on the body of the instrument. His voice climbs in the second part until it is closer to shouting than singing. There is no chorus and no resolution; the flood simply keeps coming.","The Great Mississippi Flood of 1927 broke the levees and put a region the size of a small country under water, displacing hundreds of thousands, most of them Black sharecroppers, many of them held in refugee camps and made to work on the levees at gunpoint. Patton recorded this in October 1929, two years after, for Paramount. It was one of the first commercial records to treat a disaster as news rather than as metaphor, and it helped push the Great Migration story onto shellac.","Follow the geography. The towns are listed in a route that moves the singer north and east ahead of the water, so the record is a map of an actual escape. Notice too that Patton drums on the guitar hardest exactly where the words describe the levee breaking — the instrument is doing the flood while the voice does the reporting.","Long treated as folklore, the record is now read by historians as testimony, cross-checked against relief records and newspaper accounts of the camps. Its authority rests on Patton having been in the Delta when it happened. Debate continues over whether the second part's near-shout is exhaustion at the end of a long session or a deliberate rise — the two parts were almost certainly cut back to back.","Hear both parts in order and without a gap; split across two sides, they were designed as one arc. Keep a map of the Mississippi Delta open and follow the town names. Then listen to Memphis Minnie's 'When the Levee Breaks', made the same year about the same flood, and notice how differently a duo handles the subject."],"charley-patton-down-the-dirt-road-blues":["A travelling song, and one of the clearest recordings of Patton's guitar hand. The bass strings walk a repeating figure while the treble strings snap out a high answering phrase; between them the voice growls verses about leaving, about a country where every day feels like a wrong day, about going where he has never been. The tempo sits slightly faster than a walk. Patton interrupts himself with spoken asides, half-audible, addressed to nobody in the room.","Cut at the same 1929 Paramount sessions as 'Pony Blues'. Patton spent his life moving between Delta plantations, and mobility for a Black sharecropper in Mississippi was both the one available freedom and a legal hazard: vagrancy laws and debt peonage made leaving a plantation dangerous. The record was also released under the alternative title 'Over the Sea Blues', a reminder of how loosely Paramount handled the material it bought.","Count the beats and you will keep losing the thread: Patton adds or drops a half-bar wherever the words need room, a habit that vanished once blues bands had to play together. The spoken interjections are the other clue — juke-joint practice, where the singer kept talking to the dancers, preserved by accident on a studio disc.","Once dismissed as a lesser cousin of 'Pony Blues' because it shares much of its material, the song is now valued precisely for that: it shows a working musician recycling a frame rather than composing fixed songs. Transcribers still disagree about several lines, and the two issued titles have caused decades of discographical confusion.","Ignore the words for one pass and follow only the low strings; the walking figure is the ancestor of a thousand boogie bass lines. Then listen for the asides. Pair it with Florence Price's First Symphony, written in the same decade from the same Southern material, and hear what happens when the idiom is scored for an orchestra."],"son-house-my-black-mama":["Two parts on two sides of one disc, and a single unbroken argument. House plays bottleneck slide in an open tuning, so the guitar answers every vocal line with a whining, voice-like figure that slides up to its note rather than arriving at it. The singing is loud and unrelenting, closer to a preacher at full volume than to crooning. Part two turns to a death — a telegram, a body, a coffin — and the guitar's answers get shorter and harder as it goes.","Recorded in May 1930 at Paramount's Grafton, Wisconsin studio, on a trip Charley Patton arranged: Patton, House, Willie Brown and the pianist Louise Johnson drove up together. House had been ordained as a Baptist preacher and had served time at Parchman Farm; he came to the blues late and never stopped treating it as the devil's competition for the same congregation. The sides sold almost nothing — the Depression had gutted the race-record market within months.","Part two contains the material that House later rebuilt, nearly line for line, as 'Death Letter Blues' thirty-five years on. Hearing the two together is like watching a photograph taken twice. Listen also to how the slide never quite lands on a fixed pitch: House lets it hover a fraction under, which is exactly what gives the blues note its ache.","Paramount pressed so few copies that for decades the record existed mainly as a rumour among collectors; the surviving copies are worn almost to noise. Its status rose sharply when House was rediscovered in 1964 and could demonstrate what the crackle had hidden. Scholars now treat these 1930 sides as the missing link between Patton's stomp and Robert Johnson's tighter, more written songs.","Start with part two, where the story is, then go back for part one. Listen for the exact moment the slide enters after a sung line — House almost never lets voice and guitar sound together. Follow it immediately with his 1965 'Death Letter Blues' to hear the same song at a different speed and a different age."],"son-house-preachin-the-blues":["House announces that he is going to get religion, join the Baptist church, and be a preacher so he will not have to work — and then spends two record sides demolishing the idea. The slide guitar hammers a single repeating figure at a hard, driving tempo; the voice rides over it in long shouted phrases with the cadence and the breath control of an actual sermon. The joke turns bitter as it goes: the church, the deacon, the sister in the congregation all come in for it.","Also from the Grafton sessions of May 1930. House had genuinely preached, and the conflict between pulpit and juke joint was the central fact of his life rather than a pose; he gave up the blues more than once. In the Delta of 1930 the two institutions competed for the same Saturday-night and Sunday-morning crowd, and a bluesman in church was a scandal. Paramount, chasing sales, seems to have liked the provocation.","The song's structure is a sermon's, not a ballad's: House states his text, repeats it, elaborates, then returns to it louder. Listen for the places where he stops singing and simply declaims over the guitar. Bessie Smith had recorded a different song of the same title in 1927, which makes House's version a rejoinder as much as a title.","Later writers took the song as evidence of a sacred–secular split in Black Southern music; more recent scholarship reads it the other way, as proof that the two shared a single delivery and a single audience. Its reputation grew enormously after 1964, when House performed a version on film and the sermon-like construction became visible as well as audible.","Listen for breath. House phrases exactly as a preacher does, pushing to the end of a line and then snatching air. Compare it with Blind Willie Johnson's 'John the Revelator', which uses the same guitar tradition for the opposite purpose, and with Gershwin's 'It Ain't Necessarily So', where the same doubt is set on Broadway."],"son-house-death-letter-blues":["A letter arrives saying the woman he loves is dead; he takes the train, finds her laid out on the cooling board, and cannot make himself look at her face. House sings it at the top of his voice with a steel-bodied guitar answering in slide figures that bend and scrape. The tempo lurches — he speeds up when the words press and stops outright between verses — and near the end he abandons the chords entirely and just beats the strings. Four minutes, no chorus, no consolation.","Recorded in 1965 for Columbia after three young collectors tracked House down in Rochester, New York in 1964; he had not played seriously in twenty years and had to be taught his own records back. The folk revival was at its height, Newport was booking rediscovered Delta singers alongside Bob Dylan, and a white college audience was hearing this music live for the first time. House was sixty-three and drinking heavily; the playing is unsteady and all the more frightening for it.","The song is a rebuild of part two of his 1930 'My Black Mama', slowed and expanded. Listen for the bar where he loses the count and simply waits — the guitar drops out, the voice keeps going, and the silence is the most exposed moment on the record. The White Stripes' 2000 version copies that hesitation deliberately.","Some critics hold that the 1965 recordings are diminished next to the 1930 sides and the Lomax field tapes; others argue the age and the unsteadiness are the point, and that this is the definitive performance. What is not disputed is its reach: it moved through the British blues bands into the garage rock revival, and is now probably the most covered Delta blues after Robert Johnson's.","Start here if you start anywhere in the Delta blues — it is the most immediately overwhelming of these records and the sound quality is modern. Watch the surviving film of House performing rather than only listening; the physical effort explains the sound. Then go back to 'My Black Mama' and hear the same song thirty-five years younger."],"robert-johnson-cross-road-blues":["Under three minutes, and one of the tightest things in the country blues. Johnson plays slide in an open tuning, keeping a thumping bass with his thumb while the bottleneck cries above it, so the record sounds like two guitars and a foot. The words are plain and terrifying in their plainness: he kneels at a crossroads asking for mercy, tries to flag a ride, and the sun goes down with nobody stopping. A Black man caught outside after dark in 1930s Mississippi needed no supernatural explanation.","Recorded in San Antonio in November 1936, in a makeshift studio set up in the Gunter Hotel by the producer Don Law for the American Record Corporation. Johnson was twenty-five, a travelling musician who had already absorbed Son House, Charley Patton and Lonnie Johnson, plus whatever was on the jukeboxes. He reportedly played facing the corner of the room, which engineers took for shyness and modern ears hear as a man using the wall as a reflector.","The devil is not in the lyric. Every supernatural reading of this song was added later, largely after 1966 writing built a legend from a remark of Son House's; what the words actually describe is sundown law and the danger of being stranded. Listen instead to the guitar's timing: Johnson pushes the slide figure a fraction ahead of the thumb, and that tiny disagreement is what makes the record twitch.","The record sold modestly in 1937 and was almost forgotten until Columbia's 1961 reissue. Cream's 1968 rewrite, 'Crossroads', made it a rock standard and simultaneously buried the original's meaning under guitar-hero mythology. Scholarship since the 1990s has worked hard to separate Johnson the working musician from the legend, and this song has been the main battleground.","Listen once for the thumb alone; it never stops, and everything else is hung on it. Then compare Cream's version and notice what a full band has to add and what it loses. Seek a transfer made from a clean shellac copy at the correct speed — several early reissues ran the discs fast, which raised Johnson's voice."],"robert-johnson-hellhound-on-my-trail":["The most unsettled of Johnson's records and the least regular. He keeps blurring the bar lines, so the song seems to hurry and then hang back; the guitar figure is high, thin and repeated like a nervous tic. The voice climbs into a near-falsetto on the word 'trail'. Images accumulate without a story: rain falling on his trail, hot ashes, a woman sprinkling powder round his door, leaves shaking on the tree. Three minutes that never settle into a groove.","Recorded in Dallas in June 1937, at the second and last of Johnson's two sets of sessions; he was dead fourteen months later, near Greenwood, Mississippi, aged twenty-seven. The imagery comes from hoodoo practice widespread in the Delta — goofer dust sprinkled at a threshold was an ordinary act, not a metaphor — and from the older song tradition of Skip James, whose 'Devil Got My Woman' Johnson clearly knew.","The melody and much of the harmonic shape are lifted from Skip James's 1931 'Devil Got My Woman', a record almost nobody owned; Johnson must have heard it and kept it. Listen also for how rarely he repeats a bar count. This is one of the few blues recordings where the irregularity is plainly deliberate rather than the residue of dance-hall habit.","Often described as the moment the blues becomes interior rather than social, which is probably too neat: the imagery is communal, not private. Its reputation was made by the 1961 reissue and by writers who heard modern dread in it. Performance scholars argue over the pitch — if Johnson's discs were cut slightly slow, the famous high vocal would have been a shade lower in the room.","Do not try to tap along; let the bar lines go. Listen for the guitar's high repeated figure and how it refuses to resolve. Then play Skip James's 'Devil Got My Woman' and hear where it came from, and Schubert's Winterreise for another traveller walking away from a house with something behind him."],"robert-johnson-love-in-vain":["A farewell at a railway station, told in four short verses and almost no adjectives: he carries her suitcase, the train pulls out, two lights on the back of it — one blue, one red — and then he is standing there. The guitar keeps a slow, unhurried walking pattern with none of the agitation of his faster records. At the end the words run out and he hums the last verse, which is the most expressive thing on the record.","From the June 1937 Dallas sessions, the last Johnson recorded. It was issued in 1939, after his death. Its skeleton comes from a much older song associated with Leroy Carr, the polished Indianapolis singer-pianist whose records Johnson studied; the borrowing is typical, and shows how far a Delta musician's listening reached beyond the Delta by the late 1930s. The railroad departure was by then a standard blues situation and a literal one for a migrating population.","The hummed final verse is not an afterthought. Johnson hums an entire chorus in place of words, which nobody had made a habit of on record; it makes the silence of the platform audible. Listen also to the two lights on the train, blue and red — the lyric gives the colours and lets you supply the meaning, which is why the image has survived.","The Rolling Stones recorded it in 1969, credited it oddly, and turned it into one of the most familiar blues in rock; a generation met Johnson through that version. Critics divide over whether the Stones' country-rock reading honours or flattens it. Johnson's own take is now generally reckoned his most controlled performance and the best evidence against the wild-man legend.","Wait for the hum; everything before it is preparation. Listen for how steady the guitar stays while the words fall apart. Then hear the Rolling Stones' version, and Son House's 'Death Letter Blues', where a train also carries the singer towards a loss he cannot fix."],"robert-johnson-king-of-the-delta-blues-singers":["Sixteen of Johnson's twenty-nine recorded songs, drawn from the 1936 and 1937 sessions and sequenced onto one long-playing record: 'Cross Road Blues', 'Hellhound on My Trail', 'Come On in My Kitchen', 'Preachin' Blues', 'Walkin' Blues', 'If I Had Possession Over Judgment Day'. Heard in a row rather than one side at a time, the range is the surprise — dance numbers, comic songs and hoodoo nightmares by the same man. The sleeve carried a painting of a faceless figure, because no photograph was known.","Assembled by Frank Driggs for Columbia in 1961 from masters the company had owned since it absorbed Johnson's original label. Almost nobody outside a small circle of collectors had heard these songs; the 78s were rare and Johnson had been dead twenty-three years. The album landed in the middle of the American folk revival and reached Greenwich Village and, within a few years, London, where Keith Richards, Eric Clapton and Brian Jones treated it as scripture.","The faceless cover is the whole story of the reissue: the compilers had no image of Johnson and filled the gap with mystery, which is how the legend hardened. Listen also to the false starts and studio talk left on some takes — Don Law's voice, room noise, Johnson counting himself in — the few seconds that show a working session rather than a haunting.","Arguably the most influential reissue ever released: it created the British blues boom, and with it the guitar culture of the next forty years. Later scholarship has pushed back hard, arguing that the album's framing — king, mystery, devil — distorted a musician who was one of many and not the most famous in his own lifetime. The complete recordings issued in 1990 largely replaced it, and sold over a million copies.","Hear it in the 1961 running order at least once; the sequencing is part of what hit London so hard. Then move to the complete set for the alternate takes, which show how precisely Johnson repeated himself. Compare Gershwin's Porgy and Bess, which imagined the same South for Broadway two years before these sessions."]});